TIMOB-28187,12,"""Android: Add C/C++ """"prefab"""" support to Titanium AAR""","""""""*Summary:*  Android Gradle Plugin v4.0.0 added the ability to import C/C++ """"""""Prefab"""""""" packages from AAR libraries. This allows us to bundle C/C++ header files into the Titanium AAR for module builds instead of installing a separate {{./android/native}} folder on the system.  https://developer.android.com/studio/build/native-dependencies  https://google.github.io/prefab/    *Note:*  This is intended to simplify module builds and makefile generation. The C/C++ header files bundled into the Titanium AAR will not end up in the app's built APK.""""""",Improvement,"Title: Android: Add C/C++ """"prefab"""" support to Titanium AAR
Description: """"*Summary:* Android Gradle Plugin v4.0.0 added the ability to import C/C++ """"""""Prefab"""""""" packages from AAR libraries. This allows us to bundle C/C++ header files into the Titanium AAR for module builds instead of installing a separate {{./android/native}} folder on the system. https://developer.android.com/studio/build/native-dependencies https://google.github.io/prefab/ *Note:* This is intended to simplify module builds and makefile generation. The C/C++ header files bundled into the Titanium AAR will not end up in the app's built APK.""""",title+description
TIMOB-28186,12,"""macOS: Ti.Platform.osname can never return """"mac""""""","""""""In Xcode 12, there is a new checkbox """"""""Optimized for Mac UI"""""""" which can be checked. This is not possible so far. And because of that, the device idiom is always """"""""ipad"""""""". But even if the checkbox would be checked in the generated Xcode project, it would never be """"""""mac"""""""", because Titanium only checks for iPads and fallbacks to iPhones if not matching. This can be extended easily in the PlatformModule.m.""""""",Bug,"Title: macOS: Ti.Platform.osname can never return """"mac""""
Description: """"In Xcode 12, there is a new checkbox """"""""Optimized for Mac UI"""""""" which can be checked. This is not possible so far. And because of that, the device idiom is always """"""""ipad"""""""". But even if the checkbox would be checked in the generated Xcode project, it would never be """"""""mac"""""""", because Titanium only checks for iPads and fallbacks to iPhones if not matching. This can be extended easily in the PlatformModule.m.""""",title+description
TIMOB-28182,12,"""Android: WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE permission should only be applied up to Android 9""","""""""*Summary:*  The """"""""AndroidManifest.xml"""""""" permission {{WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE}} is ignored when targeting Android 10+ and running on those Android OS versions. This is because of scoped-storage and Google only allows an app to write in very particular folder on external storage now and write permission is no longer needed.  https://developer.android.com/training/data-storage/shared/media#request-permissions    When Titanium injects this permission into the """"""""AndroidManifest.xml"""""""", it should add a {{maxSdkVersion}} attribute set to Android 9. We still need permission to write photos/videos to the gallery on older OS versions.      *Note:*  We should NOT apply the """"""""maxSdkVersion"""""""" attribute if """"""""tiapp.xml"""""""" sets:  * The {{WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE}} permission. _(It should override our default.)_  * The [requestLegacyExternalStorage|https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/application-element#requestLegacyExternalStorage] attribute is set in {{<application/>}}. _(This attribute only works when targeting Android 10 and is ignored when targeting Android 11.)_  """"""",Improvement,"Title: Android: WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE permission should only be applied up to Android 9
Description: """"*Summary:* The """"""""AndroidManifest.xml"""""""" permission {{WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE}} is ignored when targeting Android 10+ and running on those Android OS versions. This is because of scoped-storage and Google only allows an app to write in very particular folder on external storage now and write permission is no longer needed. https://developer.android.com/training/data-storage/shared/media#request-permissions When Titanium injects this permission into the """"""""AndroidManifest.xml"""""""", it should add a {{maxSdkVersion}} attribute set to Android 9. We still need permission to write photos/videos to the gallery on older OS versions. *Note:* We should NOT apply the """"""""maxSdkVersion"""""""" attribute if """"""""tiapp.xml"""""""" sets: * The {{WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE}} permission. _(It should override our default.)_ * The [requestLegacyExternalStorage|https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/application-element#requestLegacyExternalStorage] attribute is set in {{<application/>}}. _(This attribute only works when targeting Android 10 and is ignored when targeting Android 11.)_ """"",title+description
TIMOB-28175,12,"""iOS: Titanium should add view controllers as children""","""""""Currently, Titanium does not properly add child view controllers to their parent, causing issues in some SDK's and could even be the reason for some random crashes from time to time. See the GitHub issue for details: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/issues/11651 """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Titanium should add view controllers as children
Description: """"Currently, Titanium does not properly add child view controllers to their parent, causing issues in some SDK's and could even be the reason for some random crashes from time to time. See the GitHub issue for details: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/issues/11651 """"",title+description
