Description: """"Titanium SDK master branch is giving build error for Xcode < 11. It is regression of [PR #11457|https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/11457].""""",title+description
TIMOB-27894,12,"""iOS: Navigation bar flickers on open (SDK 9.0.2 regression, iOS 13+)""","""""""A well known issue seems to have come back between SDK 9.0.1.GA and 9.0.2 / 9.1.0 master.    When opening a window, the navigation bar color flickers for half a second, resulting in a bad UI glitch. It can be reproduced in our app and is a show-stopper for the SDK 9 migration. Manually reverting [this commit|https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/11670/commits/84a093095332415922e58b14a6449c472355a510] fixed the issue for us.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Navigation bar flickers on open (SDK 9.0.2 regression, iOS 13+)
Description: """"A well known issue seems to have come back between SDK 9.0.1.GA and 9.0.2 / 9.1.0 master. When opening a window, the navigation bar color flickers for half a second, resulting in a bad UI glitch. It can be reproduced in our app and is a show-stopper for the SDK 9 migration. Manually reverting [this commit|https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/11670/commits/84a093095332415922e58b14a6449c472355a510] fixed the issue for us.""""",title+description
TIMOB-27893,12,"""Android: Setting views to """"opacity: 0.0"""" does still trigger touch events (parity)""","""""""On Android, setting views to {{myView.opacity = 0.0}} still triggers the touch events of the view(s) that are hidden. This does not happen for iOS and is a parity issue on Android as views that are hidden should really be hidden completely. """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Setting views to """"opacity: 0.0"""" does still trigger touch events (parity)
Description: """"On Android, setting views to {{myView.opacity = 0.0}} still triggers the touch events of the view(s) that are hidden. This does not happen for iOS and is a parity issue on Android as views that are hidden should really be hidden completely. """"",title+description
TIMOB-27889,12,"""Android: Implement Ti.UI.Shortcut""",""""""" iOS has Ti.UI.iOS.ApplicationShortcuts and android has implemented Ti.UI.ShortcutItem for shortcut. To have parity on both platform , it is discussed to move Shortcut APIs under Ti.UI.Shortcut. Following are the decided APIs -        """"""",Improvement,"Title: Android: Implement Ti.UI.Shortcut
Description: """" iOS has Ti.UI.iOS.ApplicationShortcuts and android has implemented Ti.UI.ShortcutItem for shortcut. To have parity on both platform , it is discussed to move Shortcut APIs under Ti.UI.Shortcut. Following are the decided APIs - """"",title+description
TIMOB-27888,12,"""iOS: circular references in require don't work""","""""""While Android's require implementation is able to handle circular references - iOS is not.    This is due to the underlying implementations - where Android is written in JS and mimics Node.js, while iOS is native. There's multiple possible solutions here:  - Use rollup under the hood on apps as part of our build <USER>  - Migrate iOS to use a shared require implementation with Android    I personally prefer the second, but it'd take some re-architecting of how iOS starts up - and it's unclear to me how we'd tweak Android to use a require implementation under common rather than the one baked into the the runtime (the JS is baked in as bytes and executed early).""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: circular references in require don't work
Description: """"While Android's require implementation is able to handle circular references - iOS is not. This is due to the underlying implementations - where Android is written in JS and mimics Node.js, while iOS is native. There's multiple possible solutions here: - Use rollup under the hood on apps as part of our build <USER> - Migrate iOS to use a shared require implementation with Android I personally prefer the second, but it'd take some re-architecting of how iOS starts up - and it's unclear to me how we'd tweak Android to use a require implementation under common rather than the one baked into the the runtime (the JS is baked in as bytes and executed early).""""",title+description
TIMOB-27886,12,"""iOS: Counter does not start of at 0 and different iOS versions return different results""","""""""With the following test case the counter starts of at '2' on iOS 13.4 and and starts at '1' using iOS 12.2 (Tested on iPad and iPhone Sims). The counter should be starting at 0 and only changing when an orientation has been made.    *Test Case:*       *Expected result*  Counter should start of at 0     *Actual result*  iOS 13.4 - Counter starts at 2  iOS 12.2 - Counter starts at 1""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Counter does not start of at 0 and different iOS versions return different results
Description: """"With the following test case the counter starts of at '2' on iOS 13.4 and and starts at '1' using iOS 12.2 (Tested on iPad and iPhone Sims). The counter should be starting at 0 and only changing when an orientation has been made. *Test Case:* *Expected result* Counter should start of at 0 *Actual result* iOS 13.4 - Counter starts at 2 iOS 12.2 - Counter starts at 1""""",title+description
