Description: """"Is this flag showsBackgroundLocationIndicator in Ti.Geolocation available?""""",title+description
TIMOB-25996,12,"""CLI: build process does not lookup modules the same way as ti module""","""""""When i set a custom module path using *appc ti config paths.modules """"""""mycustom/path""""""""*, the modules I put in here are not recognized in the build command.    I checked using the *appc ti module* command, and my module is listed under *Configured Path Modules*, and not under *Global Modules* (which is correct). However, the one under Configured Path Modules points to the correct path but it is not recognized during build, and therefore it throws:        The desired situation checks both the default directory (the global one under """"""""~/Library/Application Support/Titanium/modules""""""""), but also the custom directory added using the config file.    """"""",Bug,"Title: CLI: build process does not lookup modules the same way as ti module
Description: """"When i set a custom module path using *appc ti config paths.modules """"""""mycustom/path""""""""*, the modules I put in here are not recognized in the build command. I checked using the *appc ti module* command, and my module is listed under *Configured Path Modules*, and not under *Global Modules* (which is correct). However, the one under Configured Path Modules points to the correct path but it is not recognized during build, and therefore it throws: The desired situation checks both the default directory (the global one under """"""""~/Library/Application Support/Titanium/modules""""""""), but also the custom directory added using the config file. """"",title+description
TIMOB-25992,12,"""Add default clang-format to the Android/iOS module template""","""""""Having the Axway clang-format files is nice to check the modules syntax and adjust it to the same coding style.     This PR will add it to the default iOS/Android module template so it can be used in all modules right away: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/10015    A linting section was added to the README """"""",Story,"Title: Add default clang-format to the Android/iOS module template
Description: """"Having the Axway clang-format files is nice to check the modules syntax and adjust it to the same coding style. This PR will add it to the default iOS/Android module template so it can be used in all modules right away: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/10015 A linting section was added to the README """"",title+description
TIMOB-25991,12,"""Android: RefreshControl in ListView only works when dragging content""","""""""*Problem:*  When creating a ListView with a RefreshControl you can only drag the view down when you drag&drop the content. If you ListView is empty you don't have any handle to drag.    *Code*        Dragging below {{Row 3}} won't  show the RefreshControl it will only work when you start dragging on the 3 items.     *What to expect*  The whole ListView space (in the example Ti.UI.FILL) should work as a drag area. Otherwise you can't use it with an empty ListView    *Testsetup*  * Android 7.0.1 / HTC A9  * SDK 7.1.0.GA    """"""",Improvement,"Title: Android: RefreshControl in ListView only works when dragging content
Description: """"*Problem:* When creating a ListView with a RefreshControl you can only drag the view down when you drag&drop the content. If you ListView is empty you don't have any handle to drag. *Code* Dragging below {{Row 3}} won't show the RefreshControl it will only work when you start dragging on the 3 items. *What to expect* The whole ListView space (in the example Ti.UI.FILL) should work as a drag area. Otherwise you can't use it with an empty ListView *Testsetup* * Android 7.0.1 / HTC A9 * SDK 7.1.0.GA """"",title+description
TIMOB-25987,12,"""Android: Image sometimes fails to load if ImageView and a non-ImageView both load same file at the same time""","""""""*Summary:*  There is a race condition that only happens on app startup where if an ImageView attempts to load an image file asynchronously at the same time a non-ImageView attempts to load an image synchronously, one of the views may fail to load a file.    *Steps to reproduce:*  # Create a Titanium classic app project. _(It comes with """"""""tab1.png"""""""" and """"""""tab2.png"""""""" files needed by the below test code.)_  # Add the below test code to the project.  # Build and run on a low-end Android device or old Android OS version. _(Race condition is more likely to happen.)_  # Note that the app will attempt to display 4 images from top to bottom. Top 2 are image views and bottom 2 are buttons.  # Check if the 1st button failed to display its image.  # Check the log for message: """"""""Could not open stream to get bitmap""""""""  # If the error did not happen, back out of the app, force quit it via the task manager, and then restart it.          *Cause:*  The Java {{TiFileHelper.openInputStream()}} method has a race condition here...  [TiFileHelper.java#L188|https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/android/titanium/src/java/org/<USER>titanium/util/TiFileHelper.java#L188]    If the given {{path}} argument starts with {{RESOURCE_ROOT_ASSETS}}, then it'll populate its static {{foundResourcePathCache}}, {{notFoundResourcePathCache}}, and {{resourcePathCache}} dictionaries the first time a file is loaded. But the problem here is that it synchronizes dictionary access only while populating the dictionaries and not when doing the {{contain()}} checks. So, the 2nd thread can be blocked by the synchronized block while the other thread is populating it and once the 2nd thread gets in, the {{found}} local variable will remain false because {{resourcePathCache}} will then contain the file.    This was caused by a code change made back in Titanium 1.5.0 to optimize the case where the given file was not found.  https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-1538  https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/04a83da343b0bcf6419c0cab9624042716e03ff3    *Recommended Solution:*  I don't think we need to optimize for the error case. Removing the static HashSet variables and adding a try/catch around {{AssetManager.open()}} should be enough. Alternatively, we can acquire the entire asset file list via a static initializer which is thread safe in Java.  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Image sometimes fails to load if ImageView and a non-ImageView both load same file at the same time
Description: """"*Summary:* There is a race condition that only happens on app startup where if an ImageView attempts to load an image file asynchronously at the same time a non-ImageView attempts to load an image synchronously, one of the views may fail to load a file. *Steps to reproduce:* # Create a Titanium classic app project. _(It comes with """"""""tab1.png"""""""" and """"""""tab2.png"""""""" files needed by the below test code.)_ # Add the below test code to the project. # Build and run on a low-end Android device or old Android OS version. _(Race condition is more likely to happen.)_ # Note that the app will attempt to display 4 images from top to bottom. Top 2 are image views and bottom 2 are buttons. # Check if the 1st button failed to display its image. # Check the log for message: """"""""Could not open stream to get bitmap"""""""" # If the error did not happen, back out of the app, force quit it via the task manager, and then restart it. *Cause:* The Java {{TiFileHelper.openInputStream()}} method has a race condition here... [TiFileHelper.java#L188|https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/android/titanium/src/java/org/<USER>titanium/util/TiFileHelper.java#L188] If the given {{path}} argument starts with {{RESOURCE_ROOT_ASSETS}}, then it'll populate its static {{foundResourcePathCache}}, {{notFoundResourcePathCache}}, and {{resourcePathCache}} dictionaries the first time a file is loaded. But the problem here is that it synchronizes dictionary access only while populating the dictionaries and not when doing the {{contain()}} checks. So, the 2nd thread can be blocked by the synchronized block while the other thread is populating it and once the 2nd thread gets in, the {{found}} local variable will remain false because {{resourcePathCache}} will then contain the file. This was caused by a code change made back in Titanium 1.5.0 to optimize the case where the given file was not found. https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-1538 https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/04a83da343b0bcf6419c0cab9624042716e03ff3 *Recommended Solution:* I don't think we need to optimize for the error case. Removing the static HashSet variables and adding a try/catch around {{AssetManager.open()}} should be enough. Alternatively, we can acquire the entire asset file list via a static initializer which is thread safe in Java. """"",title+description
TIMOB-25985,12,"""TiAPI: Disable accessibility features on Ti.UI.Button by using AttributedString""","""""""In my apps i use font-icons for:  - title of buttons  - text of label    *Example:*      If the user active this option _Settings / General / Accessibility / Button Shapes_ , the result is the attached image, an underline on every buttons. I know this is normal, but i would like to know if it was possible to disable this effect.    I tried to active this option *accessibilityHidden*, but without result.  I also thought about using the *attributeString* (with the param [Titanium.UI.ATTRIBUTE_UNDERLINE_STYLE_NONE|http://docs.<USER>com/platform/latest/#!/guide/Attributed_Strings-section-src-37538231_AttributedStrings-Underline]), but it's only for _Label_, _TextArea_ or _TextField_.    Info link1: [https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23246543/remove-underline-on-uibutton-in-ios-7|https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23246543/remove-underline-on-uibutton-in-ios-7]  Info link2: [https://developer.apple.com/documentation/uikit/accessibility/uiaccessibility]""""""",New Feature,"Title: TiAPI: Disable accessibility features on Ti.UI.Button by using AttributedString
Description: """"In my apps i use font-icons for: - title of buttons - text of label *Example:* If the user active this option _Settings / General / Accessibility / Button Shapes_ , the result is the attached image, an underline on every buttons. I know this is normal, but i would like to know if it was possible to disable this effect. I tried to active this option *accessibilityHidden*, but without result. I also thought about using the *attributeString* (with the param [Titanium.UI.ATTRIBUTE_UNDERLINE_STYLE_NONE|http://docs.<USER>com/platform/latest/#!/guide/Attributed_Strings-section-src-37538231_AttributedStrings-Underline]), but it's only for _Label_, _TextArea_ or _TextField_. Info link1: [https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23246543/remove-underline-on-uibutton-in-ios-7|https://stackoverflow.com/questions/23246543/remove-underline-on-uibutton-in-ios-7] Info link2: [https://developer.apple.com/documentation/uikit/accessibility/uiaccessibility]""""",title+description
TIMOB-25978,12,"""Android: animate() called more than once on a view does not work""","""""""Calling {{animate()}} on a View that has already been animated does not work. The callback is called after the duration, but the transformation is based on the initial state of the view and it is not affected by further animations.    _Note:_ Adding {{autoReverse: true}} to the Ti.UI.Animation results in successfully animating the view every time.  _Note2:_ {{anchorPoint}} property will work after the changes from TIMOB-16596 are applied.    *Test case:*  _app.js_        *Result:* After the first click the view is rotate, the button disabled for the duration and enabled at the animation's end. Every following click results in *only* the button being disabled and reenabled after the animation duration.    *Expected result:* The animation should be properly applied no matter how many times the button is clicked.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: animate() called more than once on a view does not work
Description: """"Calling {{animate()}} on a View that has already been animated does not work. The callback is called after the duration, but the transformation is based on the initial state of the view and it is not affected by further animations. _Note:_ Adding {{autoReverse: true}} to the Ti.UI.Animation results in successfully animating the view every time. _Note2:_ {{anchorPoint}} property will work after the changes from TIMOB-16596 are applied. *Test case:* _app.js_ *Result:* After the first click the view is rotate, the button disabled for the duration and enabled at the animation's end. Every following click results in *only* the button being disabled and reenabled after the animation duration. *Expected result:* The animation should be properly applied no matter how many times the button is clicked.""""",title+description
TIMOB-25974,12,"""Android: Ti.Geolocation updates rarely on Android 8.0 while app is backgrounded""","""""""*Issue Overview:*  I need my app to get the location every 5 seconds.   It seems when the phone becomes 'locked' and the screen goes off, the location updates very rarely. If I request the location the timestamp of the location object doesn't change.  I've tried manual mode but I get the same result.    How can I get the realtime location when the phone is on or off?    *Reproduction:*  Run the test code  Check the log and observe that the timestamp never changes. So it seems the geolocation doens't expire after 10 seconds.    *index.js*      *Output*    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Ti.Geolocation updates rarely on Android 8.0 while app is backgrounded
Description: """"*Issue Overview:* I need my app to get the location every 5 seconds. It seems when the phone becomes 'locked' and the screen goes off, the location updates very rarely. If I request the location the timestamp of the location object doesn't change. I've tried manual mode but I get the same result. How can I get the realtime location when the phone is on or off? *Reproduction:* Run the test code Check the log and observe that the timestamp never changes. So it seems the geolocation doens't expire after 10 seconds. *index.js* *Output* """"",title+description
TIMOB-25973,12,"""Android: Unknown error when packaging modules""","""""""Whenever you create a new module, after the first compilation it will show an error. However, the error is only shown on the main icon of the Project Explorer, but not in the rest of the project, making difficult or impossible to know what happened    To reproduce, just create a new module for Android with Ti SDK 7.0.1 and compile without modifying anything.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Unknown error when packaging modules
Description: """"Whenever you create a new module, after the first compilation it will show an error. However, the error is only shown on the main icon of the Project Explorer, but not in the rest of the project, making difficult or impossible to know what happened To reproduce, just create a new module for Android with Ti SDK 7.0.1 and compile without modifying anything.""""",title+description
TIMOB-25970,12,"""Android: 'postlayout' event not working for Picker with type Titanium.UI.PICKER_TYPE_DATE""","""""""Picker with type Titanium.UI.PICKER_TYPE_DATE does not fire {{postlayout}} after {{showDatePickerDialog()}} is called.   It is the same for Picker added in the layout for Android  Lollipop (API level 19) or newer. For API level below 19, Picker added in the layout the event is fired twice.    *Test case:*  _app.js_      """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: 'postlayout' event not working for Picker with type Titanium.UI.PICKER_TYPE_DATE
Description: """"Picker with type Titanium.UI.PICKER_TYPE_DATE does not fire {{postlayout}} after {{showDatePickerDialog()}} is called. It is the same for Picker added in the layout for Android Lollipop (API level 19) or newer. For API level below 19, Picker added in the layout the event is fired twice. *Test case:* _app.js_ """"",title+description
TIMOB-25966,12,"""Android: Use conditional compiling to decrease binary size""","""""""This is a proposal to split the Android core into different (.so) sub-modules in order to decrease the general APK binary size (~ 28MB after the most recent 64 Bit changes). On iOS, we do this since a few years - triggered by the same reasons, practically by wrapping all classes in precompiler statements like {{USE_TI_UITABLEVIEW}}. The macros are generated [by the CLI|https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/iphone/cli/commands/_build.js#L6075] and represent the Titanium symbols used within the app. For Android, this could be done similar, but will also require major refactoring of the package structure itself.""""""",Story,"Title: Android: Use conditional compiling to decrease binary size
Description: """"This is a proposal to split the Android core into different (.so) sub-modules in order to decrease the general APK binary size (~ 28MB after the most recent 64 Bit changes). On iOS, we do this since a few years - triggered by the same reasons, practically by wrapping all classes in precompiler statements like {{USE_TI_UITABLEVIEW}}. The macros are generated [by the CLI|https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/iphone/cli/commands/_build.js#L6075] and represent the Titanium symbols used within the app. For Android, this could be done similar, but will also require major refactoring of the package structure itself.""""",title+description
TIMOB-25962,12,"""Android: Ti.App.resume and Ti.App.pause Addition - Google has added Lifecycle Components (LifecycleOwner) specifically for this""","""""""This is a Feature request to add Ti.App.resume and Ti.App.pause event to Android.    Google recently added [Lifecycle Components|https://developer.android.com/topic/libraries/architecture/lifecycle.html] and more specifically [LifecycleOwner|https://developer.android.com/reference/android/arch/lifecycle/LifecycleOwner.html] that has been designed specifically for this.    From the Lifecycle Owner Docs:   """"""""A class that has an Android lifecycle. These events can be used by custom components to handle lifecycle changes without implementing any code inside the Activity or the Fragment.""""""""    There is also a very good Blog post with examples and exactly what is needed here:  https://proandroiddev.com/detecting-when-an-android-app-backgrounds-in-2018-4b5a94977d5c    It would be great if we could add this to the core to finally have Ti.App.resume and Ti.App.pause events and not have to count on all kinds of hacks, tools, modules etc.    I will be working on getting this setup in my test project with Hyperloop and report back with a working example.""""""",Improvement,"Title: Android: Ti.App.resume and Ti.App.pause Addition - Google has added Lifecycle Components (LifecycleOwner) specifically for this
Description: """"This is a Feature request to add Ti.App.resume and Ti.App.pause event to Android. Google recently added [Lifecycle Components|https://developer.android.com/topic/libraries/architecture/lifecycle.html] and more specifically [LifecycleOwner|https://developer.android.com/reference/android/arch/lifecycle/LifecycleOwner.html] that has been designed specifically for this. From the Lifecycle Owner Docs: """"""""A class that has an Android lifecycle. These events can be used by custom components to handle lifecycle changes without implementing any code inside the Activity or the Fragment."""""""" There is also a very good Blog post with examples and exactly what is needed here: https://proandroiddev.com/detecting-when-an-android-app-backgrounds-in-2018-4b5a94977d5c It would be great if we could add this to the core to finally have Ti.App.resume and Ti.App.pause events and not have to count on all kinds of hacks, tools, modules etc. I will be working on getting this setup in my test project with Hyperloop and report back with a working example.""""",title+description
TIMOB-25960,12,"""Hyperloop: iOS - Property returns function instead of string ""","""""""Instead of strings, I only get functions that produce errors when processing the Hyperloop results. That said: console.log prints the correct value for the unmodified variable. I need to access the string that console.log prints.  {code:js}  var HMHomeManager = require('HomeKit/HMHomeManager');  var NSArray = require('Foundation/NSArray');     var HMHomeManagerDelegate = Hyperloop.defineClass('HMHomeManagerDelegate', 'NSObject');      HMHomeManagerDelegate.addMethod({   selector: 'homeManagerDidUpdateHomes:',   instance: true,   arguments: ['HMHomeManager'],   callback: function(sender) {    if (this.homeManagerDidUpdateHomes) {     this.homeManagerDidUpdateHomes(sender);    }   }  });    var HMHome = require('HomeKit/HMHome');      var delegate = new HMHomeManagerDelegate();  delegate.homeManagerDidUpdateHomes = function(sender) {      var name = sender.primaryHome.name;   console.log('name: '+ name);   console.log(JSON.stringify({""""""""name"""""""":""""""""name"""""""",""""""""value"""""""":name}));  };    var homeManager = new HMHomeManager();  homeManager.delegate = delegate;    [INFO] :   name: My Home    [INFO] :   name: My Home  [INFO] :  {""""""""name"""""""":""""""""name"""""""",""""""""value"""""""":""""""""My Home""""""""}  {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: Hyperloop: iOS - Property returns function instead of string
Description: """"Instead of strings, I only get functions that produce errors when processing the Hyperloop results. That said: console.log prints the correct value for the unmodified variable. I need to access the string that console.log prints. {code:js} var HMHomeManager = require('HomeKit/HMHomeManager'); var NSArray = require('Foundation/NSArray'); var HMHomeManagerDelegate = Hyperloop.defineClass('HMHomeManagerDelegate', 'NSObject'); HMHomeManagerDelegate.addMethod({ selector: 'homeManagerDidUpdateHomes:', instance: true, arguments: ['HMHomeManager'], callback: function(sender) { if (this.homeManagerDidUpdateHomes) { this.homeManagerDidUpdateHomes(sender); } } }); var HMHome = require('HomeKit/HMHome'); var delegate = new HMHomeManagerDelegate(); delegate.homeManagerDidUpdateHomes = function(sender) { var name = sender.primaryHome.name; console.log('name: '+ name); console.log(JSON.stringify({""""""""name"""""""":""""""""name"""""""",""""""""value"""""""":name})); }; var homeManager = new HMHomeManager(); homeManager.delegate = delegate; [INFO] : name: My Home [INFO] : name: My Home [INFO] : {""""""""name"""""""":""""""""name"""""""",""""""""value"""""""":""""""""My Home""""""""} {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-25959,12,"""Android: Module build does not fail if building with an invalid NDK version""","""""""h5.Description    If you're building an Android module with an NDK version we don't support, we should fail build. But we don't    h5.Steps to reproduce    1. Get NDK r10e  2. Build a module    h5.Actual    Build does not fail    h5.Expected    Build should fail""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Module build does not fail if building with an invalid NDK version
Description: """"h5.Description If you're building an Android module with an NDK version we don't support, we should fail build. But we don't h5.Steps to reproduce 1. Get NDK r10e 2. Build a module h5.Actual Build does not fail h5.Expected Build should fail""""",title+description
TIMOB-25958,12,"""Android: Log Android NDK version on module build""","""""""h5.Description    For TIMOB-25948, I was trying to track down the NDK version used. I had to introduce a step in the Jenkinsfile that cat'd the releases.txt file in order to find this out seeing as this might have the potential to cause bugs we should log the NDK version used in the build <USER>""""",Improvement,"Title: Android: Log Android NDK version on module build
Description: """"h5.Description For TIMOB-25948, I was trying to track down the NDK version used. I had to introduce a step in the Jenkinsfile that cat'd the releases.txt file in order to find this out seeing as this might have the potential to cause bugs we should log the NDK version used in the build <USER>""",title+description
