TIMOB-24467,12,"""Unify .jar dupe detection in Hyperloop and Titanium SDK""","""""""As of now Hyperloop and the Android builder in our SDK both include a check for duplicate .jar files.    [~<USER> made a few suggestions in https://github.com/<USER>hyperloop.next/pull/129 to optimize this:  - Reuse the same code if possible  - do the check before we run through dexer (and after hyperloop/plugins/hooks may have modified the list of JARs)""""""",Improvement,"Title: Unify .jar dupe detection in Hyperloop and Titanium SDK
Description: """"As of now Hyperloop and the Android builder in our SDK both include a check for duplicate .jar files. [~<USER> made a few suggestions in https://github.com/<USER>hyperloop.next/pull/129 to optimize this: - Reuse the same code if possible - do the check before we run through dexer (and after hyperloop/plugins/hooks may have modified the list of JARs)""""",title+description
TIMOB-24466,12,"""Hyperloop: Android - R classes not generated from AAR""","""""""*Steps to reproduce the behavior*  1. Create a Hyperloop enabled classic project  2. Drop the attached .aar that contains resources into {{platform/android}} folder  3. Add the following code to {{Resources/app.js}}      *Actual behavior*  The following error is thrown      *Expected behavior*  The string """"""""Hello World!"""""""" should be printed to debug log.    *Additional notes*  This is kind of an edge use-case and only occurs if you want to access the specific R class from an .aar file. The library itself still works because AAPT will create a new R class which contains the resource ids from the App and all .aar files. The same issue occurs if two .aar files depend on each other and use the other one's R class.    TIMOB-18565 introduces AAR handling for Ti modules which includes code that will generated the missing R files. The code from there should be reused and also be applied to Hyperloop.""""""",Bug,"Title: Hyperloop: Android - R classes not generated from AAR
Description: """"*Steps to reproduce the behavior* 1. Create a Hyperloop enabled classic project 2. Drop the attached .aar that contains resources into {{platform/android}} folder 3. Add the following code to {{Resources/app.js}} *Actual behavior* The following error is thrown *Expected behavior* The string """"""""Hello World!"""""""" should be printed to debug log. *Additional notes* This is kind of an edge use-case and only occurs if you want to access the specific R class from an .aar file. The library itself still works because AAPT will create a new R class which contains the resource ids from the App and all .aar files. The same issue occurs if two .aar files depend on each other and use the other one's R class. TIMOB-18565 introduces AAR handling for Ti modules which includes code that will generated the missing R files. The code from there should be reused and also be applied to Hyperloop.""""",title+description
TIMOB-24464,12,"""Android: Ti.Calendar.Calendar.getEventsBetweenDates returns same events for all calendars""","""""""On my test device, there are 15 calendars, some are duplicates (via being linked through different google accounts on the same device), and some are not duplicates.    Let X be the events returned from the first calendar. When executing the """"""""Ti.Calendar.Calendar.getEventsBetweenDates"""""""" method on all calendars, they all return X. This is problematic, because we then cannot differentiate between which Calendars from which data is being pulled. A use cannot exclude certain calendars from being consumed by the application, which is a major issue.    NOTE: On iOS, this exact same scenario and code results in the proper data being returned from the specific Calendars.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Ti.Calendar.Calendar.getEventsBetweenDates returns same events for all calendars
Description: """"On my test device, there are 15 calendars, some are duplicates (via being linked through different google accounts on the same device), and some are not duplicates. Let X be the events returned from the first calendar. When executing the """"""""Ti.Calendar.Calendar.getEventsBetweenDates"""""""" method on all calendars, they all return X. This is problematic, because we then cannot differentiate between which Calendars from which data is being pulled. A use cannot exclude certain calendars from being consumed by the application, which is a major issue. NOTE: On iOS, this exact same scenario and code results in the proper data being returned from the specific Calendars.""""",title+description
TIMOB-24462,12,"""Decouple liveview from Appcelerator Studio""","""""""The docs state that liveview is bound to be used with <USER>Studio. I am not using <USER>Studio for the simple fact that it is hellishly slow: the frequent project indexing, opening files, tiapp.xml editor, dashboard... most of the functionalities are sub-par and I'd therefore prefer using a proper editor like PhpStorm.    However, the *--liveview* directive does not seem to work by itself when I execute the build command like *appc ti build -p ios -T simulator --liveview*. I've had it working in the past _without the use of <USER>Studio_, by reasons I could not explain (possibly installing the <USER>Studio changed some config settings in the CLI?).    I am a 100% sure using the studio is not a technical requirement. Having the --liveview option available to everyone is a requirement though, because development is already slow enough using this platform. Replacement modules like TiShadow, and buggy implementations like [here|https://jolicode.com/blog/alloy-selective-compilation-for-an-even-faster-titanium-development-<USER> arent an option since they're 1) hard to implement 2) underdeveloped and 3) buggy.    I wonder what <USER>s view is on this.""""""",Improvement,"Title: Decouple liveview from Appcelerator Studio
Description: """"The docs state that liveview is bound to be used with <USER>Studio. I am not using <USER>Studio for the simple fact that it is hellishly slow: the frequent project indexing, opening files, tiapp.xml editor, dashboard... most of the functionalities are sub-par and I'd therefore prefer using a proper editor like PhpStorm. However, the *--liveview* directive does not seem to work by itself when I execute the build command like *appc ti build -p ios -T simulator --liveview*. I've had it working in the past _without the use of <USER>Studio_, by reasons I could not explain (possibly installing the <USER>Studio changed some config settings in the CLI?). I am a 100% sure using the studio is not a technical requirement. Having the --liveview option available to everyone is a requirement though, because development is already slow enough using this platform. Replacement modules like TiShadow, and buggy implementations like [here|https://jolicode.com/blog/alloy-selective-compilation-for-an-even-faster-titanium-development-<USER> arent an option since they're 1) hard to implement 2) underdeveloped and 3) buggy. I wonder what <USER>s view is on this.""""",title+description
