TIMOB-24167,12,"""Failed to use Android-SDK-embedded libs with Hyperloop""","""""""I'm trying to run a Firebase project on Android using Hyperloop and I'm not being able to do it with any of the SDK / hyperloop versions.    If I use the SDK 5.5.1.GA and Hyperloop version 1.2.7 or 1.2.8 I get the issue attached on the file """"""""issue-551GA"""""""".  If I use the SDK 6.0.0 and Hyperloop version 2.0.0 I get the issue attached on the file """"""""issue-600"""""""".    If I could get what the issue is I would say that it's something related to how the Firebase works with the Android SDK. It's not a standalone project where you just have to include in your .gradle file and export your .aar file. Firebase works a bit differently because it's embedded in the Android SDK (Because it's a CORE package in the Android SDK). I think Hyperloop is not ready for this kind of scenario, but I can be wrong.""""""",Bug,"Title: Failed to use Android-SDK-embedded libs with Hyperloop
Description: """"I'm trying to run a Firebase project on Android using Hyperloop and I'm not being able to do it with any of the SDK / hyperloop versions. If I use the SDK 5.5.1.GA and Hyperloop version 1.2.7 or 1.2.8 I get the issue attached on the file """"""""issue-551GA"""""""". If I use the SDK 6.0.0 and Hyperloop version 2.0.0 I get the issue attached on the file """"""""issue-600"""""""". If I could get what the issue is I would say that it's something related to how the Firebase works with the Android SDK. It's not a standalone project where you just have to include in your .gradle file and export your .aar file. Firebase works a bit differently because it's embedded in the Android SDK (Because it's a CORE package in the Android SDK). I think Hyperloop is not ready for this kind of scenario, but I can be wrong.""""",title+description
TIMOB-24166,12,"""Unable to adding support-compat-24.2.0.jar to Android Module""","""""""h6.Problem    Custom facing problem:    """"""""I'm trying to import android.support.v4.app.NotificationCompat class in my <USER>Android Module project.  To do this I have added jar 'support-compat-24.2.0.jar' in the lib folder. This has resolved all the import errors in the module.""""""""    h6.Reproduce    1. Attached test Module customer created.   2. Simply import and require it into a default app.   Like:   3. Run the app. Will get following error.    h6.Error  """"""",Bug,"Title: Unable to adding support-compat-24.2.0.jar to Android Module
Description: """"h6.Problem Custom facing problem: """"""""I'm trying to import android.support.v4.app.NotificationCompat class in my <USER>Android Module project. To do this I have added jar 'support-compat-24.2.0.jar' in the lib folder. This has resolved all the import errors in the module."""""""" h6.Reproduce 1. Attached test Module customer created. 2. Simply import and require it into a default app. Like: 3. Run the app. Will get following error. h6.Error """"",title+description
TIMOB-24163,12,"""Hyperloop: iOS - Metabase generation fails for Swift-only classes from system frameworks""","""""""Classes from system frameworks that are Swift exclusive can not be used in custom Swift code in Hyperloop. Example class: https://developer.apple.com/reference/foundation/url    *Steps to reproduce the behavior*  1. Create a new Hyperloop enabled project  2. Add the following sample code under {{src/MySwift.swift}}:  {code:swift}  import Foundation    public class MySwift {    public func getUrl() -> URL {      return URL(string: """"""""http://<USER>com"""""""")!;    }  }    module.exports = {   type: 'app',   group: 'titanium',   hyperloop: {    ios: {     thirdparty: {      'MyFramework': {       source: ['src'],       header: 'src',       resource: 'src'      }     }    }   }  };  {code}  4. Add {{var MySwift = require('MyFramework/MySwift');}} to {{app/controller/index.js}} to trigger Hyperloop's metabase generation  5. Build the project with {{appc run -p ios}}    *Actual behavior*  The build fails with the following error: Swift Generation failed with unknown or unsupported type (URL) found while compiling /Users/<USER>Development/appc/swift-url/src/MySwift.swift    *Expected behavior*  The build succeeds and the metabase for Swift-only classes will be generated    *Additional notes*  The reason why this fails is because the {{import Foundation}} statement in the Swift source will be mapped to the Objective-C equivalent {{import <Foundation/Foundation.h>}}. Due to {{URL}} being Swift-only the metabase generator can not properly determine a type and fails. SourceKit may be a  possible solution and needs some more investigation.    See also: https://github.com/apple/swift-evolution/blob/master/proposals/0086-drop-foundation-ns.md""""""",Bug,"Title: Hyperloop: iOS - Metabase generation fails for Swift-only classes from system frameworks
Description: """"Classes from system frameworks that are Swift exclusive can not be used in custom Swift code in Hyperloop. Example class: https://developer.apple.com/reference/foundation/url *Steps to reproduce the behavior* 1. Create a new Hyperloop enabled project 2. Add the following sample code under {{src/MySwift.swift}}: {code:swift} import Foundation public class MySwift { public func getUrl() -> URL { return URL(string: """"""""http://<USER>com"""""""")!; } } module.exports = { type: 'app', group: 'titanium', hyperloop: { ios: { thirdparty: { 'MyFramework': { source: ['src'], header: 'src', resource: 'src' } } } } }; {code} 4. Add {{var MySwift = require('MyFramework/MySwift');}} to {{app/controller/index.js}} to trigger Hyperloop's metabase generation 5. Build the project with {{appc run -p ios}} *Actual behavior* The build fails with the following error: Swift Generation failed with unknown or unsupported type (URL) found while compiling /Users/<USER>Development/appc/swift-url/src/MySwift.swift *Expected behavior* The build succeeds and the metabase for Swift-only classes will be generated *Additional notes* The reason why this fails is because the {{import Foundation}} statement in the Swift source will be mapped to the Objective-C equivalent {{import <Foundation/Foundation.h>}}. Due to {{URL}} being Swift-only the metabase generator can not properly determine a type and fails. SourceKit may be a possible solution and needs some more investigation. See also: https://github.com/apple/swift-evolution/blob/master/proposals/0086-drop-foundation-ns.md""""",title+description
TIMOB-24160,12,"""Hyperloop: iOS - Exclude CocoaPods products from being removed""","""""""In TIMOB-24057 the output directory for built CocoaPods products was changed. This introduced a regression with sequential builds. The {{build.ios.removeFiles}} hook deletes files from the iOS build directory that were not previously unmarked with {{unmarkBuildDirFile}}. The CocoaPods libraries need to be explicitly unmarked so they won't get deleted during this hook.    *Steps to reproduce the behavior*  1. Create a new Hyperloop enabled project or use the hyperloop-examples project  2. Make sure the build dir is clean by either deleting it or running {{appc ti clean}}  3. Build the project with {{appc run -p ios}}  4. Re-run the build again    *Expected behavior*  Able to successfully run subsequent builds    *Actual behavior*  The build fails due to missing libraries during linking  """"""",Bug,"Title: Hyperloop: iOS - Exclude CocoaPods products from being removed
Description: """"In TIMOB-24057 the output directory for built CocoaPods products was changed. This introduced a regression with sequential builds. The {{build.ios.removeFiles}} hook deletes files from the iOS build directory that were not previously unmarked with {{unmarkBuildDirFile}}. The CocoaPods libraries need to be explicitly unmarked so they won't get deleted during this hook. *Steps to reproduce the behavior* 1. Create a new Hyperloop enabled project or use the hyperloop-examples project 2. Make sure the build dir is clean by either deleting it or running {{appc ti clean}} 3. Build the project with {{appc run -p ios}} 4. Re-run the build again *Expected behavior* Able to successfully run subsequent builds *Actual behavior* The build fails due to missing libraries during linking """"",title+description
TIMOB-24156,12,"""Module build: Python and CLI builds produce different binaries""","""""""It may be all fine, but I wanted to file this ticket to investigate it.    When using the {{build.py}} in modules (e.g. ti.touchid), the resulting binary size is different to when using {{appc ti build -p ios --build-only}}. The first one is about twice the size compared to the latter one. Both are attached to this ticket.""""""",Bug,"Title: Module build: Python and CLI builds produce different binaries
Description: """"It may be all fine, but I wanted to file this ticket to investigate it. When using the {{build.py}} in modules (e.g. ti.touchid), the resulting binary size is different to when using {{appc ti build -p ios --build-only}}. The first one is about twice the size compared to the latter one. Both are attached to this ticket.""""",title+description
TIMOB-24155,12,"""Module build: Allow multiple platforms to be built and bundled together""","""""""So, we changed our module CLI to use the appc-cli instead of the now deprecated python scripts. This works great for compiling and packaging module ({{appc ti build -p ios --build-only}}) and ({{appc ti build -p android --build-only}}). But for some modules (<USER>https, <USER>encrypteddatabase, ti.geofence, ...) we use one script to compile and package both platform together, e.g. [this one|*strong text*]. Unfortunately, it calls the platform-specific python scripts instead of the CLI, so stuff like packaging hooks (TIMOB-24041) won't work.    So we either change the python script to call the CLI commands or create a node file to do the same (like we've done with our scons-commands).     For now, we can manually package the modules and put them together, but everybody should be aware that using the python scripts will now produce invalid modules and we may want to remove them from the repos completely.""""""",Improvement,"Title: Module build: Allow multiple platforms to be built and bundled together
Description: """"So, we changed our module CLI to use the appc-cli instead of the now deprecated python scripts. This works great for compiling and packaging module ({{appc ti build -p ios --build-only}}) and ({{appc ti build -p android --build-only}}). But for some modules (<USER>https, <USER>encrypteddatabase, ti.geofence, ...) we use one script to compile and package both platform together, e.g. [this one|*strong text*]. Unfortunately, it calls the platform-specific python scripts instead of the CLI, so stuff like packaging hooks (TIMOB-24041) won't work. So we either change the python script to call the CLI commands or create a node file to do the same (like we've done with our scons-commands). For now, we can manually package the modules and put them together, but everybody should be aware that using the python scripts will now produce invalid modules and we may want to remove them from the repos completely.""""",title+description
TIMOB-24152,12,"""iOS: Ti.Media.saveToPhotoGallery crashes on device in SDK 6.0.0.GA""","""""""With SDK *6.0.0.G.A* there is a problem with the function *Ti.Media.saveToPhotoGallery()*;    - On *simulator* works ok.  - On *device* when call the function the result is this :       With SDK *5.5.1.G.A* works ok also on device""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Ti.Media.saveToPhotoGallery crashes on device in SDK 6.0.0.GA
