TIMOB-23535,12,"""Android: Memory leak with Ti.UI.TabGroup""","""""""here my issue is, in TabGroups I have created four tabs and mapped four controllers to those tabs each. after navigating to any other controller im unable to clear heapmemory or unable to close opened controllers in four tabs    I have check heap memory creation in ddms tool. It is crashing my android app.    Test Code:        Once I navigate to any other controller like naxtController.js or back to previousController.js I have to destroy all these four tabs associated tab controllers. Where in each controller there consist's a lot of view and other UI objects created which need to garbage collected.    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Memory leak with Ti.UI.TabGroup
Description: """"here my issue is, in TabGroups I have created four tabs and mapped four controllers to those tabs each. after navigating to any other controller im unable to clear heapmemory or unable to close opened controllers in four tabs I have check heap memory creation in ddms tool. It is crashing my android app. Test Code: Once I navigate to any other controller like naxtController.js or back to previousController.js I have to destroy all these four tabs associated tab controllers. Where in each controller there consist's a lot of view and other UI objects created which need to garbage collected. """"",title+description
TIMOB-23534,12,"""ReverseGeocoder Not getting the real address""","""""""The address for that lat and long values is:  701 N Vista Ridge Blvd  Cedar Park, TX 78613  30.527807, -97.792323    // try to get address  Titanium.Geolocation.reverseGeocoder(30.527935, -97.792357, function(evt) {      //here we will store address information            if (evt.success) {          var places = evt.places;          alert(JSON.stringify(places));          if (places && places.length) {              street = places[0].street;              cCityChannel = places[0].city;              country = places[0].country_code;          } else {           cCityChannel = '';              address = """"""""No address found"""""""";          }      }  });    Also with other positions i get different address. Using google api returns a correct value.  """"""",Bug,"Title: ReverseGeocoder Not getting the real address
Description: """"The address for that lat and long values is: 701 N Vista Ridge Blvd Cedar Park, TX 78613 30.527807, -97.792323 // try to get address Titanium.Geolocation.reverseGeocoder(30.527935, -97.792357, function(evt) { //here we will store address information if (evt.success) { var places = evt.places; alert(JSON.stringify(places)); if (places && places.length) { street = places[0].street; cCityChannel = places[0].city; country = places[0].country_code; } else { cCityChannel = ''; address = """"""""No address found""""""""; } } }); Also with other positions i get different address. Using google api returns a correct value. """"",title+description
TIMOB-23532,12,"""Android: Expose versionCode and versionName in TiApp Editor View""","""""""Would be nice to expose the <manifest android:versionCode="""""""""""""""" android:versionName=""""""""""""""""> values to the user inside the App-Editor view when you open the tiapp.xml.   It's always an error source if users trying to create an update for their app and upload it. You have to know that you have to edit the xml file and increase the versionCode""""""",Story,"Title: Android: Expose versionCode and versionName in TiApp Editor View
Description: """"Would be nice to expose the <manifest android:versionCode="""""""""""""""" android:versionName=""""""""""""""""> values to the user inside the App-Editor view when you open the tiapp.xml. It's always an error source if users trying to create an update for their app and upload it. You have to know that you have to edit the xml file and increase the versionCode""""",title+description
TIMOB-23528,12,"""iOS10: Support new CoreSpotlight APIs""","""""""iOS 10 offers new API's for the {{CoreSpotlight}} framework. In detail, these are new attributes for the {{CSSearchableItemAttributeSet}} as well as the {{CSSearchQuery}} to query an attribute set by it's attribute name. An API should be proposed here first.""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS10: Support new CoreSpotlight APIs
Description: """"iOS 10 offers new API's for the {{CoreSpotlight}} framework. In detail, these are new attributes for the {{CSSearchableItemAttributeSet}} as well as the {{CSSearchQuery}} to query an attribute set by it's attribute name. An API should be proposed here first.""""",title+description
TIMOB-23520,12,"""Android: parseInt() works in octal instead of decimal""","""""""h3. Steps to reproduce    for(var i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {   var text = '0' + i;   Ti.API.info(parseInt(text));   }    h3. Expected results  [INFO] :   0  [INFO] :   1  [INFO] :   2  [INFO] :   3  [INFO] :   4  [INFO] :   5  [INFO] :   6  [INFO] :   7  [INFO] :   8  [INFO] :   9    h3. Actual results  On iOS it works as expected.  On Android for '08', '09' parseInt returns '0'.  [INFO] :   0  [INFO] :   1  [INFO] :   2  [INFO] :   3  [INFO] :   4  [INFO] :   5  [INFO] :   6  [INFO] :   7  [INFO] :   0  [INFO] :   0    h3. Extra info  It is considered as a bug, because it returns values different to any code playground/ browser would and different than iOS with same function. Working in octal is also least expected.  There was similar ticket few years ago [TIMOB-15564|https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-15564], but error was for iOS. I suppose it was fixed for iOS but not for Android.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: parseInt() works in octal instead of decimal
Description: """"h3. Steps to reproduce for(var i = 0; i < 10; ++i) { var text = '0' + i; Ti.API.info(parseInt(text)); } h3. Expected results [INFO] : 0 [INFO] : 1 [INFO] : 2 [INFO] : 3 [INFO] : 4 [INFO] : 5 [INFO] : 6 [INFO] : 7 [INFO] : 8 [INFO] : 9 h3. Actual results On iOS it works as expected. On Android for '08', '09' parseInt returns '0'. [INFO] : 0 [INFO] : 1 [INFO] : 2 [INFO] : 3 [INFO] : 4 [INFO] : 5 [INFO] : 6 [INFO] : 7 [INFO] : 0 [INFO] : 0 h3. Extra info It is considered as a bug, because it returns values different to any code playground/ browser would and different than iOS with same function. Working in octal is also least expected. There was similar ticket few years ago [TIMOB-15564|https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-15564], but error was for iOS. I suppose it was fixed for iOS but not for Android.""""",title+description
TIMOB-23515,12,"""iOS10: Support new CoreMotion APIs""","""""""We have some new CoreMotion APIs (https://developer.apple.com/library/prerelease/content/releasenotes/General/iOS10APIDiffs/Objective-C/CoreMotion.html) that could be covered in our ti.coremotion module. Specifically the {{CMAltimeter}} for measuring significant elevation updates and {{CMPedometerEvent}} for pedometer event-details.""""""",Improvement,"Title: iOS10: Support new CoreMotion APIs
Description: """"We have some new CoreMotion APIs (https://developer.apple.com/library/prerelease/content/releasenotes/General/iOS10APIDiffs/Objective-C/CoreMotion.html) that could be covered in our ti.coremotion module. Specifically the {{CMAltimeter}} for measuring significant elevation updates and {{CMPedometerEvent}} for pedometer event-details.""""",title+description
TIMOB-23508,12,"""iOS: Use native NSData base64 encode API to replace our current methods""","""""""Replacing our base64encode/decode methods with a more versatile methods, using [base64EncodedDataWithOptions|https://developer.apple.com/library/ios/documentation/Cocoa/Reference/Foundation/Classes/NSData_Class/#//apple_ref/occ/instm/NSData/base64EncodedDataWithOptions:].     Demo Code:      *Expected Result*  * The encoded string inside the alert should have Carriage Return  * """"""""Match!"""""""" should be logged to confirm the decoded string matches the original""""""",Improvement,"Title: iOS: Use native NSData base64 encode API to replace our current methods
Description: """"Replacing our base64encode/decode methods with a more versatile methods, using [base64EncodedDataWithOptions|https://developer.apple.com/library/ios/documentation/Cocoa/Reference/Foundation/Classes/NSData_Class/#//apple_ref/occ/instm/NSData/base64EncodedDataWithOptions:]. Demo Code: *Expected Result* * The encoded string inside the alert should have Carriage Return * """"""""Match!"""""""" should be logged to confirm the decoded string matches the original""""",title+description
TIMOB-23506,12,"""Add event listener for applicationWillTerminate""","""""""I have an app that has a background service enabled, the user can elect to get a notification that alerts of the app being killed so that the user can manually restart the app.    There are some other apps that have this feature, examples are Mile IQ and Kevo (http://stackoverflow.com/questions/22938387/how-to-detect-if-an-ios-app-is-killed-when-it-is-not-in-forground-ios7).    When the app is about to shutdown iOS seems to call *applicationWillTerminate* and gives about 5 seconds, which is enough time to trigger a local notification.    Looking at the Titanium SDK code, you are already listening for this event, it would be great if we could surface it as a *Titanium.App.addEventListener('willterminate')* event.""""""",Improvement,"Title: Add event listener for applicationWillTerminate
Description: """"I have an app that has a background service enabled, the user can elect to get a notification that alerts of the app being killed so that the user can manually restart the app. There are some other apps that have this feature, examples are Mile IQ and Kevo (http://stackoverflow.com/questions/22938387/how-to-detect-if-an-ios-app-is-killed-when-it-is-not-in-forground-ios7). When the app is about to shutdown iOS seems to call *applicationWillTerminate* and gives about 5 seconds, which is enough time to trigger a local notification. Looking at the Titanium SDK code, you are already listening for this event, it would be great if we could surface it as a *Titanium.App.addEventListener('willterminate')* event.""""",title+description
TIMOB-23505,12,"""iOS: CLI doesn't always tail logs (or delays connecting initially for some time)""","""""""When running the mocha test suite locally the application logs for the iOS/iPhone simulator sometimes just don't connect at all on short suites. And if I make the test suite we run long enough, it may """"""""delay"""""""" showing the actual logs from Ti.API until we're far into the test run. We effectively totally drop the initial set of logs (and for our mocha test suite that means dropping information on tests that have been run).""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: CLI doesn't always tail logs (or delays connecting initially for some time)
Description: """"When running the mocha test suite locally the application logs for the iOS/iPhone simulator sometimes just don't connect at all on short suites. And if I make the test suite we run long enough, it may """"""""delay"""""""" showing the actual logs from Ti.API until we're far into the test run. We effectively totally drop the initial set of logs (and for our mocha test suite that means dropping information on tests that have been run).""""",title+description
TIMOB-23504,12,"""iOS: Titanium proxies don't extend Object.prototype properly""","""""""Our Titanium proxies do not extend Object.prototype properly on iOS, resulting in some unexpected behavior and necessary workarounds. In general, it means our proxies don't fully act like actual JS objects.    Some examples?   - Well should.js should just work fine to do something like {{Ti.App.should.exist;}} but does not, because should is a function added to Object.prototype and since our proxies don't extend it, it'll fail with """"""""undefined is not a function"""""""". You need to do {{should(Ti.App).exist;}} to work around it.  - We can't do something like {{Ti.App.hasOwnProperty('apiName');}}, so we need to hack it to be called like so: {{Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(Ti.App, 'apiName');}}    This is showing up in my mocha unit tests since iOS fails any checks that end up looking for own properties on Titanium proxies.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Titanium proxies don't extend Object.prototype properly
Description: """"Our Titanium proxies do not extend Object.prototype properly on iOS, resulting in some unexpected behavior and necessary workarounds. In general, it means our proxies don't fully act like actual JS objects. Some examples? - Well should.js should just work fine to do something like {{Ti.App.should.exist;}} but does not, because should is a function added to Object.prototype and since our proxies don't extend it, it'll fail with """"""""undefined is not a function"""""""". You need to do {{should(Ti.App).exist;}} to work around it. - We can't do something like {{Ti.App.hasOwnProperty('apiName');}}, so we need to hack it to be called like so: {{Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(Ti.App, 'apiName');}} This is showing up in my mocha unit tests since iOS fails any checks that end up looking for own properties on Titanium proxies.""""",title+description
TIMOB-23501,12,"""iOS: Ti.UI.Label.ellipsize should default to Ti.UI.TEXT_ELLIPSIZE_TRUNCATE_END, but is undefined""","""""""According to docs, it should default to false. See https://github.com/<USER>titanium-mobile-mocha-suite/blob/master/Resources/ti.ui.label.test.js#L95 for the unit test, but here's the snippet:  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Ti.UI.Label.ellipsize should default to Ti.UI.TEXT_ELLIPSIZE_TRUNCATE_END, but is undefined
Description: """"According to docs, it should default to false. See https://github.com/<USER>titanium-mobile-mocha-suite/blob/master/Resources/ti.ui.label.test.js#L95 for the unit test, but here's the snippet: """"",title+description
TIMOB-23495,12,"""iOS: Support parent property on Ti.Filesystem.File""","""""""iOS is the only platform that doesn't support Ti.Filesystem.File.parent property. We should ideally support it for parity's sake.""""""",Improvement,"Title: iOS: Support parent property on Ti.Filesystem.File
Description: """"iOS is the only platform that doesn't support Ti.Filesystem.File.parent property. We should ideally support it for parity's sake.""""",title+description
TIMOB-23491,12,"""Keyboard Layout Issue in iPad Pro""","""""""Hi,  We are facing a problem with TextField with  latest Ti SDK(5.2.2 GA) and iPad Pro device. I did not try with other versions  Here is code  {code:title=index.xml|borderStyle=solid}  <Alloy>  <Window class=""""""""container"""""""">  <View width=""""""""Ti.UI.FILL"""""""" height=""""""""Ti.UI.FILL"""""""">  <View id=""""""""okCancelView"""""""">  <TextField id=""""""""txtField""""""""/>  </View>  </View>  </Window>  </Alloy>    We are getting onscreen keyboard, like this with above code in iPad pro  !http://i.stack.imgur.com/TS1Jh.png!    But if I create sample app in Xcode with textField, We are getting like this.  !http://i.stack.imgur.com/Py9ai.png!    How to get default keyboard layout, same as XCode in Titanium.""""""",Bug,"Title: Keyboard Layout Issue in iPad Pro
Description: """"Hi, We are facing a problem with TextField with latest Ti SDK(5.2.2 GA) and iPad Pro device. I did not try with other versions Here is code {code:title=index.xml|borderStyle=solid} <Alloy> <Window class=""""""""container""""""""> <View width=""""""""Ti.UI.FILL"""""""" height=""""""""Ti.UI.FILL""""""""> <View id=""""""""okCancelView""""""""> <TextField id=""""""""txtField""""""""/> </View> </View> </Window> </Alloy> We are getting onscreen keyboard, like this with above code in iPad pro !http://i.stack.imgur.com/TS1Jh.png! But if I create sample app in Xcode with textField, We are getting like this. !http://i.stack.imgur.com/Py9ai.png! How to get default keyboard layout, same as XCode in Titanium.""""",title+description
TIMOB-23488,12,"""Closing a window with a map view added crashes the app (android)""","""""""When a window containing a map view is closed on android, it crashes with the exception included below. This used to work fine in 5.1.2.    To close the window just use the device back button (closing the window programatically, i.e. using the action bar home button causes the same effect).    Below I include a test case. Please note that you will have to configure some valid API KEY in your tiapp.xml file in order to test it          Here is the exception rised.     """"""",Bug,"Title: Closing a window with a map view added crashes the app (android)
Description: """"When a window containing a map view is closed on android, it crashes with the exception included below. This used to work fine in 5.1.2. To close the window just use the device back button (closing the window programatically, i.e. using the action bar home button causes the same effect). Below I include a test case. Please note that you will have to configure some valid API KEY in your tiapp.xml file in order to test it Here is the exception rised. """"",title+description
TIMOB-23487,12,"""Windows: e.source.apiName inconsistancy with iOS & Android""","""""""h2. Problem    All the methods from alloy (onClick, onBlur, onReturn, etc.) are returning a different e.source.apiName on Windows 8.1 & 10 then iOS & Android. Windows is returning Titanium.UI.<method>, while iOS & Android return Ti.Ui.<method>    h2. Expected    Windows perform the same as iOS & Android.    h3. Sample Code    {code:lang=javascript|title=index.js}  function doClick(e) {      alert(e.source.apiName);  }    $.index.open();      {code:lang=css|title=index.tss}  """""""".container"""""""": {   backgroundColor:""""""""white""""""""  }    """"""""Label"""""""": {   width: Ti.UI.SIZE,   height: Ti.UI.SIZE,   color: """"""""#000""""""""  }    """"""""#label"""""""": {   font: {    fontSize: 12   }  }  {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: Windows: e.source.apiName inconsistancy with iOS & Android
Description: """"h2. Problem All the methods from alloy (onClick, onBlur, onReturn, etc.) are returning a different e.source.apiName on Windows 8.1 & 10 then iOS & Android. Windows is returning Titanium.UI.<method>, while iOS & Android return Ti.Ui.<method> h2. Expected Windows perform the same as iOS & Android. h3. Sample Code {code:lang=javascript|title=index.js} function doClick(e) { alert(e.source.apiName); } $.index.open(); {code:lang=css|title=index.tss} """""""".container"""""""": { backgroundColor:""""""""white"""""""" } """"""""Label"""""""": { width: Ti.UI.SIZE, height: Ti.UI.SIZE, color: """"""""#000"""""""" } """"""""#label"""""""": { font: { fontSize: 12 } } {code}""""",title+description
