Description: """"On iOS the `currentPage` property of the Ti.UI.ScrollableView is always returning null. Expectation is that this should return the currentIndex of the visible page.""""",title+description
TIMOB-23314,12,"""Windows: CLI should find JDK like Studio does""","""""""When I build a Windows app from Studio it works fine, but when I build the same app via CLI it fails with:        Which originates from:  https://github.com/<USER>titanium_prep/blob/247eaecf56160dce4273a8fd64977b83da671f06/android/launcher/main.jdk16.c#L139    Of course I could set {{JAVA_HOME}} but if Studio manages to find Java (and apparently pass it on to the CLI) then why can't the CLI?    IMHO the CLI should be able to do anything Studio can. Even better: Studio should not do anything the CLI so that Studio is as light as it can be and relies on CLI.""""""",Story,"Title: Windows: CLI should find JDK like Studio does
Description: """"When I build a Windows app from Studio it works fine, but when I build the same app via CLI it fails with: Which originates from: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_prep/blob/247eaecf56160dce4273a8fd64977b83da671f06/android/launcher/main.jdk16.c#L139 Of course I could set {{JAVA_HOME}} but if Studio manages to find Java (and apparently pass it on to the CLI) then why can't the CLI? IMHO the CLI should be able to do anything Studio can. Even better: Studio should not do anything the CLI so that Studio is as light as it can be and relies on CLI.""""",title+description
TIMOB-23306,12,"""Include auto-focus-camera in UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities to fix focus issues on some iPhone/SDK combinations""","""""""I know you hate tickets like this...I can't provide a reproduceable test case. But I'm going to file it anyway so that it's documented for someone else who might encounter the same issue.    I’ve been struggling with camera focus issues on iOS. In our app, focus on some iPhones is fixed at 15-20 cm. But I can’t reproduce it outside our app. You can try with the attached, which mimics our app's use of the camera. On affected devices, it doesn't matter whether you use the full-screen camera or the camera with an overlay like our app uses. And, it's not consistent to device type...some iPhone 6's are affected, others are not for example. But on those devices affected, the issue is constant. On my iPhone 5, the camera is always fixed focus with our app (though works fine in other apps, native camera app, etc.).    Adding {{auto-focus-camera}} to the UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities in tiapp.xml seems to have fixed it. I would assume that that key is added automatically to the plist whenever you use the camera. But, maybe not?    """"""",Bug,"Title: Include auto-focus-camera in UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities to fix focus issues on some iPhone/SDK combinations
Description: """"I know you hate tickets like this...I can't provide a reproduceable test case. But I'm going to file it anyway so that it's documented for someone else who might encounter the same issue. I’ve been struggling with camera focus issues on iOS. In our app, focus on some iPhones is fixed at 15-20 cm. But I can’t reproduce it outside our app. You can try with the attached, which mimics our app's use of the camera. On affected devices, it doesn't matter whether you use the full-screen camera or the camera with an overlay like our app uses. And, it's not consistent to device type...some iPhone 6's are affected, others are not for example. But on those devices affected, the issue is constant. On my iPhone 5, the camera is always fixed focus with our app (though works fine in other apps, native camera app, etc.). Adding {{auto-focus-camera}} to the UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities in tiapp.xml seems to have fixed it. I would assume that that key is added automatically to the plist whenever you use the camera. But, maybe not? """"",title+description
TIMOB-23304,12,"""style android (spinner, picker, alert) in themes (config.json)""","""""""This is a request for styling android pickers, spinners, alert dialogs, etc...    In order to avoid creating a xml file with a android theme in app/platform/android/res/values, should be able to define the background and text color inside the titanium themes app/themes/NAME/config.json on app/config.json when there are no themes.    [stackoverflow android picker style|http://stackoverflow.com/questions/36726908/android-picker-style-theme/36768102?noredirect=1#comment61522891_36768102]""""""",New Feature,"Title: style android (spinner, picker, alert) in themes (config.json)
Description: """"This is a request for styling android pickers, spinners, alert dialogs, etc... In order to avoid creating a xml file with a android theme in app/platform/android/res/values, should be able to define the background and text color inside the titanium themes app/themes/NAME/config.json on app/config.json when there are no themes. [stackoverflow android picker style|http://stackoverflow.com/questions/36726908/android-picker-style-theme/36768102?noredirect=1#comment61522891_36768102]""""",title+description
TIMOB-23295,12,"""Android: parity in Ti.API.log with iOS / Windows""","""""""While addressing TIMOB-23237 it was found that there were inconsistencies in Ti.API.log between all platforms. For Android to achieve parity with Windows / iOS, custom log messages need to be ignored.     h2. TEST CODE:      *Logging on Windows Platform:*      *Logging on Android Platform:*      h2. Steps to Reproduce:  1. Create a Classic Titanium application.  2. Apply test code to app.js  3. Run app to Android device / emulator.  4. Observe console and compare returned log messages to Windows Platform.    h2. Actual Result:    Custom log-level messages are printed on the Android Platform.    h2. Expected Result    Custom log-level messages should be ignored on the Android Platform.""""""",Improvement,"Title: Android: parity in Ti.API.log with iOS / Windows
Description: """"While addressing TIMOB-23237 it was found that there were inconsistencies in Ti.API.log between all platforms. For Android to achieve parity with Windows / iOS, custom log messages need to be ignored. h2. TEST CODE: *Logging on Windows Platform:* *Logging on Android Platform:* h2. Steps to Reproduce: 1. Create a Classic Titanium application. 2. Apply test code to app.js 3. Run app to Android device / emulator. 4. Observe console and compare returned log messages to Windows Platform. h2. Actual Result: Custom log-level messages are printed on the Android Platform. h2. Expected Result Custom log-level messages should be ignored on the Android Platform.""""",title+description
