TIMOB-20012,12,"""Android: module build requires API level 21""","""""""When attempting to build a native Android module, I realized we are forced to the default API level from the python code, which is 21. I assumed the android.platform value in the build.properties file would choose what API level we're targeting. Mine was pointing at android-23, but from what I can tell we're forced to use 21.    https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/support/android/androidsdk.py#L8""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: module build requires API level 21
Description: """"When attempting to build a native Android module, I realized we are forced to the default API level from the python code, which is 21. I assumed the android.platform value in the build.properties file would choose what API level we're targeting. Mine was pointing at android-23, but from what I can tell we're forced to use 21. https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/support/android/androidsdk.py#L8""""",title+description
TIMOB-20011,12,"""Allow JavaScript files outside the Resources directory to be bundled into the app""","""""""There is a need to allow JavaScript files and possibly other resources to be bundled into an app that live outside the Resources directory. In the case of JavaScript files, special handling needs to be done so that they are AST parsed for Titanium APIs and then encrypted for non-development builds.    This feature would be exposed to CLI hooks. A CLI hook could simply modify the list of jsFiles that were found while recursing the Resources directory before performing the analysis and encryption.    Because we're allowing external files to be bundled, we may need to expose differential build logic. If just injecting files, then it should be fine, but if modifying any existing files will most likely require touching the {{currentBuildManifest}} state.    Here's a related community thread: https://community.<USER>com/topic/4063/cli-hook-copy-resources-to-build/.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Allow JavaScript files outside the Resources directory to be bundled into the app
Description: """"There is a need to allow JavaScript files and possibly other resources to be bundled into an app that live outside the Resources directory. In the case of JavaScript files, special handling needs to be done so that they are AST parsed for Titanium APIs and then encrypted for non-development builds. This feature would be exposed to CLI hooks. A CLI hook could simply modify the list of jsFiles that were found while recursing the Resources directory before performing the analysis and encryption. Because we're allowing external files to be bundled, we may need to expose differential build logic. If just injecting files, then it should be fine, but if modifying any existing files will most likely require touching the {{currentBuildManifest}} state. Here's a related community thread: https://community.<USER>com/topic/4063/cli-hook-copy-resources-to-build/.""""",title+description
TIMOB-20006,12,"""Support build and publish to top MAM/EMM providers (AirWatch, MobileIron, Good) from CLI""","""""""Today we offer plugin support for Studio to publish into the AirWatch and MobileIron workflows. Apperian also offers a plugin that they provide and support that does the same.    We should make it equally easy to configure and push builds from the CLI as well.    Initial support should duplicate what we have today - AirWatch / MobileIron.    Additional Systems to keep on the radar:    * Good Technology  * Citrix  * Apperian  * Maas360 / Mocana  """"""",New Feature,"Title: Support build and publish to top MAM/EMM providers (AirWatch, MobileIron, Good) from CLI
Description: """"Today we offer plugin support for Studio to publish into the AirWatch and MobileIron workflows. Apperian also offers a plugin that they provide and support that does the same. We should make it equally easy to configure and push builds from the CLI as well. Initial support should duplicate what we have today - AirWatch / MobileIron. Additional Systems to keep on the radar: * Good Technology * Citrix * Apperian * Maas360 / Mocana """"",title+description
