TIMOB-19273,12,"""iOS: Running Watch app doesn't launch compatible simulator""","""""""When running an Apple Watch App on a simulator using {{appc run -p ios}}, a compatible simulator (iPhone) is not launched by default.    *Steps to reproduce issue*  1. Create an Apple Watch App  2. Run the app on a simulator using {{appc run -p ios}}    *Actual Results*  Simulator is an iPad sim, need iPhone    *Expected Results*  A compatible simulator (iPhone) is launched""""""",Improvement,"Title: iOS: Running Watch app doesn't launch compatible simulator
Description: """"When running an Apple Watch App on a simulator using {{appc run -p ios}}, a compatible simulator (iPhone) is not launched by default. *Steps to reproduce issue* 1. Create an Apple Watch App 2. Run the app on a simulator using {{appc run -p ios}} *Actual Results* Simulator is an iPad sim, need iPhone *Expected Results* A compatible simulator (iPhone) is launched""""",title+description
TIMOB-19261,12,"""Android: Ti.Network.createHTTPClient memory leak""","""""""Using the testcase below objects keeps building up (thus increasing heap size) and never gets released.    To test open a new alloy project in titanium and edit tiapp.xml so it's debuggable and can be analyzed in Android Monitor.     {code:title=index.xml|borderStyle=solid}  <Alloy>   <Window class=""""""""container"""""""">    <Label id=""""""""label"""""""" onClick=""""""""doClick"""""""">Hello, World</Label>   </Window>  </Alloy>      {code:title= testHTTP.xml|borderStyle=solid}  <Alloy>   <Window id=""""""""container"""""""">    <Label text=""""""""test http"""""""" onClick=""""""""close""""""""></Label>   </Window>  </Alloy>      To test open the testHTTP window many times and watch how objects increase. In Android Monitor press """"""""Cause GC"""""""" from time to time. Waiting to see if the automatic GC picks it up does not make a difference.    The example code is simple but as soon as the page opened gets more complex the number of objects/heap size increases faster and eventually leads to an app crash.    Note. I've tested this on my Galaxy S3 with Android 4.3""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Ti.Network.createHTTPClient memory leak
Description: """"Using the testcase below objects keeps building up (thus increasing heap size) and never gets released. To test open a new alloy project in titanium and edit tiapp.xml so it's debuggable and can be analyzed in Android Monitor. {code:title=index.xml|borderStyle=solid} <Alloy> <Window class=""""""""container""""""""> <Label id=""""""""label"""""""" onClick=""""""""doClick"""""""">Hello, World</Label> </Window> </Alloy> {code:title= testHTTP.xml|borderStyle=solid} <Alloy> <Window id=""""""""container""""""""> <Label text=""""""""test http"""""""" onClick=""""""""close""""""""></Label> </Window> </Alloy> To test open the testHTTP window many times and watch how objects increase. In Android Monitor press """"""""Cause GC"""""""" from time to time. Waiting to see if the automatic GC picks it up does not make a difference. The example code is simple but as soon as the page opened gets more complex the number of objects/heap size increases faster and eventually leads to an app crash. Note. I've tested this on my Galaxy S3 with Android 4.3""""",title+description
TIMOB-19260,12,"""iOS: Tab - focus event fired with no index, previousIndex, tab, previousTab""","""""""To re-produce, create a two-tabbed alloy project with the following:    index.xml      index.js  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Tab - focus event fired with no index, previousIndex, tab, previousTab
Description: """"To re-produce, create a two-tabbed alloy project with the following: index.xml index.js """"",title+description
TIMOB-19259,12,"""iOS: WebView loses the DOM on a subsequent """"open"""" of the containing window""","""""""The WebView used to keep its DOM across multiple open-close cycles of the containing window.  We could even remove the WebView instance from one window and add it to another window, without losing the DOM.  This was a great feature for performance.  Somewhere between 3.4.1.GA and 4.1.0.GA, this behavior changed.  Now, the WebView reloads on each open of the containing Window.  I think this was done to fire the """"""""load"""""""" event on the WebView on each window.open().      If you set the HTML of the WebView, as opposed to setting its URL, then the WebView tries to load an empty local URL on subsequent window.open(), with just the App's directory, and fails.    To recreate this issue:    # Create a master-detail app from the template  # Replace DetailView.js with the attached file:    Run and click on a master row.  The detail window will show the details.  Go back and click another master row.    [Please keep the old behavior *_as an option_*.  We should be able to keep the WebView instance across multiple open-close cycles without losing its DOM.  We should even be able to move the WebView instance across windows.]""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: WebView loses the DOM on a subsequent """"open"""" of the containing window
Description: """"The WebView used to keep its DOM across multiple open-close cycles of the containing window. We could even remove the WebView instance from one window and add it to another window, without losing the DOM. This was a great feature for performance. Somewhere between 3.4.1.GA and 4.1.0.GA, this behavior changed. Now, the WebView reloads on each open of the containing Window. I think this was done to fire the """"""""load"""""""" event on the WebView on each window.open(). If you set the HTML of the WebView, as opposed to setting its URL, then the WebView tries to load an empty local URL on subsequent window.open(), with just the App's directory, and fails. To recreate this issue: # Create a master-detail app from the template # Replace DetailView.js with the attached file: Run and click on a master row. The detail window will show the details. Go back and click another master row. [Please keep the old behavior *_as an option_*. We should be able to keep the WebView instance across multiple open-close cycles without losing its DOM. We should even be able to move the WebView instance across windows.]""""",title+description
TIMOB-19258,12,"""iOS: $.UI.create(""""ScrollView"""") leads to crash on device only""","""""""Using Alloy 1.6.2 and calling $.UI.create(""""""""ScrollView"""""""") will cause the app to crash but only on real device with the following message:    {color:red}[ERROR] message = """"""""invalid method (createScrollView) passed to UIModule"""""""";{color}    I am testing with a completely empty new and fresh app on an iPad mini. This is working fine on Simulator or on Android.   Any idea?""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: $.UI.create(""""ScrollView"""") leads to crash on device only
Description: """"Using Alloy 1.6.2 and calling $.UI.create(""""""""ScrollView"""""""") will cause the app to crash but only on real device with the following message: {color:red}[ERROR] message = """"""""invalid method (createScrollView) passed to UIModule"""""""";{color} I am testing with a completely empty new and fresh app on an iPad mini. This is working fine on Simulator or on Android. Any idea?""""",title+description
TIMOB-19257,12,"""Android:  hide() doesn't work every time on Activity Indicator at launch""","""""""Create a default Alloy project with the following:    index.xml:      index.js      At launch, sometimes the indicator still shows. Not every time you'll see this issue, so you have to keep exiting and relaunching the app to see it happen.    My app loads external url at launch. It shows an indicator when loading, and hides it when finished or failed. In cases like network failure, the indicator.hide() is called almost right away, but not actually hided every time.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: hide() doesn't work every time on Activity Indicator at launch
Description: """"Create a default Alloy project with the following: index.xml: index.js At launch, sometimes the indicator still shows. Not every time you'll see this issue, so you have to keep exiting and relaunching the app to see it happen. My app loads external url at launch. It shows an indicator when loading, and hides it when finished or failed. In cases like network failure, the indicator.hide() is called almost right away, but not actually hided every time.""""",title+description
TIMOB-19256,12,"""Android: Expose setting renderPriority and layerType for Android WebView""","""""""Expose android webView attributes renderPriority and layerType so that we can gain some performance improvement on the webViews on Android.    Should be able to do something that translates to this native android code:  webview.getSettings().setRenderPriority(RenderPriority.HIGH);  webView.setLayerType(WebView.LAYER_TYPE_SOFTWARE, null);    Kindly refer to this article:  http://stackoverflow.com/questions/7422427/android-webview-slow""""""",Improvement,"Title: Android: Expose setting renderPriority and layerType for Android WebView
Description: """"Expose android webView attributes renderPriority and layerType so that we can gain some performance improvement on the webViews on Android. Should be able to do something that translates to this native android code: webview.getSettings().setRenderPriority(RenderPriority.HIGH); webView.setLayerType(WebView.LAYER_TYPE_SOFTWARE, null); Kindly refer to this article: http://stackoverflow.com/questions/7422427/android-webview-slow""""",title+description
TIMOB-19255,12,"""iOS: Empty ListView crashes with NSRangeException in 'scrollstart'""","""""""If you have an empty ListView and a register event listener to 'scrollstart' the app crashes directly after starting scrolling with:      h5. Test Case  # Start the app  # Start scrolling the ListView      h5. Console Log      h5. Possible reason  In the {{fireScrollEvent}} method of the TiUIListView class there is no check if the array is empty:   """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Empty ListView crashes with NSRangeException in 'scrollstart'
Description: """"If you have an empty ListView and a register event listener to 'scrollstart' the app crashes directly after starting scrolling with: h5. Test Case # Start the app # Start scrolling the ListView h5. Console Log h5. Possible reason In the {{fireScrollEvent}} method of the TiUIListView class there is no check if the array is empty: """"",title+description
