TIMOB-18592,12,"""Android: Titanium.UI.Android.ProgressIndicator cancelable property is not working.""","""""""Description:    This is regression as it is working with Titanium SDK 3.4.1 GA.  When cancelable property is set to false, clicking on back button should not allow user to cancel the progress dialog by pressing the BACK button.    STEP TO REPRODUCE:    1. Create classic app and replace it app.js with attached one.  2. Run the app  3. Click on the back button    EXPECTED RESULT:  3. Progress Dialog shouldn't be canceled on clicking the back button.    ACTUAL RESULT:  3. Clicking on back button cancels the progress dialog even the cancelable property is set to false.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Titanium.UI.Android.ProgressIndicator cancelable property is not working.
Description: """"Description: This is regression as it is working with Titanium SDK 3.4.1 GA. When cancelable property is set to false, clicking on back button should not allow user to cancel the progress dialog by pressing the BACK button. STEP TO REPRODUCE: 1. Create classic app and replace it app.js with attached one. 2. Run the app 3. Click on the back button EXPECTED RESULT: 3. Progress Dialog shouldn't be canceled on clicking the back button. ACTUAL RESULT: 3. Clicking on back button cancels the progress dialog even the cancelable property is set to false.""""",title+description
TIMOB-18590,12,"""CLI: Android: Difference in behavior of the CLI when using --no-launch on Genymotion emulators and Android emulators""","""""""h5.Description    When building an Android project with the flag --no-launch there is a difference between Genymotion emulators and Android emulators behavior. When built to Genymotion the <USER>exits after the build has finished but when building to an Android emulator the <USER>does not exit.    h5.Steps To Reproduce    1. Build a project to an Android emulator using ti build -p android -T emulator --no-launch    2. Build a project to a Genymotion emulator using ti build -p android -T emulator --no-launch    h5.Actual Result    1. When the build finishes the <USER>will not exit    2. When the build finishes the <USER>will exit    h5.Expected Result    I am unsure which of the two is meant to be the correct behavior but I believe it may be that the <USER>should exit after the build.  """"""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Android: Difference in behavior of the CLI when using --no-launch on Genymotion emulators and Android emulators
Description: """"h5.Description When building an Android project with the flag --no-launch there is a difference between Genymotion emulators and Android emulators behavior. When built to Genymotion the <USER>exits after the build has finished but when building to an Android emulator the <USER>does not exit. h5.Steps To Reproduce 1. Build a project to an Android emulator using ti build -p android -T emulator --no-launch 2. Build a project to a Genymotion emulator using ti build -p android -T emulator --no-launch h5.Actual Result 1. When the build finishes the <USER>will not exit 2. When the build finishes the <USER>will exit h5.Expected Result I am unsure which of the two is meant to be the correct behavior but I believe it may be that the <USER>should exit after the build. """"",title+description
TIMOB-18586,12,"""TiAPI: Create Node-compatible http module API""","""""""It'd be useful to create equivalents of some of the core Node modules in Titanium so that users could port/re-use node codebases on mobile devices. Some of the more common modules could map relatively easily to devices, and would be worth investigating.    http://nodejs.org/api/http.html    Some of the http module - specifically the http client - could map to similar APIs as our existing docs.<USER>com/titanium/3.0/#!/api/Titanium.Network.HTTPClient""""""",New Feature,"Title: TiAPI: Create Node-compatible http module API
Description: """"It'd be useful to create equivalents of some of the core Node modules in Titanium so that users could port/re-use node codebases on mobile devices. Some of the more common modules could map relatively easily to devices, and would be worth investigating. http://nodejs.org/api/http.html Some of the http module - specifically the http client - could map to similar APIs as our existing docs.<USER>com/titanium/3.0/#!/api/Titanium.Network.HTTPClient""""",title+description
TIMOB-18585,12,"""TiAPI: Create Node-compatible url module API""","""""""It'd be useful to create equivalents of some of the core Node modules in Titanium so that users could port/re-use node codebases on mobile devices. Some of the more common modules could map relatively easily to devices, and would be worth investigating.    http://nodejs.org/api/url.html""""""",New Feature,"Title: TiAPI: Create Node-compatible url module API
Description: """"It'd be useful to create equivalents of some of the core Node modules in Titanium so that users could port/re-use node codebases on mobile devices. Some of the more common modules could map relatively easily to devices, and would be worth investigating. http://nodejs.org/api/url.html""""",title+description
TIMOB-18579,12,"""iOS: Titanium.Network.HTTPClient setTlsVersion( tlsVersion ) doesn't handle invalid tlsVersion values.""","""""""Description:    This is not a regression.  setTlsVersion( tlsVersion ) method doesn't handle invalid values. It should make a check for invalid value rather than setting any given value.    STEPS TO REPRODUCE:  1.Create a classic app and replace it app.js with the attached one.  2. Run the app on device and check the console message.    ACTUAL RESULT:  2. It sets the value of Tls to '400' which is an invalid value.    EXPECTED RESULT:  2. It should give error message to user for setting invalid value.    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Titanium.Network.HTTPClient setTlsVersion( tlsVersion ) doesn't handle invalid tlsVersion values.
Description: """"Description: This is not a regression. setTlsVersion( tlsVersion ) method doesn't handle invalid values. It should make a check for invalid value rather than setting any given value. STEPS TO REPRODUCE: 1.Create a classic app and replace it app.js with the attached one. 2. Run the app on device and check the console message. ACTUAL RESULT: 2. It sets the value of Tls to '400' which is an invalid value. EXPECTED RESULT: 2. It should give error message to user for setting invalid value. """"",title+description
TIMOB-18578,12,"""iOS: When a popover shows, the iOS status bar is automatically displayed""","""""""With the current TI SDK and iOS 8.1, whenever I show a popover in my iPad application, the iOS navigation bar is displayed automatically (the iPad wifi symbol, current time and battery symbol on top). As soon as the popover is closed, the ios nav bar is hidden again.    How can I prevent the iOS navigation bar from appearing?""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: When a popover shows, the iOS status bar is automatically displayed
Description: """"With the current TI SDK and iOS 8.1, whenever I show a popover in my iPad application, the iOS navigation bar is displayed automatically (the iPad wifi symbol, current time and battery symbol on top). As soon as the popover is closed, the ios nav bar is hidden again. How can I prevent the iOS navigation bar from appearing?""""",title+description
TIMOB-18577,12,"""CLI/Studio: """"generate"""" command for i18n strings and more""","""""""Like Alloy, Titanium/<USER>could very well use a {{generate}} command and related Studio wizards as well.    Examples:    * {{ti generate i18n-strings nl}} to create {{i18n/nl/strings.xml}} with template  * {{ti generate i18n-app nl}} to create {{i18n/nl/app.xml}} with template  * {{ti generate android-theme mine}} to create {{platform/android/res/values/mine.xml}} with template    I'm sure there's many other common-used files that we could improve the UX with this way.""""""",New Feature,"Title: CLI/Studio: """"generate"""" command for i18n strings and more
Description: """"Like Alloy, Titanium/<USER>could very well use a {{generate}} command and related Studio wizards as well. Examples: * {{ti generate i18n-strings nl}} to create {{i18n/nl/strings.xml}} with template * {{ti generate i18n-app nl}} to create {{i18n/nl/app.xml}} with template * {{ti generate android-theme mine}} to create {{platform/android/res/values/mine.xml}} with template I'm sure there's many other common-used files that we could improve the UX with this way.""""",title+description
TIMOB-18576,12,"""LiveView: Console doesn't show pick up logs after restarting app in iOS Simulator""","""""""If a LiveView-enabled app crashes in iOS Simulator or when you manually force quit and restart the app, the console shows the client reconnecting but fails to show the app logs.    This is different from device, where the logs are displayed after an app restart. This is the preferred behaviour because it doesn't require you to re-deploy the app to the device after it crashes.    The location of the Simulator remains the same between app restarts so LiveView should be able to just pick it up again when the client reconnects.    h1. To reproduce    1. Run an app with LiveView in iOS Simulator  2. Force-close the app  3. Re-open the app  4. Watch the logs to see the client reconnects but doesn't show logs.    h1. Example log    """"""",Bug,"Title: LiveView: Console doesn't show pick up logs after restarting app in iOS Simulator
Description: """"If a LiveView-enabled app crashes in iOS Simulator or when you manually force quit and restart the app, the console shows the client reconnecting but fails to show the app logs. This is different from device, where the logs are displayed after an app restart. This is the preferred behaviour because it doesn't require you to re-deploy the app to the device after it crashes. The location of the Simulator remains the same between app restarts so LiveView should be able to just pick it up again when the client reconnects. h1. To reproduce 1. Run an app with LiveView in iOS Simulator 2. Force-close the app 3. Re-open the app 4. Watch the logs to see the client reconnects but doesn't show logs. h1. Example log """"",title+description
TIMOB-18575,12,"""RightNavButtons should be rendered from left to right""","""""""The order in which you push buttons to an array for {{rightNavButtons}} do not correspondent (in a logical way) with the order in which they are rendered.    h1. To reproduce    Take this Alloy example (it is not an Alloy bug, it's just more clear):        Which compiles to:    {code:js}      $.__views.__alloyId0 = Ti.UI.createWindow({          title: """"""""Title"""""""",          id: """"""""__alloyId0""""""""      });      var leftNavButtons = [];      $.__views.__alloyId2 = Ti.UI.createButton({          title: """"""""A"""""""",          id: """"""""__alloyId2""""""""      });      leftNavButtons.push($.__views.__alloyId2);      $.__views.__alloyId3 = Ti.UI.createButton({          title: """"""""B"""""""",          id: """"""""__alloyId3""""""""      });      leftNavButtons.push($.__views.__alloyId3);      $.__views.__alloyId0.leftNavButtons = leftNavButtons;      var rightNavButtons = [];      $.__views.__alloyId5 = Ti.UI.createButton({          title: """"""""C"""""""",          id: """"""""__alloyId5""""""""      });      rightNavButtons.push($.__views.__alloyId5);      $.__views.__alloyId6 = Ti.UI.createButton({          title: """"""""D"""""""",          id: """"""""__alloyId6""""""""      });      rightNavButtons.push($.__views.__alloyId6);      $.__views.__alloyId0.rightNavButtons = rightNavButtons;  {code}    You'd expect the window to have A+B on the left and then C+D on the right, but instead you see A+B on the left and D+C on the right.    See screenshot.""""""",Bug,"Title: RightNavButtons should be rendered from left to right
Description: """"The order in which you push buttons to an array for {{rightNavButtons}} do not correspondent (in a logical way) with the order in which they are rendered. h1. To reproduce Take this Alloy example (it is not an Alloy bug, it's just more clear): Which compiles to: {code:js} $.__views.__alloyId0 = Ti.UI.createWindow({ title: """"""""Title"""""""", id: """"""""__alloyId0"""""""" }); var leftNavButtons = []; $.__views.__alloyId2 = Ti.UI.createButton({ title: """"""""A"""""""", id: """"""""__alloyId2"""""""" }); leftNavButtons.push($.__views.__alloyId2); $.__views.__alloyId3 = Ti.UI.createButton({ title: """"""""B"""""""", id: """"""""__alloyId3"""""""" }); leftNavButtons.push($.__views.__alloyId3); $.__views.__alloyId0.leftNavButtons = leftNavButtons; var rightNavButtons = []; $.__views.__alloyId5 = Ti.UI.createButton({ title: """"""""C"""""""", id: """"""""__alloyId5"""""""" }); rightNavButtons.push($.__views.__alloyId5); $.__views.__alloyId6 = Ti.UI.createButton({ title: """"""""D"""""""", id: """"""""__alloyId6"""""""" }); rightNavButtons.push($.__views.__alloyId6); $.__views.__alloyId0.rightNavButtons = rightNavButtons; {code} You'd expect the window to have A+B on the left and then C+D on the right, but instead you see A+B on the left and D+C on the right. See screenshot.""""",title+description
TIMOB-18574,12,"""iOS: Unable to build to iOS sim or device (on Node 0.12)""","""""""h5.Description    When attempting to build for iOS the SDK throws a longjohn error shown below.        *To work around the above error*  Remove liveview from your config.json file manually, using the liveview rm clihook *may not fix the issue*. This can be found at Users -> <UserName> -> .titanium. In the hooks section remove:          If I change the SDK in the tiapp.xml to a previous SDK that worked then the build fails instantly with the error below        h5.Steps To Reproduce    1. Download the latest SDK ti sdk install -b master -d  2. Attempt to build to an iOS device or sim    h5.Actual Result    The build will fail and throw a longjohn error    h5.Expected Result    The build should not fail""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Unable to build to iOS sim or device (on Node 0.12)
Description: """"h5.Description When attempting to build for iOS the SDK throws a longjohn error shown below. *To work around the above error* Remove liveview from your config.json file manually, using the liveview rm clihook *may not fix the issue*. This can be found at Users -> <UserName> -> .titanium. In the hooks section remove: If I change the SDK in the tiapp.xml to a previous SDK that worked then the build fails instantly with the error below h5.Steps To Reproduce 1. Download the latest SDK ti sdk install -b master -d 2. Attempt to build to an iOS device or sim h5.Actual Result The build will fail and throw a longjohn error h5.Expected Result The build should not fail""""",title+description
TIMOB-18570,12,"""i18n: Only en strings load on simulator""","""""""Given an app using internationalized strings, and a simulator set to a non-English language, I would expect that i18n strings would be output in the configured language. Instead, the English strings are output.    The same app works as expected on the Android emulator, Android devices, and iOS devices.    This maybe a problem with the iOS simulator and not Titanium.    To reproduce:    1. Build the attached app for the iOS simulator. You'll get English values for the labels  2. Open the Settings app, tap General, tap Language & Region, tap iPhone Language, choose Espanol. Tap OK and confirm.  3. Re-open the i18n app. Note the status bar is in Spanish but the app's labels are English""""""",Bug,"Title: i18n: Only en strings load on simulator
Description: """"Given an app using internationalized strings, and a simulator set to a non-English language, I would expect that i18n strings would be output in the configured language. Instead, the English strings are output. The same app works as expected on the Android emulator, Android devices, and iOS devices. This maybe a problem with the iOS simulator and not Titanium. To reproduce: 1. Build the attached app for the iOS simulator. You'll get English values for the labels 2. Open the Settings app, tap General, tap Language & Region, tap iPhone Language, choose Espanol. Tap OK and confirm. 3. Re-open the i18n app. Note the status bar is in Spanish but the app's labels are English""""",title+description
