TIMOB-18131,12,"""iOS: When an application error is thrown no reason is given""","""""""h5.Description    When an error is thrown using the 3.5.0 SDK the following is printed out in the console        In the error logs in the console there should be a message telling the user where the error is as shown when using 3.4.1.GA SDK           This *is a regression* as context is given in 3.4.1.GA  On the device there are also differences, see the attached images for examples of these.    h5.Steps To Reproduce    1. Add the code below to an exisiting project and run the project on a device or sim    h5.Actual Result    The app will crash and will only show a reference to the line numbers rather than providing context    h5.Expected Result    The app will crash and should provide context, e.g. it should say """"""""message = """"""""'undefined' is not a function (evaluating 'e.foobar()')"""""""";""""""""""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: When an application error is thrown no reason is given
Description: """"h5.Description When an error is thrown using the 3.5.0 SDK the following is printed out in the console In the error logs in the console there should be a message telling the user where the error is as shown when using 3.4.1.GA SDK This *is a regression* as context is given in 3.4.1.GA On the device there are also differences, see the attached images for examples of these. h5.Steps To Reproduce 1. Add the code below to an exisiting project and run the project on a device or sim h5.Actual Result The app will crash and will only show a reference to the line numbers rather than providing context h5.Expected Result The app will crash and should provide context, e.g. it should say """"""""message = """"""""'undefined' is not a function (evaluating 'e.foobar()')"""""""";""""""""""""",title+description
TIMOB-18130,12,"""CLI: Ti build command fails for all platforms due to """"wrench undefined""""""","""""""h5. Description:  Our automated SDK validations board went red for all platforms with SDK 3.5.0.v20141203112513. After working with Tim, it seems that a missing """"""""require wrench"""""""" is the root of the problem. All build commands """"""""ti build"""""""" fail with """"""""wrench is undefined"""""""" There is some variance by platform:    For iOS and blackberry, a simple error is shown:      For mobileweb and Android, you get the following:       THIS IS A REGRESSION    h5. Steps to reproduce:  1) Download SDK 3.5.0.v20141203112513  2) Set this sdk as """"""""selected"""""""" in the ti config or with the """"""""-d"""""""" flag or by """"""""ti sdk"""""""" command  3) Create a project with this sdk in the tiapp.xml  4) Run command """"""""ti build"""""""" and choose any platform    h5. Result:  Build failure due to missing wrench    h5. Expected Result:  Build completes successfully """"""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Ti build command fails for all platforms due to """"wrench undefined""""
Description: """"h5. Description: Our automated SDK validations board went red for all platforms with SDK 3.5.0.v20141203112513. After working with Tim, it seems that a missing """"""""require wrench"""""""" is the root of the problem. All build commands """"""""ti build"""""""" fail with """"""""wrench is undefined"""""""" There is some variance by platform: For iOS and blackberry, a simple error is shown: For mobileweb and Android, you get the following: THIS IS A REGRESSION h5. Steps to reproduce: 1) Download SDK 3.5.0.v20141203112513 2) Set this sdk as """"""""selected"""""""" in the ti config or with the """"""""-d"""""""" flag or by """"""""ti sdk"""""""" command 3) Create a project with this sdk in the tiapp.xml 4) Run command """"""""ti build"""""""" and choose any platform h5. Result: Build failure due to missing wrench h5. Expected Result: Build completes successfully """"",title+description
TIMOB-18128,12,"""Android: Repeated call children.length leads to generate exception """"local reference table overflow""""""","""""""App fail with error when call many time children.length.  {code:title=index.xml|borderStyle=solid}  <Alloy>   <Window class=""""""""container"""""""" layout=""""""""vertical"""""""">    <Label id=""""""""label"""""""" onClick=""""""""doClick"""""""">Click me</Label>    <View id=""""""""myChildren"""""""" layout=""""""""vertical""""""""></View>   </Window>  </Alloy>  {code}  {code:title=index.js|borderStyle=solid}  function doClick(e) {      for(var i=0;i<200;i++) {          var c = $.index.children[1];          c.children.length;          c.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({text: 'label '+i}));          c=null;      }  }    $.index.open();  {code}      """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Repeated call children.length leads to generate exception """"local reference table overflow""""
Description: """"App fail with error when call many time children.length. {code:title=index.xml|borderStyle=solid} <Alloy> <Window class=""""""""container"""""""" layout=""""""""vertical""""""""> <Label id=""""""""label"""""""" onClick=""""""""doClick"""""""">Click me</Label> <View id=""""""""myChildren"""""""" layout=""""""""vertical""""""""></View> </Window> </Alloy> {code} {code:title=index.js|borderStyle=solid} function doClick(e) { for(var i=0;i<200;i++) { var c = $.index.children[1]; c.children.length; c.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({text: 'label '+i})); c=null; } } $.index.open(); {code} """"",title+description
TIMOB-18127,12,"""iOS: animating the textfield which is added to a tableview causes the keyboard to flicker""","""""""When a textfield is added to a tableview and when the height of the textfield is animated upon focus, the keyboard appears and immediately disappears. This would happen if you click on the textfield about 2-3 times and clicking/focusing the textfield several times would cause the keyboard to flicker.     This is a regression as the keyboard appears properly with SDK 3.4.1 and it does not flicker if the textfield is focused continuously. However the error message indicated in step 2 under actual results is still reproducible in 3.4.1    Steps to reproduce:  1. Run the attached app.js   2. focus on the textfield  3. focus on the textfield several times continuously     Actual Results:  2. Following error message is displayed:      and keyboard disappears immediately  3. Keyboard flickers.    Expected Results:  2. No error message and keyboard should stay unless 'return' key is pressed.  3. Keyboard should not flicker""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: animating the textfield which is added to a tableview causes the keyboard to flicker
Description: """"When a textfield is added to a tableview and when the height of the textfield is animated upon focus, the keyboard appears and immediately disappears. This would happen if you click on the textfield about 2-3 times and clicking/focusing the textfield several times would cause the keyboard to flicker. This is a regression as the keyboard appears properly with SDK 3.4.1 and it does not flicker if the textfield is focused continuously. However the error message indicated in step 2 under actual results is still reproducible in 3.4.1 Steps to reproduce: 1. Run the attached app.js 2. focus on the textfield 3. focus on the textfield several times continuously Actual Results: 2. Following error message is displayed: and keyboard disappears immediately 3. Keyboard flickers. Expected Results: 2. No error message and keyboard should stay unless 'return' key is pressed. 3. Keyboard should not flicker""""",title+description
TIMOB-18125,12,"""Android: CloudPush - trayClickLaunchedApp event not fired if listener added after window is open""","""""""When an app using cloudpush is killed, a push received, and the tray notification clicked, there are two events that fire.    # callback  # trackClickLaunchedApp    Both event get fired as they should if the event listeners are added before any window is opened. If you wait for the window to open then the `trackClickLaunchedApp` event does not get fired.    This happens because PROPERTY_TRAY_LAUNCHED_APP is added to the launch intent. When the event listener is added, the current activity is checked for the PROPERTY_TRAY_LAUNCHED_APP boolean and if it is found then the event is fired. If the event listener is added before a window is opened, it works fine because the current activity is the root activity and the intent has the property. However, once the new window is open, the activity returned by getActivity() is not the root activity, but the current activity that does not have the property set.    h5. Steps to reproduce:  # Run the example code below  # Ensure that you are able to receive a push  # Hit the back button to kill the app  # Send a push  # Click on the notification to launch the app  # Notice that only one of the alerts (callback) is displayed, trayClickLaunchedApp is never fired    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: CloudPush - trayClickLaunchedApp event not fired if listener added after window is open
Description: """"When an app using cloudpush is killed, a push received, and the tray notification clicked, there are two events that fire. # callback # trackClickLaunchedApp Both event get fired as they should if the event listeners are added before any window is opened. If you wait for the window to open then the `trackClickLaunchedApp` event does not get fired. This happens because PROPERTY_TRAY_LAUNCHED_APP is added to the launch intent. When the event listener is added, the current activity is checked for the PROPERTY_TRAY_LAUNCHED_APP boolean and if it is found then the event is fired. If the event listener is added before a window is opened, it works fine because the current activity is the root activity and the intent has the property. However, once the new window is open, the activity returned by getActivity() is not the root activity, but the current activity that does not have the property set. h5. Steps to reproduce: # Run the example code below # Ensure that you are able to receive a push # Hit the back button to kill the app # Send a push # Click on the notification to launch the app # Notice that only one of the alerts (callback) is displayed, trayClickLaunchedApp is never fired """"",title+description
TIMOB-18124,12,"""iOS: Xcode - """"No signing identity found"""" warning appears if you launch the Xcode project from the build folder""","""""""*Details:* After building a Titanium project for iOS and launching the Xcode project from the build folder, """"""""No signing identity found"""""""" warning message appears in Xcode.    *Note:*  * This is *not* a regression since this occurs with SDK 3.4.1.GA  * You can still install and launch the Xcode project onto an iOS device with the warning  * However, if you do select a valid signing identity in the Build Settings, you will get compile error: see compile_error.png and no_matching.png attachments  * Here is the compile error for copy and paste purposes:      *Steps to reproduce:*  1. Create a classic Titanium app  2. In the project, run the following CLI command: {{ti build -p ios -b}}  3. In the build folder, double-click on the Xcode project    *Actual:* In Xcode, you will see """"""""No signing identity found"""""""" warning message; see warning.png attachment.    *Expected:* You should not see the warning message.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Xcode - """"No signing identity found"""" warning appears if you launch the Xcode project from the build folder
Description: """"*Details:* After building a Titanium project for iOS and launching the Xcode project from the build folder, """"""""No signing identity found"""""""" warning message appears in Xcode. *Note:* * This is *not* a regression since this occurs with SDK 3.4.1.GA * You can still install and launch the Xcode project onto an iOS device with the warning * However, if you do select a valid signing identity in the Build Settings, you will get compile error: see compile_error.png and no_matching.png attachments * Here is the compile error for copy and paste purposes: *Steps to reproduce:* 1. Create a classic Titanium app 2. In the project, run the following CLI command: {{ti build -p ios -b}} 3. In the build folder, double-click on the Xcode project *Actual:* In Xcode, you will see """"""""No signing identity found"""""""" warning message; see warning.png attachment. *Expected:* You should not see the warning message.""""",title+description
TIMOB-18123,12,"""Windows: Generate final packaged windows SDK support""","""""""Once we have the cli pieces, templates, and the DLLs/libraries all generated we need to package them up under a """"""""windows"""""""" folder and zip that up. Then we need to create a script that adds the windows support to an existing 3.5/3.6.x build of Titanium SDK off master. This script will basically unzip to add the """"""""windows"""""""" folder into the SDK install and will edit the package.json to add """"""""windows"""""""" in the supported platform list.    Looking at blackberry as an example, this is the likely structure:  - 3.6.0  -- windows  --- cli  ---- commands  ---- hooks  ---- vendor  --- lib  ---- jsc  ----- arm  ----- include  ----- x86  ---- tiwin  ----- arm  ----- include  ----- x86  ---- ticore  ----- arm  ----- include  ----- x86  --- package.json  --- templates""""""",Improvement,"Title: Windows: Generate final packaged windows SDK support
Description: """"Once we have the cli pieces, templates, and the DLLs/libraries all generated we need to package them up under a """"""""windows"""""""" folder and zip that up. Then we need to create a script that adds the windows support to an existing 3.5/3.6.x build of Titanium SDK off master. This script will basically unzip to add the """"""""windows"""""""" folder into the SDK install and will edit the package.json to add """"""""windows"""""""" in the supported platform list. Looking at blackberry as an example, this is the likely structure: - 3.6.0 -- windows --- cli ---- commands ---- hooks ---- vendor --- lib ---- jsc ----- arm ----- include ----- x86 ---- tiwin ----- arm ----- include ----- x86 ---- ticore ----- arm ----- include ----- x86 --- package.json --- templates""""",title+description
TIMOB-18122,12,"""Windows: Build Titanium DLLs on Jenkins""","""""""We should be building Titanium/TitaniumWindows/JavaScriptCoreCPP libraries on Jenkins as a CI build so that we can take the results to use for packaging our Titanium SDK windows support.""""""",Improvement,"Title: Windows: Build Titanium DLLs on Jenkins
Description: """"We should be building Titanium/TitaniumWindows/JavaScriptCoreCPP libraries on Jenkins as a CI build so that we can take the results to use for packaging our Titanium SDK windows support.""""",title+description
TIMOB-18121,12,"""ti.moddevguide module for iOS 64 bit""",,Epic,Title: ti.moddevguide module for iOS 64 bit,title-only
TIMOB-18120,12,"""iOS: UI - iOS System Texture Color constants are not working""","""""""iOS System Texture Color constants are not working.     For example, following constants are having no effect on the code.  Ti.UI.iOS.COLOR_SCROLLVIEW_BACKGROUND  Ti.UI.iOS.COLOR_VIEW_FLIPSIDE_BACKGROUND  Ti.UI.iOS.COLOR_UNDER_PAGE_BACKGROUND    This is a *regression* since it doesn't occur in 3.4.1 GA    h5. Steps To Reproduce  1. Create a classic app using following code      2. Run the app  3. Click VIEW_FLIPSIDE   4. Click UNDER_PAGE    h5. Actual Result  White background is shown for all steps    h5. Expected Result  Step 2: Should see a textured gray background   Step 3: Should see a textured dark gray background   Step 4: Should see a textured light gray background """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: UI - iOS System Texture Color constants are not working
Description: """"iOS System Texture Color constants are not working. For example, following constants are having no effect on the code. Ti.UI.iOS.COLOR_SCROLLVIEW_BACKGROUND Ti.UI.iOS.COLOR_VIEW_FLIPSIDE_BACKGROUND Ti.UI.iOS.COLOR_UNDER_PAGE_BACKGROUND This is a *regression* since it doesn't occur in 3.4.1 GA h5. Steps To Reproduce 1. Create a classic app using following code 2. Run the app 3. Click VIEW_FLIPSIDE 4. Click UNDER_PAGE h5. Actual Result White background is shown for all steps h5. Expected Result Step 2: Should see a textured gray background Step 3: Should see a textured dark gray background Step 4: Should see a textured light gray background """"",title+description
TIMOB-18119,12,"""iOS: Ti.UI.iPhone.StatusBar constants generate invalid warnings on Studio console.""","""""""Ti.UI.iPhone.StatusBar constants are generating following warning message.      This message is invalid since there is no release version called 3.4.2.    h5. Steps to reproduce  1. Create a classic app using following code    2. Run on iPhone device    h5. Actual Result  Invalid warning message is thrown in studio console.    h5. Expected Result  Warning message should be valid. It should use valid release versions and constant names.  For Eg: *OPAQUE_BLACK* and *LIGHT_CONTENT* are not used in code.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Ti.UI.iPhone.StatusBar constants generate invalid warnings on Studio console.
Description: """"Ti.UI.iPhone.StatusBar constants are generating following warning message. This message is invalid since there is no release version called 3.4.2. h5. Steps to reproduce 1. Create a classic app using following code 2. Run on iPhone device h5. Actual Result Invalid warning message is thrown in studio console. h5. Expected Result Warning message should be valid. It should use valid release versions and constant names. For Eg: *OPAQUE_BLACK* and *LIGHT_CONTENT* are not used in code.""""",title+description
TIMOB-18118,12,"""iOS: ti.cloud - App crashes on launch if you require in ti.cloud module with SDK 3.5.0""","""""""*Details:* If you require in the GA ti.cloud module (3.2.7) into your app running SDK 3.5.0, then your app will crash on launch. Specifically, this line of code from ti.cloud.js: {{if (null != b && null != e)}}. However, if you use strict equal, e.g. {{if (null !== b && null !== e)}}, then the app will launch fine.    *Notes:*  * This is a *regression* as this does not occur in SDK 3.4.1.GA  * -This issue it *not reproducible* if you require in APM-    *Steps to reproduce:*  1. Create the default Titanium classic app  2. Include this snippet of code in app.js (snippet of code is from ti.cloud.js):    3. Install app to device  4. Launch app    *Actual:* App will crash on launch; see crash.log and console.log.  *Expected:* App should not crash if you require in the GA ti.cloud module.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: ti.cloud - App crashes on launch if you require in ti.cloud module with SDK 3.5.0
Description: """"*Details:* If you require in the GA ti.cloud module (3.2.7) into your app running SDK 3.5.0, then your app will crash on launch. Specifically, this line of code from ti.cloud.js: {{if (null != b && null != e)}}. However, if you use strict equal, e.g. {{if (null !== b && null !== e)}}, then the app will launch fine. *Notes:* * This is a *regression* as this does not occur in SDK 3.4.1.GA * -This issue it *not reproducible* if you require in APM- *Steps to reproduce:* 1. Create the default Titanium classic app 2. Include this snippet of code in app.js (snippet of code is from ti.cloud.js): 3. Install app to device 4. Launch app *Actual:* App will crash on launch; see crash.log and console.log. *Expected:* App should not crash if you require in the GA ti.cloud module.""""",title+description
TIMOB-18115,12,"""CLI: Drop support for Node.js 0.8""","""""""We need to drop support for Node.js 0.8. There are a number of Node dependencies that no longer support it and so we can no longer support it.    Dropping support includes:  * Updating the """"""""engine"""""""" version in the package.json of both the Titanium CLI and node-appc.  * Update the Titanium CLI Node.js version enforcement: https://github.com/<USER>titanium/blob/master/lib/titanium.js#L29-L37  * Publish a new version of node-appc  * Update Titanium CLI's node-appc version  * Publish a new version of Titanium CLI    NOTE: As soon as node-appc has been updated, you can rev it and publish it.""""""",Improvement,"Title: CLI: Drop support for Node.js 0.8
Description: """"We need to drop support for Node.js 0.8. There are a number of Node dependencies that no longer support it and so we can no longer support it. Dropping support includes: * Updating the """"""""engine"""""""" version in the package.json of both the Titanium CLI and node-appc. * Update the Titanium CLI Node.js version enforcement: https://github.com/<USER>titanium/blob/master/lib/titanium.js#L29-L37 * Publish a new version of node-appc * Update Titanium CLI's node-appc version * Publish a new version of Titanium CLI NOTE: As soon as node-appc has been updated, you can rev it and publish it.""""",title+description
TIMOB-18112,12,"""MobileWeb: TableView ignores clicks when sections are present""","""""""h4. Test code    {code:js}  /*jslint node: true */  """"""""use strict"""""""";  /*global Titanium */    function rowClicked(e) {      Titanium.API.trace(""""""""ROW CLICKED: e = """""""" + JSON.stringify(e));  }    var with_sections = true,      sectionOne = Titanium.UI.createTableViewSection({          headerTitle: 'section_one'      }),      sectionTwo = Titanium.UI.createTableViewSection({          headerTitle: 'section_two'      }),      tableview = Titanium.UI.createTableView({          height: Titanium.UI.FILL,          width: Titanium.UI.FILL,          top: 0,          showVerticalScrollIndicator: true,          minRowHeight: 48,      }),      rowOne = Titanium.UI.createTableViewRow({          title: 'row_one'      }),      rowTwo = Titanium.UI.createTableViewRow({          title: 'row_two'      }),      win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor:  '#fff' });    if (with_sections) {      sectionOne.add(rowOne);      sectionTwo.add(rowTwo);      tableview.setData([sectionOne, sectionTwo]);  } else {      tableview.setData([rowOne, rowTwo]);  }  tableview.addEventListener('click', rowClicked);  win.add(tableview);  win.open();  {code}    h4. Behaviour under iOS    - with_sections = true: responds to row clicks, prints sensible e object  - with_sections = false: responds to row clicks, prints sensible e object    h4. Behaviour under MobileWeb  - with_sections = true: IGNORES row clicks  - with_sections = false: responds to row clicks ALTHOUGH gives """"""""Uncaught TypeError: Converting circular structure to JSON"""""""" error  """"""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: TableView ignores clicks when sections are present
Description: """"h4. Test code {code:js} /*jslint node: true */ """"""""use strict""""""""; /*global Titanium */ function rowClicked(e) { Titanium.API.trace(""""""""ROW CLICKED: e = """""""" + JSON.stringify(e)); } var with_sections = true, sectionOne = Titanium.UI.createTableViewSection({ headerTitle: 'section_one' }), sectionTwo = Titanium.UI.createTableViewSection({ headerTitle: 'section_two' }), tableview = Titanium.UI.createTableView({ height: Titanium.UI.FILL, width: Titanium.UI.FILL, top: 0, showVerticalScrollIndicator: true, minRowHeight: 48, }), rowOne = Titanium.UI.createTableViewRow({ title: 'row_one' }), rowTwo = Titanium.UI.createTableViewRow({ title: 'row_two' }), win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: '#fff' }); if (with_sections) { sectionOne.add(rowOne); sectionTwo.add(rowTwo); tableview.setData([sectionOne, sectionTwo]); } else { tableview.setData([rowOne, rowTwo]); } tableview.addEventListener('click', rowClicked); win.add(tableview); win.open(); {code} h4. Behaviour under iOS - with_sections = true: responds to row clicks, prints sensible e object - with_sections = false: responds to row clicks, prints sensible e object h4. Behaviour under MobileWeb - with_sections = true: IGNORES row clicks - with_sections = false: responds to row clicks ALTHOUGH gives """"""""Uncaught TypeError: Converting circular structure to JSON"""""""" error """"",title+description
TIMOB-18108,12,"""Deprecate Ti.Filesystem.File.rename, or make it the same as Ti.Filesystem.File.move""","""""""According to the documentation, the {{Ti.Filesystem.File.rename()}} function does exactly the same thing as the {{Ti.Filesystem.File.move()}} function, except that {{rename}} has the added stipulation that the file must still reside in the current directory after the rename. Considering that pretty much every system in the world that implements {{rename}} and {{move}} gives them the exact same functionality, our implementation is confusing and prone to unnecessary mistakes.    I would propose one of two solutions:    # *Deprecate and eventually remove Ti.Filesystem.File.rename.* All it is is an under-powered, error-prone version of {{move}}. There's literally no reason for it functionality-wise. We should phase it out and remove it entirely.  # *Give Ti.Filesystem.File.rename the same exact functionality as Ti.Filesystem.File.move.* This would keep both functions, breaking no existing code, but would have them both behave exactly the same way, meeting the world's expectations of what these functions do. It leaves us with redundant APIs, but it seems making no breaking changes and removing this confusing difference between the 2 might be the best choice.""""""",Improvement,"Title: Deprecate Ti.Filesystem.File.rename, or make it the same as Ti.Filesystem.File.move
Description: """"According to the documentation, the {{Ti.Filesystem.File.rename()}} function does exactly the same thing as the {{Ti.Filesystem.File.move()}} function, except that {{rename}} has the added stipulation that the file must still reside in the current directory after the rename. Considering that pretty much every system in the world that implements {{rename}} and {{move}} gives them the exact same functionality, our implementation is confusing and prone to unnecessary mistakes. I would propose one of two solutions: # *Deprecate and eventually remove Ti.Filesystem.File.rename.* All it is is an under-powered, error-prone version of {{move}}. There's literally no reason for it functionality-wise. We should phase it out and remove it entirely. # *Give Ti.Filesystem.File.rename the same exact functionality as Ti.Filesystem.File.move.* This would keep both functions, breaking no existing code, but would have them both behave exactly the same way, meeting the world's expectations of what these functions do. It leaves us with redundant APIs, but it seems making no breaking changes and removing this confusing difference between the 2 might be the best choice.""""",title+description
