TIMOB-17555,12,"""Android: Implement TextureView Android for Media Streams ""","""""""According to the docs of Android Developers:    ??citation??  """"""""A TextureView can be used to display a content stream. Such a content stream can for instance be a video or an OpenGL scene. The content stream can come from the application's <USER>as well as a remote <USER>    TextureView can only be used in a hardware accelerated window. When rendered in software, TextureView will draw nothing.    Unlike SurfaceView, TextureView does not create a separate window but behaves as a regular View. This key difference allows a TextureView to be moved, transformed, animated, etc. For instance, you can make a TextureView semi-translucent by calling myView.setAlpha(0.5f).""""""""  ??citation??    This TextureView allows us to create features like youtube has implemented to drag video's and such.    Also we will be able to (I think) use video's inside scroll views using these TextureViews as they act the same as a normal view, which means they can be transformed and thus scrolled.    Is it possible to add this feature in the future?""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Implement TextureView Android for Media Streams
Description: """"According to the docs of Android Developers: ??citation?? """"""""A TextureView can be used to display a content stream. Such a content stream can for instance be a video or an OpenGL scene. The content stream can come from the application's <USER>as well as a remote <USER> TextureView can only be used in a hardware accelerated window. When rendered in software, TextureView will draw nothing. Unlike SurfaceView, TextureView does not create a separate window but behaves as a regular View. This key difference allows a TextureView to be moved, transformed, animated, etc. For instance, you can make a TextureView semi-translucent by calling myView.setAlpha(0.5f)."""""""" ??citation?? This TextureView allows us to create features like youtube has implemented to drag video's and such. Also we will be able to (I think) use video's inside scroll views using these TextureViews as they act the same as a normal view, which means they can be transformed and thus scrolled. Is it possible to add this feature in the future?""""",title+description
TIMOB-17553,12,"""Android: showDatePickerDialog cancel property always false and callback called twice""","""""""h4. Problem Description  On android the datepicker always returns """"""""cancel:false"""""""" (if you cancel or accept)    If you press cancel (back button or press outside the modal popup)  the function callback gets called 1time    if you accept the date  the function callback gets called 2 times    h4. Steps to reproduce  - Create a simple project.  - Paste the testcase into  app.js  - Run on android tab    h4. Testcase      h4. Workaround  To avoid this bug I ignore the cancel property in the callback, and I check how many times the callback function gets executed.   """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: showDatePickerDialog cancel property always false and callback called twice
Description: """"h4. Problem Description On android the datepicker always returns """"""""cancel:false"""""""" (if you cancel or accept) If you press cancel (back button or press outside the modal popup) the function callback gets called 1time if you accept the date the function callback gets called 2 times h4. Steps to reproduce - Create a simple project. - Paste the testcase into app.js - Run on android tab h4. Testcase h4. Workaround To avoid this bug I ignore the cancel property in the callback, and I check how many times the callback function gets executed. """"",title+description
TIMOB-17552,12,"""Android: Actionbar shows up although you disable it with a costom theme when opacity is used in window.""","""""""h4. Problem Description   When you disable the actionbar in the new 3.3.0.GA SDK release and set the opacity or the backgroundColor to something transparent in a window, the actionbar will show up.    h4. Sample Code   h4. app.js  {code:app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({   backgroundColor : 'white',   opacity: '0.5'  });  var image = Ti.UI.createView({    backgroundColor : 'red',  });  win.add(image);  win.open();      h4. tiapp.xml  {code:tiapp.xml}  <android xmlns:android=""""""""http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"""""""">      <manifest>          <application android:theme=""""""""@style/Theme.NoActionBar""""""""/>          <!-- Need to specify at least API level 11 for Titanium SDK 3.2.x and prior -->          <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion=""""""""14"""""""" android:targetSdkVersion=""""""""19""""""""/>      </manifest>  </android>  {code}    h4. Steps to reproduce  1. Create a new mobile project  2. Paste the testcase where needed   3. Test in device    h4. Actual Results   Actionbar shows when opacity property is used in window, even though Actionbar is disabled using custom theme file in the project.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Actionbar shows up although you disable it with a costom theme when opacity is used in window.
Description: """"h4. Problem Description When you disable the actionbar in the new 3.3.0.GA SDK release and set the opacity or the backgroundColor to something transparent in a window, the actionbar will show up. h4. Sample Code h4. app.js {code:app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor : 'white', opacity: '0.5' }); var image = Ti.UI.createView({ backgroundColor : 'red', }); win.add(image); win.open(); h4. tiapp.xml {code:tiapp.xml} <android xmlns:android=""""""""http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android""""""""> <manifest> <application android:theme=""""""""@style/Theme.NoActionBar""""""""/> <!-- Need to specify at least API level 11 for Titanium SDK 3.2.x and prior --> <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion=""""""""14"""""""" android:targetSdkVersion=""""""""19""""""""/> </manifest> </android> {code} h4. Steps to reproduce 1. Create a new mobile project 2. Paste the testcase where needed 3. Test in device h4. Actual Results Actionbar shows when opacity property is used in window, even though Actionbar is disabled using custom theme file in the project.""""",title+description
