TIMOB-17427,12,"""iOS: Analytics - SQLite database error appears if you upgrade and downgrade your sdk several times""","""""""*Details:* SQLite database error appears if you upgrade and downgrade your sdk several times.    *Steps to reproduce:*  1. Create a default classic Titanium app with *SDK 3.3.0.GA*  2. Add Ti.Analytics.navEvent and Ti.Analytics.featureEvent any where in app.js:      3. Install app to device/simulator  4. After app launches, rebuild project with *SDK 3.2.3.GA* and install app again onto same device/simulator  5. After app launches, rebuild project with *SDK 3.3.0.GA* and install app again onto same device/simulator    *Actual:*  SQLite database error appears in the console (see attachment).  *Expected:* SQLite database error should not appear.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Analytics - SQLite database error appears if you upgrade and downgrade your sdk several times
Description: """"*Details:* SQLite database error appears if you upgrade and downgrade your sdk several times. *Steps to reproduce:* 1. Create a default classic Titanium app with *SDK 3.3.0.GA* 2. Add Ti.Analytics.navEvent and Ti.Analytics.featureEvent any where in app.js: 3. Install app to device/simulator 4. After app launches, rebuild project with *SDK 3.2.3.GA* and install app again onto same device/simulator 5. After app launches, rebuild project with *SDK 3.3.0.GA* and install app again onto same device/simulator *Actual:* SQLite database error appears in the console (see attachment). *Expected:* SQLite database error should not appear.""""",title+description
TIMOB-17426,12,"""iOS: ListView - a lot of items, changes others like clones""","""""""h4. Problem Description   If you change an item, the change will be reflected on other items as well by error.     h4. Steps to reproduce  1. Create a new mobile app (classic titanium)   2. Paste the testcode into the app.js     3. Run the app and click on the second item.   4. Scroll down and you will see that the second row of the next """"""""page/screen"""""""" changed as well (like if they were clones)    Extra info:  If I set down itemCount = 10; then this behavior will not happen.    - Other examples here: http://developer.<USER>com/question/176217/did-someone-have-seen-this  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: ListView - a lot of items, changes others like clones
Description: """"h4. Problem Description If you change an item, the change will be reflected on other items as well by error. h4. Steps to reproduce 1. Create a new mobile app (classic titanium) 2. Paste the testcode into the app.js 3. Run the app and click on the second item. 4. Scroll down and you will see that the second row of the next """"""""page/screen"""""""" changed as well (like if they were clones) Extra info: If I set down itemCount = 10; then this behavior will not happen. - Other examples here: http://developer.<USER>com/question/176217/did-someone-have-seen-this """"",title+description
TIMOB-17424,12,"""Android: Add ListView """"change"""" event to be fired when items change""","""""""Infinite scroll is a common concept used in long lists/tables. Both I (https://github.com/FokkeZB/nl.<USER>infiniteScroll/pull/24) and yourself (https://github.com/<USER>se/InfiniteListView/blob/master/app/controllers/index.js) have been trying to implement this in the new ListView.    When you keep it synchronous and integrated like the second example this is simple, but wrapped in a generic widget you run into the issue on setting and moving the marker when the list is first populated or items are added later through other means then through the widget detecting the user scrolling to the end.    I think the {{marker}}-event is fine and I understand why the {{scroll}}-event needed to be removed to improve performance. However, I think it would be even better if complimented by a {{change}}-event that would fire when the content of the list changes:    * A section is added/removed (/changed?)  * An item is added/removed (/changed?)    With this event such a widget would be able to re-set the marker to the last item.    An alternative that might be even better is to have a {{end}}-event that fires when the list is scrolled to the end. This would *not* allow you to already start loading when the user is *almost* at the end though, so I would still call for the {{change}}-event (as well).""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Add ListView """"change"""" event to be fired when items change
Description: """"Infinite scroll is a common concept used in long lists/tables. Both I (https://github.com/FokkeZB/nl.<USER>infiniteScroll/pull/24) and yourself (https://github.com/<USER>se/InfiniteListView/blob/master/app/controllers/index.js) have been trying to implement this in the new ListView. When you keep it synchronous and integrated like the second example this is simple, but wrapped in a generic widget you run into the issue on setting and moving the marker when the list is first populated or items are added later through other means then through the widget detecting the user scrolling to the end. I think the {{marker}}-event is fine and I understand why the {{scroll}}-event needed to be removed to improve performance. However, I think it would be even better if complimented by a {{change}}-event that would fire when the content of the list changes: * A section is added/removed (/changed?) * An item is added/removed (/changed?) With this event such a widget would be able to re-set the marker to the last item. An alternative that might be even better is to have a {{end}}-event that fires when the list is scrolled to the end. This would *not* allow you to already start loading when the user is *almost* at the end though, so I would still call for the {{change}}-event (as well).""""",title+description
TIMOB-17423,12,"""iOS: Add ListView """"change"""" event to be fired when items change""","""""""Infinite scroll is a common concept used in long lists/tables. Both I (https://github.com/FokkeZB/nl.<USER>infiniteScroll/pull/24) and yourself (https://github.com/<USER>se/InfiniteListView/blob/master/app/controllers/index.js) have been trying to implement this in the new ListView.    When you keep it synchronous and integrated like the second example this is simple, but wrapped in a generic widget you run into the issue on setting and moving the marker when the list is first populated or items are added later through other means then through the widget detecting the user scrolling to the end.    I think the {{marker}}-event is fine and I understand why the {{scroll}}-event needed to be removed to improve performance. However, I think it would be even better if complimented by a {{change}}-event that would fire when the content of the list changes:    * A section is added/removed (/changed?)  * An item is added/removed (/changed?)    With this event such a widget would be able to re-set the marker to the last item.    An alternative that might be even better is to have a {{end}}-event that fires when the list is scrolled to the end. This would *not* allow you to already start loading when the user is *almost* at the end though, so I would still call for the {{change}}-event (as well).""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Add ListView """"change"""" event to be fired when items change
Description: """"Infinite scroll is a common concept used in long lists/tables. Both I (https://github.com/FokkeZB/nl.<USER>infiniteScroll/pull/24) and yourself (https://github.com/<USER>se/InfiniteListView/blob/master/app/controllers/index.js) have been trying to implement this in the new ListView. When you keep it synchronous and integrated like the second example this is simple, but wrapped in a generic widget you run into the issue on setting and moving the marker when the list is first populated or items are added later through other means then through the widget detecting the user scrolling to the end. I think the {{marker}}-event is fine and I understand why the {{scroll}}-event needed to be removed to improve performance. However, I think it would be even better if complimented by a {{change}}-event that would fire when the content of the list changes: * A section is added/removed (/changed?) * An item is added/removed (/changed?) With this event such a widget would be able to re-set the marker to the last item. An alternative that might be even better is to have a {{end}}-event that fires when the list is scrolled to the end. This would *not* allow you to already start loading when the user is *almost* at the end though, so I would still call for the {{change}}-event (as well).""""",title+description
TIMOB-17422,12,"""iOS8: KitchenSink > PhotoGallery: Unable to see selected photo in Window view on iPad""","""""""*Steps to reproduce:*  1. Install KS on iOS 8 device(iPad).  2. Navigate to Phone> Photo Gallery  Should be able to choose photo from gallery which pops up.  3.click any on photo and then press """"""""Use""""""""      {color:green}*Expected Result:*{color}  Should see selected photo in window view    {color:red}*Actual Result:*{color}  Unable to see selected photo in window view    Issues appears in iPad only, its working fine on iPhone devices. Tested with iPhone5S.  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS8: KitchenSink > PhotoGallery: Unable to see selected photo in Window view on iPad
