TIMOB-16877,12,"""Android: Build errors setting a targetSdkVersion lower than 14""","""""""h5. Problem description  If specifying an Android targetSdkVersion lower than '14' in the Android manifest, the build fails.    h5. Steps to reproduce  1. Create a new Titanium classic or Alloy project  2. Edit TiApp.xml and add the following lines:    3. Build the app.    Result: build fails with errors relative to Holo themes and activebars. See attached log.    4. Edit TiApp.xml and set """"""""14"""""""" as the targetSdkVersion.  Result: build is successful and app runs as expected.    The issue is only reproducible using TiSDK 3.3.0.  Not reproducible with TiSDK 3.2.X. It could be a regression.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Build errors setting a targetSdkVersion lower than 14
Description: """"h5. Problem description If specifying an Android targetSdkVersion lower than '14' in the Android manifest, the build fails. h5. Steps to reproduce 1. Create a new Titanium classic or Alloy project 2. Edit TiApp.xml and add the following lines: 3. Build the app. Result: build fails with errors relative to Holo themes and activebars. See attached log. 4. Edit TiApp.xml and set """"""""14"""""""" as the targetSdkVersion. Result: build is successful and app runs as expected. The issue is only reproducible using TiSDK 3.3.0. Not reproducible with TiSDK 3.2.X. It could be a regression.""""",title+description
TIMOB-16876,12,"""Include Git hashes of all included repos in manifest.json""","""""""Currently, manifest.json includes the git hash for just the commit in titanium_mobile.    Ideally, every repo which makes up the actual build for the Titanium SDK should have the git hash in question listed as well. One suggestion is that we have some sort of list of organization/repo_name: hash for everything listed in https://github.com/<USER>titanium_releases/blob/3_2_X/titanium.json.    So:  * <USER>titanium_mobile  * <USER>titanium_mobile_blackberry  * <USER>modules/ti.facebook  * <USER>modules/ti.cloud  * <USER>modules/ti.cloudpush  * <USER>modules/ti.map  * <USER>modules/urlSession  """"""",Improvement,"Title: Include Git hashes of all included repos in manifest.json
Description: """"Currently, manifest.json includes the git hash for just the commit in titanium_mobile. Ideally, every repo which makes up the actual build for the Titanium SDK should have the git hash in question listed as well. One suggestion is that we have some sort of list of organization/repo_name: hash for everything listed in https://github.com/<USER>titanium_releases/blob/3_2_X/titanium.json. So: * <USER>titanium_mobile * <USER>titanium_mobile_blackberry * <USER>modules/ti.facebook * <USER>modules/ti.cloud * <USER>modules/ti.cloudpush * <USER>modules/ti.map * <USER>modules/urlSession """"",title+description
TIMOB-16875,12,"""iOS: Change in """"insertSubview"""" (via TIMOB-11586) causes crash""","""""""For the last couple of releases of the mobile SDK (I just upgraded to 3.2.2) I've had to apply the patch as described in http://developer.<USER>com/question/153366/crash-on-310-related-with-timob-11586, or the app would crash instantly.    I'm unclear on the implications of applying this patch, but I see no more crashes in my app.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Change in """"insertSubview"""" (via TIMOB-11586) causes crash
Description: """"For the last couple of releases of the mobile SDK (I just upgraded to 3.2.2) I've had to apply the patch as described in http://developer.<USER>com/question/153366/crash-on-310-related-with-timob-11586, or the app would crash instantly. I'm unclear on the implications of applying this patch, but I see no more crashes in my app.""""",title+description
TIMOB-16874,12,"""Android: require() from an included file consistently throws an error if app was started for service (not through app.js)""","""""""We wanted to use Android's AlarmManager functionality through titanium. As suggested in this issue - https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-2752  We used bencoding alarmmanager - https://github.com/benbahrenburg/benCoding.AlarmManager.    We noticed a bug in titanium while using this module. We scheduled a service to be run using AlarmManager. When the app is not running, if the service is started by alarmmanger after configured interval, require() inside an included file from service consistently throws the following error.  {color:red}   [WARN] :   TiExceptionHandler: (main) [0,67831] Activity is null or already finishing, skipping dialog.  [ERROR] :  V8Exception: Exception occurred at ti:/titanium.js:140: Uncaught TypeError: undefined is not a function  {color}    Once the service throws this error, if we start the app, it prints the following error in console and shows an error dialog as attached (Error.png).  {color:red}   [ERROR] : TiExceptionHandler: (main) [115,1154] ----- Titanium Javascript Runtime Error -----  [ERROR] : TiExceptionHandler: (main) [1,1155] - In ti:/titanium.js:140,10  [ERROR] : TiExceptionHandler: (main) [4,1159] - Message: Uncaught TypeError: undefined is not a function  [ERROR] : TiExceptionHandler: (main) [2,1161] - Source: return contextRequire(path, context, sourceUrl);  [ERROR] : V8Exception: Exception occurred at ti:/titanium.js:140: Uncaught TypeError: undefined is not a function  {color}    As per the module owner, it is not a bug in his module - https://github.com/benbahrenburg/benCoding.AlarmManager/issues/37. After going through titanium.js and module.js, it appears like a bug in titanium code:    https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/android/runtime/common/src/js/titanium.js  // The require function we want to wrap for this context  var contextRequire = global.require;  if (ti.global) {  contextRequire = ti.global.require;  }  return contextRequire(path, context, sourceUrl);    Here contextRequire is undefined which means global.require is undefined in this context. global.require seems to be assigned only if app is started using app.js and not through service means in https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/android/runtime/common/src/js/module.js  // This """"""""first time"""""""" run is really only for app.js, AFAICT, and needs  // an activity. If app was restarted for Service only, we don't want  // to go this route. So added currentActivity check. (bill)  if (self.id == '.' && self.context.currentActivity) {  global.require = require;  Titanium.Android.currentActivity = self.context.currentActivity;    return runInThisContext(source, filename, true);  }    As we use MVC pattern we can't avoid using Ti.include() and require() from withing included files for better code organisation and to avoid polluting global namespace. So the suggestion of don't use include() won't work for us. Please investigate and confirm whether the bug is with titanium.    Attached Project.zip which contains the sample code that demonstrates this scenario and error cases using bencoding alarmamanger.  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: require() from an included file consistently throws an error if app was started for service (not through app.js)
Description: """"We wanted to use Android's AlarmManager functionality through titanium. As suggested in this issue - https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-2752 We used bencoding alarmmanager - https://github.com/benbahrenburg/benCoding.AlarmManager. We noticed a bug in titanium while using this module. We scheduled a service to be run using AlarmManager. When the app is not running, if the service is started by alarmmanger after configured interval, require() inside an included file from service consistently throws the following error. {color:red} [WARN] : TiExceptionHandler: (main) [0,67831] Activity is null or already finishing, skipping dialog. [ERROR] : V8Exception: Exception occurred at ti:/titanium.js:140: Uncaught TypeError: undefined is not a function {color} Once the service throws this error, if we start the app, it prints the following error in console and shows an error dialog as attached (Error.png). {color:red} [ERROR] : TiExceptionHandler: (main) [115,1154] ----- Titanium Javascript Runtime Error ----- [ERROR] : TiExceptionHandler: (main) [1,1155] - In ti:/titanium.js:140,10 [ERROR] : TiExceptionHandler: (main) [4,1159] - Message: Uncaught TypeError: undefined is not a function [ERROR] : TiExceptionHandler: (main) [2,1161] - Source: return contextRequire(path, context, sourceUrl); [ERROR] : V8Exception: Exception occurred at ti:/titanium.js:140: Uncaught TypeError: undefined is not a function {color} As per the module owner, it is not a bug in his module - https://github.com/benbahrenburg/benCoding.AlarmManager/issues/37. After going through titanium.js and module.js, it appears like a bug in titanium code: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/android/runtime/common/src/js/titanium.js // The require function we want to wrap for this context var contextRequire = global.require; if (ti.global) { contextRequire = ti.global.require; } return contextRequire(path, context, sourceUrl); Here contextRequire is undefined which means global.require is undefined in this context. global.require seems to be assigned only if app is started using app.js and not through service means in https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/blob/master/android/runtime/common/src/js/module.js // This """"""""first time"""""""" run is really only for app.js, AFAICT, and needs // an activity. If app was restarted for Service only, we don't want // to go this route. So added currentActivity check. (bill) if (self.id == '.' && self.context.currentActivity) { global.require = require; Titanium.Android.currentActivity = self.context.currentActivity; return runInThisContext(source, filename, true); } As we use MVC pattern we can't avoid using Ti.include() and require() from withing included files for better code organisation and to avoid polluting global namespace. So the suggestion of don't use include() won't work for us. Please investigate and confirm whether the bug is with titanium. Attached Project.zip which contains the sample code that demonstrates this scenario and error cases using bencoding alarmamanger. """"",title+description
TIMOB-16873,12,"""iOS: Switch Controls in ListViews are not reused correctly""","""""""When using Switches in ListViews that contain a large enough number of records so that the user must scroll (I'm using 40 in this test), the Switch components are not reused correctly. In the attached test, you can see that tapping the switch in the first row will cause it to change in other rows as those rows are later rendered (and the switch components are reused).    h3. Steps to reproduce  # Create an app with the following files:  {code:title=index.js}    var items = [];  for(var x=0;x<40;x++){   items.push({    template: """"""""tpl1"""""""",    lbl: {     text: """"""""Switch #"""""""" + (x+1)    },    done: {     itemId: """"""""sw_""""""""+x,     value: false    }   });  }  $.main.setItems(items);    $.index.open();    function handleChange(e){   console.log(JSON.stringify(e,null,2));  }    {code:title=index.tss}  """""""".container"""""""": {   top: 40,   backgroundColor:""""""""white""""""""  }  {code}  # Run the app and click on the first switch in the list  # Scroll down and look at the other switches (need to at least scroll past the last record on the first page)    h3. Expected Results  Only the switch that was tapped should change.  h3. Actual Results  Other switch components are changed as well after scrolling.  h3. Additional Info  Sometimes, scrolling back to the top will show that the wrong switch is modified (depending on the size of your list).  I've also been able to reproduce this with text fields if they all start out with the same value or and empty string ("""""""""""""""").  Seems the reuse issue is somehow related to the initial value of the editable component.  In this example, all switches start out in the false ( off ) state.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Switch Controls in ListViews are not reused correctly
Description: """"When using Switches in ListViews that contain a large enough number of records so that the user must scroll (I'm using 40 in this test), the Switch components are not reused correctly. In the attached test, you can see that tapping the switch in the first row will cause it to change in other rows as those rows are later rendered (and the switch components are reused). h3. Steps to reproduce # Create an app with the following files: {code:title=index.js} var items = []; for(var x=0;x<40;x++){ items.push({ template: """"""""tpl1"""""""", lbl: { text: """"""""Switch #"""""""" + (x+1) }, done: { itemId: """"""""sw_""""""""+x, value: false } }); } $.main.setItems(items); $.index.open(); function handleChange(e){ console.log(JSON.stringify(e,null,2)); } {code:title=index.tss} """""""".container"""""""": { top: 40, backgroundColor:""""""""white"""""""" } {code} # Run the app and click on the first switch in the list # Scroll down and look at the other switches (need to at least scroll past the last record on the first page) h3. Expected Results Only the switch that was tapped should change. h3. Actual Results Other switch components are changed as well after scrolling. h3. Additional Info Sometimes, scrolling back to the top will show that the wrong switch is modified (depending on the size of your list). I've also been able to reproduce this with text fields if they all start out with the same value or and empty string (""""""""""""""""). Seems the reuse issue is somehow related to the initial value of the editable component. In this example, all switches start out in the false ( off ) state.""""",title+description
TIMOB-16872,12,"""Titanium.appendRow() should be animated by default""","""""""h2. description    {{Ti.UI.TableView}} has a number of functions that add or remove rows. Those functions take a parameter that allows you to specify an animation for these operations. All of the functions will perform an animation by default, which the exception of [appendRow()|http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.UI.TableView-method-appendRow]. I think {{appendRow()}} should default to having an animation, just like all the other functions.    h2. expected    [Ti.UI.TableView.appendRow()|http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.UI.TableView-method-appendRow] should be animated by default.    h2. actual    [Ti.UI.TableView.appendRow()|http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.UI.TableView-method-appendRow] is not animated by default.    h2. test case    The test case here shows that while {{insertRowBefore()}} and {{deleteRow()}} are animated by default, {{appendRow()}} is not.      """"""",Improvement,"Title: Titanium.appendRow() should be animated by default
Description: """"h2. description {{Ti.UI.TableView}} has a number of functions that add or remove rows. Those functions take a parameter that allows you to specify an animation for these operations. All of the functions will perform an animation by default, which the exception of [appendRow()|http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.UI.TableView-method-appendRow]. I think {{appendRow()}} should default to having an animation, just like all the other functions. h2. expected [Ti.UI.TableView.appendRow()|http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.UI.TableView-method-appendRow] should be animated by default. h2. actual [Ti.UI.TableView.appendRow()|http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.UI.TableView-method-appendRow] is not animated by default. h2. test case The test case here shows that while {{insertRowBefore()}} and {{deleteRow()}} are animated by default, {{appendRow()}} is not. """"",title+description
TIMOB-16871,12,"""Android: iOS: JavaScript files are not being symlinked""","""""""When building Android or iOS app, JavaScript flies are not being symlinked. They should be symlinked whenever the JS files are not being minified. The change needs to take place at line 20 below:    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: iOS: JavaScript files are not being symlinked
Description: """"When building Android or iOS app, JavaScript flies are not being symlinked. They should be symlinked whenever the JS files are not being minified. The change needs to take place at line 20 below: """"",title+description
TIMOB-16870,12,"""CLI: Expose build configuration via command line options""","""""""This ticket is phase 3 of 3 phases:    1. Decouple settings from the deploy type and expose the defaults in an XML file that is in the SDK (TIMOB-12025)  2. Allow setting overrides in the tiapp.xml (TIMOB-16869)  3. Expose build configurations via command line options that allow selection of custom build configuration(this ticket)    The new option will be called {{--build-configuration <value>}}. It is designed to mimic the deploy type.    h4. Build configuration Resolution    The follow is the order in which the build configurations are merged:        Read the global build configurations      Merge custom build configurations from tiapp.xml into the global build configurations          Debug log all overwritten values      Define any new extended build configurations          Debug log the new configuration and all overwritten values      Use the --deploy-type, --build-configuration, or default deploy type to select which build-configuration to use          Debug log which configuration and deploy type have been selected      Merge build configuration specific settings, tags, etc into the tiapp.xml root          Debug log what tiapp.xml values have been overwritten by the build configuration values      The build will reference the tiapp.xml object as it always has, but now it has new options (i.e. minify-js, allow-debugging, etc)""""""",New Feature,"Title: CLI: Expose build configuration via command line options
Description: """"This ticket is phase 3 of 3 phases: 1. Decouple settings from the deploy type and expose the defaults in an XML file that is in the SDK (TIMOB-12025) 2. Allow setting overrides in the tiapp.xml (TIMOB-16869) 3. Expose build configurations via command line options that allow selection of custom build configuration(this ticket) The new option will be called {{--build-configuration <value>}}. It is designed to mimic the deploy type. h4. Build configuration Resolution The follow is the order in which the build configurations are merged: Read the global build configurations Merge custom build configurations from tiapp.xml into the global build configurations Debug log all overwritten values Define any new extended build configurations Debug log the new configuration and all overwritten values Use the --deploy-type, --build-configuration, or default deploy type to select which build-configuration to use Debug log which configuration and deploy type have been selected Merge build configuration specific settings, tags, etc into the tiapp.xml root Debug log what tiapp.xml values have been overwritten by the build configuration values The build will reference the tiapp.xml object as it always has, but now it has new options (i.e. minify-js, allow-debugging, etc)""""",title+description
TIMOB-16869,12,"""CLI: Allow build configuration overrides in the tiapp.xml""","""""""This ticket is phase 2 of 3 phases:    1. Decouple settings from the deploy type and expose the defaults in an XML file that is in the SDK (TIMOB-12025)  2. Allow setting overrides in the tiapp.xml (this ticket)  3. Expose build configurations via command line options that allow selection of custom build configuration (TIMOB-16870)    Project level build configurations can override or extend the global build configurations. Below is an example where the """"""""development"""""""" build configuration is overwritten and the """"""""test"""""""" build configuration is extended.    """"""",New Feature,"Title: CLI: Allow build configuration overrides in the tiapp.xml
Description: """"This ticket is phase 2 of 3 phases: 1. Decouple settings from the deploy type and expose the defaults in an XML file that is in the SDK (TIMOB-12025) 2. Allow setting overrides in the tiapp.xml (this ticket) 3. Expose build configurations via command line options that allow selection of custom build configuration (TIMOB-16870) Project level build configurations can override or extend the global build configurations. Below is an example where the """"""""development"""""""" build configuration is overwritten and the """"""""test"""""""" build configuration is extended. """"",title+description
TIMOB-16868,12,"""Android: Parent Template can return the children objects on iOS but not Android""","""""""h6.Issue description   ​ListView does not enables any reference to get the child views of the list view. On iOS it gets the child view element to animate it.     h6.Steps to reproduce  # Please grab attached 'app.js' for demonstrating the issue.   # Click somewhere the Image or Labels for rotate of ImageView.  # Notice difference between Platforms.    h6.Console output  {quote}  Ti.API.info('*evt: ' + JSON.stringify(evt.source.apiName));  evt.source.children && Ti.API.info('@@@evt: ' + JSON.stringify(evt.source.children[4]));  {quote}    h5.iOS output:      h5.Android Output:  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Parent Template can return the children objects on iOS but not Android
Description: """"h6.Issue description ​ListView does not enables any reference to get the child views of the list view. On iOS it gets the child view element to animate it. h6.Steps to reproduce # Please grab attached 'app.js' for demonstrating the issue. # Click somewhere the Image or Labels for rotate of ImageView. # Notice difference between Platforms. h6.Console output {quote} Ti.API.info('*evt: ' + JSON.stringify(evt.source.apiName)); evt.source.children && Ti.API.info('@@@evt: ' + JSON.stringify(evt.source.children[4])); {quote} h5.iOS output: h5.Android Output: """"",title+description
