TIMOB-16792,12,"""Android: swipe - On Android 4.X, it is difficult to trigger the swipe event on Ti.UI.View and the direction is reported incorrectly""","""""""*Details:* On Android 4.X, if you swipe on a Ti.UI.View, it will be difficult to trigger the swipe event and, if the swipe event does get triggered, the direction will not be reported correctly.    *Note:*  1. This is *not* a regression as this occurs in SDK 3.2.2.GA  2. This occurs only on a 4.X device; on the Droid 3 (2.3.4), the swipe event can be easily triggered and reports the correct direction  3. Cannot reproduce this issue on iOS    *Steps to reproduce:*  1. Install and launch the following app.js code:      2. Try to swipe up, down, left or right    *Actual:* There will be difficulty triggering the swipe event. If you do trigger the swipe event, the direction will be reported incorrectly; see swipe_left.png (reported right) and swipe_down.png (reported up).     *Expected:* Should be able to trigger the swipe event with ease and the direction should be reported correctly.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: swipe - On Android 4.X, it is difficult to trigger the swipe event on Ti.UI.View and the direction is reported incorrectly
Description: """"*Details:* On Android 4.X, if you swipe on a Ti.UI.View, it will be difficult to trigger the swipe event and, if the swipe event does get triggered, the direction will not be reported correctly. *Note:* 1. This is *not* a regression as this occurs in SDK 3.2.2.GA 2. This occurs only on a 4.X device; on the Droid 3 (2.3.4), the swipe event can be easily triggered and reports the correct direction 3. Cannot reproduce this issue on iOS *Steps to reproduce:* 1. Install and launch the following app.js code: 2. Try to swipe up, down, left or right *Actual:* There will be difficulty triggering the swipe event. If you do trigger the swipe event, the direction will be reported incorrectly; see swipe_left.png (reported right) and swipe_down.png (reported up). *Expected:* Should be able to trigger the swipe event with ease and the direction should be reported correctly.""""",title+description
TIMOB-16791,12,"""BlackBerry: Create Map module""","""""""Currently BB uses an older map api and there are a few bugs and parity issues as well as performance issues.   The other platforms, iOS and Android, have maps a separate module. We need to implant the Map api as a separate module for BlackBerry as well and use the new BB Maps API  """"""",New Feature,"Title: BlackBerry: Create Map module
Description: """"Currently BB uses an older map api and there are a few bugs and parity issues as well as performance issues. The other platforms, iOS and Android, have maps a separate module. We need to implant the Map api as a separate module for BlackBerry as well and use the new BB Maps API """"",title+description
TIMOB-16788,12,"""Android: WebView cannot find assets in ApplicationData directory""","""""""I have a Titanium app that wraps a huge HTML web-app which relies on many assets including JS, CSS, fonts etc. Because of TIMOB-2478 and to workaround Alloy compile and Titanium build taking ages or even failing processing all these files I have zipped the web-app and extract it on the app's first use. This means the {{Ti.UI.WebView}} needs to load the web-app from the device's {{applicationDataDirectory}}, {{applicationSupportDirectory}} (iOS-only) or {{externalStorageDirectory}} (Android-only).    Unfortunately the HTML fails to find its assets on Android. On iOS it works fine.    I know about {{https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-9593}} and related issues, but this only fixes it for {{resourcesDirectory}}.    The attached test-project shows exactly what methods of loading the HTML in the WebView work on both OS and which don't. Run the app for both iOS and Android and click a test in the table. The colors show which tests I found passing and failing for the specific OS. See the screenshots for reference.    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: WebView cannot find assets in ApplicationData directory
Description: """"I have a Titanium app that wraps a huge HTML web-app which relies on many assets including JS, CSS, fonts etc. Because of TIMOB-2478 and to workaround Alloy compile and Titanium build taking ages or even failing processing all these files I have zipped the web-app and extract it on the app's first use. This means the {{Ti.UI.WebView}} needs to load the web-app from the device's {{applicationDataDirectory}}, {{applicationSupportDirectory}} (iOS-only) or {{externalStorageDirectory}} (Android-only). Unfortunately the HTML fails to find its assets on Android. On iOS it works fine. I know about {{https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-9593}} and related issues, but this only fixes it for {{resourcesDirectory}}. The attached test-project shows exactly what methods of loading the HTML in the WebView work on both OS and which don't. Run the app for both iOS and Android and click a test in the table. The colors show which tests I found passing and failing for the specific OS. See the screenshots for reference. """"",title+description
