TIMOB-16015,12,"""CLI: Android build fails if app icon is not named """"appicon.png""""""","""""""There is a bug where if the app icon is not named """"""""appicon.png"""""""", the build fails with the following error:        Nadav original found this bug and reported it here: http://www.<USER>com/blog/2013/12/3-2-0-rc-of-sdkstudio-now-available/.    {quote}  <USER>  Posted December 14, 2013 @ 12:52 pm    Actually I downloaded the new version of Studio and now it works fine.  But now my project fails to build.  It says Error: No resource found that matches the given name (at 'icon' with value '@drawable/appicon' event though I do not use appicon and my own icon in the tiapp.xml  I changed the name to appicon and it works fine. but now the icons is titanium default.  {quote}""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Android build fails if app icon is not named """"appicon.png""""
Description: """"There is a bug where if the app icon is not named """"""""appicon.png"""""""", the build fails with the following error: Nadav original found this bug and reported it here: http://www.<USER>com/blog/2013/12/3-2-0-rc-of-sdkstudio-now-available/. {quote} <USER> Posted December 14, 2013 @ 12:52 pm Actually I downloaded the new version of Studio and now it works fine. But now my project fails to build. It says Error: No resource found that matches the given name (at 'icon' with value '@drawable/appicon' event though I do not use appicon and my own icon in the tiapp.xml I changed the name to appicon and it works fine. but now the icons is titanium default. {quote}""""",title+description
TIMOB-16013,12,"""BlackBerry: Kitchen Sink - invalid values that specify parameters for identifying, installing, and launching native app""","""""""When building Kitchen Sink project on to BB device or Simulator, the console log shows Manifest Errors regarding Application-Version and Package Version.  In a meantime, Application has been installed and run.    h5. To reproduce  1. KS> Run> BlackBerry Device/Simulator  2. Observe the console   Log    h5. Actual result  Errors in the log, App installed and can be run.  """"""",Bug,"Title: BlackBerry: Kitchen Sink - invalid values that specify parameters for identifying, installing, and launching native app
Description: """"When building Kitchen Sink project on to BB device or Simulator, the console log shows Manifest Errors regarding Application-Version and Package Version. In a meantime, Application has been installed and run. h5. To reproduce 1. KS> Run> BlackBerry Device/Simulator 2. Observe the console Log h5. Actual result Errors in the log, App installed and can be run. """"",title+description
TIMOB-16012,12,"""iOS: Module.Facebook: in login event, 'data' property is a Javascript object on iOS, a JSON string on Android""","""""""The 'data' property in the event object of the 'login' event, as per documentation and previous Titanium SDK releases, """"""""is in JSON format, and includes information such as user name, locale and gender"""""""".    On Android, this holds true and 'data' is a string containing JSON.    On iOS, however, the property contains the JSON.parsed object.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Module.Facebook: in login event, 'data' property is a Javascript object on iOS, a JSON string on Android
Description: """"The 'data' property in the event object of the 'login' event, as per documentation and previous Titanium SDK releases, """"""""is in JSON format, and includes information such as user name, locale and gender"""""""". On Android, this holds true and 'data' is a string containing JSON. On iOS, however, the property contains the JSON.parsed object.""""",title+description
TIMOB-16009,12,"""Tooling: Release 3.1.4 of Titanium CLI""","""""""We need to release a 3.1.4 version of the CLI to support people using 3.1.X of the SDK in the 3.1.3 stack of Studio.""""""",Story,"Title: Tooling: Release 3.1.4 of Titanium CLI
Description: """"We need to release a 3.1.4 version of the CLI to support people using 3.1.X of the SDK in the 3.1.3 stack of Studio.""""",title+description
TIMOB-16008,12,"""Tooling: Per-project installs of NPM modules""","""""""There is a desire to have per-project selection for Alloy version, and CLI version? Maybe inside Tiapp.xml?    One logical solution to this is npm installs local to the project for titanium, Alloy, etc… For example:        Would put alloy in the project’s local node_modules folder. Alloy could be invoked from that folder like this:        While this can be done manually, the idea long-term solution is for titanium projects to have a package.json file pre-propulated with these dependencies and versions so that all a developer or Studio needs to do is invoke {{npm install}} to automatically pull in all these dependencies locally. This would all come hand in hand with a more focused integration with npm in the Titanium toolchain.    For Studio to be able to take advantage of this:    * *Titanium CLI* – All of Studio’s call to “titanium” should first check for the existence of the “./node_modules/.bin/titanium” binary. It should use that for all titanium calls, and fallback to the globally installed one if no local version is present.  * *Alloy* – Alloy’s CLI hook would need to be updated to do roughly the same thing. If Studio makes any calls directly to “alloy” (not sure if you do), they would have to do the same check as above.  """"""",New Feature,"Title: Tooling: Per-project installs of NPM modules
Description: """"There is a desire to have per-project selection for Alloy version, and CLI version? Maybe inside Tiapp.xml? One logical solution to this is npm installs local to the project for titanium, Alloy, etc… For example: Would put alloy in the project’s local node_modules folder. Alloy could be invoked from that folder like this: While this can be done manually, the idea long-term solution is for titanium projects to have a package.json file pre-propulated with these dependencies and versions so that all a developer or Studio needs to do is invoke {{npm install}} to automatically pull in all these dependencies locally. This would all come hand in hand with a more focused integration with npm in the Titanium toolchain. For Studio to be able to take advantage of this: * *Titanium CLI* – All of Studio’s call to “titanium” should first check for the existence of the “./node_modules/.bin/titanium” binary. It should use that for all titanium calls, and fallback to the globally installed one if no local version is present. * *Alloy* – Alloy’s CLI hook would need to be updated to do roughly the same thing. If Studio makes any calls directly to “alloy” (not sure if you do), they would have to do the same check as above. """"",title+description
TIMOB-16006,12,"""iOS: Black bar overwrites the status bar""","""""""h2. update (12/17/2013)    This issue is not Alloy-specific. The same issue occurs with the following test case:        h2. original description    With 3.2.0 CI build, black bar overwrites the status bar. However, the status bar is seen correct with 3.1.3.GA SDK version.    Please find the following attachments to see the difference between 3.2.0 CI build and 3.1.3.GA SDK version.  *Steps To Reproduce* :    1. Create the default Alloy project in Titanium Studio.    2. Run it on iOS7 using 3.2.0 CI build and see the status bar.    *NOTE* : Tested with Classic Titanium also but didn't get the issue there.  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Black bar overwrites the status bar
Description: """"h2. update (12/17/2013) This issue is not Alloy-specific. The same issue occurs with the following test case: h2. original description With 3.2.0 CI build, black bar overwrites the status bar. However, the status bar is seen correct with 3.1.3.GA SDK version. Please find the following attachments to see the difference between 3.2.0 CI build and 3.1.3.GA SDK version. *Steps To Reproduce* : 1. Create the default Alloy project in Titanium Studio. 2. Run it on iOS7 using 3.2.0 CI build and see the status bar. *NOTE* : Tested with Classic Titanium also but didn't get the issue there. """"",title+description
