TIMOB-15859,12,"""Windows: Analytics not sent from Windows phone""","""""""h5.Description  Applications running on Windows phones will not send analytic data.    h5.Steps To Reproduce  1. Create and run a project onto a windows phone.  2. Check for analytic data on the server    h5.Expected Result  Analytic data sent and viewable on the server    h5.Actual Result  Nothing is sent, but the application runs fine""""""",Bug,"Title: Windows: Analytics not sent from Windows phone
Description: """"h5.Description Applications running on Windows phones will not send analytic data. h5.Steps To Reproduce 1. Create and run a project onto a windows phone. 2. Check for analytic data on the server h5.Expected Result Analytic data sent and viewable on the server h5.Actual Result Nothing is sent, but the application runs fine""""",title+description
TIMOB-15858,12,"""CLI: Error while cleaning a project after build""","""""""If ADB is not running, then doing an emulator or device build will start up ADB, but for some reason, ADB has a lock on the build directory and """"""""ti clean"""""""" commands fail.        A possible solution may be to run all ADB commands from the filesystem root directory and not the current dir.""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Error while cleaning a project after build
Description: """"If ADB is not running, then doing an emulator or device build will start up ADB, but for some reason, ADB has a lock on the build directory and """"""""ti clean"""""""" commands fail. A possible solution may be to run all ADB commands from the filesystem root directory and not the current dir.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15857,12,"""CLI: Android build fails when doing a device build, then doing an emulator build on Windows""","""""""If you do an Android device build, then do an emulator build without cleaning, the following error occurs:        This happens on Windows 8 while building an Alloy app, though I'm not sure if it's Windows or Alloy specific.""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Android build fails when doing a device build, then doing an emulator build on Windows
Description: """"If you do an Android device build, then do an emulator build without cleaning, the following error occurs: This happens on Windows 8 while building an Alloy app, though I'm not sure if it's Windows or Alloy specific.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15856,12,"""iOS: Titanium.UI.iOS.AttributedString clipping problems when used with ATTRIBUTE_BASELINE_OFFSET""","""""""When using the new AttributedString on a label with the attribute of ATTRIBUTE_BASELINE_OFFSET the text within the label is clipped.  This appears in differently depending on choice of font (default or custom) the value set for the offset.    * When you use the default typeface then the top line of the text is clipped off.         Values for *verticalAlign* and *height* do not affect the problem.    * Custom fonts make the problem much worse - whilst this may be the fault of the font, the fact that this occurs on the default font must mean it is the clipping routine at fault.    * If you use the documented sample code and add only the ATTRIBUTE_BASELINE_OFFSET attribute this also affects the display of the string by truncated the display with ellipsis.     """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Titanium.UI.iOS.AttributedString clipping problems when used with ATTRIBUTE_BASELINE_OFFSET
Description: """"When using the new AttributedString on a label with the attribute of ATTRIBUTE_BASELINE_OFFSET the text within the label is clipped. This appears in differently depending on choice of font (default or custom) the value set for the offset. * When you use the default typeface then the top line of the text is clipped off. Values for *verticalAlign* and *height* do not affect the problem. * Custom fonts make the problem much worse - whilst this may be the fault of the font, the fact that this occurs on the default font must mean it is the clipping routine at fault. * If you use the documented sample code and add only the ATTRIBUTE_BASELINE_OFFSET attribute this also affects the display of the string by truncated the display with ellipsis. """"",title+description
TIMOB-15855,12,"""iOS: dynamic zIndex value not being applied correctly""","""""""h3. Description    When assigning a dynamic zIndex value to a view from a touchend event the value is assigned but not always get displayed on iPad.    h3. Steps to reproduce    Run the code on ipad and do a fast horizontal scrolling to move the views around, randomly the views will start not showing the zindex that was setted.    h3. Test Case    {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid}  var w = Ti.UI.createWindow({orientationModes : [       Titanium.UI.LANDSCAPE_LEFT,       Titanium.UI.LANDSCAPE_RIGHT   ],  });    var card=[];  var centrePosition = (512);  var cardSize = 150;  var distance;  var touchX;     var colors=['red','blue','yellow','orange','purple','white','gray'];  var position = [(centrePosition-300),(centrePosition-200),(centrePosition-100),(centrePosition),(centrePosition+100),(centrePosition+200),(centrePosition+300)];    for (var i=0;i<=6;i++){   card[i]=Ti.UI.createView({    width:150,    height:100,    backgroundColor:colors[i],    left:position[i]   });    w.add(card[i]);  }    var lbl = Ti.UI.createLabel({   color:'white',   top:100  });    w.add(lbl);    function arrangeZIndex(){      for (var i=0; i<=6; i++) {              var distanceFromCentre = (card[i].left - centrePosition) + (cardSize/2);          card[i].zIndex = Math.round(100 - (Math.sqrt(distanceFromCentre*distanceFromCentre)/100));          Ti.API.info(""""""""card """"""""+(i)+"""""""" zindex:""""""""+card[i].zIndex);      }       lbl.text = """"""""card red zindex:""""""""+card[0].zIndex+"""""""" card blue zindex:""""""""+card[1].zIndex+"""""""" card yellow zindex:""""""""+card[2].zIndex+""""""""card orange zindex:""""""""+card[3].zIndex+"""""""" card purple zindex:""""""""+card[4].zIndex+"""""""" card white zindex:""""""""+card[5].zIndex+"""""""" card gray zindex:""""""""+card[6].zIndex;  }    function fingerDown(e) {   touchX = e.x;  }     function moveCards(e) {      var distance = 0;      if(touchX != e.x){          distance = e.x - touchX;          touchX = e.x;       }   for (var i=0; i<=6; i++) {       card[i].left += distance;   }  }    w.addEventListener('touchstart', fingerDown);   w.addEventListener('touchmove', moveCards);  w.addEventListener('touchend', arrangeZIndex);    arrangeZIndex();  w.open();      [INFO] :   card red zindex:95  [INFO] :   card blue zindex:96  [INFO] :   card yellow zindex:97  [INFO] :   card orange zindex:98  [INFO] :   card purple zindex:99  [INFO] :   card white zindex:100  [INFO] :   card gray zindex:99    {code}    As you can see in the attachment even when the white view has a zindex value of 100 the gray view with a 99 zindex value is shown on top.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: dynamic zIndex value not being applied correctly
Description: """"h3. Description When assigning a dynamic zIndex value to a view from a touchend event the value is assigned but not always get displayed on iPad. h3. Steps to reproduce Run the code on ipad and do a fast horizontal scrolling to move the views around, randomly the views will start not showing the zindex that was setted. h3. Test Case {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid} var w = Ti.UI.createWindow({orientationModes : [ Titanium.UI.LANDSCAPE_LEFT, Titanium.UI.LANDSCAPE_RIGHT ], }); var card=[]; var centrePosition = (512); var cardSize = 150; var distance; var touchX; var colors=['red','blue','yellow','orange','purple','white','gray']; var position = [(centrePosition-300),(centrePosition-200),(centrePosition-100),(centrePosition),(centrePosition+100),(centrePosition+200),(centrePosition+300)]; for (var i=0;i<=6;i++){ card[i]=Ti.UI.createView({ width:150, height:100, backgroundColor:colors[i], left:position[i] }); w.add(card[i]); } var lbl = Ti.UI.createLabel({ color:'white', top:100 }); w.add(lbl); function arrangeZIndex(){ for (var i=0; i<=6; i++) { var distanceFromCentre = (card[i].left - centrePosition) + (cardSize/2); card[i].zIndex = Math.round(100 - (Math.sqrt(distanceFromCentre*distanceFromCentre)/100)); Ti.API.info(""""""""card """"""""+(i)+"""""""" zindex:""""""""+card[i].zIndex); } lbl.text = """"""""card red zindex:""""""""+card[0].zIndex+"""""""" card blue zindex:""""""""+card[1].zIndex+"""""""" card yellow zindex:""""""""+card[2].zIndex+""""""""card orange zindex:""""""""+card[3].zIndex+"""""""" card purple zindex:""""""""+card[4].zIndex+"""""""" card white zindex:""""""""+card[5].zIndex+"""""""" card gray zindex:""""""""+card[6].zIndex; } function fingerDown(e) { touchX = e.x; } function moveCards(e) { var distance = 0; if(touchX != e.x){ distance = e.x - touchX; touchX = e.x; } for (var i=0; i<=6; i++) { card[i].left += distance; } } w.addEventListener('touchstart', fingerDown); w.addEventListener('touchmove', moveCards); w.addEventListener('touchend', arrangeZIndex); arrangeZIndex(); w.open(); [INFO] : card red zindex:95 [INFO] : card blue zindex:96 [INFO] : card yellow zindex:97 [INFO] : card orange zindex:98 [INFO] : card purple zindex:99 [INFO] : card white zindex:100 [INFO] : card gray zindex:99 {code} As you can see in the attachment even when the white view has a zindex value of 100 the gray view with a 99 zindex value is shown on top.""""",title+description
