TIMOB-15752,12,"""iOS: Backgrounding - BACKGROUNDFETCHINTERVAL_MIN and BACKGROUNDFETCHINTERVAL_NEVER returns undefined""","""""""*Details:* Ti.App.iOS.BACKGROUNDFETCHINTERVAL_MIN and Ti.App.iOS.BACKGROUNDFETCHINTERVAL_NEVER returns 'undefined' which are used by Ti.App.iOS.setMinimumBackgroundFetchInterval(); the function accepts only number type arguments.    Note: You can still pass a literal number parameter to setMinimumBackgroundFetchInterval function e.g. Ti.App.iOS.setMinimumBackgroundFetchInterval(12).    *Steps to reproduce:*  1. Create default app  2. In app.js, add these two line of codes:    3. Launch app in either device or simulator  4. Check xcode console    *Actual:* The above code returns 'undefined' values.      *Expected:* The above code should return a number values.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Backgrounding - BACKGROUNDFETCHINTERVAL_MIN and BACKGROUNDFETCHINTERVAL_NEVER returns undefined
Description: """"*Details:* Ti.App.iOS.BACKGROUNDFETCHINTERVAL_MIN and Ti.App.iOS.BACKGROUNDFETCHINTERVAL_NEVER returns 'undefined' which are used by Ti.App.iOS.setMinimumBackgroundFetchInterval(); the function accepts only number type arguments. Note: You can still pass a literal number parameter to setMinimumBackgroundFetchInterval function e.g. Ti.App.iOS.setMinimumBackgroundFetchInterval(12). *Steps to reproduce:* 1. Create default app 2. In app.js, add these two line of codes: 3. Launch app in either device or simulator 4. Check xcode console *Actual:* The above code returns 'undefined' values. *Expected:* The above code should return a number values.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15751,12,"""Android: height: Ti.UI.SIZE doesn't work correctly when descendant has height: Ti.UI.FILL""","""""""h2. Problem  A view with height set to Ti.UI.SIZE and fixed height children will incorrectly behave like Ti.UI.FILL *IF* the child contains its own child view with height set to Ti.UI.FILL.    h2. Test case    {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid}  var window = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor: """"""""white""""""""});    var dynamicHeight = Ti.UI.createView({height: Ti.UI.SIZE, backgroundColor: """"""""yellow""""""""});  window.add(dynamicHeight);    var fixedHeight = Ti.UI.createView({height: 100, backgroundColor: """"""""red""""""""});  dynamicHeight.add(fixedHeight);    var fillHeight = Ti.UI.createView({height: Ti.UI.FILL, width: """"""""50%"""""""", backgroundColor: """"""""blue""""""""});  fixedHeight.add(fillHeight);    // Toggle height between fill and fixed  window.addEventListener('click', function() {    fillHeight.height = (fillHeight.height == Ti.UI.FILL) ? 100 : Ti.UI.FILL;   });    window.open();  {code}     Expected: dynamicHeight should be the same size as its fixed height child.  Actual: dynamicHeight fills the screen.    h2. Workaround  Don't use FILL anywhere inside a SIZE if you value your sanity.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: height: Ti.UI.SIZE doesn't work correctly when descendant has height: Ti.UI.FILL
Description: """"h2. Problem A view with height set to Ti.UI.SIZE and fixed height children will incorrectly behave like Ti.UI.FILL *IF* the child contains its own child view with height set to Ti.UI.FILL. h2. Test case {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid} var window = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor: """"""""white""""""""}); var dynamicHeight = Ti.UI.createView({height: Ti.UI.SIZE, backgroundColor: """"""""yellow""""""""}); window.add(dynamicHeight); var fixedHeight = Ti.UI.createView({height: 100, backgroundColor: """"""""red""""""""}); dynamicHeight.add(fixedHeight); var fillHeight = Ti.UI.createView({height: Ti.UI.FILL, width: """"""""50%"""""""", backgroundColor: """"""""blue""""""""}); fixedHeight.add(fillHeight); // Toggle height between fill and fixed window.addEventListener('click', function() { fillHeight.height = (fillHeight.height == Ti.UI.FILL) ? 100 : Ti.UI.FILL; }); window.open(); {code} Expected: dynamicHeight should be the same size as its fixed height child. Actual: dynamicHeight fills the screen. h2. Workaround Don't use FILL anywhere inside a SIZE if you value your sanity.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15750,12,"""Android: Enable Android Hardware Acceleration by default in tiapp.xml""","""""""By default Titanium apps don't come with Android hardware acceleration enabled. This causes apps to be significantly slower, especially on Android tablets.     Native Android projects with newer SDK's enable hardware acceleration by default. I propose that Titanium do the same thing, when a new Titanium project is created, add into the tiapp.xml to enable hardware acceleration.        The implication of not doing this is that you're leaving it up to the developer to know that they should enable hardware acceleration for Android. Titanium developers without native Android experience will not know to do this, and then will be wondering why their Titanium apps don't perform as well on Android as native apps do.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Enable Android Hardware Acceleration by default in tiapp.xml
Description: """"By default Titanium apps don't come with Android hardware acceleration enabled. This causes apps to be significantly slower, especially on Android tablets. Native Android projects with newer SDK's enable hardware acceleration by default. I propose that Titanium do the same thing, when a new Titanium project is created, add into the tiapp.xml to enable hardware acceleration. The implication of not doing this is that you're leaving it up to the developer to know that they should enable hardware acceleration for Android. Titanium developers without native Android experience will not know to do this, and then will be wondering why their Titanium apps don't perform as well on Android as native apps do.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15749,12,"""iOS: Backgrounding - 'silentpush' event is not triggered when an app is backgrounded and a ACS push is sent""","""""""*Details:* 'silentpush' event is not triggered when an app is backgrounded and a ACS push is sent.    *Steps to reproduce:*  1. Create a new Titanium project  2. Include the app.js and tiapp.xml into the project  3. Install the app to an iOS 7 device and background the app  4. Next, go to http://cloud.<USER>com/apps/e36432ae-51c8-43b7-8ecb-7c9db78cd0a7    username: <EMAIL>  pw: bnorhwe    5. Send a custom push: {""""""""alert"""""""":""""""""here goes alert"""""""", """"""""content-available"""""""":1}    *Actual:* 'silentpush' event is not triggered.    *Expected:* 'silentpush' event should be triggered which will trigger the background download.    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Backgrounding - 'silentpush' event is not triggered when an app is backgrounded and a ACS push is sent
Description: """"*Details:* 'silentpush' event is not triggered when an app is backgrounded and a ACS push is sent. *Steps to reproduce:* 1. Create a new Titanium project 2. Include the app.js and tiapp.xml into the project 3. Install the app to an iOS 7 device and background the app 4. Next, go to http://cloud.<USER>com/apps/e36432ae-51c8-43b7-8ecb-7c9db78cd0a7 username: <EMAIL> pw: bnorhwe 5. Send a custom push: {""""""""alert"""""""":""""""""here goes alert"""""""", """"""""content-available"""""""":1} *Actual:* 'silentpush' event is not triggered. *Expected:* 'silentpush' event should be triggered which will trigger the background download. """"",title+description
TIMOB-15748,12,"""Running 'titanium analyze' without the titanium-code-processor installed or target information generates an error  (3.2.x nightly)""","""""""h3. Unexpected Behavior  Run ti analyze in a project directory without having installed the titanium-code-processor yields the following error.    h4. Without Inspector installed      h4. With Inspector installed:      h3. Expected Behavior  user should be prompted to install the titanium-code-processor from npm or display additional help    h5. Notes:  Should be noted that I do NOT have the latest <USER>Studio installed, and will be more than happy to raise an additional ticket inquiring why the weight of appc studio is required to run command line tooling. (passing target params to ti analyze fail because of that update)""""""",Story,"Title: Running 'titanium analyze' without the titanium-code-processor installed or target information generates an error (3.2.x nightly)
Description: """"h3. Unexpected Behavior Run ti analyze in a project directory without having installed the titanium-code-processor yields the following error. h4. Without Inspector installed h4. With Inspector installed: h3. Expected Behavior user should be prompted to install the titanium-code-processor from npm or display additional help h5. Notes: Should be noted that I do NOT have the latest <USER>Studio installed, and will be more than happy to raise an additional ticket inquiring why the weight of appc studio is required to run command line tooling. (passing target params to ti analyze fail because of that update)""""",title+description
TIMOB-15746,12,"""Android : Blob which returned by toImage Method has undefined mimeType (parity issue)""","""""""h4. Problem Description   Blob which returned by toImage Method has undefined mimeType in Android. In iOS is working fine.     h4. Steps to reproduce  1. Create a new mobile project  2. Add support for android  3. Paste the code into app.js      h4. Expected Behavior:  {panel:title=Expected Alert Message}  blob.width : 100  blob.height : 100  blob.mimeType : image/png  {panel}    h4. Actual Behavior:  iOS work properly, Buy Android alert below message  {panel:title=Actual Alert Message}  blob.width : 100  blob.height : 100  blob.mimeType : undefined  {panel}      """"""",Bug,"Title: Android : Blob which returned by toImage Method has undefined mimeType (parity issue)
Description: """"h4. Problem Description Blob which returned by toImage Method has undefined mimeType in Android. In iOS is working fine. h4. Steps to reproduce 1. Create a new mobile project 2. Add support for android 3. Paste the code into app.js h4. Expected Behavior: {panel:title=Expected Alert Message} blob.width : 100 blob.height : 100 blob.mimeType : image/png {panel} h4. Actual Behavior: iOS work properly, Buy Android alert below message {panel:title=Actual Alert Message} blob.width : 100 blob.height : 100 blob.mimeType : undefined {panel} """"",title+description
TIMOB-15745,12,"""CLI: Android: Add support for adhoc test builds""","""""""The Android build does not support adhoc builds. The closest thing we have today is a --target device --build-only. We simply need a new target, --dist-adhoc, to support this and match parity with iOS.""""""",New Feature,"Title: CLI: Android: Add support for adhoc test builds
Description: """"The Android build does not support adhoc builds. The closest thing we have today is a --target device --build-only. We simply need a new target, --dist-adhoc, to support this and match parity with iOS.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15743,12,"""CLI: Add CLI config option to bypass SSL certificate validation""","""""""The Titanium CLI uses HTTP requests for authentication, analytics, and downloading Titanium SDKs. If any of these use an invalid SSL cert, especially in the case of a proxy server, then the requests fail. This may be remedied by adding a CLI option that disabled the """"""""rejectUnauthorized"""""""" request parameter.""""""",New Feature,"Title: CLI: Add CLI config option to bypass SSL certificate validation
Description: """"The Titanium CLI uses HTTP requests for authentication, analytics, and downloading Titanium SDKs. If any of these use an invalid SSL cert, especially in the case of a proxy server, then the requests fail. This may be remedied by adding a CLI option that disabled the """"""""rejectUnauthorized"""""""" request parameter.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15741,12,"""iOS: Cannot launch iOS simulator with Xcode 5.1b4 ""","""""""*Details:* Cannot launch simulator using xcode 5.1beta4    *Steps to reproduce:*  0. Download Xcode 5.1beta4  1. Create a Titanium project in either Studio or via CLI  2. In the project, run the following build command:    """"""""/usr/local/bin/titanium"""""""" """"""""\-\-no-colors"""""""" """"""""\-\-no-progress-bars"""""""" """"""""\-\-no\-prompt"""""""" """"""""build"""""""" """"""""\-\-platform"""""""" """"""""iphone"""""""" """"""""\-\-sdk"""""""" """"""""3.2.0.v20131117001643"""""""" """"""""\-\-log-level"""""""" """"""""trace"""""""" """"""""\-\-target"""""""" """"""""simulator"""""""" """"""""\-\-device\-family"""""""" """"""""universal"""""""" """"""""\-\-ios\-version"""""""" """"""""7.1"""""""" """"""""\-\-sim-version"""""""" """"""""7.1"""""""" """"""""\-\-device-id"""""""" """"""""iPhone Retina (3.5 inch)"""""""" """"""""\-\-skip\-js\-minify""""""""    *Actual:* The app cannot be launched to simulator and the CLI returns: [ERROR] Project failed to build after 14s 561ms (see console.log for the trace logs).    *Expected:* Should be able to install an app to simulator  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Cannot launch iOS simulator with Xcode 5.1b4
Description: """"*Details:* Cannot launch simulator using xcode 5.1beta4 *Steps to reproduce:* 0. Download Xcode 5.1beta4 1. Create a Titanium project in either Studio or via CLI 2. In the project, run the following build command: """"""""/usr/local/bin/titanium"""""""" """"""""\-\-no-colors"""""""" """"""""\-\-no-progress-bars"""""""" """"""""\-\-no\-prompt"""""""" """"""""build"""""""" """"""""\-\-platform"""""""" """"""""iphone"""""""" """"""""\-\-sdk"""""""" """"""""3.2.0.v20131117001643"""""""" """"""""\-\-log-level"""""""" """"""""trace"""""""" """"""""\-\-target"""""""" """"""""simulator"""""""" """"""""\-\-device\-family"""""""" """"""""universal"""""""" """"""""\-\-ios\-version"""""""" """"""""7.1"""""""" """"""""\-\-sim-version"""""""" """"""""7.1"""""""" """"""""\-\-device-id"""""""" """"""""iPhone Retina (3.5 inch)"""""""" """"""""\-\-skip\-js\-minify"""""""" *Actual:* The app cannot be launched to simulator and the CLI returns: [ERROR] Project failed to build after 14s 561ms (see console.log for the trace logs). *Expected:* Should be able to install an app to simulator """"",title+description
TIMOB-15740,12,"""CLI: Info command displays extra incorrect Xcode info when 3.1.x SDK is selected""","""""""When a 3.1.x SDK is selected, the legacy Xcode info prints that it's not supported and the Titanium SDK version is undefined. The problem is this text should not have been rendered at all.    Expected:      Actual:      To reproduce:  1) Select a 3.1.x SDK: """"""""ti sdk select 3.1.4.GA""""""""  2) Run """"""""ti info -t ios""""""""  3) Look at the Xcode info section""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Info command displays extra incorrect Xcode info when 3.1.x SDK is selected
