TIMOB-15714,12,"""CLI: Module zip files are not extracted automatically""","""""""If you put a module zip file ~/Library/Application Support/Titanium, the zip file is never extracted.    To reproduce:  1) place module zip file in ~/Library/Application Support/Titanium  2) create an app in either Studio or CLI  3) add any module to tiapp.xml  4) build app for any platform/target  5) watch log output near top for """"""""[INFO]  Installing module: whatever-module-1.0.zip""""""""  6) verify module was extracted to ~/Library/Application Support/Titanium/modules and that zip file was deleted    Actual:  The zip file is never extracted.    Expected:  Zip file is extracted and deleted.""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Module zip files are not extracted automatically
Description: """"If you put a module zip file ~/Library/Application Support/Titanium, the zip file is never extracted. To reproduce: 1) place module zip file in ~/Library/Application Support/Titanium 2) create an app in either Studio or CLI 3) add any module to tiapp.xml 4) build app for any platform/target 5) watch log output near top for """"""""[INFO] Installing module: whatever-module-1.0.zip"""""""" 6) verify module was extracted to ~/Library/Application Support/Titanium/modules and that zip file was deleted Actual: The zip file is never extracted. Expected: Zip file is extracted and deleted.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15713,12,"""CLI: iOS: Cannot build directly from Xcode ""","""""""When opening the Xcode project after an iOS build (any target), you are unable to build the project due to CLI errors with trying to prompt for the missing options (which in itself is also a bug since it should not be prompting in the first place).    Steps to reproduce:    1) create a project in Studio or from the CLI  2) build for ios sim in Studio or from the CLI  3) exit sim/build/studio  4) open xcode project in build/iphone dir  5) build project in xcode for ios sim    You can also try the same thing for device builds.    Actual:  Titanium CLI crash in the Xcode build log during pre-compile phase.    Expected:  Xcode project to build without errors or dragons.""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: iOS: Cannot build directly from Xcode
Description: """"When opening the Xcode project after an iOS build (any target), you are unable to build the project due to CLI errors with trying to prompt for the missing options (which in itself is also a bug since it should not be prompting in the first place). Steps to reproduce: 1) create a project in Studio or from the CLI 2) build for ios sim in Studio or from the CLI 3) exit sim/build/studio 4) open xcode project in build/iphone dir 5) build project in xcode for ios sim You can also try the same thing for device builds. Actual: Titanium CLI crash in the Xcode build log during pre-compile phase. Expected: Xcode project to build without errors or dragons.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15711,12,"""iOS: 6.X - On Mavericks, installing an app to a 6.X devices will fail with signing issues""","""""""*Details:* On Mavericks, installing an app to a 6.X iOS devices will fail with signing issues.    Some things to note:  1. We were not able to reproduce the issue on Mountain Lion  2. We were not able to reproduce the issue on a 7.X iOS device  3. The iOS certs and provisioning profiles that we used are in a valid state   4. [~<USER> repeated the below reproducible steps 18 times and she was able to install the app successfully *once* to a 6.X iOS device (see installed_on_device.txt); 94.44% error rate.    *Steps to reproduce:*  1. Create a default project from Studio    2. Next, from terminal, cd to the project and install the app straight to an iOS device using the following CLI command:      *Actual:*  One of the following two errors would appear:        OR        3. Do a *ti clean* on the project  4. And repeat steps 2-3     *Expected:* Should be able to install to a 6.X iOS device without any issues.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: 6.X - On Mavericks, installing an app to a 6.X devices will fail with signing issues
Description: """"*Details:* On Mavericks, installing an app to a 6.X iOS devices will fail with signing issues. Some things to note: 1. We were not able to reproduce the issue on Mountain Lion 2. We were not able to reproduce the issue on a 7.X iOS device 3. The iOS certs and provisioning profiles that we used are in a valid state 4. [~<USER> repeated the below reproducible steps 18 times and she was able to install the app successfully *once* to a 6.X iOS device (see installed_on_device.txt); 94.44% error rate. *Steps to reproduce:* 1. Create a default project from Studio 2. Next, from terminal, cd to the project and install the app straight to an iOS device using the following CLI command: *Actual:* One of the following two errors would appear: OR 3. Do a *ti clean* on the project 4. And repeat steps 2-3 *Expected:* Should be able to install to a 6.X iOS device without any issues.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15709,12,"""CLI: 3.2.0 CLI install warns of out-of-date dependency """"github-flavored-markdown@1.0.1""""""","""""""h5. Description:  When I installed the CLI today I noticed the following npm warning:      Since the CLI install could potentially fail if this dependency is no longer available at some time in the future, we should consider replacing it soon.    h5. Steps to reproduce:  1) Install the cli from github with:  sudo npm install -g git://github.com/<USER>titanium.git  2) Watch the terminal output    h5. Result:  npm warning about """"""""out of date"""""""" package    h5. Expected Result:  No warnings, no out-of-date packages as dependencies""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: 3.2.0 CLI install warns of out-of-date dependency """"github-flavored-markdown@1.0.1""""
Description: """"h5. Description: When I installed the CLI today I noticed the following npm warning: Since the CLI install could potentially fail if this dependency is no longer available at some time in the future, we should consider replacing it soon. h5. Steps to reproduce: 1) Install the cli from github with: sudo npm install -g git://github.com/<USER>titanium.git 2) Watch the terminal output h5. Result: npm warning about """"""""out of date"""""""" package h5. Expected Result: No warnings, no out-of-date packages as dependencies""""",title+description
TIMOB-15708,12,"""Code Analyzer Report should display some information about the app that was analyzed (Name, GUID, version, etc...)""","""""""Code Analyzer Report should display some information about the app that was analyzed (Name, GUID, version, etc...)    Attached is a report generated by the Code Analyzer (Processor). No where in the report is there an indication of what the application it pertains to.    I hope the code analyzer can display some information, perhaps from tiapp.xml, so that a viewer of the report has an idea what the report corresponds to.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Code Analyzer Report should display some information about the app that was analyzed (Name, GUID, version, etc...)
Description: """"Code Analyzer Report should display some information about the app that was analyzed (Name, GUID, version, etc...) Attached is a report generated by the Code Analyzer (Processor). No where in the report is there an indication of what the application it pertains to. I hope the code analyzer can display some information, perhaps from tiapp.xml, so that a viewer of the report has an idea what the report corresponds to.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15707,12,"""iOS: Left alignment for scaled images""","""""""h6.Issue description  If you create an imageView of a predetermined height but do not specify the width, an image of unknown dimensions is scaled correctly to fit.     If we know the exact dimensions of the image we can specify both the width and height of the image view to be proportional and the left indent is respected. However there is some misbehaviour that occurs with PNG format images that are remote. They aren't left aligned correctly in iOS. Android position this remote PNG image correctly. Screen shoot attached: ImageView_Remote_iOS_PNG_format.jpg    h6.Test Case      h6.Work around  To ensure an incoming image is scaled to 23 pixels in height and is left justified by 10 pixels (both platforms), without knowing the incoming image dimensions:  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Left alignment for scaled images
Description: """"h6.Issue description If you create an imageView of a predetermined height but do not specify the width, an image of unknown dimensions is scaled correctly to fit. If we know the exact dimensions of the image we can specify both the width and height of the image view to be proportional and the left indent is respected. However there is some misbehaviour that occurs with PNG format images that are remote. They aren't left aligned correctly in iOS. Android position this remote PNG image correctly. Screen shoot attached: ImageView_Remote_iOS_PNG_format.jpg h6.Test Case h6.Work around To ensure an incoming image is scaled to 23 pixels in height and is left justified by 10 pixels (both platforms), without knowing the incoming image dimensions: """"",title+description
