TIMOB-15119,12,"""iOS: Support multiple JS files in native/Hybrid module development.""","""""""When we add multiple JS files inside assets folder and use common JS (require pattern) between JS calling in Native iOS Module development then it does not work.    Steps to reproduce:  1: Create a native iOS module. let say module name is test and id is com.example.test.  2: Create two or more java script files inside assets folder.  3: Rename one js file to **com.example.test.js** and require another js file inside com.example.test.js  4: Try to use objective C API exposed through module as well as common JS pattern based APIs exposed from JS files in app.js.  5: try to build and run the module now.    Result: it will fail, as it won't be able to find JS file which is required in **com.example.test.js**.      Note: For shorter and quick test about the above described <USER> simply use the below mentioned/attached module zips and after unzip try to build them.  Error message will describe the issue properly.    test.zip and testone.zip are two module zips created using TiSDK version 3.1.2.GA and 2.1.4.GA respectively.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Support multiple JS files in native/Hybrid module development.
Description: """"When we add multiple JS files inside assets folder and use common JS (require pattern) between JS calling in Native iOS Module development then it does not work. Steps to reproduce: 1: Create a native iOS module. let say module name is test and id is com.example.test. 2: Create two or more java script files inside assets folder. 3: Rename one js file to **com.example.test.js** and require another js file inside com.example.test.js 4: Try to use objective C API exposed through module as well as common JS pattern based APIs exposed from JS files in app.js. 5: try to build and run the module now. Result: it will fail, as it won't be able to find JS file which is required in **com.example.test.js**. Note: For shorter and quick test about the above described <USER> simply use the below mentioned/attached module zips and after unzip try to build them. Error message will describe the issue properly. test.zip and testone.zip are two module zips created using TiSDK version 3.1.2.GA and 2.1.4.GA respectively.""""",title+description
TIMOB-15117,12,"""iOS7: CLI: Add support for iOS 7 simulator 64 bit""","""""""  Description:  With the release of iOS 7, a new iOS simulator option is available via Xcode 5 GM seed. This option is iOS 7 only, iPhone 5 (4-inch 64-bit). There is no way to configure a launch of this item via the command line interface.    Steps to reproduce:  1) Install Xcode 5 GM seed  2) Install command-line tools  3) Open iOS simulator and navigate to hardware>device - note the new simulator version    Result:  The new simulator is available via the simulator menu but not configurable from CLI    Expected Result:  CLI simulator launch flags support all available simulator options""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS7: CLI: Add support for iOS 7 simulator 64 bit
Description: """" Description: With the release of iOS 7, a new iOS simulator option is available via Xcode 5 GM seed. This option is iOS 7 only, iPhone 5 (4-inch 64-bit). There is no way to configure a launch of this item via the command line interface. Steps to reproduce: 1) Install Xcode 5 GM seed 2) Install command-line tools 3) Open iOS simulator and navigate to hardware>device - note the new simulator version Result: The new simulator is available via the simulator menu but not configurable from CLI Expected Result: CLI simulator launch flags support all available simulator options""""",title+description
TIMOB-15116,12,"""iOS7: iOS sim needs to add support for 64 bit iOS 7 simulator""","""""""h5. Description:  With the release of iOS 7, a new iOS simulator option is available via Xcode 5 GM seed. This option is iOS 7 only, iPhone 5 (4-inch 64-bit).    h5. Steps to reproduce:  1) Install Xcode 5 GM seed  2) Install command-line tools  3) Open iOS simulator and navigate to hardware>device    h5. Result:  The new simulator is available via the simulator menu but not configurable from iOS sim    h5. Expected Result:  iOS sim supports all available options""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS7: iOS sim needs to add support for 64 bit iOS 7 simulator
Description: """"h5. Description: With the release of iOS 7, a new iOS simulator option is available via Xcode 5 GM seed. This option is iOS 7 only, iPhone 5 (4-inch 64-bit). h5. Steps to reproduce: 1) Install Xcode 5 GM seed 2) Install command-line tools 3) Open iOS simulator and navigate to hardware>device h5. Result: The new simulator is available via the simulator menu but not configurable from iOS sim h5. Expected Result: iOS sim supports all available options""""",title+description
TIMOB-15114,12,"""Android: Add ability to supply XXHDPI launcher icons""","""""""h6.Feature Request  In Android to add ability to supply XXHDPI launcher icons for developer Android tablet releases.    So XXHDPI images can be supported on one of these folders:    - drawable-xxhdpi or   - drawable-480dpi folder.    According Google+ link extrahigh density (320 dpi) falls into XHDPI density bucket so this might be a duplicate request of https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-7827.    h6.Google+   {quote}  The gorgeous screen on the Nexus 10 falls into the XHDPI density bucket.  On tablets, Launcher uses icons from one density bucket up [0] to render them slightly larger.  To ensure that your launcher icon (arguably your apps most important asset) is crisp you need to add a 144*144px icon in the drawable-xxhdpi or drawable-480dpi folder.  {quote}    Link: https://plus.google.com/+NickButcher/posts/ePQya3KsTjW""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Add ability to supply XXHDPI launcher icons
Description: """"h6.Feature Request In Android to add ability to supply XXHDPI launcher icons for developer Android tablet releases. So XXHDPI images can be supported on one of these folders: - drawable-xxhdpi or - drawable-480dpi folder. According Google+ link extrahigh density (320 dpi) falls into XHDPI density bucket so this might be a duplicate request of https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-7827. h6.Google+ {quote} The gorgeous screen on the Nexus 10 falls into the XHDPI density bucket. On tablets, Launcher uses icons from one density bucket up [0] to render them slightly larger. To ensure that your launcher icon (arguably your apps most important asset) is crisp you need to add a 144*144px icon in the drawable-xxhdpi or drawable-480dpi folder. {quote} Link: https://plus.google.com/+NickButcher/posts/ePQya3KsTjW""""",title+description
