TIMOB-14888,12,"""iOS: TableView touchmove only fires once when dragging views in a TableViewRow""","""""""*Problem*  With Titanium SDK 3.1.1, the touchmove event is only fired once and then stops once it tries to animate the view being touched. In 3.1.0, the touchmove event is fired repeatedly as expected, so the problem was introduced in 3.1.1.    *Steps to reproduce*  Add a view inside of a TableViewRow and add a touchmove event listener with an animation.    *Test case*  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: TableView touchmove only fires once when dragging views in a TableViewRow
Description: """"*Problem* With Titanium SDK 3.1.1, the touchmove event is only fired once and then stops once it tries to animate the view being touched. In 3.1.0, the touchmove event is fired repeatedly as expected, so the problem was introduced in 3.1.1. *Steps to reproduce* Add a view inside of a TableViewRow and add a touchmove event listener with an animation. *Test case* """"",title+description
TIMOB-14887,12,"""iOS: TableViewRow hasCheck, hasChild and hasDetail properties prevent selection fading""","""""""*Problem*  When the hasCheck, hasChild or hasDetail properties are set to true on a TableViewRow, the selection fade effect no longer occurs.    *Steps to reproduce*  1. Run the test case on the iOS simulator  2. Select the rows and see that the select color suddenly appears with no fade effect  3. Comment out the click event listener  4. Run again, select the rows, and see that there is a fade effect    *Test case*  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: TableViewRow hasCheck, hasChild and hasDetail properties prevent selection fading
Description: """"*Problem* When the hasCheck, hasChild or hasDetail properties are set to true on a TableViewRow, the selection fade effect no longer occurs. *Steps to reproduce* 1. Run the test case on the iOS simulator 2. Select the rows and see that the select color suddenly appears with no fade effect 3. Comment out the click event listener 4. Run again, select the rows, and see that there is a fade effect *Test case* """"",title+description
TIMOB-14886,12,"""iOS: Label padding not working""","""""""*Problem*  Label padding is not working on iOS. No padding is displayed. Background image is scaled according to padding but it is displayed cropped.    *Test case 1*      *Test case 2*      *Logs*  No relevant console output.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Label padding not working
Description: """"*Problem* Label padding is not working on iOS. No padding is displayed. Background image is scaled according to padding but it is displayed cropped. *Test case 1* *Test case 2* *Logs* No relevant console output.""""",title+description
TIMOB-14885,12,"""Android: VideoPlayer can't play certain remote mp4 files""","""""""h6.Issue  Despite the fact both links are working and have the same conditions (same encoding and the same server) just fail to play.    h5.Video formats  Both video files have Format H.264, Audio decoder AAC Tag 255.    h6.On new HTC 4.1.2  This file can be played:    - http://d269mq2d9sw4la.cloudfront.net/eSchool-video/13Q1-DannySilk-CultureOfHonor/13Q1-DannySilk-CultureOfHonor01.mp4    But not this one:    - http://d269mq2d9sw4la.cloudfront.net/eSchool-video/09Q2-MikeBickle-1stCommandment/09Q2-MikeBickle-1stCommandment02.mp4    h6.On Galaxy Note 4.1.2  None of them work    h6.Droid Razr 2.3.5  Both are working    h6.Snippet code  To reproduce just throw this code into an app.js and wait till video starts.      h6.Stack traces  https://gist.github.com/egomez99/50b4fd78eeba02119c2a    h6.Additional info  Logs seem to evidence an error related to AwesomePlayer where is """"""""Error: 1,-2147483648"""""""". Discussion topics suggest adding permissions in to manifest sections. E.g.   http://www.weston-fl.com/blog/?p=2988  http://stackoverflow.com/questions/11777595/playing-a-downloaded-video-error-1-2147483648    However does not seem to work.      If this is an AwesomePlayer issue that is equipped already on recent devices, is there a way to detect it? Possibly using the Package manager to get information related to the application packages. http://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/pm/PackageManager.html """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: VideoPlayer can't play certain remote mp4 files
Description: """"h6.Issue Despite the fact both links are working and have the same conditions (same encoding and the same server) just fail to play. h5.Video formats Both video files have Format H.264, Audio decoder AAC Tag 255. h6.On new HTC 4.1.2 This file can be played: - http://d269mq2d9sw4la.cloudfront.net/eSchool-video/13Q1-DannySilk-CultureOfHonor/13Q1-DannySilk-CultureOfHonor01.mp4 But not this one: - http://d269mq2d9sw4la.cloudfront.net/eSchool-video/09Q2-MikeBickle-1stCommandment/09Q2-MikeBickle-1stCommandment02.mp4 h6.On Galaxy Note 4.1.2 None of them work h6.Droid Razr 2.3.5 Both are working h6.Snippet code To reproduce just throw this code into an app.js and wait till video starts. h6.Stack traces https://gist.github.com/egomez99/50b4fd78eeba02119c2a h6.Additional info Logs seem to evidence an error related to AwesomePlayer where is """"""""Error: 1,-2147483648"""""""". Discussion topics suggest adding permissions in to manifest sections. E.g. http://www.weston-fl.com/blog/?p=2988 http://stackoverflow.com/questions/11777595/playing-a-downloaded-video-error-1-2147483648 However does not seem to work. If this is an AwesomePlayer issue that is equipped already on recent devices, is there a way to detect it? Possibly using the Package manager to get information related to the application packages. http://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/pm/PackageManager.html """"",title+description
TIMOB-14884,12,"""iOS: device builds don't use platform-specific files for commonjs modules""","""""""h2. problem    When referencing commonjs modules in code, iOS device builds do not reference platform specific modules if they are present, instead they reference the non-platform-specific ones. This does not occur on iOS simulator builds, which behave as expected. This makes it impossible to use platform-specific versions of Javascript files on iOS device builds, being a blocker for the linked tickets.    h2. test case    h4. app.js      h4. lib/mylib.js      h4. iphone/lib/mylib.js      h2. reproduce    # Create a traditional titanium app, adding the files mentioned above.  # Run for iOS simulator  # Confirm that you get the message from the platform-specific mylib.js file: """"""""hello from the iphone CFG.js""""""""  # Now run for an iOS device  # You will get the error described here, where the non-platform-specific module is referenced, giving you the message: """"""""hello from the CFG.js (you should never see this)""""""""""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: device builds don't use platform-specific files for commonjs modules
Description: """"h2. problem When referencing commonjs modules in code, iOS device builds do not reference platform specific modules if they are present, instead they reference the non-platform-specific ones. This does not occur on iOS simulator builds, which behave as expected. This makes it impossible to use platform-specific versions of Javascript files on iOS device builds, being a blocker for the linked tickets. h2. test case h4. app.js h4. lib/mylib.js h4. iphone/lib/mylib.js h2. reproduce # Create a traditional titanium app, adding the files mentioned above. # Run for iOS simulator # Confirm that you get the message from the platform-specific mylib.js file: """"""""hello from the iphone CFG.js"""""""" # Now run for an iOS device # You will get the error described here, where the non-platform-specific module is referenced, giving you the message: """"""""hello from the CFG.js (you should never see this)""""""""""""",title+description
