TIMOB-14793,12,"""Android: With fastdev set """"true"""" in tiapp.xml and a fastdev server running, device builds will hang on splash until network request timeout""","""""""h5. Description:  When a developer launches an Android emulator, unless they disable fastdev, a fastdev server will start for the project. If that project's tiapp.xml has the fastdev property and it's set to """"""""true"""""""", subsequent builds for device will seem to hang on the splash screen until the fastdev network request times out.     According to Hieu, this is caused by the tiapp.xml property being evaluated by the SDK despite the fact that Studio ignores this setting for device builds.    h5. Steps to reproduce:  1) Create an android project  2) Set the tiapp.xml property:  <property name=""""""""ti.android.fastdev"""""""" type=""""""""bool"""""""">true</property>  3) Run the project in the emulator  4) Verify fastdev server start  5) Run the project on device    h5. Result:  The app launches, and sits on the splash screen until the network request times out.    h5. Expected Result:  Fastdev setting has no affect on device builds""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: With fastdev set """"true"""" in tiapp.xml and a fastdev server running, device builds will hang on splash until network request timeout
Description: """"h5. Description: When a developer launches an Android emulator, unless they disable fastdev, a fastdev server will start for the project. If that project's tiapp.xml has the fastdev property and it's set to """"""""true"""""""", subsequent builds for device will seem to hang on the splash screen until the fastdev network request times out. According to Hieu, this is caused by the tiapp.xml property being evaluated by the SDK despite the fact that Studio ignores this setting for device builds. h5. Steps to reproduce: 1) Create an android project 2) Set the tiapp.xml property: <property name=""""""""ti.android.fastdev"""""""" type=""""""""bool"""""""">true</property> 3) Run the project in the emulator 4) Verify fastdev server start 5) Run the project on device h5. Result: The app launches, and sits on the splash screen until the network request times out. h5. Expected Result: Fastdev setting has no affect on device builds""""",title+description
TIMOB-14791,12,"""Android: Disable fastdev on device""","""""""We need to disable fastdev on device to prevent app hanging in the edge case described below:    Testing steps:    1. Run any project on emulator. Check to make sure fastDev <USER>is running.  2. Turn on fastdev property in tiapp.xml.    3. Run the same project on device.    Note: Step 1 and 2 does NOT need to be done in order (i.e: you can turn on the property before you launch the emulator, since it wouldn't make any difference). Also, note that if you run Emulator 4.2 or 4.3 in step 1, your app will crash (if run using Titanium Studio), or hang (if run using <USER>Studio), but the fast dev server SHOULD be running.    After step 3, you would see that the app on device hangs on splash screen. It shouldn't.    Repeat step 3 on multiple devices (2.3, 4.0, 4.1, 4.2, 4.3)""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Disable fastdev on device
Description: """"We need to disable fastdev on device to prevent app hanging in the edge case described below: Testing steps: 1. Run any project on emulator. Check to make sure fastDev <USER>is running. 2. Turn on fastdev property in tiapp.xml. 3. Run the same project on device. Note: Step 1 and 2 does NOT need to be done in order (i.e: you can turn on the property before you launch the emulator, since it wouldn't make any difference). Also, note that if you run Emulator 4.2 or 4.3 in step 1, your app will crash (if run using Titanium Studio), or hang (if run using <USER>Studio), but the fast dev server SHOULD be running. After step 3, you would see that the app on device hangs on splash screen. It shouldn't. Repeat step 3 on multiple devices (2.3, 4.0, 4.1, 4.2, 4.3)""""",title+description
TIMOB-14789,12,"""CLI: Help command displays prompt for platform instead of printing help""","""""""When running """"""""ti help build"""""""" or """"""""ti build -h"""""""", it prompts for the platform instead of displaying the platform.""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Help command displays prompt for platform instead of printing help
Description: """"When running """"""""ti help build"""""""" or """"""""ti build -h"""""""", it prompts for the platform instead of displaying the platform.""""",title+description
TIMOB-14783,12,"""BlackBerry: Investigate exposing BlackBerry V8 runtime debugging to Titanium Studio""",,New Feature,Title: BlackBerry: Investigate exposing BlackBerry V8 runtime debugging to Titanium Studio,title-only
TIMOB-14781,12,"""BlackBerry: Project file timestamps are changed after building for BlackBerry""","""""""h5. Problem description    After building a BlackBerry project, all project files timestamps are updated to the build time.    No other changes seem to take place.     h5. Steps to reproduce  1. Create a project and make a copy.    2. Build the project for BlackBerry (either from CLI or from <USER>Studio). Perform a diff between the 2 projects files (for example all files in the 'app' folders for Alloy projects).  Result: no differences are reported.    3. Perform an md5 and compare the checksum of a project file and his copy.  Result: md5 checksum are identical.    4. Check timestamps of the built project files.  Result: files are reporting all timestamps are being modified (Created, Modified and Last Opened).    5. Build for Android  Result: no modification is made to project files timestamps.    h5. Note  Issue is reproducible also with CLI and TiSDK 3.1.1 GA.  Not a regression.      h5. <USER>Studio behavior  Using <USER>Studio, I've experienced a inconsistent behavior.  If there are opened project files, the user is usually being being prompted with a message reporting that the file has been modified and should be reloaded (there are no content code differences).  However this behavior seems to be inconsistent or intermittent and it happened that no popup message has been displayed.  """"""",Bug,"Title: BlackBerry: Project file timestamps are changed after building for BlackBerry
Description: """"h5. Problem description After building a BlackBerry project, all project files timestamps are updated to the build time. No other changes seem to take place. h5. Steps to reproduce 1. Create a project and make a copy. 2. Build the project for BlackBerry (either from CLI or from <USER>Studio). Perform a diff between the 2 projects files (for example all files in the 'app' folders for Alloy projects). Result: no differences are reported. 3. Perform an md5 and compare the checksum of a project file and his copy. Result: md5 checksum are identical. 4. Check timestamps of the built project files. Result: files are reporting all timestamps are being modified (Created, Modified and Last Opened). 5. Build for Android Result: no modification is made to project files timestamps. h5. Note Issue is reproducible also with CLI and TiSDK 3.1.1 GA. Not a regression. h5. <USER>Studio behavior Using <USER>Studio, I've experienced a inconsistent behavior. If there are opened project files, the user is usually being being prompted with a message reporting that the file has been modified and should be reloaded (there are no content code differences). However this behavior seems to be inconsistent or intermittent and it happened that no popup message has been displayed. """"",title+description
TIMOB-14779,12,"""MobileWeb: Detect if being served via file:// and show error saying a web server is required""","""""""Titanium Mobile Web requires a web server. It should be possible to detect when NOT served via a web server by checking if the location.protocol == 'file:' and if so, show an error message. Perhaps have a config option to disable this error check or make this check more of a warning than a hard error.""""""",New Feature,"Title: MobileWeb: Detect if being served via file:// and show error saying a web server is required
Description: """"Titanium Mobile Web requires a web server. It should be possible to detect when NOT served via a web server by checking if the location.protocol == 'file:' and if so, show an error message. Perhaps have a config option to disable this error check or make this check more of a warning than a hard error.""""",title+description
TIMOB-14778,12,"""As a developer, I would like an easier way to create a Holo-themed Android app""","""""""As a developer, I would like to create Holo (Android 4.x) themed apps using a simpler <USER>than required now. Currently, doing so requires multiple XML changes to the tiapp.xml and the creation of multiple android theme files and resources.    Android supports multiple themes on a per target OS basis (see the 4th & 5th code samples on http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/guide/Android_Themes). Thus, our tooling could create Holo theme apps that gracefully fall back to 2.x look and feel by default.     If a user chooses to build for an SDK less than 14 (Android 4.0), the existing theme could be used.    If the user chooses to build for a target newer than SDK 14, the dual Holo/current nature could be enabled. This would involve adding some XML to tiapp.xml and the copying of some stock theme & resource files to the platform/android directory.    A reference project is available here: http://adampaxton.com/alloy-action-jackson-an-android-action-bar-demo-app-for-<USER>titanium.aspx    If implemented, the only work required for a developer to create a Holo-themed app would be to choose their build target.    At the time of this ticket, according to http://developer.android.com/about/dashboards/index.html , the current mix of end-user OS usage is:    Android 4.x usage = 63%  Android 2.x usage = 36.8%    """"""",Story,"Title: As a developer, I would like an easier way to create a Holo-themed Android app
Description: """"As a developer, I would like to create Holo (Android 4.x) themed apps using a simpler <USER>than required now. Currently, doing so requires multiple XML changes to the tiapp.xml and the creation of multiple android theme files and resources. Android supports multiple themes on a per target OS basis (see the 4th & 5th code samples on http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/guide/Android_Themes). Thus, our tooling could create Holo theme apps that gracefully fall back to 2.x look and feel by default. If a user chooses to build for an SDK less than 14 (Android 4.0), the existing theme could be used. If the user chooses to build for a target newer than SDK 14, the dual Holo/current nature could be enabled. This would involve adding some XML to tiapp.xml and the copying of some stock theme & resource files to the platform/android directory. A reference project is available here: http://adampaxton.com/alloy-action-jackson-an-android-action-bar-demo-app-for-<USER>titanium.aspx If implemented, the only work required for a developer to create a Holo-themed app would be to choose their build target. At the time of this ticket, according to http://developer.android.com/about/dashboards/index.html , the current mix of end-user OS usage is: Android 4.x usage = 63% Android 2.x usage = 36.8% """"",title+description
TIMOB-14777,12,"""Android: NullPointerException on ListView + ImageView""","""""""Application would sometimes crash (NPE) when aggressively scrolling a ListView with remote images.    Seems there were some safety checks missing in the code (although they were used elsewhere in the same class). Added the safety checks as needed.    Testing code:       1. Run code  2. Scroll aggressively. App should not crash.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: NullPointerException on ListView + ImageView
Description: """"Application would sometimes crash (NPE) when aggressively scrolling a ListView with remote images. Seems there were some safety checks missing in the code (although they were used elsewhere in the same class). Added the safety checks as needed. Testing code: 1. Run code 2. Scroll aggressively. App should not crash.""""",title+description
TIMOB-14776,12,"""Android: Add support for pagingControlHeight to ScrollableView""","""""""The size of the triangle controls on the Android scrollableview cannot be changed. They are way too large by default, made even worse if the scrollableview doesn't fill the screen.    Expected Behavior:  I should be able to change the height/width of the triangle controls with code like this:        Actual Behavior:   That code has no effect. See the attached screen shot of a deliberately small scrollableview (the green box).""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Add support for pagingControlHeight to ScrollableView
Description: """"The size of the triangle controls on the Android scrollableview cannot be changed. They are way too large by default, made even worse if the scrollableview doesn't fill the screen. Expected Behavior: I should be able to change the height/width of the triangle controls with code like this: Actual Behavior: That code has no effect. See the attached screen shot of a deliberately small scrollableview (the green box).""""",title+description
