TIMOB-14429,12,"""Android xhdpi resource folder (and splash screen) not created with new app""","""""""Android xhdpi resource folder (and splash screen) not created with new app    When you create a new app in Studio, we create ldpi, mdpi and hdpi image resource folder (with a corresponding splash screen).    I think we are missing the xdpi. Android developer site describes the 4 resolutions (http://developer.android.com/guide/practices/screens_support.html).""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android xhdpi resource folder (and splash screen) not created with new app
Description: """"Android xhdpi resource folder (and splash screen) not created with new app When you create a new app in Studio, we create ldpi, mdpi and hdpi image resource folder (with a corresponding splash screen). I think we are missing the xdpi. Android developer site describes the 4 resolutions (http://developer.android.com/guide/practices/screens_support.html).""""",title+description
TIMOB-14428,12,"""res-long-port-hdpi splash screen is 800px high and should be 854px""","""""""res-long-port-hdpi splash screen is 800px high and should be 854px    When a new app is created we provide android image folders.    I think the """"""""long"""""""" should refer to the 854px device and we have a 480x800px splash screen that gets distorted on a WVGA854 device (AVD) - see attached screen which shows a slight distortion (Appc logo triangle is equilateral and looks isosceles in the screen shot).    The reason we should fix this is because clients will most likely use our image as a reference.""""""",Story,"Title: res-long-port-hdpi splash screen is 800px high and should be 854px
Description: """"res-long-port-hdpi splash screen is 800px high and should be 854px When a new app is created we provide android image folders. I think the """"""""long"""""""" should refer to the 854px device and we have a 480x800px splash screen that gets distorted on a WVGA854 device (AVD) - see attached screen which shows a slight distortion (Appc logo triangle is equilateral and looks isosceles in the screen shot). The reason we should fix this is because clients will most likely use our image as a reference.""""",title+description
