"""":""""""""WIFI"""""""",""""""""deploytype"""""""":""""""""test"""""""",""""""""app_version"""""""":""""""""1.0"""""""",""""""""osver"""""""":""""""""4.2.1"""""""",""""""""version"""""""":""""""""3.1.1""""""""},""""""""seq"""""""":15,""""""""mid"""""""":""""""""df9547607f27ccd6"""""""",""""""""ver"""""""":""""""""2"""""""",""""""""type"""""""":""""""""ti.start"""""""",""""""""aguid"""""""":""""""""d8504b70-8106-4423-b35a-128a56fb30db""""""""}] analytics events. As it can be seen, the ti.end events are not being sent successfully and hence dashboard does not know that the session has ended and hence messes up session results like active number of sessions, avg session length calculations. """"",title+description
TIMOB-14058,12,"""Code Processor - Alloy: analyzed code results can be different from the expected ones""","""""""h5. Problem description    Code Processor results for Alloy projects are based an Analysis on the compiled code in the Resources folder.  This could generate unexpected results and parity issues for the end user whose expectations are that the Code Processor is checking his code, as included in the Alloy project 'app' folder, even if the Code Processor is actually correctly analyzing the javascript code compiled by Alloy.    h5. Example:    h6. """"""""ContextTest"""""""" sample project  (https://github.com/<USER>titanium-code-processor/tree/master/test_projects/ContextTest or attached here in the Jira)    In this case, the code to be analyzed is:      and the result, as expected, is that:                     By placing the same identical code in an Alloy project index.js controller file, the result is a bit different.    Attached in the Jira there is the ContextTest sample for Alloy too.    After being compiled, the Resources/alloy/controllers/index.js looks like this:      so, lines 14 to 26 are the ones regarding the code put in the controller. The code is a bit different (for example the function """"""""bar()"""""""" is declared before the 'var x = 10') and the Code Analyzer result is a bit different:          In this case the Code Processor is likely behaving correctly, in fact putting the generated code from lines 14 to 26 in a Classic Titanium project, I'm getting the same as the Alloy one.    The issue relates in the fact that from the end-user standpoint, the two results should be identical and the user could be easily confused by those results.      Attached both the classic and the Alloy 'ContextTest' sample projects and the Code Analyzer Results for each.       """"""",Story,"Title: Code Processor - Alloy: analyzed code results can be different from the expected ones
Description: """"h5. Problem description Code Processor results for Alloy projects are based an Analysis on the compiled code in the Resources folder. This could generate unexpected results and parity issues for the end user whose expectations are that the Code Processor is checking his code, as included in the Alloy project 'app' folder, even if the Code Processor is actually correctly analyzing the javascript code compiled by Alloy. h5. Example: h6. """"""""ContextTest"""""""" sample project (https://github.com/<USER>titanium-code-processor/tree/master/test_projects/ContextTest or attached here in the Jira) In this case, the code to be analyzed is: and the result, as expected, is that: By placing the same identical code in an Alloy project index.js controller file, the result is a bit different. Attached in the Jira there is the ContextTest sample for Alloy too. After being compiled, the Resources/alloy/controllers/index.js looks like this: so, lines 14 to 26 are the ones regarding the code put in the controller. The code is a bit different (for example the function """"""""bar()"""""""" is declared before the 'var x = 10') and the Code Analyzer result is a bit different: In this case the Code Processor is likely behaving correctly, in fact putting the generated code from lines 14 to 26 in a Classic Titanium project, I'm getting the same as the Alloy one. The issue relates in the fact that from the end-user standpoint, the two results should be identical and the user could be easily confused by those results. Attached both the classic and the Alloy 'ContextTest' sample projects and the Code Analyzer Results for each. """"",title+description
TIMOB-14057,12,"""Problems building projects concurrently with ti build? (OperationalError: database is locked)""","""""""We have a continuous integration server (Jenkins) that builds an application via the CLI (ti build).     We recently just duplicated the target in Jenkins so we can build both a debug and release variant. After we did this, we *sometimes* (not always) see a build failure. In the logs, the failure point seems to be this message:  [ERROR] OperationalError: database is locked    There shouldn't be any shared dependencies between our two targets (each gets its own copies of data and its own workspace directories)  My best guess is there is there is some shared resource invoked by 'ti build'. Is this true? Is there something that can be done to improve this?    This is not a showstopper at the moment. I can tell Jenkins to only build one project at a time and I think the problem goes away (hard to verify since the problem is intermittent). But it would be nice to not have this limitation.    Full build log is pasted below:    Started by an SCM change  Building in workspace /Users/lanica/Jenkins/Home/jobs/GardensOfTimeAndroid_Debug/workspace  Checkout:workspace / /Users/lanica/Jenkins/Home/jobs/GardensOfTimeAndroid_Debug/workspace - hudson.remoting.LocalChannel@2f56a6be  Using strategy: Default  Last Built Revision: Revision 64b7da5d1dfc4c4d83f78e47a54944bfe52615d8 (origin/master)  Fetching changes from 1 remote Git repository  Fetching upstream changes from origin  Commencing build of Revision 70971df323ec76200a45e467e339b8ef29b6ddd1 (origin/master)  Checking out Revision 70971df323ec76200a45e467e339b8ef29b6ddd1 (origin/master)  Cleaning workspace  Resetting working tree  [workspace] $ /bin/sh -xe /var/folders/cb/116ms69s1sd31zlvttqgxl8c0000gn/T/hudson1399061169376581932.sh  + export PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/local/bin  + PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/local/bin  + [[ ! -z '' ]]  [workspace] $ /bin/sh -xe /var/folders/cb/116ms69