Description: """"*Actual result* The TableView 'longclick' event is fired twice. *Expected result* The event should only fire once. *Steps to reproduce* 1. Run the test case 2. Search for LONGCLICK in DDMS 3. Hold finger down on one of the rows 4. Verify that it's called twice *Test case* """"",title+description
TIMOB-13722,12,"""Auto-add """"Core"""" Modules to a project as appropriate""","""""""When you download a module from the marketplace or github, in order to have it work in a project, you need to add it to that project.    But """"""""Core"""""""" Modules, which really are part of our SDK, you should not have to """"""""add"""""""" the module to the project.  If you use the API, then the module should automatically be added to the project.  If the the API is not used, then it shouldn't be there.      """"""""Core"""""""" module should work like regular APIs, or as close as possible to regular APIs.  The end user shouldn't have to know that they are built as modules.    Today they are provided on the marketplace, but in the near future they will be packaged with the SDK.    """"""",New Feature,"Title: Auto-add """"Core"""" Modules to a project as appropriate
Description: """"When you download a module from the marketplace or github, in order to have it work in a project, you need to add it to that project. But """"""""Core"""""""" Modules, which really are part of our SDK, you should not have to """"""""add"""""""" the module to the project. If you use the API, then the module should automatically be added to the project. If the the API is not used, then it shouldn't be there. """"""""Core"""""""" module should work like regular APIs, or as close as possible to regular APIs. The end user shouldn't have to know that they are built as modules. Today they are provided on the marketplace, but in the near future they will be packaged with the SDK. """"",title+description
TIMOB-13721,12,"""Android: TextField fires change event on first initialization (SDK 3.2)""","""""""*Problem description*  When a label is initialized, it fires of a change event even though nothing has changed yet    *Steps to reproduce*  1. Deploy the test case to device.  2. Check the logcat output.  3. The change event is printed.    *Test case*      *Note*  This works correctly in 3.1.0.GA but seems to be broken in CI builds. It doesn't seem to make much sense since nothing changed yet, this is the starting value.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: TextField fires change event on first initialization (SDK 3.2)
Description: """"*Problem description* When a label is initialized, it fires of a change event even though nothing has changed yet *Steps to reproduce* 1. Deploy the test case to device. 2. Check the logcat output. 3. The change event is printed. *Test case* *Note* This works correctly in 3.1.0.GA but seems to be broken in CI builds. It doesn't seem to make much sense since nothing changed yet, this is the starting value.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13720,12,"""CLI: iOS: /Resources/fonts/ directory is ignored with new tiapp.xml <ios> directive""","""""""*Problem description*  If you have a /Resources/fonts/ directory with custom fonts in it, and define those fonts in tiapp.xml 's new <ios> directive, the fonts directory is not copied over to the build.    *Test case*      *Workaround*  If you copy the fonts/ directory to /platform/iphone/ and /platform/android/ they are included in the build.""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: iOS: /Resources/fonts/ directory is ignored with new tiapp.xml <ios> directive
Description: """"*Problem description* If you have a /Resources/fonts/ directory with custom fonts in it, and define those fonts in tiapp.xml 's new <ios> directive, the fonts directory is not copied over to the build. *Test case* *Workaround* If you copy the fonts/ directory to /platform/iphone/ and /platform/android/ they are included in the build.""""",title+description
