TIMOB-13667,12,"""CLI: Build error with CLI 3.1 and Node.js 0.8.22""","""""""*Problem description*  I created and deployed an app successfully in Titanium Studio; however, when I try to build the same app with the CLI I get the error listed below.    *Note*  This occurs with any app build with CLI. The log below is after attempting to build a brand new project.    *Log*      I'm using CLI 3.1.0 and Node.js 0.8.22. I've followed the setup steps to installing and configuring the CLI in the documentation and have verified my path settings from the same documentation.     I asked a question in the forum (http://developer.<USER>com/question/151112/does-this-look-familiar-to-anyone#comment-169184) regarding this issue. When that question was posted, I was using cli 3.0.24 and it was recommended that I update to 3.1.0 to incorporate several bug fixes included in that version. However, I have updated to the latest version of the cli and am still seeing the same issue.""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Build error with CLI 3.1 and Node.js 0.8.22
Description: """"*Problem description* I created and deployed an app successfully in Titanium Studio; however, when I try to build the same app with the CLI I get the error listed below. *Note* This occurs with any app build with CLI. The log below is after attempting to build a brand new project. *Log* I'm using CLI 3.1.0 and Node.js 0.8.22. I've followed the setup steps to installing and configuring the CLI in the documentation and have verified my path settings from the same documentation. I asked a question in the forum (http://developer.<USER>com/question/151112/does-this-look-familiar-to-anyone#comment-169184) regarding this issue. When that question was posted, I was using cli 3.0.24 and it was recommended that I update to 3.1.0 to incorporate several bug fixes included in that version. However, I have updated to the latest version of the cli and am still seeing the same issue.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13666,12,"""iOS: switching between views with animate fails after the first attempt""","""""""h2. Description of the problem  When switching between views using animate, the operation works (back and forth) only once.    h2. Steps to reproduce  Use the following code, switch to view 2, back to view 1, and again: it will only work once, then back to view1 it will not go anymore to view2.        h2. Notes  The code sample above is re-creating the views all the times, as - according to the documentation - the view gets destroyed once the animation is over. In fact, if the views are not re-created, the app will crash trying to switch back.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: switching between views with animate fails after the first attempt
Description: """"h2. Description of the problem When switching between views using animate, the operation works (back and forth) only once. h2. Steps to reproduce Use the following code, switch to view 2, back to view 1, and again: it will only work once, then back to view1 it will not go anymore to view2. h2. Notes The code sample above is re-creating the views all the times, as - according to the documentation - the view gets destroyed once the animation is over. In fact, if the views are not re-created, the app will crash trying to switch back.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13665,12,"""Ti API: Provide a means for native modules to be active during app startup before app.js loads""","""""""There are some times where external code must be run very early on, or during specific times. Native Titaniums need to provide a means for:    # Indicating a module needs to start early. This is a property of the module.  # Allow a module to optionally run during the app startup (EG, AppDidFinishLaunching on iOS)  # Allow a module to optionally run before the app.js is processed and run.  # Allow a module to optionally run after the app.js is processed and run.    Due to the short time needed, these will be OS-specific, but allow for commonality where logical.    Implementation will probably be thusly:    * A module's manifest includes a boolean to allow it to run early. (Does this need to happen? Perhaps we can introspect it?)  * The module template and/or a protocol will indicate what class/static method names will be.  * The application build scripts/CLI will read from the module manifests and leave some message for the application to know what module classes to call the method.  * The application, on startup, will know what module classes to method call, and call thusly.  * The application will broadcast (NSNotifications during AppDidFinishLaunching, before calling willStartNewContext, after calling didStartNewContext; and android equiv )  * By adding notificationListeners/(Android equiv) during the early method call, the module can execute whatever it wants during those specific points.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Ti API: Provide a means for native modules to be active during app startup before app.js loads
Description: """"There are some times where external code must be run very early on, or during specific times. Native Titaniums need to provide a means for: # Indicating a module needs to start early. This is a property of the module. # Allow a module to optionally run during the app startup (EG, AppDidFinishLaunching on iOS) # Allow a module to optionally run before the app.js is processed and run. # Allow a module to optionally run after the app.js is processed and run. Due to the short time needed, these will be OS-specific, but allow for commonality where logical. Implementation will probably be thusly: * A module's manifest includes a boolean to allow it to run early. (Does this need to happen? Perhaps we can introspect it?) * The module template and/or a protocol will indicate what class/static method names will be. * The application build scripts/CLI will read from the module manifests and leave some message for the application to know what module classes to call the method. * The application, on startup, will know what module classes to method call, and call thusly. * The application will broadcast (NSNotifications during AppDidFinishLaunching, before calling willStartNewContext, after calling didStartNewContext; and android equiv ) * By adding notificationListeners/(Android equiv) during the early method call, the module can execute whatever it wants during those specific points.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13664,12,"""Android: Garbage collection resulting in crash occurring when passing objects to Common JS modules""","""""""*Problem description*  Garbage collection is occurring when it shouldn't be on Android only (see test case). There could be a conflict between JavaScript and Java's garbage collection mechanisms.    *Steps to reproduce*  1. Run attached project on Android device  2. Tap """"""""Click to get to screen""""""""  3. Wait 20 (or more) seconds  4. Tap """"""""Wait 20 seconds"""""""" button  5. Observe crash    *Test case*  See attached project.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Garbage collection resulting in crash occurring when passing objects to Common JS modules
Description: """"*Problem description* Garbage collection is occurring when it shouldn't be on Android only (see test case). There could be a conflict between JavaScript and Java's garbage collection mechanisms. *Steps to reproduce* 1. Run attached project on Android device 2. Tap """"""""Click to get to screen"""""""" 3. Wait 20 (or more) seconds 4. Tap """"""""Wait 20 seconds"""""""" button 5. Observe crash *Test case* See attached project.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13663,12,"""CLI: iOS """"build only"""" option in conjunction with target """"device"""" or """"dist-adhoc"""" does not produce an ipa""","""""""h5. Description:  While investigating a bug, it was evident that using dist-adhoc target with flag build-only, you do not get an ipa produced.    h5. Steps to reproduce:  1) Create a project with ios support  2) Using the CLI, build for adhoc with build-only option  example:   ti build -p ios -T dist-adhoc -F iphone -V """"""""<USER>Merriman"""""""" -P 207TC916-0727-45F8-83E2-FC13B26F9F3S -I 6.1 -K <KEYSTORE PATH HERE> -O /Users/<USER>Desktop --log-level debug    h5. Result:  No ipa is produced at output directory location.    h5. Expected Result:  ipa is produced""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: iOS """"build only"""" option in conjunction with target """"device"""" or """"dist-adhoc"""" does not produce an ipa
Description: """"h5. Description: While investigating a bug, it was evident that using dist-adhoc target with flag build-only, you do not get an ipa produced. h5. Steps to reproduce: 1) Create a project with ios support 2) Using the CLI, build for adhoc with build-only option example: ti build -p ios -T dist-adhoc -F iphone -V """"""""<USER>Merriman"""""""" -P 207TC916-0727-45F8-83E2-FC13B26F9F3S -I 6.1 -K <KEYSTORE PATH HERE> -O /Users/<USER>Desktop --log-level debug h5. Result: No ipa is produced at output directory location. h5. Expected Result: ipa is produced""""",title+description
TIMOB-13662,12,"""iOS: Setting html property in web view prevents setUrl from loading page""","""""""*Problem description*  I have a webview that on start up is empty on an iOS as it acts to show detail. So I used the html property to effectively set a default blank page. It seems that setting this property stops setUrl from loading a local resource.    *Steps to reproduce*  1. Run test case, with testpage.html as an additional resource  2. See that """"""""I'm the property"""""""" displays rather than """"""""I'm the local page""""""""  3. Comment out the html property to see that now """"""""I'm the local page"""""""" displays    *app.js*      *testpage.html*  {code:html}  <html>   <body>    I'm the local page   </body>  </html>  {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Setting html property in web view prevents setUrl from loading page
Description: """"*Problem description* I have a webview that on start up is empty on an iOS as it acts to show detail. So I used the html property to effectively set a default blank page. It seems that setting this property stops setUrl from loading a local resource. *Steps to reproduce* 1. Run test case, with testpage.html as an additional resource 2. See that """"""""I'm the property"""""""" displays rather than """"""""I'm the local page"""""""" 3. Comment out the html property to see that now """"""""I'm the local page"""""""" displays *app.js* *testpage.html* {code:html} <html> <body> I'm the local page </body> </html> {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-13661,12,"""iOS: Fading between backgroundGradients in tableViewRows""","""""""h6.Problem  In the previous SDK (3.0.0 GA) the behavior of changing the backgroundGradient for a tableViewRow was instantaneous. Now there seems to be a premade fading transition from one backgroundGradient to the other.     h6.Snippet sample  - app.js      - FirstView.js  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Fading between backgroundGradients in tableViewRows
Description: """"h6.Problem In the previous SDK (3.0.0 GA) the behavior of changing the backgroundGradient for a tableViewRow was instantaneous. Now there seems to be a premade fading transition from one backgroundGradient to the other. h6.Snippet sample - app.js - FirstView.js """"",title+description
TIMOB-13660,12,"""iOS: Using Ti.UI.ScrollViews as pages of Ti.UI.ScrollableView produces unexpected results""","""""""*Problem description*  If you use Ti.UI.ScrollView objects as the views of a Ti.UI.ScrollableView, things get strange with the 4th+ views. It appears to be related to the memory management built in to ScrollableView, not drawing views until they are +/- 2 views away.    *Note*  If you do dumps of the Ti.UI.ScrollView.size properties, x/y/height/width are all correct, but the display puts them wherever it feels like.    *Workaround*  It works if you wrap the Ti.UI.ScrollView in a Ti.UI.View.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Using Ti.UI.ScrollViews as pages of Ti.UI.ScrollableView produces unexpected results
Description: """"*Problem description* If you use Ti.UI.ScrollView objects as the views of a Ti.UI.ScrollableView, things get strange with the 4th+ views. It appears to be related to the memory management built in to ScrollableView, not drawing views until they are +/- 2 views away. *Note* If you do dumps of the Ti.UI.ScrollView.size properties, x/y/height/width are all correct, but the display puts them wherever it feels like. *Workaround* It works if you wrap the Ti.UI.ScrollView in a Ti.UI.View.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13659,12,"""iOS: Date() value gets changed when passed via events""","""""""*Problem description*  I have built a simple Alloy test case that passes a date object between two controllers by firing Ti.App.fireEvent. Tested in iOS 6.1 simulator on Mountain Lion. Titantium 3.1.0.GA. But this occurs with 3.0.2.GA as well.    *Test case*      *Console output*  {code}  [INFO] starting time: {""""""""eventTime"""""""":""""""""2013-04-21T15:45:00.000Z""""""""}  [INFO]  in event time: Sun Apr 21 2013 11:46:02 GMT-0400 (EDT)  {code}    *Note*  The time prior to firing the event is 15:45, but when it arrives the minutes are now: 46.02  I presume this is a problem with the serializing of the date object.  Perhaps Data() objects are not legal for events, but they used to work, this is old code that has now stopped working.  If they are not legal, Titanium should throw an error or it should be documented.    *Workaround*  Pass the date as:  {code:javascript}newDate().toString(){code}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Date() value gets changed when passed via events
Description: """"*Problem description* I have built a simple Alloy test case that passes a date object between two controllers by firing Ti.App.fireEvent. Tested in iOS 6.1 simulator on Mountain Lion. Titantium 3.1.0.GA. But this occurs with 3.0.2.GA as well. *Test case* *Console output* {code} [INFO] starting time: {""""""""eventTime"""""""":""""""""2013-04-21T15:45:00.000Z""""""""} [INFO] in event time: Sun Apr 21 2013 11:46:02 GMT-0400 (EDT) {code} *Note* The time prior to firing the event is 15:45, but when it arrives the minutes are now: 46.02 I presume this is a problem with the serializing of the date object. Perhaps Data() objects are not legal for events, but they used to work, this is old code that has now stopped working. If they are not legal, Titanium should throw an error or it should be documented. *Workaround* Pass the date as: {code:javascript}newDate().toString(){code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-13658,12,"""iOS: Window swipe event blocks touches in non-scrollable part of web view""","""""""*Problem description*  If I have a swipe event attached to a container holding a webview, the nonscrollable part of the webview will not work.    *Steps to reproduce*  1 - Compile to iOS simulator  2 - Try to click sign up yellow button (it won't work), or other part of the twitter header.  3 - Remove the """"""""doSwipe"""""""" from the test case  4 - Test again and it will work    *Test case*  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Window swipe event blocks touches in non-scrollable part of web view
Description: """"*Problem description* If I have a swipe event attached to a container holding a webview, the nonscrollable part of the webview will not work. *Steps to reproduce* 1 - Compile to iOS simulator 2 - Try to click sign up yellow button (it won't work), or other part of the twitter header. 3 - Remove the """"""""doSwipe"""""""" from the test case 4 - Test again and it will work *Test case* """"",title+description
