TIMOB-13652,12,"""New regression with Node.js 0.10.X as behavior of path.join changed""","""""""With recent changes of Node, starting with 0.10.X path.join will throw an error when an argument is not of type String. Thus, the runtime stops at a point in node-appc/lib/android.js where mistakenly is assumed, a variable is not undefined. This crashes Titanium CLI and all applications with node-appc as dependency.""""""",Bug,"Title: New regression with Node.js 0.10.X as behavior of path.join changed
Description: """"With recent changes of Node, starting with 0.10.X path.join will throw an error when an argument is not of type String. Thus, the runtime stops at a point in node-appc/lib/android.js where mistakenly is assumed, a variable is not undefined. This crashes Titanium CLI and all applications with node-appc as dependency.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13651,12,"""LiveView: no code changes are being applied on Android Emulator""","""""""h5. Problem description  LiveView is not applying changes to a project build/run on Android Emulator, both classic or Alloy, from <USER>Studio or from Console CLI.    """"""",Bug,"Title: LiveView: no code changes are being applied on Android Emulator
Description: """"h5. Problem description LiveView is not applying changes to a project build/run on Android Emulator, both classic or Alloy, from <USER>Studio or from Console CLI. """"",title+description
TIMOB-13650,12,"""When building Alloy projects, Resources folder should be auto-generated when not present""","""""""For Alloy projects, the Resources folder should not be touched - its generated code, so typically it is left out of GIT repos.     This causes a problem when trying to build a project imported from GIT - the application will fail to compile due to the Resources folder not being present.    To reproduce:   - Import an Alloy based Titanium project from github, make sure there is no resources folder stored there  - Build / Run the project    Actual:  Build fails due to missing resources file    Expected:   Titanium Studio should detect the missing resources folder and rebuild using the Alloy standard folder structure""""""",Bug,"Title: When building Alloy projects, Resources folder should be auto-generated when not present
Description: """"For Alloy projects, the Resources folder should not be touched - its generated code, so typically it is left out of GIT repos. This causes a problem when trying to build a project imported from GIT - the application will fail to compile due to the Resources folder not being present. To reproduce: - Import an Alloy based Titanium project from github, make sure there is no resources folder stored there - Build / Run the project Actual: Build fails due to missing resources file Expected: Titanium Studio should detect the missing resources folder and rebuild using the Alloy standard folder structure""""",title+description
TIMOB-13649,12,"""DashboardView fires delete events for all items even when not in edit mode""","""""""According to the [documentation|http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.UI.DashboardView-event-delete], the delete event for DashboardView is    {quote}  Fired when an item is deleted in edit mode.  {quote}    However, a delete event will be fired for every item in a dashboardview even when not in edit mode. If you, for example, called {{setData()}} programmatically to reset the DashboardView, an individual delete event is fired for all items that are going to be replaced, which probably doesn't make sense in the context of a wholesale replace. It is also pretty noisy. In addition, {{setData()}} is used by Alloy data binding to reset the dashboard when the model is updated. """"""",Bug,"Title: DashboardView fires delete events for all items even when not in edit mode
Description: """"According to the [documentation|http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.UI.DashboardView-event-delete], the delete event for DashboardView is {quote} Fired when an item is deleted in edit mode. {quote} However, a delete event will be fired for every item in a dashboardview even when not in edit mode. If you, for example, called {{setData()}} programmatically to reset the DashboardView, an individual delete event is fired for all items that are going to be replaced, which probably doesn't make sense in the context of a wholesale replace. It is also pretty noisy. In addition, {{setData()}} is used by Alloy data binding to reset the dashboard when the model is updated. """"",title+description
TIMOB-13648,12,"""LiveView: parse error displayed when modifying the code on iOS Alloy projects""","""""""h5. Problem description  After modifying an Alloy project code after the first run, a red screen with Parse error is displayed.  Also, errors in the log are always displayed.    h5. Steps to reproduce  1. Create an Alloy project and run using LiveView on iOS  2. Modify a UI element (for example a label).  Result: red screen with 'parse error' is displayed and parse errors are displayed in the console log  3. Click on dismiss  Result: code is changed as expected  4. Modify again any UI element  Result: no error message screen is displayed, however errors in the log are always displayed.    Attaching screenshot and error logs.""""""",Bug,"Title: LiveView: parse error displayed when modifying the code on iOS Alloy projects
Description: """"h5. Problem description After modifying an Alloy project code after the first run, a red screen with Parse error is displayed. Also, errors in the log are always displayed. h5. Steps to reproduce 1. Create an Alloy project and run using LiveView on iOS 2. Modify a UI element (for example a label). Result: red screen with 'parse error' is displayed and parse errors are displayed in the console log 3. Click on dismiss Result: code is changed as expected 4. Modify again any UI element Result: no error message screen is displayed, however errors in the log are always displayed. Attaching screenshot and error logs.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13647,12,"""LiveView: Android: new app instance is opened on top every time an update is saved""","""""""h5. Problem description  On Android, LiveView is changing the app code after any update. However, instead of just modifying the current opened app, a new instance of the app is opened displaying the code modifications.    h5. Steps to reproduce  1. Create a project (classic or Alloy) and run on Android enabling LiveView  2. Modify some UI code (for example a label)  Result: a new instance of the app is opened showing the app with the modification done.  3. Press the back button  Result: new instance of the app is closed. The first run app is displayed as opened below it.""""""",Bug,"Title: LiveView: Android: new app instance is opened on top every time an update is saved
Description: """"h5. Problem description On Android, LiveView is changing the app code after any update. However, instead of just modifying the current opened app, a new instance of the app is opened displaying the code modifications. h5. Steps to reproduce 1. Create a project (classic or Alloy) and run on Android enabling LiveView 2. Modify some UI code (for example a label) Result: a new instance of the app is opened showing the app with the modification done. 3. Press the back button Result: new instance of the app is closed. The first run app is displayed as opened below it.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13646,12,"""Validate.py should accept multiple directories""","""""""validate.py should be able to be run against module docs that have references to Titanium.* Currently, the module docs need to be copied to the apidoc folder in titanium_mobile so that it can validate without throwing errors that Titanium.* could not be found.     validate.py should be able to be run against module documentation and validate with or without the Titanium documentation""""""",Sub-task,"Title: Validate.py should accept multiple directories
Description: """"validate.py should be able to be run against module docs that have references to Titanium.* Currently, the module docs need to be copied to the apidoc folder in titanium_mobile so that it can validate without throwing errors that Titanium.* could not be found. validate.py should be able to be run against module documentation and validate with or without the Titanium documentation""""",title+description
TIMOB-13645,12,"""CLI: ti build - iOS - package with no options returns error and not  all missing options are being prompted""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  Please create the project and navigate to it's directory.  1) Try  CLI command (build android package with no options)  """"""""ti build -T dist-appstore""""""""  2) Enter target platform after it is prompted    Result:  [ERROR] Unable to find an iOS Distribution Certificate for name """"""""""""""""    Expected result:  It would be nice to have all missing options  prompted""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: ti build - iOS - package with no options returns error and not all missing options are being prompted
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: Please create the project and navigate to it's directory. 1) Try CLI command (build android package with no options) """"""""ti build -T dist-appstore"""""""" 2) Enter target platform after it is prompted Result: [ERROR] Unable to find an iOS Distribution Certificate for name """""""""""""""" Expected result: It would be nice to have all missing options prompted""""",title+description
TIMOB-13644,12,"""Platform Performance Improvements""","""""""The performance of all <USER>platforms shall be systemically  and systematically measured and improved. Performance improvements  shall include all of these elements:    1. An Experimental Apparatus, which includes all of the following:       1. A fully specified physical device (e.g. Android Nexus 4, iPhone 5).       2. A fully specified operating system (e.g. Android 4.2.1, iOS 6.1.3).       3. Consistent, configurable TIMOB instrumentation. The spec for        this is still under development but will include the capability        to configure specialized """"""""profile enabled SDKs"""""""" to facilitate        performance data acquisition.       4. A data acquisition system for performance data. This includes        both software on the device, workstation tools, and procedures        encapsulating """"""""best practices"""""""" for acquiring data.       5. A well-defined data format that includes not only the raw        performance data, but also the context of the data like device        model, os version, SDK version, date, time, engineer, and JIRA        ticket.    2. A fully specified repeatable experiment that measures a reported     performance issue. The experiment shall be sufficiently described     such that a fellow colleague can reproduce the experiment.    3. A fully developed hypothesis of an observed performance issue based     on the analysis of experimental data.    4. A """"""""performance improvement"""""""" change to the SDK is introduced based     on the hypothesis, and a repeat of the experiment is performed to     either prove or disprove the hypotheis.  """"""",Epic,"Title: Platform Performance Improvements
Description: """"The performance of all <USER>platforms shall be systemically and systematically measured and improved. Performance improvements shall include all of these elements: 1. An Experimental Apparatus, which includes all of the following: 1. A fully specified physical device (e.g. Android Nexus 4, iPhone 5). 2. A fully specified operating system (e.g. Android 4.2.1, iOS 6.1.3). 3. Consistent, configurable TIMOB instrumentation. The spec for this is still under development but will include the capability to configure specialized """"""""profile enabled SDKs"""""""" to facilitate performance data acquisition. 4. A data acquisition system for performance data. This includes both software on the device, workstation tools, and procedures encapsulating """"""""best practices"""""""" for acquiring data. 5. A well-defined data format that includes not only the raw performance data, but also the context of the data like device model, os version, SDK version, date, time, engineer, and JIRA ticket. 2. A fully specified repeatable experiment that measures a reported performance issue. The experiment shall be sufficiently described such that a fellow colleague can reproduce the experiment. 3. A fully developed hypothesis of an observed performance issue based on the analysis of experimental data. 4. A """"""""performance improvement"""""""" change to the SDK is introduced based on the hypothesis, and a repeat of the experiment is performed to either prove or disprove the hypotheis. """"",title+description
TIMOB-13643,12,"""iOS: ImageView - Image changes its position on the screen if zoomScale is greater than 1""","""""""Image changes its position on the screen if orientation is changed. This is not regression since the issue occurs on 3.0.2 GA as well.    Steps to reproduce:  1. Create an app using the code below.  2. Launch the test app.Wait for the image to load.  3. Change Orientation to landscape and then again to portrait.    Actual:  The position of the image is changed on the screen.    Expected:  Position of the image should be same on the screen.    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: ImageView - Image changes its position on the screen if zoomScale is greater than 1
Description: """"Image changes its position on the screen if orientation is changed. This is not regression since the issue occurs on 3.0.2 GA as well. Steps to reproduce: 1. Create an app using the code below. 2. Launch the test app.Wait for the image to load. 3. Change Orientation to landscape and then again to portrait. Actual: The position of the image is changed on the screen. Expected: Position of the image should be same on the screen. """"",title+description
TIMOB-13642,12,"""Android: Remove need for leading slash '/' to load images""","""""""*Summary:*  The {{image}} and {{backgroundImage}} properties require a leading slash '/' on Android when loading an image file from the """"""""Resources"""""""" directory or else it will fail to load. iOS and Windows do not require the leading slash, although they will accept it too. Android should be changed to be consistent with iOS and Windows.    For example, if you have an image file in the following location in a Classic app...  {{./Resources/assets/images/Test.png}}    On Android, you have to load the above image file like this...      On iOS and Windows, you can load the image with or without the leading slash...      Changing Android to support both would make it less confusing for developers who work on the iOS side first.    *Note:*  This is not an issue when loading files via {{Ti.Filesystem.getFile()}}. Accessing files via a relative path works in this case. This is an issue with accessing files via relative paths (ie: no leading slash).    ----  *Original Bug Report:*  As can be seen here (http://developer.<USER>com/question/148590/alloy-android-not-getting-images) to load images on android using alloy we need to use a forward slash \(/\)  before the image name, I know it's not a big work for us, but we don't need it using default <USER>""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Remove need for leading slash '/' to load images
Description: """"*Summary:* The {{image}} and {{backgroundImage}} properties require a leading slash '/' on Android when loading an image file from the """"""""Resources"""""""" directory or else it will fail to load. iOS and Windows do not require the leading slash, although they will accept it too. Android should be changed to be consistent with iOS and Windows. For example, if you have an image file in the following location in a Classic app... {{./Resources/assets/images/Test.png}} On Android, you have to load the above image file like this... On iOS and Windows, you can load the image with or without the leading slash... Changing Android to support both would make it less confusing for developers who work on the iOS side first. *Note:* This is not an issue when loading files via {{Ti.Filesystem.getFile()}}. Accessing files via a relative path works in this case. This is an issue with accessing files via relative paths (ie: no leading slash). ---- *Original Bug Report:* As can be seen here (http://developer.<USER>com/question/148590/alloy-android-not-getting-images) to load images on android using alloy we need to use a forward slash \(/\) before the image name, I know it's not a big work for us, but we don't need it using default <USER>""""",title+description
TIMOB-13641,12,"""Set Orientation Modes for android""","""""""Could we have the possibility to set supported orientation modes for Android in an ios-like way? So we would not need to do this on the actual way, wich is no very good in terms of """"""""acceleration"""""""".        <android>          <orientations>              <orientation>Ti.UI.PORTRAIT</orientation>              <orientation>Ti.UI.UPSIDE_PORTRAIT</orientation>              <orientation>Ti.UI.LANDSCAPE_LEFT</orientation>              <orientation>Ti.UI.LANDSCAPE_RIGHT</orientation>          </orientations>      </android>""""""",Story,"Title: Set Orientation Modes for android
Description: """"Could we have the possibility to set supported orientation modes for Android in an ios-like way? So we would not need to do this on the actual way, wich is no very good in terms of """"""""acceleration"""""""". <android> <orientations> <orientation>Ti.UI.PORTRAIT</orientation> <orientation>Ti.UI.UPSIDE_PORTRAIT</orientation> <orientation>Ti.UI.LANDSCAPE_LEFT</orientation> <orientation>Ti.UI.LANDSCAPE_RIGHT</orientation> </orientations> </android>""""",title+description
