TIMOB-13444,12,"""TiAPI: getDirectoryListing used with compiled js files""","""""""using the code above you can test a difference in behavior in dev and production.  given the assets in attachment (unzip in your ressources dir), in development you will get a list of js files in the console.  In production you will get an empty list.    This is normal as the js are compiled and not really there. My question is could we get a behavior as with Titanium.Filesystem.File.exists which works on compiled js files (as shown is KitchenSink).    Thanks  """"""",New Feature,"Title: TiAPI: getDirectoryListing used with compiled js files
Description: """"using the code above you can test a difference in behavior in dev and production. given the assets in attachment (unzip in your ressources dir), in development you will get a list of js files in the console. In production you will get an empty list. This is normal as the js are compiled and not really there. My question is could we get a behavior as with Titanium.Filesystem.File.exists which works on compiled js files (as shown is KitchenSink). Thanks """"",title+description
TIMOB-13443,12,"""Android: Deprecate incrementServiceRefCount, decrementServiceRefCount and getServiceRefCount before removing them""","""""""this [commit](https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/48fd5c12f6f7b4941affd5ce8d6a0ab9f65113fc) broke backward compatibility of modules when using :  incrementServiceRefCount  decrementServiceRefCount  getServiceRefCount    Method names have changed without supporting deprecated. As a consequence a module built before will crash using a newer sdk and vice versa""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Deprecate incrementServiceRefCount, decrementServiceRefCount and getServiceRefCount before removing them
Description: """"this [commit](https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/48fd5c12f6f7b4941affd5ce8d6a0ab9f65113fc) broke backward compatibility of modules when using : incrementServiceRefCount decrementServiceRefCount getServiceRefCount Method names have changed without supporting deprecated. As a consequence a module built before will crash using a newer sdk and vice versa""""",title+description
