TIMOB-13400,12,"""BlackBerry: Titanium.Network.HTTPClient invalid url when using encodeURIComponent()""","""""""h2. Problem  For reading Google RSS feeds it is required to encode the second url part.  With iOS this is working as expected, with BlackBerry not.    I did some logging using Wireshark an it looks like that during GET the already encoded url gets encoded once more.  See logs below.    h3. actual behavior  wrong encoding during GET    h3. expected behavior  correct encoding    h2. Test case (app.js)      h2. Logs    h3. BlackBerry Titanium App log      h3. iOS Titanium App log      h3. BlackBerry Wireshark log      h3. iOS Wireshark log  """"""",Bug,"Title: BlackBerry: Titanium.Network.HTTPClient invalid url when using encodeURIComponent()
Description: """"h2. Problem For reading Google RSS feeds it is required to encode the second url part. With iOS this is working as expected, with BlackBerry not. I did some logging using Wireshark an it looks like that during GET the already encoded url gets encoded once more. See logs below. h3. actual behavior wrong encoding during GET h3. expected behavior correct encoding h2. Test case (app.js) h2. Logs h3. BlackBerry Titanium App log h3. iOS Titanium App log h3. BlackBerry Wireshark log h3. iOS Wireshark log """"",title+description
TIMOB-13399,12,"""iOS: UI.iPhone.NavigationGroup cropping navigation bar by 2 pixels""","""""""2 pixels are cropped off of the navigation bar when using UI.iPhone.NavigationGroup. Please see attached for expected outcome, achieved using UI.TabGroup, and the actual outcome, when using UI.iPhone.NavigationGroup.    *app.js: actual_navigationbar_implementation*      *app.js: expected_tabbar_implementation*  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: UI.iPhone.NavigationGroup cropping navigation bar by 2 pixels
Description: """"2 pixels are cropped off of the navigation bar when using UI.iPhone.NavigationGroup. Please see attached for expected outcome, achieved using UI.TabGroup, and the actual outcome, when using UI.iPhone.NavigationGroup. *app.js: actual_navigationbar_implementation* *app.js: expected_tabbar_implementation* """"",title+description
TIMOB-13397,12,"""iOS: Add ability to override audio routes""","""""""When using Titanium.Media.AUDIO_SESSION_MODE_PLAY_AND_RECORD, the audio output is routed to the receiver (the speaker in the earpiece of iPhone). This request would allow you to override this behavior to make the audio play out of the speaker (the speakerphone, located near the doc connector on iPhone).""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Add ability to override audio routes
Description: """"When using Titanium.Media.AUDIO_SESSION_MODE_PLAY_AND_RECORD, the audio output is routed to the receiver (the speaker in the earpiece of iPhone). This request would allow you to override this behavior to make the audio play out of the speaker (the speakerphone, located near the doc connector on iPhone).""""",title+description
TIMOB-13396,12,"""CLI: Improve adhoc system""","""""""Trying to improve my publishing system i realised a few quirks in CLI for ad-hoc release:  - ad-hoc uses deploytype production. it should use """"""""test"""""""". Why because it s the only way to difference a real production build (store) from adhoc build.For example in my tiapp.xml i mention that hockeystick(hockeyapp)and testflight modules are only present in test release. This is great cause it means i have those modules for adhoc builds, and i am still sure they wont be in my production builds!  - for deploytype """"""""test"""""""", js are compiled on ios and not on android.    With those too fixes i have a great publishing system:  -development: no compiled js, logs, no testflight/hockeystick  -test: compiled js (so same behavior as production!), logs, testflight/hockeystick  -production: compiled js, no logs, no testflight/hockeystick    I fixed that in my branch. if you agree with me i ll will create a pull request""""""",Epic,"Title: CLI: Improve adhoc system
Description: """"Trying to improve my publishing system i realised a few quirks in CLI for ad-hoc release: - ad-hoc uses deploytype production. it should use """"""""test"""""""". Why because it s the only way to difference a real production build (store) from adhoc build.For example in my tiapp.xml i mention that hockeystick(hockeyapp)and testflight modules are only present in test release. This is great cause it means i have those modules for adhoc builds, and i am still sure they wont be in my production builds! - for deploytype """"""""test"""""""", js are compiled on ios and not on android. With those too fixes i have a great publishing system: -development: no compiled js, logs, no testflight/hockeystick -test: compiled js (so same behavior as production!), logs, testflight/hockeystick -production: compiled js, no logs, no testflight/hockeystick I fixed that in my branch. if you agree with me i ll will create a pull request""""",title+description
TIMOB-13395,12,"""Android: native modules broken with android-ndk-r8e""","""""""Google made a change to their build system in NDK version r8e (released March 21st, 2013) which exposed a bug in support/module/android/generated/Android.mk    This will affect all customers who upgrade to NDK version r8e on any release of TIMOB going back to 2011-12-06. The fix is simple and I have already submitted a pull request.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: native modules broken with android-ndk-r8e
Description: """"Google made a change to their build system in NDK version r8e (released March 21st, 2013) which exposed a bug in support/module/android/generated/Android.mk This will affect all customers who upgrade to NDK version r8e on any release of TIMOB going back to 2011-12-06. The fix is simple and I have already submitted a pull request.""""",title+description
TIMOB-13394,12,"""CLI: add installed modules to ti info""","""""""i called 'ti info' to see the installed ti modules and was surprised to see no modules listed.    this is because 'ti info' does not currently list ti modules.    however, there is currently a command to list modules called 'ti modules'.    yea/nay for including ti modules in 'ti info'    yea: ti modules are included with the sdk, seems like basic environment, and this would make the information easily accessible for the larger community and troubleshooting    nay: this could add a lot of extra information, and 'ti modules' does the same thing""""""",New Feature,"Title: CLI: add installed modules to ti info
Description: """"i called 'ti info' to see the installed ti modules and was surprised to see no modules listed. this is because 'ti info' does not currently list ti modules. however, there is currently a command to list modules called 'ti modules'. yea/nay for including ti modules in 'ti info' yea: ti modules are included with the sdk, seems like basic environment, and this would make the information easily accessible for the larger community and troubleshooting nay: this could add a lot of extra information, and 'ti modules' does the same thing""""",title+description
