TIMOB-13138,12,"""Android: Ti.Filesystem.getFile().write() segfaults for large images loaded remotely""","""""""*Problem description*  When writing large images downloaded remotely, the test case below demonstrates a segfault when calling loadIcon.write().    *Steps to reproduce*  1. Run the test case, tap Cache  2. The app should segfault    *Test case*      *Workaround*  Move localIcon variable to onload scope and all images, large or small, load without segfaulting:        *Thoughts*  loadIcon may somehow be garbage collected by the time the onload event is called. Large images may be initiating the garbage collector to save memory. However, in theory this should not be the case. And according to the ECMA spec, the variable should still be valid within the scope of the onload event. This needs clarification.    *Logcat (Bundle ID: com.AppcTest)*      *Log extract provided by customer*  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Ti.Filesystem.getFile().write() segfaults for large images loaded remotely
Description: """"*Problem description* When writing large images downloaded remotely, the test case below demonstrates a segfault when calling loadIcon.write(). *Steps to reproduce* 1. Run the test case, tap Cache 2. The app should segfault *Test case* *Workaround* Move localIcon variable to onload scope and all images, large or small, load without segfaulting: *Thoughts* loadIcon may somehow be garbage collected by the time the onload event is called. Large images may be initiating the garbage collector to save memory. However, in theory this should not be the case. And according to the ECMA spec, the variable should still be valid within the scope of the onload event. This needs clarification. *Logcat (Bundle ID: com.AppcTest)* *Log extract provided by customer* """"",title+description
