TIMOB-12872,12,"""iOS: adding same rows array to multiple tables results in layout issues if rows contain other view like image etc.""","""""""My project requirements were to create multiple views, each containing a table. The first view contains all the data from all tables and other views contain their specific data only.*If you add another view to a row, the whole view appears only in one table and completely disappears in second table*. Below is the code for sample project i made, note that the properties icon is not visible in second view even when the data is same.    *app.js*      *ChildView.js*    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: adding same rows array to multiple tables results in layout issues if rows contain other view like image etc.
Description: """"My project requirements were to create multiple views, each containing a table. The first view contains all the data from all tables and other views contain their specific data only.*If you add another view to a row, the whole view appears only in one table and completely disappears in second table*. Below is the code for sample project i made, note that the properties icon is not visible in second view even when the data is same. *app.js* *ChildView.js* """"",title+description
TIMOB-12871,12,"""Map rightButton and TableView rightImage not scaling properly in Android""","""""""I am using images for buttons as well as map pin (rightButton) and TableView (rightImage). Only the button image is scaling properly on different resolution Androids. I even tried to proxy the image through an imageView using getImage() because I suspected that while buttons provide a means for setting height and width, the map and tableview images do not. This did not work.    A sample project and screen shots are provided.    The image is the same for the button, map pin rightButton and TableView rightImage. But, you can see that as the Android device screen resolution gets higher and higher, only the button image scales appropriately. The others do not (even though they are the same png file).""""""",Bug,"Title: Map rightButton and TableView rightImage not scaling properly in Android
Description: """"I am using images for buttons as well as map pin (rightButton) and TableView (rightImage). Only the button image is scaling properly on different resolution Androids. I even tried to proxy the image through an imageView using getImage() because I suspected that while buttons provide a means for setting height and width, the map and tableview images do not. This did not work. A sample project and screen shots are provided. The image is the same for the button, map pin rightButton and TableView rightImage. But, you can see that as the Android device screen resolution gets higher and higher, only the button image scales appropriately. The others do not (even though they are the same png file).""""",title+description
TIMOB-12870,12,"""mapsv2 Module: Setting """"user location"""" map property to """"true"""" after map creation sometimes requires two clicks on the """"locate"""" button to change to user location""","""""""While using the map test app, I toggled the """"""""user location"""""""" property on an existing map which caused the user location button and the user location indicator to appear on the map. The first click of this button did nothing. The second click took me to the user location.        Steps to reproduce:  1) Incorporate test code into your existing app with google maps api v2 key  2) Launch test app  3) If your location is on the map, move to another location  4) Click """"""""toggle user""""""""  5) Click the """"""""User location"""""""" target button in the upper-right portion of the screen    Result:  Nothing happens. A second click will take you to the location. In some cases, the first click will work.    Expected:  The user location button works on the first click.    Note:  I expect this may be an unusual use-case, with the more prevalent option being setting the property during map creation.""""""",Bug,"Title: mapsv2 Module: Setting """"user location"""" map property to """"true"""" after map creation sometimes requires two clicks on the """"locate"""" button to change to user location
Description: """"While using the map test app, I toggled the """"""""user location"""""""" property on an existing map which caused the user location button and the user location indicator to appear on the map. The first click of this button did nothing. The second click took me to the user location. Steps to reproduce: 1) Incorporate test code into your existing app with google maps api v2 key 2) Launch test app 3) If your location is on the map, move to another location 4) Click """"""""toggle user"""""""" 5) Click the """"""""User location"""""""" target button in the upper-right portion of the screen Result: Nothing happens. A second click will take you to the location. In some cases, the first click will work. Expected: The user location button works on the first click. Note: I expect this may be an unusual use-case, with the more prevalent option being setting the property during map creation.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12869,12,"""Ensure all AST modifications are pipelined resulting in a single source map file""","""""""As part of the deployment <USER> we consider a simple, common case: an Alloy application that has been minified.    We need to make sure that the resulting source map packaged with the app includes the results of both sets of transformations--first those made by Alloy, and then those made by build <USER>itself.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Ensure all AST modifications are pipelined resulting in a single source map file
Description: """"As part of the deployment <USER> we consider a simple, common case: an Alloy application that has been minified. We need to make sure that the resulting source map packaged with the app includes the results of both sets of transformations--first those made by Alloy, and then those made by build <USER>itself.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12868,12,"""Android: Update error reporting return original line numbers, not minified versions""","""""""As part of the Crittercism work, we exposed a way for users to get a stack trace of the current error. Minification destroys the line numbers. This ticket encompasses reading the source map to report the original line numbers, function and variable names as part of the error report.""""""",Sub-task,"Title: Android: Update error reporting return original line numbers, not minified versions
