TIMOB-12812,12,"""BlackBerry: Document orientationModes behaviors.""","""""""BlackBerry has some special behaviors for orientationModes  we need to document so our developers are aware of them.    h3. No orientation direction restrictions supported.  BlackBerry will not support restricting an orientation to a specific  direction. For example if you only set the modes to LANDSCAPE_LEFT, it  will NOT prevent LANDSCAPE_RIGHT orientation changes. If either  LANDSCAPE_LEFT or LANDSCAPE_RIGHT are specified full landscape orientation  changes will be allowed.    h3. Not all orientation directions are allowed.  BlackBerry will refuse to allow some orientation directions from occurring.  For example on a phone form factor the device will never rotate the UI  to be facing the south direction (UPSIDE_PORTRAIT). Something similar may  happen on tablet form factors, but it may be a different direction. We should  verify the behavior on tablet hardware.    h3. New LANDSCAPE constant.  BlackBerry adds the LANDSCAPE constant. This is used to enable  full landscape orientation support. This is the same as using  both LANDSCAPE_LEFT and LANDSCAPE_RIGHT constants.    h3. Empty orientationModes array behavior.  If the array is left empty the default behavior will be restored.  This behavior is defined by what orientation setting are used in the  application manifest. See the [OrientationSupport|http://developer.blackberry.com/cascades/reference/bb__cascades__orientationsupport.html] guide for more details.""""""",Story,"Title: BlackBerry: Document orientationModes behaviors.
Description: """"BlackBerry has some special behaviors for orientationModes we need to document so our developers are aware of them. h3. No orientation direction restrictions supported. BlackBerry will not support restricting an orientation to a specific direction. For example if you only set the modes to LANDSCAPE_LEFT, it will NOT prevent LANDSCAPE_RIGHT orientation changes. If either LANDSCAPE_LEFT or LANDSCAPE_RIGHT are specified full landscape orientation changes will be allowed. h3. Not all orientation directions are allowed. BlackBerry will refuse to allow some orientation directions from occurring. For example on a phone form factor the device will never rotate the UI to be facing the south direction (UPSIDE_PORTRAIT). Something similar may happen on tablet form factors, but it may be a different direction. We should verify the behavior on tablet hardware. h3. New LANDSCAPE constant. BlackBerry adds the LANDSCAPE constant. This is used to enable full landscape orientation support. This is the same as using both LANDSCAPE_LEFT and LANDSCAPE_RIGHT constants. h3. Empty orientationModes array behavior. If the array is left empty the default behavior will be restored. This behavior is defined by what orientation setting are used in the application manifest. See the [OrientationSupport|http://developer.blackberry.com/cascades/reference/bb__cascades__orientationsupport.html] guide for more details.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12810,12,"""Android: Maps V2 Module - Missing properties and methods from Ti.Map module""","""""""The following API items are not accessible by the Google Maps v2 module, but are available with the Ti.Map module.    The View Class is missing the setLocation and zoom methods.  * http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.Map.View-method-setLocation  * http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.Map.View-method-zoom    The Annotation class is missing the leftButton, leftView, rightButton, rightView, subtitleid and titleid properties.    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Maps V2 Module - Missing properties and methods from Ti.Map module
Description: """"The following API items are not accessible by the Google Maps v2 module, but are available with the Ti.Map module. The View Class is missing the setLocation and zoom methods. * http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.Map.View-method-setLocation * http://docs.<USER>com/titanium/latest/#!/api/Titanium.Map.View-method-zoom The Annotation class is missing the leftButton, leftView, rightButton, rightView, subtitleid and titleid properties. """"",title+description
TIMOB-12809,12,"""CLI: iOS log output gets munged""","""""""If an app in the iOS Simulator throws an error that contains the word """"""""backtrace"""""""", it will munge the output and the remaining lines are printed as """"""""debug"""""""" instead of """"""""error"""""""".""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: iOS log output gets munged
Description: """"If an app in the iOS Simulator throws an error that contains the word """"""""backtrace"""""""", it will munge the output and the remaining lines are printed as """"""""debug"""""""" instead of """"""""error"""""""".""""",title+description
TIMOB-12806,12,"""I need to convert a crash report into a Titanium stack trace""","""""""h6.Rationale  Having an app that extensively uses dynamic data, coupled with location-specific events, it is very hard to exactly reproduce errors that occur in the application. It is absolutely necessary for there to be a global onerror event that can be accessed in a production environment, that can grab the error that occurred and report it back along with the stack.    h6.Problem  Crash reports did get through with no JS nor Ti references. _Non native developers_ wouldn't be able to catch up such errors in a timely fashion.    h6.Use case  A *NSRangeException* from a Crittercism crash report, is being thrown multiple times on production environment:      h6.Concept  A global onerror event that can be accessed in a production environment will allow to figure out root cause problem quicker.    With a app-wide error event, We would like to retrieve:    1. the file that the error occurred in (including path)  2. the line number of the error  3. the error that was thrown  4. the stack trace that lead to the error    h6.Proposed Ti equivalent      It seems trivial to add such a hook to the titanium SDK, as in development, a red window appears showing the error. Whatever event that is hooking into needs to be exposed to the developer as well.""""""",New Feature,"Title: I need to convert a crash report into a Titanium stack trace
Description: """"h6.Rationale Having an app that extensively uses dynamic data, coupled with location-specific events, it is very hard to exactly reproduce errors that occur in the application. It is absolutely necessary for there to be a global onerror event that can be accessed in a production environment, that can grab the error that occurred and report it back along with the stack. h6.Problem Crash reports did get through with no JS nor Ti references. _Non native developers_ wouldn't be able to catch up such errors in a timely fashion. h6.Use case A *NSRangeException* from a Crittercism crash report, is being thrown multiple times on production environment: h6.Concept A global onerror event that can be accessed in a production environment will allow to figure out root cause problem quicker. With a app-wide error event, We would like to retrieve: 1. the file that the error occurred in (including path) 2. the line number of the error 3. the error that was thrown 4. the stack trace that lead to the error h6.Proposed Ti equivalent It seems trivial to add such a hook to the titanium SDK, as in development, a red window appears showing the error. Whatever event that is hooking into needs to be exposed to the developer as well.""""",title+description
