TIMOB-12625,12,"""iOS: TableView.updateSection argument order is wrong""","""""""The TableView updateSection method on iOS has the arguments in the wrong order. Unlike the other platforms (and updateRow), it expects the first argument to be a section object and the second argument to be an index.    The following code runs on Android and Mobile Web, but fails on iOS.      """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: TableView.updateSection argument order is wrong
Description: """"The TableView updateSection method on iOS has the arguments in the wrong order. Unlike the other platforms (and updateRow), it expects the first argument to be a section object and the second argument to be an index. The following code runs on Android and Mobile Web, but fails on iOS. """"",title+description
TIMOB-12623,12,"""iOS: Automatically embed images in Resources/iphone into Xcode project""","""""""*Problem description*  Currently whenever I want to package up an enterprise or app store version of an ipa, I have to open the xcodeproj file in build/iphone in Xcode and edit certain values: release build, target universal device, armv7, provisioning profile, etc... This is reasonable because everyone will have different preferences for these values.    However, the most annoying thing is that I have to drag & drop all of the iTunes images from Resources/iphone into xCode to set the app icons and splash screens. These images have standardized names (see attached screenshot) so Titanium should be smart enough to auto-embed them into the project so that we don't have to drag & drop them in every single time.    A rebuttal may be that you only need to set them once and then never delete the xcodeproj file, however doing a clean project or touching tiapp.xml erases the changes and regenerates the xcodeproj file so this is not practical, especially since cleaning project is a must in many situations such as updating the Ti SDK version or updating a jss file.""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Automatically embed images in Resources/iphone into Xcode project
Description: """"*Problem description* Currently whenever I want to package up an enterprise or app store version of an ipa, I have to open the xcodeproj file in build/iphone in Xcode and edit certain values: release build, target universal device, armv7, provisioning profile, etc... This is reasonable because everyone will have different preferences for these values. However, the most annoying thing is that I have to drag & drop all of the iTunes images from Resources/iphone into xCode to set the app icons and splash screens. These images have standardized names (see attached screenshot) so Titanium should be smart enough to auto-embed them into the project so that we don't have to drag & drop them in every single time. A rebuttal may be that you only need to set them once and then never delete the xcodeproj file, however doing a clean project or touching tiapp.xml erases the changes and regenerates the xcodeproj file so this is not practical, especially since cleaning project is a must in many situations such as updating the Ti SDK version or updating a jss file.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12621,12,"""BlackBerry 10.1 Beta : The window & controls in kitchen sink do not adjust themselves to the BB 10.1 beta simulator screen size""","""""""Description:  ------------  1.Build & run KS (given by pedro or josh) on BB 10.1 beta simulator  2.Goto any test  3.Tap & open the test    Actual Result:  --------------  1.The test appear cut as the window & controls do not self adjust to the BB 10.1 simulator screen size    Expected Result:  ----------------  1.The window & the controls should self adjust to the screen size    Attached are the screen shots showing the difference between BB 10 & BB 10.1 beta""""""",Bug,"Title: BlackBerry 10.1 Beta : The window & controls in kitchen sink do not adjust themselves to the BB 10.1 beta simulator screen size
Description: """"Description: ------------ 1.Build & run KS (given by pedro or josh) on BB 10.1 beta simulator 2.Goto any test 3.Tap & open the test Actual Result: -------------- 1.The test appear cut as the window & controls do not self adjust to the BB 10.1 simulator screen size Expected Result: ---------------- 1.The window & the controls should self adjust to the screen size Attached are the screen shots showing the difference between BB 10 & BB 10.1 beta""""",title+description
