Description: """"When running """"""""scons"""""""", it errors if there's no blackberry folder: """"",title+description
TIMOB-12511,12,"""BlackBerry: All modules should support event listeners.""","""""""Developers should be able to add and fire events on module objects (ex: API, App).    h3. Acceptance Test      1. Run the test case.  2. You should see events fire and log the module from which they     came from in the logs.  3. You should also see a 'foo' event logged which was fired from the button.  4. Try clicking the button and you will see """"""""click!"""""""" printed to confirm native events still work.""""""",Bug,"Title: BlackBerry: All modules should support event listeners.
Description: """"Developers should be able to add and fire events on module objects (ex: API, App). h3. Acceptance Test 1. Run the test case. 2. You should see events fire and log the module from which they came from in the logs. 3. You should also see a 'foo' event logged which was fired from the button. 4. Try clicking the button and you will see """"""""click!"""""""" printed to confirm native events still work.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12510,12,"""BlackBerry: JavaScript exceptions are not always reported properly.""","""""""If a JavaScript exception is thrown from a module (ex: require()) the  exception is not reported correctly (wrong filename) and no stack is provided.  The goal of this ticket is to provide a better report of such exceptions.    h3. Acceptance Test    1. Create a project that contains your main code (app.js)     which loads a module using require (ex: require('foo')).  2. Write a module (ex: foo.js) that causes a runtime error.  3. Run the program.  4. Confirm in the output from stderr the line on which the exception     was thrown and a stack trace is printed. Confirm filename and location is correct.  5. Edit the module to replace the runtime error with a syntax error.  6. Run the program again.  7. Confirm the line at which the syntax error happened was printed along with a stack     that provides the location at which the script was compiled (ex: require() called).""""""",Bug,"Title: BlackBerry: JavaScript exceptions are not always reported properly.
Description: """"If a JavaScript exception is thrown from a module (ex: require()) the exception is not reported correctly (wrong filename) and no stack is provided. The goal of this ticket is to provide a better report of such exceptions. h3. Acceptance Test 1. Create a project that contains your main code (app.js) which loads a module using require (ex: require('foo')). 2. Write a module (ex: foo.js) that causes a runtime error. 3. Run the program. 4. Confirm in the output from stderr the line on which the exception was thrown and a stack trace is printed. Confirm filename and location is correct. 5. Edit the module to replace the runtime error with a syntax error. 6. Run the program again. 7. Confirm the line at which the syntax error happened was printed along with a stack that provides the location at which the script was compiled (ex: require() called).""""",title+description
TIMOB-12509,12,"""TiAPI: Power management - Keep Screen On""","""""""Developers may sometimes require the ability to prevent a device  from powering off its display and/or CPU during certain periods  of time while their application performs some task.    Most of our native platforms provide an API for these use cases.    Android for example has WakeLock which provides various levels which  affect the device's power management. You may also set a flag at the View  which will keep the screen on (we already expose this in Titanium).    BlackBerry provides an """"""""idle mode"""""""" flag on the main window which has  two modes: Normal and KeepAwake.    The goal of this ticket is to come up with some common API  that we may expose to our developers which is suitable for the  various platform which are capable of this feature.""""""",New Feature,"Title: TiAPI: Power management - Keep Screen On
Description: """"Developers may sometimes require the ability to prevent a device from powering off its display and/or CPU during certain periods of time while their application performs some task. Most of our native platforms provide an API for these use cases. Android for example has WakeLock which provides various levels which affect the device's power management. You may also set a flag at the View which will keep the screen on (we already expose this in Titanium). BlackBerry provides an """"""""idle mode"""""""" flag on the main window which has two modes: Normal and KeepAwake. The goal of this ticket is to come up with some common API that we may expose to our developers which is suitable for the various platform which are capable of this feature.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12508,12,"""BlackBerry: The tabs in the default app in studio are showing as half windows""","""""""Description:  1.Make a default app for BB in studio.  2.Set one of the tab window color as '#000' ie.black  3.Run the app on device or simulator.  4.Observe the app after it launches.    Actual Results:  1.The windows appear half colored.    Expected Result:  1.The window should appear fully colored or might be shifted to right.    Screenshot attached for reference. """"""",Bug,"Title: BlackBerry: The tabs in the default app in studio are showing as half windows
Description: """"Description: 1.Make a default app for BB in studio. 2.Set one of the tab window color as '#000' ie.black 3.Run the app on device or simulator. 4.Observe the app after it launches. Actual Results: 1.The windows appear half colored. Expected Result: 1.The window should appear fully colored or might be shifted to right. Screenshot attached for reference. """"",title+description
TIMOB-12507,12,"""Backport: CLI: Build fails setting tiapp.xml SDK version to 3.0.0GA and using 3.0.2 as CLI SDK ""","""""""h5. Problem  Build fails setting tiapp.xml SDK version to 3.0.0GA and using 3.0.2 as CLI SDK when building from terminal using CLI    h5. Steps to reproduce  - Set up a project tiapp.xml SDK to 3.0.0 GA  - Set SDK from CLI to latest 3.0.2 version (3.0.2.v20130129171611)  - cd to the project and run the build.   Tested with:  $ ti build  $ ti build -p ios  $ ti build -p ios --sdk 3.0.0.GA    Result: build fails.    Log:        Building from Studio works fine as expected.""""""",Sub-task,"Title: Backport: CLI: Build fails setting tiapp.xml SDK version to 3.0.0GA and using 3.0.2 as CLI SDK
Description: """"h5. Problem Build fails setting tiapp.xml SDK version to 3.0.0GA and using 3.0.2 as CLI SDK when building from terminal using CLI h5. Steps to reproduce - Set up a project tiapp.xml SDK to 3.0.0 GA - Set SDK from CLI to latest 3.0.2 version (3.0.2.v20130129171611) - cd to the project and run the build. Tested with: $ ti build $ ti build -p ios $ ti build -p ios --sdk 3.0.0.GA Result: build fails. Log: Building from Studio works fine as expected.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12506,12,"""Builds in CI are combined together for a particular day even though the build version is different.""","""""""The builds on the CI page in the master branch are seen under the same day even though the build versions & the time stamp are different.  Normally,we should have a different section for a new build version even though they are pushed the same day.    Screenshots attached for the same.  If you see in the screenshot.The build in master for 1/30/13 has 3 builds with different version & time stamp.""""""",Bug,"Title: Builds in CI are combined together for a particular day even though the build version is different.
