TIMOB-12451,12,"""NavigationBar on iOS has a height of only 43dp""","""""""h1. Problem description    The height of the NavigationBar seems to be only 43dp instead of 44dp.    h1. Steps to reproduce    1. Create an Alloy app with the follwing index.xml:        2. Run the code and check the height of the bar (e.g. using a screenshot); it is 43px and not 44 as stated in Apple's docs.    h1. More info  See also http://developer.<USER>com/question/147150/alloy---navbar-height-is-wrong    When the TabBar is also visible, the NavigationBar has the correct height of 44dp.""""""",Bug,"Title: NavigationBar on iOS has a height of only 43dp
Description: """"h1. Problem description The height of the NavigationBar seems to be only 43dp instead of 44dp. h1. Steps to reproduce 1. Create an Alloy app with the follwing index.xml: 2. Run the code and check the height of the bar (e.g. using a screenshot); it is 43px and not 44 as stated in Apple's docs. h1. More info See also http://developer.<USER>com/question/147150/alloy---navbar-height-is-wrong When the TabBar is also visible, the NavigationBar has the correct height of 44dp.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12450,12,"""Android: TableViewRow with className crash when adding a view and scrolling""","""""""h1. Problem description    Adding a view to a row on the fly, if the row has className enabled, results in a crash as soon as the table scrolls.    h1. Comments    Lets say you want to implement a menu on row. The """"""""lighter"""""""" way is to add the menu when necessary and remove it as soon as possible.    If you are using the 'className' property of the row then a crash occurs while scrolling (reuse of a cell).    If you remove the className property in the code below, then it works.    In more complex code of mine I can see that the reuse works perfectly without className. So using className should work as well (even better).    h1. Steps to reproduce    Use the following code; click on a row, then scroll down to see the crash.        The error reported:    E/TiApplication( 4545): java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: length=0; index=0  E/TiApplication( 4545):  at ti.modules.titanium.ui.widget.tableview.TiTableViewRowProxyItem.applyChildProperties(TiTableViewRowProxyItem.java:176)  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: TableViewRow with className crash when adding a view and scrolling
Description: """"h1. Problem description Adding a view to a row on the fly, if the row has className enabled, results in a crash as soon as the table scrolls. h1. Comments Lets say you want to implement a menu on row. The """"""""lighter"""""""" way is to add the menu when necessary and remove it as soon as possible. If you are using the 'className' property of the row then a crash occurs while scrolling (reuse of a cell). If you remove the className property in the code below, then it works. In more complex code of mine I can see that the reuse works perfectly without className. So using className should work as well (even better). h1. Steps to reproduce Use the following code; click on a row, then scroll down to see the crash. The error reported: E/TiApplication( 4545): java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: length=0; index=0 E/TiApplication( 4545): at ti.modules.titanium.ui.widget.tableview.TiTableViewRowProxyItem.applyChildProperties(TiTableViewRowProxyItem.java:176) """"",title+description
