TIMOB-12432,12,"""iOS: Transport.py - Add the ability to re-create the iphone in a similar way as Titanium Studio does""","""""""h6.Feature Request  Add the ability to _transport.py_ to be """"""""smart"""""""" enough to re-create the iphone in a similar way as Titanium Studio does.    - """"""""iphone/transport.py""""""""        When you run clean in Titanium Studio (Project -> Clean... -> Clean All Projects for instance) it deletes the iphone directory under build.      - If project has no iphone it won't migrate resources:        - Build Titanium project to get it to work (iphone build present).     """"""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Transport.py - Add the ability to re-create the iphone in a similar way as Titanium Studio does
Description: """"h6.Feature Request Add the ability to _transport.py_ to be """"""""smart"""""""" enough to re-create the iphone in a similar way as Titanium Studio does. - """"""""iphone/transport.py"""""""" When you run clean in Titanium Studio (Project -> Clean... -> Clean All Projects for instance) it deletes the iphone directory under build. - If project has no iphone it won't migrate resources: - Build Titanium project to get it to work (iphone build present). """"",title+description
TIMOB-12430,12,"""Android images at multiple resolutions are selected in wrong order""","""""""If you have the following images:    Resources/images/test.png  Resources/android/images/test.png  Resources/android/images/res-notlong-port-mdpi/test.png    and somewhere in your code you assign:    backgroundImage : '/images/test.png'    and run it on a res-notlong-port-mdpi device (the emulator at 320x480 is such a device) it would be natural if:    DESIRED BEHAVIOUR  ================    The image selected was the one from the specific platform and resolution (Resources/android/images/res-notlong-port-mdpi/test.png)    If no platform/resolution specific image is found - look for a platform specific: (Resources/android/images/test.png)    If no platform specific image is found, look for the generic image (Resources/images/test.png)    Instead you get this:    ACTUAL BEHAVIOUR  ================    Is there a generic image (Resources/images/test.png) ?    If so use it, and abort further search!      CONCLUSION  ==========    The system ends up preferring images that was NOT meant for the particular device, even though better images are present, meaning you have to code around it and have an elaborate """"""""which platform and resolution"""""""" logic in the appcode.     It is the exact same reversal of logic with 9-patch images. if you have test.png and test.9.png - Ti will always use test.png.    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android images at multiple resolutions are selected in wrong order
Description: """"If you have the following images: Resources/images/test.png Resources/android/images/test.png Resources/android/images/res-notlong-port-mdpi/test.png and somewhere in your code you assign: backgroundImage : '/images/test.png' and run it on a res-notlong-port-mdpi device (the emulator at 320x480 is such a device) it would be natural if: DESIRED BEHAVIOUR ================ The image selected was the one from the specific platform and resolution (Resources/android/images/res-notlong-port-mdpi/test.png) If no platform/resolution specific image is found - look for a platform specific: (Resources/android/images/test.png) If no platform specific image is found, look for the generic image (Resources/images/test.png) Instead you get this: ACTUAL BEHAVIOUR ================ Is there a generic image (Resources/images/test.png) ? If so use it, and abort further search! CONCLUSION ========== The system ends up preferring images that was NOT meant for the particular device, even though better images are present, meaning you have to code around it and have an elaborate """"""""which platform and resolution"""""""" logic in the appcode. It is the exact same reversal of logic with 9-patch images. if you have test.png and test.9.png - Ti will always use test.png. """"",title+description
TIMOB-12427,12,"""Mobile Web: Support the property """"search"""" in Ti.UI.TableView""","""""""Add support of the property """"""""search"""""""" in Ti.UI.TableView.    (Note: this is implemented in the Tizen repo; a pull request for Mobile Web will be issued shortly.)""""""",New Feature,"Title: Mobile Web: Support the property """"search"""" in Ti.UI.TableView
Description: """"Add support of the property """"""""search"""""""" in Ti.UI.TableView. (Note: this is implemented in the Tizen repo; a pull request for Mobile Web will be issued shortly.)""""",title+description
TIMOB-12426,12,"""MobileWeb: Implement removeAllChildren on Views""","""""""Implement the removeAllChildren on viewProxy . iOS and Android where implement through http://jira.<USER>org/browse/TC-1193. Test case is also in that ticket.     When updating documentation to show the new platform support, note that there are several exceptions to the existing support for removeAllChildren, as documented here: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/3411/files    Some views (such as SearchBar) do not advertise support for add(), so they do not need to support removeAllChildren(). In other cases (such as Window), the exceptions are purely a result of the existing implementation. If the implementation on MW/BB differs from the implementation on Android/iOS, the appropriate docs should be updated as well. For example, removing removeAllChildren from the {{excludes:}} list, and adding a minimal definition to the {{methods:}} list, including the method name and platforms field (everything else can be inherited). i.e.:      """"""",New Feature,"Title: MobileWeb: Implement removeAllChildren on Views
Description: """"Implement the removeAllChildren on viewProxy . iOS and Android where implement through http://jira.<USER>org/browse/TC-1193. Test case is also in that ticket. When updating documentation to show the new platform support, note that there are several exceptions to the existing support for removeAllChildren, as documented here: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/3411/files Some views (such as SearchBar) do not advertise support for add(), so they do not need to support removeAllChildren(). In other cases (such as Window), the exceptions are purely a result of the existing implementation. If the implementation on MW/BB differs from the implementation on Android/iOS, the appropriate docs should be updated as well. For example, removing removeAllChildren from the {{excludes:}} list, and adding a minimal definition to the {{methods:}} list, including the method name and platforms field (everything else can be inherited). i.e.: """"",title+description
TIMOB-12425,12,"""Android: WebView: the """"load"""" event is fired twice if setHtml() is called before the window opens""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  1. Run the code attached below.  2. Two alerts show which means the """"""""load"""""""" event is fired twice. Expected behavior: load should be only fired once.  Note:   i) if commenting out webview.setHtml(""""""""xxx"""""""") but use webview.html=""""""""xxx"""""""", the """"""""load"""""""" event will be only fired once.  ii) if running on Galaxy Nexus (4.0.4), a warning shows in the console, like  {quote}  W/webview ( 2264): java.lang.Throwable: Warning: A WebView method was called on thread 'KrollRuntimeThread'. All WebView methods must be called on the UI thread. Future versions of WebView may not support use on other threads.  {quote}  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: WebView: the """"load"""" event is fired twice if setHtml() is called before the window opens
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Run the code attached below. 2. Two alerts show which means the """"""""load"""""""" event is fired twice. Expected behavior: load should be only fired once. Note: i) if commenting out webview.setHtml(""""""""xxx"""""""") but use webview.html=""""""""xxx"""""""", the """"""""load"""""""" event will be only fired once. ii) if running on Galaxy Nexus (4.0.4), a warning shows in the console, like {quote} W/webview ( 2264): java.lang.Throwable: Warning: A WebView method was called on thread 'KrollRuntimeThread'. All WebView methods must be called on the UI thread. Future versions of WebView may not support use on other threads. {quote} """"",title+description
TIMOB-12424,12,"""Android: View can't be added to tableViewRow issue""","""""""h6.Issue  If trying to add a view to a tableviewrow after view creation, then nothing happen. Which would also mean a potential memory leak    Use the testcase, click on row. A view should be added nothing happens. If you change and the view to row.view, then it works    h6.Tested on  Tested on physical device(Galaxy S2 - 4.2) and Android simulator 4.0    h6.Repro sequence  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: View can't be added to tableViewRow issue
Description: """"h6.Issue If trying to add a view to a tableviewrow after view creation, then nothing happen. Which would also mean a potential memory leak Use the testcase, click on row. A view should be added nothing happens. If you change and the view to row.view, then it works h6.Tested on Tested on physical device(Galaxy S2 - 4.2) and Android simulator 4.0 h6.Repro sequence """"",title+description
TIMOB-12423,12,"""CLI: Switch authentication library to use request module""","""""""The auth library in node-appc uses node's built-in http client which does not support proxy servers. We need to rip out node's http request library and use the """"""""request"""""""" module like we do everywhere else.    Users in the Q&A are having problems logging in: http://developer.<USER>com/question/146837/unable-to-login-with-titanium-cli-version-3022.""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Switch authentication library to use request module
Description: """"The auth library in node-appc uses node's built-in http client which does not support proxy servers. We need to rip out node's http request library and use the """"""""request"""""""" module like we do everywhere else. Users in the Q&A are having problems logging in: http://developer.<USER>com/question/146837/unable-to-login-with-titanium-cli-version-3022.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12422,12,"""iOS build using deploy-type parameter does not exclude/include modules""","""""""The deploy-type parameter is enabled for iOS, but it doesn't perform as expected.     Steps:  1. Create titanium project  2. Add ti.cloud module  3. Update the app.js with the following code:     4. Set deploy-type in the tiapp.xml for ti.cloud to be """"""""development""""""""  5. Perform a build using deploy-type=""""""""test""""""""      Expected results:  App launch should fail because the ti.cloud module was not loaded since it wasn't present    Actual results:  ti.cloud module is present, app loads""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS build using deploy-type parameter does not exclude/include modules
Description: """"The deploy-type parameter is enabled for iOS, but it doesn't perform as expected. Steps: 1. Create titanium project 2. Add ti.cloud module 3. Update the app.js with the following code: 4. Set deploy-type in the tiapp.xml for ti.cloud to be """"""""development"""""""" 5. Perform a build using deploy-type=""""""""test"""""""" Expected results: App launch should fail because the ti.cloud module was not loaded since it wasn't present Actual results: ti.cloud module is present, app loads""""",title+description
TIMOB-12421,12,"""iOS: Horizontal Wrap: Ti.UI.FILL doesn't seem to be used correctly""","""""""h6.Problem  Using the 'horizontal' layout with the horizontalWrap to false.    h6.Expected behavior  Allow to create auto resizing views. Important part of it is using Ti.UI.FILL layout.    _An use case_, for example if you have  tableviewrow with an image on the left a label in the middle and an optional button on the right, you can easily layout the whole of it.    _Yet there's one catch_, when a view layouts its children, if a child has a Ti.UI.FILL width, it doesnt take in account the """"""""next"""""""" siblings. It just takes the remaning width. Consequently and """"""""next"""""""" child will be out of the view.    Views with Ti.UI.FILL should be seen as """"""""spacer"""""""" and take as much width as possible but computed last! This is the behavior of spacers in toolbars.    h6.Working sample  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Horizontal Wrap: Ti.UI.FILL doesn't seem to be used correctly
Description: """"h6.Problem Using the 'horizontal' layout with the horizontalWrap to false. h6.Expected behavior Allow to create auto resizing views. Important part of it is using Ti.UI.FILL layout. _An use case_, for example if you have tableviewrow with an image on the left a label in the middle and an optional button on the right, you can easily layout the whole of it. _Yet there's one catch_, when a view layouts its children, if a child has a Ti.UI.FILL width, it doesnt take in account the """"""""next"""""""" siblings. It just takes the remaning width. Consequently and """"""""next"""""""" child will be out of the view. Views with Ti.UI.FILL should be seen as """"""""spacer"""""""" and take as much width as possible but computed last! This is the behavior of spacers in toolbars. h6.Working sample """"",title+description
TIMOB-12420,12,"""CLI: Change iOS logging calls from debug() to info() on full rebuilds""","""""""Currently all the logging calls in the {{checkIfShouldForceRebuild()}} function in ios's \_build.js module are debug() calls. I feel that a forced rebuild has a large enough impact on build time that it merits listing the output as an info() call, so that developers will see it by default, rather than only when they increase their log level to """"""""debug"""""""". Clarity on these messages can significantly improve the user experience as they provide good, actionable information.  """"""",Story,"Title: CLI: Change iOS logging calls from debug() to info() on full rebuilds
Description: """"Currently all the logging calls in the {{checkIfShouldForceRebuild()}} function in ios's \_build.js module are debug() calls. I feel that a forced rebuild has a large enough impact on build time that it merits listing the output as an info() call, so that developers will see it by default, rather than only when they increase their log level to """"""""debug"""""""". Clarity on these messages can significantly improve the user experience as they provide good, actionable information. """"",title+description
TIMOB-12419,12,"""CLI: Provide option for full simulator builds""","""""""By default Ti speeds up simulator builds by sym linking and not pre-compiling js, but for uploads to systems such as pieceable a """"""""full"""""""" non-symlinked .app build is required.    Pieceable provided some patches for SDK 1.8 and 2.0. See: https://github.com/pieceable/titanium-patches but none since.    I think it would be a useful option anyway and would avoid having to patch.    Please consider adding a """"""""buildfull"""""""" option to the ti cmd line build options.      """"""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Provide option for full simulator builds
Description: """"By default Ti speeds up simulator builds by sym linking and not pre-compiling js, but for uploads to systems such as pieceable a """"""""full"""""""" non-symlinked .app build is required. Pieceable provided some patches for SDK 1.8 and 2.0. See: https://github.com/pieceable/titanium-patches but none since. I think it would be a useful option anyway and would avoid having to patch. Please consider adding a """"""""buildfull"""""""" option to the ti cmd line build options. """"",title+description
