TIMOB-12324,12,"""iOS: improve RTE for addEventListener with one arg""","""""""iOS: improve RTE for addEventListener with one arg.    If only one arg is passed to addEventListener, the iOS RTE does not indicate the nature of the problem:        Test Code:        The Android Error message is informative:        The iOS Error message for an undefined callback is informative:        Undefined callback test code:        MobileWeb has no RTE: TIMOB-10412    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Run code:        Actual Result:    Index out of bounds runtime error.    Expected Result:    RTE indicating wrong number of args, or unexpected arg type.""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: improve RTE for addEventListener with one arg
Description: """"iOS: improve RTE for addEventListener with one arg. If only one arg is passed to addEventListener, the iOS RTE does not indicate the nature of the problem: Test Code: The Android Error message is informative: The iOS Error message for an undefined callback is informative: Undefined callback test code: MobileWeb has no RTE: TIMOB-10412 Steps to Reproduce: 1. Run code: Actual Result: Index out of bounds runtime error. Expected Result: RTE indicating wrong number of args, or unexpected arg type.""""",title+description
TIMOB-12323,12,"""Android: JellyBean (4.1.1) createImageView() with image from camera/gallery shows shrunk image. Pictures taken in portrait mode are autorotated as well. ""","""""""When returning an image from the Camera (took a picture) or from the gallery, the aspect ratio of the image is skewed when placed in an image view. The code attached works as expected (no stretch or shrink of the image) on every android version except for 4.1.1. The customer wants to know why his code breaks in only 4.1.1. The small hackaround I found was to manually resize the imageView to be to the exact proportions of the phones camera resolution.     Line 278 is (one of) the method in question. The image property is grabbing the image but not with the right aspect ratio it seems.    Also when an image is taken in portrait mode, the photo is rotated when applied to the image view. This does not happen in any other android version. At first I thought it was this issue: https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-3887    But after testing the testcase on device, that problem seems to have been fixed and this is a new issue.    Steps to reproduce:    1. Click on the black imageView  2. Either choose take picture or select from gallery when options dialogue appears.  3. Take picture in landscape mode.   4. Notice shrinking effect of image inside imageView      To see rotating issue:  1. Do steps 1 & 2  2. Take picture in portrait mode  3. Notice shrink affect as well as rotated image in imageView    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: JellyBean (4.1.1) createImageView() with image from camera/gallery shows shrunk image. Pictures taken in portrait mode are autorotated as well.
Description: """"When returning an image from the Camera (took a picture) or from the gallery, the aspect ratio of the image is skewed when placed in an image view. The code attached works as expected (no stretch or shrink of the image) on every android version except for 4.1.1. The customer wants to know why his code breaks in only 4.1.1. The small hackaround I found was to manually resize the imageView to be to the exact proportions of the phones camera resolution. Line 278 is (one of) the method in question. The image property is grabbing the image but not with the right aspect ratio it seems. Also when an image is taken in portrait mode, the photo is rotated when applied to the image view. This does not happen in any other android version. At first I thought it was this issue: https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-3887 But after testing the testcase on device, that problem seems to have been fixed and this is a new issue. Steps to reproduce: 1. Click on the black imageView 2. Either choose take picture or select from gallery when options dialogue appears. 3. Take picture in landscape mode. 4. Notice shrinking effect of image inside imageView To see rotating issue: 1. Do steps 1 & 2 2. Take picture in portrait mode 3. Notice shrink affect as well as rotated image in imageView """"",title+description
TIMOB-12322,12,"""MobileWeb: Clean up layouts to not reference parents""","""""""Parent nodes are passed in to mobile web layouts, but it is completely unecessary. Change to children. This is important because it makes the algorithm a lot more readable, which is becoming more and more important since the polynomial algorithm is starting to be ported""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Clean up layouts to not reference parents
Description: """"Parent nodes are passed in to mobile web layouts, but it is completely unecessary. Change to children. This is important because it makes the algorithm a lot more readable, which is becoming more and more important since the polynomial algorithm is starting to be ported""""",title+description
TIMOB-12321,12,"""CodeProcessor: Implement local dynamic analysis, global static analysis option""","""""""A key issue with the code processor is getting it to <USER>all of the code. When a node is not processed, it could be for a variety of reasons. Sometimes it's because the code is actually never executed, and we don't want to analyze it, but sometimes it's because of a limitation in the code processor (or just analyzing offline in general). Knowing which is which is difficult and, in some cases, impossible. Ultimately we want to try and make the code processor as accurate as possible, but adding an option to automatically analyze all un-analyzed code could be useful, despite the possibility for false positives.""""""",New Feature,"Title: CodeProcessor: Implement local dynamic analysis, global static analysis option
Description: """"A key issue with the code processor is getting it to <USER>all of the code. When a node is not processed, it could be for a variety of reasons. Sometimes it's because the code is actually never executed, and we don't want to analyze it, but sometimes it's because of a limitation in the code processor (or just analyzing offline in general). Knowing which is which is difficult and, in some cases, impossible. Ultimately we want to try and make the code processor as accurate as possible, but adding an option to automatically analyze all un-analyzed code could be useful, despite the possibility for false positives.""""",title+description
