TIMOB-12303,12,"""Bridge Crossing Optimizations""","""""""Note, this is similar to: https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-12212 and https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-11812    What's the potential of having the Ti.XML module do most of it's parsing in the runtime?  Javascript can be really fast at this type of parsing so some of these API's could probably be used 100% on the javascript side instead of calling across the bridge.  A good example is an app I worked on for a customer recently:  They have this so-ridiculously-large-its-sinful XML file that we have to iterate through.  Each iteration has 4-5 across-the-bridge calls to the XML api (e.g. getting the node and it's children, iterate through them, etc.).  Javascript could probably handle this just fine without the need to go to the native layer to parse and search through an XML document.  What if we converted the XML api to a runtime API?  Or at least parts of it?  Same namespace but it would run off of the runtime instead of native.    In similar fashion, this could apply to SQLite as well.  Much of the calls, requiring us to iterate through rows, are bridge calls.  It'd be a good optimization to add a method to spit out the data set as JSON, that way there's no need to make tons of bridge calls, iterating over the data set.  Pedro actually did a working sample of this for iOS and it's a lot faster.  It makes a lot of sense to have this out of the box.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Bridge Crossing Optimizations
Description: """"Note, this is similar to: https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-12212 and https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-11812 What's the potential of having the Ti.XML module do most of it's parsing in the runtime? Javascript can be really fast at this type of parsing so some of these API's could probably be used 100% on the javascript side instead of calling across the bridge. A good example is an app I worked on for a customer recently: They have this so-ridiculously-large-its-sinful XML file that we have to iterate through. Each iteration has 4-5 across-the-bridge calls to the XML api (e.g. getting the node and it's children, iterate through them, etc.). Javascript could probably handle this just fine without the need to go to the native layer to parse and search through an XML document. What if we converted the XML api to a runtime API? Or at least parts of it? Same namespace but it would run off of the runtime instead of native. In similar fashion, this could apply to SQLite as well. Much of the calls, requiring us to iterate through rows, are bridge calls. It'd be a good optimization to add a method to spit out the data set as JSON, that way there's no need to make tons of bridge calls, iterating over the data set. Pedro actually did a working sample of this for iOS and it's a lot faster. It makes a lot of sense to have this out of the box.""""",title+description
