TIMOB-12001,12,"""CLI: Target platform being asked twice when running """"titanium build""""""","""""""Description:  Target platform being asked twice when running """"""""titanium build"""""""" on Mac OS X Lion 10.7.5. Does not happen on Mac OS Mountain Lion 10.8.2.        Steps:  1. Run """"""""titanium build""""""""    Expected result:  Target platform should be asked only once    Actual result:  Target platform being asked twice""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: Target platform being asked twice when running """"titanium build""""
Description: """"Description: Target platform being asked twice when running """"""""titanium build"""""""" on Mac OS X Lion 10.7.5. Does not happen on Mac OS Mountain Lion 10.8.2. Steps: 1. Run """"""""titanium build"""""""" Expected result: Target platform should be asked only once Actual result: Target platform being asked twice""""",title+description
TIMOB-12000,12,"""Obsolete """"runOnUiThread"""" annotation elements should be removed""","""""""Although support for the """"""""runOnUiThread"""""""" annotation element was apparently removed in release 1.8.0.1, it is still used in a number of places in the platform code.    At the best, this is misleading. At the worst, it may be used in places that actually _should_ be invoked on the UI thread. It may also lead community members or module developers to use this element in their own code.""""""",Bug,"Title: Obsolete """"runOnUiThread"""" annotation elements should be removed
Description: """"Although support for the """"""""runOnUiThread"""""""" annotation element was apparently removed in release 1.8.0.1, it is still used in a number of places in the platform code. At the best, this is misleading. At the worst, it may be used in places that actually _should_ be invoked on the UI thread. It may also lead community members or module developers to use this element in their own code.""""",title+description
TIMOB-11999,12,"""Android: localStorage not persistent in WebView when loading local resources""","""""""*Problem description*  LocalStorage in the webview component on Android is not persisting data across app sessions when loading the HTML and related files as local resources.   This is not consistent with iOS webview.    *Suggestion*  Seems like adding a 'WebSettings' property to the WebView to allow the settings of 'DomStorageEnabled' and 'DatabasePath' would do the trick simply enough.    *Test case*  See attachment.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: localStorage not persistent in WebView when loading local resources
Description: """"*Problem description* LocalStorage in the webview component on Android is not persisting data across app sessions when loading the HTML and related files as local resources. This is not consistent with iOS webview. *Suggestion* Seems like adding a 'WebSettings' property to the WebView to allow the settings of 'DomStorageEnabled' and 'DatabasePath' would do the trick simply enough. *Test case* See attachment.""""",title+description
TIMOB-11998,12,"""iOS: Layout bug: horizontal layout Views with height=Ti.UI.SIZE are drawn with wrong height""","""""""h2.Problem description  # Horizontal views are not sizing correctly vertically when their height is set to Ti.UI.SIZE. The red view should not be visible, since it should be exactly the same size as the yellow view which is inside it. The yellow view is too tall... it should be exactly the same height as the black boxes. The blue view should not be visible, since it should be exactly the same size as the orange view which is inside it.  # Horizontal views that have wrapping children do not wrap correctly... the wrapped child views are vertically positioned much lower than they should be. The 3 black boxes in the orange (horizontal) view are all added to the orange view sequentially, and therefore should appear as they do in the attached Android image. In iOS, the 3rd box is wrapped with a large vertical gap.    See the attached images. The first image is from the Android emulator, and appears correctly. The second image is from the iPhone simulator and shows the problem. This is a layout parity issue.    h2.Test case  {panel:title=app.js}    {panel}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Layout bug: horizontal layout Views with height=Ti.UI.SIZE are drawn with wrong height
Description: """"h2.Problem description # Horizontal views are not sizing correctly vertically when their height is set to Ti.UI.SIZE. The red view should not be visible, since it should be exactly the same size as the yellow view which is inside it. The yellow view is too tall... it should be exactly the same height as the black boxes. The blue view should not be visible, since it should be exactly the same size as the orange view which is inside it. # Horizontal views that have wrapping children do not wrap correctly... the wrapped child views are vertically positioned much lower than they should be. The 3 black boxes in the orange (horizontal) view are all added to the orange view sequentially, and therefore should appear as they do in the attached Android image. In iOS, the 3rd box is wrapped with a large vertical gap. See the attached images. The first image is from the Android emulator, and appears correctly. The second image is from the iPhone simulator and shows the problem. This is a layout parity issue. h2.Test case {panel:title=app.js} {panel}""""",title+description
TIMOB-11997,12,"""MobileWeb: No padding between leftImage and title in a tableviewrow""","""""""Run the attached sample app.js with the image file attached ( copy it to resources ).    Expected Result: There should be some padding between the leftImage and the title.  Actual Result : There is no padding between leftImage and Title. Same behavior is not seen in iOS and Android.    Note: There is no padding between the edge and the leftImage on MobileWeb and iOS which i believe a default behavior on the platforms. On Android, there is padding between left edge, the image and the title""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: No padding between leftImage and title in a tableviewrow
Description: """"Run the attached sample app.js with the image file attached ( copy it to resources ). Expected Result: There should be some padding between the leftImage and the title. Actual Result : There is no padding between leftImage and Title. Same behavior is not seen in iOS and Android. Note: There is no padding between the edge and the leftImage on MobileWeb and iOS which i believe a default behavior on the platforms. On Android, there is padding between left edge, the image and the title""""",title+description
TIMOB-11996,12,"""CLI should exit with code on error/success""","""""""CLI should exit with standard codes on error/success `<USER>exit([code])`    Success by default processes exit with code """"""""0"""""""" which is an acceptable success code already.    EXAMPLE USAGE: External libraries calling the cli via child <USER> This will help identify error types.""""""",New Feature,"Title: CLI should exit with code on error/success
Description: """"CLI should exit with standard codes on error/success `<USER>exit([code])` Success by default processes exit with code """"""""0"""""""" which is an acceptable success code already. EXAMPLE USAGE: External libraries calling the cli via child <USER> This will help identify error types.""""",title+description
TIMOB-11995,12,"""Android: Duplicate setter in module causes cryptic packaging failure ""","""""""If a proxy object defines a property using {{propertyAccessors}} and also defines a custom setter for the property, build fails with the following cryptic (and completely misleading) message:        Sample code follows:      After commenting out the {{setHintText}} method and removing the build directory, the module builds fine.    Since a similar pattern works on iOS, people are likely to expect it will work on Android.     I will document it, but it would be good if we could provide a better error message, as well. """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Duplicate setter in module causes cryptic packaging failure
Description: """"If a proxy object defines a property using {{propertyAccessors}} and also defines a custom setter for the property, build fails with the following cryptic (and completely misleading) message: Sample code follows: After commenting out the {{setHintText}} method and removing the build directory, the module builds fine. Since a similar pattern works on iOS, people are likely to expect it will work on Android. I will document it, but it would be good if we could provide a better error message, as well. """"",title+description
TIMOB-11994,12,"""iOS: Can't use SearchBar with headerView in TableView""","""""""h2. problem    In a Ti.UI.TableView on iOS, you cannot use the *search* property's Ti.UI.SearchBar if you have also specified as *headerView*. You won't encounter any errors, you will just not get the SearchBar in your app. You will only see the headerView.    If this is the expected behavior, I did not see it documented with respect to the Ti.UI.SearchBar in the API docs.    h2. expected behavior    The SearchBar would be anchored at the top of the table with the headerView under it.    h2. test case    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Can't use SearchBar with headerView in TableView
Description: """"h2. problem In a Ti.UI.TableView on iOS, you cannot use the *search* property's Ti.UI.SearchBar if you have also specified as *headerView*. You won't encounter any errors, you will just not get the SearchBar in your app. You will only see the headerView. If this is the expected behavior, I did not see it documented with respect to the Ti.UI.SearchBar in the API docs. h2. expected behavior The SearchBar would be anchored at the top of the table with the headerView under it. h2. test case """"",title+description
