TIMOB-11990,12,"""CLI: iTunesArtwork when placed into the Studio project root directory is not being copied correctly""","""""""While testing packaging with icons and artwork, iTunesArtwork PNG when placed in the project /resources/iphone folder works, but when placing it in the root of project does not get copied into the ipa correctly.    This does not occur with 2.1.4 GA    Steps to reproduce:  1) Create a project with a 512x512 image in png format called """"""""iTunesArtwork"""""""" at the root of the project with the """""""".png"""""""" removed from the name via either the finder or studio rename.  2) Package for adhoc or distribution  2.1) if Distribution, re-sign from the archive with an ad-hoc provision  3) Drop the file into iTunes and check the icon    Result:  The icon is missing    Expected:  The icon is present""""""",Bug,"Title: CLI: iTunesArtwork when placed into the Studio project root directory is not being copied correctly
Description: """"While testing packaging with icons and artwork, iTunesArtwork PNG when placed in the project /resources/iphone folder works, but when placing it in the root of project does not get copied into the ipa correctly. This does not occur with 2.1.4 GA Steps to reproduce: 1) Create a project with a 512x512 image in png format called """"""""iTunesArtwork"""""""" at the root of the project with the """""""".png"""""""" removed from the name via either the finder or studio rename. 2) Package for adhoc or distribution 2.1) if Distribution, re-sign from the archive with an ad-hoc provision 3) Drop the file into iTunes and check the icon Result: The icon is missing Expected: The icon is present""""",title+description
TIMOB-11989,12,"""Tooling: Cannot build Titanium SDK from source on Windows""","""""""REPRODUCTION:  Follow these directions to install the tools and utilities to build the SDK from source:    https://wiki.<USER>org/display/guides2/Building+the+Titanium+SDK+From+Source    Note: Due to a scons bug, all paths should NOT contain spaces.    RESULTS:      OTHER INFO:  The git command is no longer wrapped with git.cmd and is now a git.exe binary.    See commit five months ago: https://github.com/msysgit/msysgit/commit/bcb6b06cca1cd7382055898649f4403c83743f7b#cmd/git.exe    Reverting this commit on titanium_mobile fixed the issue:  https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/79f2d4585f01f2fa9ff67e40088b9a97ec584893  """"""",Bug,"Title: Tooling: Cannot build Titanium SDK from source on Windows
Description: """"REPRODUCTION: Follow these directions to install the tools and utilities to build the SDK from source: https://wiki.<USER>org/display/guides2/Building+the+Titanium+SDK+From+Source Note: Due to a scons bug, all paths should NOT contain spaces. RESULTS: OTHER INFO: The git command is no longer wrapped with git.cmd and is now a git.exe binary. See commit five months ago: https://github.com/msysgit/msysgit/commit/bcb6b06cca1cd7382055898649f4403c83743f7b#cmd/git.exe Reverting this commit on titanium_mobile fixed the issue: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/79f2d4585f01f2fa9ff67e40088b9a97ec584893 """"",title+description
TIMOB-11988,12,"""CLI: allow user to set sdk install directory""","""""""1. Install TiSDKs to directory set in 'ti config paths.sdks' if it is set, otherwise continue with current default behavior.  2. Allow user to install to arbitrary TiSDK directory via command line args.    Current Documentation:        Proposed Interface:        If the sdk or module path is not specified, it makes sense to use 'ti config paths.sdks', followed by the default if sdks path not set, and perhaps a warning if only one path is set.    This ticket will allow Titanium Studio to have better workflow with the CLI.  """"""",New Feature,"Title: CLI: allow user to set sdk install directory
Description: """"1. Install TiSDKs to directory set in 'ti config paths.sdks' if it is set, otherwise continue with current default behavior. 2. Allow user to install to arbitrary TiSDK directory via command line args. Current Documentation: Proposed Interface: If the sdk or module path is not specified, it makes sense to use 'ti config paths.sdks', followed by the default if sdks path not set, and perhaps a warning if only one path is set. This ticket will allow Titanium Studio to have better workflow with the CLI. """"",title+description
TIMOB-11987,12,"""iOS: TableView row management makes it drastically slow""","""""""working a lot with tableview, i stumble upon a big problem:  complex rows makes the scrolling and loading a lot too slow.  trying to find the reason for that, i found actually 2 main reasons:      h2. setData with custom row should not actually create views  That makes the loading very slow.  Whats wrong with it?  Event if your rows have the same className, the each contain their own views. Which goes against the reuse of cell.      h2. reuse is not really a reuse  IOs tableviews come with a way of reusing cells, which means not create/add/remove views, but just change their properties  Titanium tries to use that feature, but in reality it doesnt. Whenever it tries to reuse a cell it removes the views from the older cell and add the views from the new one. This is what makes the scrolling so slow.    No you might tell me that examples from the KitchenSink goes pretty smoothly(Layout 2 for example). Well that s because the rows are not that complex. They only have one subview level.  I posted an example in another bug (which never got looked at, too sad!)  http://jira.<USER>org/browse/TC-1126  In that pretty complex (for titanium, on ios pure just simple), you can clearly see that the scrolling is not smooth even on a iphone 4.      h2. So what can we do?  I think there is a pretty simple solution.  The data from a tableview should only be a dictionary defining the layout of the view.  Something like this    With this we could do in cellForRowAtIndexPath:  - look for className and dequeue cell  - if cell doesnt exist, create one view corresponding views   - if cell does exist, just apply properties    I know it would mean a lot of work, but i really think we need something like this.    Please comment on this! And most of all please dont overlook this one.    I will try and start working on this    Ps: one thing bugging me is the """"""""type"""""""" thing. How to link a string to a titanium constructor. I dont think such a map already exists.         """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: TableView row management makes it drastically slow
Description: """"working a lot with tableview, i stumble upon a big problem: complex rows makes the scrolling and loading a lot too slow. trying to find the reason for that, i found actually 2 main reasons: h2. setData with custom row should not actually create views That makes the loading very slow. Whats wrong with it? Event if your rows have the same className, the each contain their own views. Which goes against the reuse of cell. h2. reuse is not really a reuse IOs tableviews come with a way of reusing cells, which means not create/add/remove views, but just change their properties Titanium tries to use that feature, but in reality it doesnt. Whenever it tries to reuse a cell it removes the views from the older cell and add the views from the new one. This is what makes the scrolling so slow. No you might tell me that examples from the KitchenSink goes pretty smoothly(Layout 2 for example). Well that s because the rows are not that complex. They only have one subview level. I posted an example in another bug (which never got looked at, too sad!) http://jira.<USER>org/browse/TC-1126 In that pretty complex (for titanium, on ios pure just simple), you can clearly see that the scrolling is not smooth even on a iphone 4. h2. So what can we do? I think there is a pretty simple solution. The data from a tableview should only be a dictionary defining the layout of the view. Something like this With this we could do in cellForRowAtIndexPath: - look for className and dequeue cell - if cell doesnt exist, create one view corresponding views - if cell does exist, just apply properties I know it would mean a lot of work, but i really think we need something like this. Please comment on this! And most of all please dont overlook this one. I will try and start working on this Ps: one thing bugging me is the """"""""type"""""""" thing. How to link a string to a titanium constructor. I dont think such a map already exists. """"",title+description
