TIMOB-11030,12,"""iOS: Applying background color constants or """"stripped"""" color to modal window results in the modal window being black""","""""""Description:  Applying background color constants or """"""""stripped"""""""" color to modal window results in the modal window being black.  This doesn't occur on iOS 5 devices even if the iOS 6.0 SDK and Xcode 4.5 are used for the build.    This is not a regression because iOS 6 was not available previously.    Steps:  1. Run the following code on iOS 6.0:    2. Press """"""""Click me"""""""" button    Expected result:  Should see a modal window with """"""""Scroll Down"""""""" label    Actual result:  The modal window turns black""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Applying background color constants or """"stripped"""" color to modal window results in the modal window being black
Description: """"Description: Applying background color constants or """"""""stripped"""""""" color to modal window results in the modal window being black. This doesn't occur on iOS 5 devices even if the iOS 6.0 SDK and Xcode 4.5 are used for the build. This is not a regression because iOS 6 was not available previously. Steps: 1. Run the following code on iOS 6.0: 2. Press """"""""Click me"""""""" button Expected result: Should see a modal window with """"""""Scroll Down"""""""" label Actual result: The modal window turns black""""",title+description
TIMOB-11029,12,"""Android: Views with a large border width cover up children (even when opacity is set to .5)""","""""""Reproduction steps:  1. Run the following code:        Expected results:  You should see a red view in the middle    Actual results:  The red view does not show.      In android, the border just overwrites the child view, so even if you set the opacity to .5, you still can't see the children.  This is a parity issue that brings up a larger parity issue with borders across platforms.  We may want to create a formal spec for borders at some point and have all the platforms follow that instead of defaulting to 'iOS' behavior.  This will require more research to see whether it's possible to do in android.  We introduced this behavior when we 'fixed' clipping in android, but we might to take a different approach after we map out some spec.    The clipping ticket is https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-9469""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Views with a large border width cover up children (even when opacity is set to .5)
Description: """"Reproduction steps: 1. Run the following code: Expected results: You should see a red view in the middle Actual results: The red view does not show. In android, the border just overwrites the child view, so even if you set the opacity to .5, you still can't see the children. This is a parity issue that brings up a larger parity issue with borders across platforms. We may want to create a formal spec for borders at some point and have all the platforms follow that instead of defaulting to 'iOS' behavior. This will require more research to see whether it's possible to do in android. We introduced this behavior when we 'fixed' clipping in android, but we might to take a different approach after we map out some spec. The clipping ticket is https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-9469""""",title+description
TIMOB-11028,12,"""Android: Window.open event fails to fire for windows in ActionBar tab groups""","""""""Using the sample code from TIMOB-8192, it appears that the open event fires (and an alert shows) when building the app with targetSdkVersion unset, but with targetSdkVersion set to 11 or higher, the open event never fires.          When the following code is added to the tiapp.xml file and the app is run on a 4.0.3 emulator, the open even doesn't fire.    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Window.open event fails to fire for windows in ActionBar tab groups
