Description: """" Expected Behavior: The height of the red view should be the height of the screen minus the top and bottom values. Actual: The height of the red view is 460 """"",title+description
TIMOB-10855,12,"""iOS : Kitchensink crashes after tapping on basic picker 2.(iOS 6 beta 4)""","""""""Cant confirm if this is a regression because of a blocker issue Timob - 10819    Description:  1.Package kitchensink using iOS 6 sdk.  2.Install it on Iphone 4S running iOS 6 beta through itunes.  3.Run kitchensink.  4.Tap on controls -> picker -> basic picker 2.  5.You will see that the kitchensink crashes.    Actual Result :  1.Kitchensink crashes after tapping on basic picker 2.    Expected Result:  1.Kitchensink should not crash after tapping on basic picker 2.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS : Kitchensink crashes after tapping on basic picker 2.(iOS 6 beta 4)
Description: """"Cant confirm if this is a regression because of a blocker issue Timob - 10819 Description: 1.Package kitchensink using iOS 6 sdk. 2.Install it on Iphone 4S running iOS 6 beta through itunes. 3.Run kitchensink. 4.Tap on controls -> picker -> basic picker 2. 5.You will see that the kitchensink crashes. Actual Result : 1.Kitchensink crashes after tapping on basic picker 2. Expected Result: 1.Kitchensink should not crash after tapping on basic picker 2.""""",title+description
TIMOB-10854,12,"""iOS: ios-sim always uses default Xcode.app location ignoring location specified by xcode-select ""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  1. Install Xcode 4.5 Preview (or Xcode 4.3) in addition to default Xcode  2. Set xcode-select to that Xcode  3. Run ios-sim showsdks    Expected:  Listed SDKs should point to the same location as xcode-select    Actual:  SDKs point to default Xcode.app location""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: ios-sim always uses default Xcode.app location ignoring location specified by xcode-select
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Install Xcode 4.5 Preview (or Xcode 4.3) in addition to default Xcode 2. Set xcode-select to that Xcode 3. Run ios-sim showsdks Expected: Listed SDKs should point to the same location as xcode-select Actual: SDKs point to default Xcode.app location""""",title+description
TIMOB-10853,12,"""iOS: Ti.App.Properties """"change"""" event should not fire when setting an existing property to its current value""","""""""1. Run this code      2. Look at log and observe infinite loop. On Android, """"""""change"""""""" is only called 4 times.    Expected Behavior:  - """"""""change"""""""" should only be called 5 times - since iOS also adds appGUID when launching the app for the first time.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Ti.App.Properties """"change"""" event should not fire when setting an existing property to its current value
Description: """"1. Run this code 2. Look at log and observe infinite loop. On Android, """"""""change"""""""" is only called 4 times. Expected Behavior: - """"""""change"""""""" should only be called 5 times - since iOS also adds appGUID when launching the app for the first time.""""",title+description
TIMOB-10852,12,"""iOS: tooling - Studio correctly passes iOS compile target, but scripts use latest iOS SDK regardless of setting""","""""""While testing iOS and Xcode 4.5 we saw a failure trying to compile with iOS 5 target. This failure was identical to the failure seen when trying to compile with an iOS 6 compile target. After closer examination, we determined that no matter what the setting was, the build would always target the latest installed SDK (as interpreted by Xcode).    e.g. Setting to iOS 5.0 when 5.0, 5.1 and iOS 6 are options, iOS 6 is used.    I have verified that Studio is sending the correct arguments:      But the build <USER>still reports:      This is not a regression. Occurs in 2.1.2GA and 2.1.1GA.  Occurs with Xcode 4.4.1 and Xcode 4.5    h3. Steps to reproduce:  1) Create a project using either studio or command line  2) Invoke your build and ensure you are using something other than the latest SDK possible, something like this:  """"""""/Users/<USER>Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/2.1.1.GA/iphone/builder.py"""""""" """"""""install"""""""" """"""""5.0"""""""" """"""""/Users/<USER>212Workspace/ericDevTest"""""""" """"""""com.<USER>devtest"""""""" """"""""ericDevTest"""""""" """"""""25360090-4A84-4F2A-8415-B53E250EAB05"""""""" """"""""<USER>9JUJ8DJW9G)"""""""" """"""""universal""""""""  3) Observe the console    h3. Result:  The build reports it is building with the latest iOS SDK you have installed.    h3. Expected:  The build reports it is building with (and actually builds with) the compile target set in studio. If it doesn't have the right value, it should warn the user about the problem.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: tooling - Studio correctly passes iOS compile target, but scripts use latest iOS SDK regardless of setting
Description: """"While testing iOS and Xcode 4.5 we saw a failure trying to compile with iOS 5 target. This failure was identical to the failure seen when trying to compile with an iOS 6 compile target. After closer examination, we determined that no matter what the setting was, the build would always target the latest installed SDK (as interpreted by Xcode). e.g. Setting to iOS 5.0 when 5.0, 5.1 and iOS 6 are options, iOS 6 is used. I have verified that Studio is sending the correct arguments: But the build <USER>still reports: This is not a regression. Occurs in 2.1.2GA and 2.1.1GA. Occurs with Xcode 4.4.1 and Xcode 4.5 h3. Steps to reproduce: 1) Create a project using either studio or command line 2) Invoke your build and ensure you are using something other than the latest SDK possible, something like this: """"""""/Users/<USER>Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/2.1.1.GA/iphone/builder.py"""""""" """"""""install"""""""" """"""""5.0"""""""" """"""""/Users/<USER>212Workspace/ericDevTest"""""""" """"""""com.<USER>devtest"""""""" """"""""ericDevTest"""""""" """"""""25360090-4A84-4F2A-8415-B53E250EAB05"""""""" """"""""<USER>9JUJ8DJW9G)"""""""" """"""""universal"""""""" 3) Observe the console h3. Result: The build reports it is building with the latest iOS SDK you have installed. h3. Expected: The build reports it is building with (and actually builds with) the compile target set in studio. If it doesn't have the right value, it should warn the user about the problem.""""",title+description
TIMOB-10851,12,"""iOS: The soft keyboard does not change orientation when we change orientation of the device(only Iphone 4S running iOS 6 beta 4).""","""""""Cant confirm if this is a regression because of a blocker issue Timob - 10819    Description:  1.Package kitchensink using iOS 6 sdk.  2.Install it on Iphone 4S running iOS 6 beta through itunes.  3.Run kitchensink.  4.Tap on controls -> text fields -> events.  5.Tap on the text field so that the soft keyboard pops up.  6.Now turn the device to landscape mode with the keyboard up.  7.Notice that the screen changes the orientation to landscape but the keyboard stays in portrait mode & does not change to landscape mode.    Actual Result :  1.The soft keyboard does not change orientation when we change orientation of the device.    Expected Result:  1.The soft keyboard should change orientation when we change orientation of the device.       """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: The soft keyboard does not change orientation when we change orientation of the device(only Iphone 4S running iOS 6 beta 4).
Description: """"Cant confirm if this is a regression because of a blocker issue Timob - 10819 Description: 1.Package kitchensink using iOS 6 sdk. 2.Install it on Iphone 4S running iOS 6 beta through itunes. 3.Run kitchensink. 4.Tap on controls -> text fields -> events. 5.Tap on the text field so that the soft keyboard pops up. 6.Now turn the device to landscape mode with the keyboard up. 7.Notice that the screen changes the orientation to landscape but the keyboard stays in portrait mode & does not change to landscape mode. Actual Result : 1.The soft keyboard does not change orientation when we change orientation of the device. Expected Result: 1.The soft keyboard should change orientation when we change orientation of the device. """"",title+description
TIMOB-10848,12,"""Blackberry: Move runtime and test application into new platform repository (titanium_mobile_blackberry).""","""""""The BB runtime (tibb) and test application (tibbapp) need to be transfered into the new BB platform repository.  The Git history should be preserved along with the transfer. We should also remove the V8 binary from Git  and keep it up on S3. It should be downloaded when the SDK is built.  """"""",Sub-task,"Title: Blackberry: Move runtime and test application into new platform repository (titanium_mobile_blackberry).
Description: """"The BB runtime (tibb) and test application (tibbapp) need to be transfered into the new BB platform repository. The Git history should be preserved along with the transfer. We should also remove the V8 binary from Git and keep it up on S3. It should be downloaded when the SDK is built. """"",title+description
TIMOB-10847,12,"""iOS: Setting .text on label Ti.UI.Label when .right is set causes jumping""","""""""h2. Problem    When setting .text on a Ti.UI.Label component when the .right prop is set to something, like eg. 50, the new text jumps while rendering. I grabbed some screenshot of it happening (attached). First from setting a new text and then back to the old one. Notice how it jumps both ways.    h2. Expected Behavior    The text should be set without any jump.    h2. Test case    {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  Ti.UI.backgroundColor = '#fff';    var win = Ti.UI.createWindow();    var label = Ti.UI.createLabel({   right: 50  });    win.add( label );  win.open();    var t = true;  setInterval(function() {   label.text = t ? 'string 1' : 'Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.';   t = !t;  }, 2000);  {code}  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Setting .text on label Ti.UI.Label when .right is set causes jumping
Description: """"h2. Problem When setting .text on a Ti.UI.Label component when the .right prop is set to something, like eg. 50, the new text jumps while rendering. I grabbed some screenshot of it happening (attached). First from setting a new text and then back to the old one. Notice how it jumps both ways. h2. Expected Behavior The text should be set without any jump. h2. Test case {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} Ti.UI.backgroundColor = '#fff'; var win = Ti.UI.createWindow(); var label = Ti.UI.createLabel({ right: 50 }); win.add( label ); win.open(); var t = true; setInterval(function() { label.text = t ? 'string 1' : 'Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.'; t = !t; }, 2000); {code} """"",title+description
