TIMOB-10398,12,"""iOS: iAd orientation issues with NavGroup""","""""""Note the following test code when run on ipad:    http://pastie.org/4412203    I have a window with an iAd opening in a navgroup.  There are several issues.    1) If we open an iAd under certain orientations we get a black screen.  This is similar to this issue -> https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-5006?focusedCommentId=209592#comment-209592    2) If we remove the navgroup and switch orientations to portrait while an ad is up the screen doesn't adjust orientations if we close the ad.    3) When using the navgroup periodically the ad will disappear or a fire a 'load' event, but not actually be there.  This doesn't happen outside a navgroup.  Please see this issue -> https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TC-1057    4) If you comment in the code for the 'action' event I have that fixes orientations and then release them in a particular order you can marginally work around the first two issues by sort of 'shaking the app loose' as it were.  This is the best we've got so far for a solution, but still doesn't help us with the third issue.  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: iAd orientation issues with NavGroup
Description: """"Note the following test code when run on ipad: http://pastie.org/4412203 I have a window with an iAd opening in a navgroup. There are several issues. 1) If we open an iAd under certain orientations we get a black screen. This is similar to this issue -> https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-5006?focusedCommentId=209592#comment-209592 2) If we remove the navgroup and switch orientations to portrait while an ad is up the screen doesn't adjust orientations if we close the ad. 3) When using the navgroup periodically the ad will disappear or a fire a 'load' event, but not actually be there. This doesn't happen outside a navgroup. Please see this issue -> https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TC-1057 4) If you comment in the code for the 'action' event I have that fixes orientations and then release them in a particular order you can marginally work around the first two issues by sort of 'shaking the app loose' as it were. This is the best we've got so far for a solution, but still doesn't help us with the third issue. """"",title+description
TIMOB-10397,12,"""Android: Ti namespace does not work in webview after reload""","""""""Run the attached app.js and sample test.html file.  The Ti namespace does not work after webview reload.  works fine on ios.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Ti namespace does not work in webview after reload
Description: """"Run the attached app.js and sample test.html file. The Ti namespace does not work after webview reload. works fine on ios.""""",title+description
TIMOB-10396,12,"""TiAPI: 9-patch workflow enhancements""","""""""backgroundImage : 'test.png'    Android will look for the image. If it can't find it, it will look for 'test.9.png'    I suggest the opposite    Android first looks for test.9.png, if it can't find it, it will look for 'test.png'    That way you don't need any branching code in your app if you are writing for Android and iOS simultaneously    Instead of:    backgroundImage : Ti.Platform.osname == 'android' ? 'test.png' : 'test_ios.png'    you could simply go:    backgroundImage : 'test.png'    Here is some testcode. Copy the attached images to the root of Resources/ - then build. You'll clearly see that Android is using 'test.png' stretched raw, rather than using 'test.9.png'.     Then delete or rename test.png, and build again (you may or may not need to clean project) and build again - now it works.    ~~~  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow(  {   backgroundColor : 'white',   exitOnClose : true,   fullscreen : false,   title : 'Click button to test'  });    var button = Ti.UI.createButton(  {   backgroundImage : '/test.png',   backgroundSelectedImage : '/test.png',   title : 'Click me!',   top : 10,   width : 300,   height : 200  });      button.addEventListener('click', function(e) {   Ti.API.info(""""""""You clicked the button"""""""");  });    win.add(button);  win.open()  ~~~      """"""",New Feature,"Title: TiAPI: 9-patch workflow enhancements
Description: """"backgroundImage : 'test.png' Android will look for the image. If it can't find it, it will look for 'test.9.png' I suggest the opposite Android first looks for test.9.png, if it can't find it, it will look for 'test.png' That way you don't need any branching code in your app if you are writing for Android and iOS simultaneously Instead of: backgroundImage : Ti.Platform.osname == 'android' ? 'test.png' : 'test_ios.png' you could simply go: backgroundImage : 'test.png' Here is some testcode. Copy the attached images to the root of Resources/ - then build. You'll clearly see that Android is using 'test.png' stretched raw, rather than using 'test.9.png'. Then delete or rename test.png, and build again (you may or may not need to clean project) and build again - now it works. ~~~ var win = Ti.UI.createWindow( { backgroundColor : 'white', exitOnClose : true, fullscreen : false, title : 'Click button to test' }); var button = Ti.UI.createButton( { backgroundImage : '/test.png', backgroundSelectedImage : '/test.png', title : 'Click me!', top : 10, width : 300, height : 200 }); button.addEventListener('click', function(e) { Ti.API.info(""""""""You clicked the button""""""""); }); win.add(button); win.open() ~~~ """"",title+description
TIMOB-10395,12,"""iOS: Support for native UITableViewCell native Cell Styles on Ti.UI.TableViewRow""","""""""The performance of native UITableViewCellStyles is far superior of the custom rows/cells and the best choice for larger table views. As of now, this is still not implemented.    I think this is due to the way Titanium deals with Rows, as it doesn't exposes the UITableViewCell to Ti high level API's thus not allowing the definition of a property mapped to UITableViewCell::initWithStyle.    So, as we can actually use the default cell style by using the title property on Ti.UI.TableViewRow, I think we could have something like:          Where the *style* property could have values like:    ROW_STYLE_DEFAULT,  ROW_STYLE_VALUE1,  ROW_STYLE_VALUE2,  ROW_STYLE_SUBTITLE    as per Apple Documentation on Native Cell Style support.    for Value1 and Value2, we could also use the subtitle parameter or have a label2 parameter where we could pass a label view.    There is a community request for that on: http://developer.<USER>com/question/45281/any-planned-support-for-iphone-native-table-cell-styles    Apple Reference on Native Cell Styles: http://developer.apple.com/library/ios/documentation/uikit/reference/UITableViewCell_Class/Reference/Reference.html#//apple_ref/c/tdef/UITableViewCellStyle  """"""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Support for native UITableViewCell native Cell Styles on Ti.UI.TableViewRow
Description: """"The performance of native UITableViewCellStyles is far superior of the custom rows/cells and the best choice for larger table views. As of now, this is still not implemented. I think this is due to the way Titanium deals with Rows, as it doesn't exposes the UITableViewCell to Ti high level API's thus not allowing the definition of a property mapped to UITableViewCell::initWithStyle. So, as we can actually use the default cell style by using the title property on Ti.UI.TableViewRow, I think we could have something like: Where the *style* property could have values like: ROW_STYLE_DEFAULT, ROW_STYLE_VALUE1, ROW_STYLE_VALUE2, ROW_STYLE_SUBTITLE as per Apple Documentation on Native Cell Style support. for Value1 and Value2, we could also use the subtitle parameter or have a label2 parameter where we could pass a label view. There is a community request for that on: http://developer.<USER>com/question/45281/any-planned-support-for-iphone-native-table-cell-styles Apple Reference on Native Cell Styles: http://developer.apple.com/library/ios/documentation/uikit/reference/UITableViewCell_Class/Reference/Reference.html#//apple_ref/c/tdef/UITableViewCellStyle """"",title+description
TIMOB-10394,12,"""Android: Two versions of V8 are shipped""","""""""h3. The Problem  A packaged app is way too big.    h3. Reason  Two version of V8 are shipped with the app.    h3. To reproduce:  1. Create a new Titanium Android app  2. Build for distribution  3. Change the app.apk name (extension) to app.zip  4. Uncompress the app.zip  5. Look inside the lib folder and you'll see two v8 folders  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Two versions of V8 are shipped
