TIMOB-10334,12,"""iOS: Request to implement 'FBFriendPickerViewController' - a new API in FB sdk 3.0""","""""""Expose FBFriendPickerViewController in ios for FB which is a new API in the latest FB sdk for ios.    https://developers.facebook.com/docs/sdk-reference/iossdk/3.0/class/FBFriendPickerViewController/    Also,implement facebook dialog API through which friends requests can be sent based on UID.    https://developers.facebook.com/docs/reference/dialogs/friends/""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Request to implement 'FBFriendPickerViewController' - a new API in FB sdk 3.0
Description: """"Expose FBFriendPickerViewController in ios for FB which is a new API in the latest FB sdk for ios. https://developers.facebook.com/docs/sdk-reference/iossdk/3.0/class/FBFriendPickerViewController/ Also,implement facebook dialog API through which friends requests can be sent based on UID. https://developers.facebook.com/docs/reference/dialogs/friends/""""",title+description
TIMOB-10333,12,"""iOS: showCamera does not support widescreen (16:9) video recording""","""""""h2. Problem description  When recording a video using the camera opened by Ti.Media.showCamera(), it is not possible to switch between 4:3 and 16:9 format.    h2. Steps to reproduce  1. Create an app that calls Ti.Media.showCamera()  2. Switch to video recording: the video is 4:3 and it is not possible to switch to widescreen (16:9)    h2. Additional notes  The native camera supports the switch: if you double tap on the screen before clicking the red button, the video format changes.""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: showCamera does not support widescreen (16:9) video recording
Description: """"h2. Problem description When recording a video using the camera opened by Ti.Media.showCamera(), it is not possible to switch between 4:3 and 16:9 format. h2. Steps to reproduce 1. Create an app that calls Ti.Media.showCamera() 2. Switch to video recording: the video is 4:3 and it is not possible to switch to widescreen (16:9) h2. Additional notes The native camera supports the switch: if you double tap on the screen before clicking the red button, the video format changes.""""",title+description
TIMOB-10332,12,"""iOS: Xcode (4.4)/OS X (10.8): There was no app icon in Xcode at first when running first packaging test""","""""""Description:  When I first packaged an application using Studio, the app icon did not appear in Xcode archive. We restarted Xcode but this had no effect. Some time later, the app icon started appearing, so this may be a one-off or intermittent issue.    I am unable to determine if this is a regression as it seems to affect only Xcode and may be an Apple issue.    Steps to reproduce:  1) Create a mobile project in TitaniumStudio  2) Right click on project, choose 'Publish' > 'Distribute - Apple iTunes Store'  3) Go through dialog window, choose correct options.  4) Push 'Publish' button.    Actual result:  There is no application icon image in opened Xcode organizer window.    Expected result:  In opened Xcode organizer window should be an application icon image.    Note:  After one of the subsequent times relaunching Xcode the problem didn't occur again.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Xcode (4.4)/OS X (10.8): There was no app icon in Xcode at first when running first packaging test
Description: """"Description: When I first packaged an application using Studio, the app icon did not appear in Xcode archive. We restarted Xcode but this had no effect. Some time later, the app icon started appearing, so this may be a one-off or intermittent issue. I am unable to determine if this is a regression as it seems to affect only Xcode and may be an Apple issue. Steps to reproduce: 1) Create a mobile project in TitaniumStudio 2) Right click on project, choose 'Publish' > 'Distribute - Apple iTunes Store' 3) Go through dialog window, choose correct options. 4) Push 'Publish' button. Actual result: There is no application icon image in opened Xcode organizer window. Expected result: In opened Xcode organizer window should be an application icon image. Note: After one of the subsequent times relaunching Xcode the problem didn't occur again.""""",title+description
TIMOB-10331,12,"""MobileWeb: scrollView - does not handle the contentHeight changing as it should""","""""""If you are scrolled to the bottom of a scrollView and then the contentHeight of the scrollView is reduced, you will be shown the bottom where the scrollView was, not the new bottom.    h4. Steps to repro:  1. run the code below  2. scroll to the bottom   3. click the button  4. notice that you no longer see the blue content view  5. touch the scroll view  6. notice that suddenly you are at the bottom of the blue view    {code:js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({   backgroundColor: 'white'  });  win.open();    var scrollView = Ti.UI.createScrollView({   top: 60,   height: 400,   contentHeight: Ti.UI.SIZE  });  win.add(scrollView);    var contentView = Ti.UI.createView({   height: 1000,   backgroundColor: 'blue',   borderWidth: 5,   borderRadius: 20,   borderColor: 'red'  });  scrollView.add(contentView);    var button = Ti.UI.createButton({   title: 'Click me',   width: 100,   height: 40,   top: 10  });  win.add(button);    button.addEventListener('click', function(){   contentView.height = 400;  });  {code}  """"""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: scrollView - does not handle the contentHeight changing as it should
Description: """"If you are scrolled to the bottom of a scrollView and then the contentHeight of the scrollView is reduced, you will be shown the bottom where the scrollView was, not the new bottom. h4. Steps to repro: 1. run the code below 2. scroll to the bottom 3. click the button 4. notice that you no longer see the blue content view 5. touch the scroll view 6. notice that suddenly you are at the bottom of the blue view {code:js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: 'white' }); win.open(); var scrollView = Ti.UI.createScrollView({ top: 60, height: 400, contentHeight: Ti.UI.SIZE }); win.add(scrollView); var contentView = Ti.UI.createView({ height: 1000, backgroundColor: 'blue', borderWidth: 5, borderRadius: 20, borderColor: 'red' }); scrollView.add(contentView); var button = Ti.UI.createButton({ title: 'Click me', width: 100, height: 40, top: 10 }); win.add(button); button.addEventListener('click', function(){ contentView.height = 400; }); {code} """"",title+description
TIMOB-10330,12,"""iOS: ScrollView: 'layout' to 'Horizontal' content is being cut-off""","""""""h6.Problem  In ScrollView when we set 'layout' to 'Horizontal' content is being cut-off (iOS_iPad_MIDDLE.jpg) when it goes beyond the bottom of the device screen.    The customer relies on a such layout content that should use 'layout' to 'Horizontal' in ScrollView UI element, growing up according the height contents dynamically.    h6.Stripped down simple sample  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: ScrollView: 'layout' to 'Horizontal' content is being cut-off
Description: """"h6.Problem In ScrollView when we set 'layout' to 'Horizontal' content is being cut-off (iOS_iPad_MIDDLE.jpg) when it goes beyond the bottom of the device screen. The customer relies on a such layout content that should use 'layout' to 'Horizontal' in ScrollView UI element, growing up according the height contents dynamically. h6.Stripped down simple sample """"",title+description
TIMOB-10328,12,"""Android: Add support for accessing and manipulating TableViewSections from a TableView""","""""""Accessing table view sections from a table view is currently limited to the data property which resets the entire table. This makes it extremely expensive to, say, add a new section to an already existing table. It has also given rise to poorly thought out API designs such as the 'header' property of TableViewRows.    We need to add methods for accessing and manipulating TableViewSections in a TableView akin to how we can access TableViewRows from a TableView.    We also need to document and deprecate the previously undocumented 'header' property.""""""",Story,"Title: Android: Add support for accessing and manipulating TableViewSections from a TableView
Description: """"Accessing table view sections from a table view is currently limited to the data property which resets the entire table. This makes it extremely expensive to, say, add a new section to an already existing table. It has also given rise to poorly thought out API designs such as the 'header' property of TableViewRows. We need to add methods for accessing and manipulating TableViewSections in a TableView akin to how we can access TableViewRows from a TableView. We also need to document and deprecate the previously undocumented 'header' property.""""",title+description
