TIMOB-10220,12,"""iOS: Annotations caret is offset from pin top and overlaps pin top""","""""""h3. Issue    In iOS the annotations on both retina and non-retina devices are offset from the pins top and they also overlap the pin. The annotation should be directly above the pin with no overlap like iOS does in its native maps application.     The screenshots illustrate this issue. Notice in the native maps application the caret is directly above the pin. It does not overlap the pin and it is not offset.     h3. Sample Code       """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Annotations caret is offset from pin top and overlaps pin top
Description: """"h3. Issue In iOS the annotations on both retina and non-retina devices are offset from the pins top and they also overlap the pin. The annotation should be directly above the pin with no overlap like iOS does in its native maps application. The screenshots illustrate this issue. Notice in the native maps application the caret is directly above the pin. It does not overlap the pin and it is not offset. h3. Sample Code """"",title+description
TIMOB-10218,12,"""iOS: Simulator sometimes launches twice""","""""""h2. Problem    When you run a Titanium app in the iPhone Simulator -- either from Titanium Studio or from the command-line """"""""builder.py run myproj"""""""" -- it sometimes launches two instances of the iPhone Simulator instead of one.  Then, one of them displays an error message complaining that the iPhone Simulator is already running.    Expected behavior: Only one instance of iPhone Simulator launches.    Note, this bug is intermittent -- it only happens once in a while.    h2. Test case    1. Create app for iPhone  2. Run it in iPhone Simulator  3. If only one iPhone Simulator opens, run it again.  Keep repeating; eventually, two simulators will open.  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Simulator sometimes launches twice
Description: """"h2. Problem When you run a Titanium app in the iPhone Simulator -- either from Titanium Studio or from the command-line """"""""builder.py run myproj"""""""" -- it sometimes launches two instances of the iPhone Simulator instead of one. Then, one of them displays an error message complaining that the iPhone Simulator is already running. Expected behavior: Only one instance of iPhone Simulator launches. Note, this bug is intermittent -- it only happens once in a while. h2. Test case 1. Create app for iPhone 2. Run it in iPhone Simulator 3. If only one iPhone Simulator opens, run it again. Keep repeating; eventually, two simulators will open. """"",title+description
TIMOB-10215,12,"""Tooling: SDK build should fail outright if JSCA file creation has a runtime error""","""""""A runtime error can occur during JSCA file creation (a late step in the SDK build <USER> and yet not stop the build.  This means that the SDK can then be packaged (zipped up) with a faulty .jsca file, which of course should not be allowed.    Instead, if the JSCA file creation <USER>contains an error, the build should fail hard.    h3. Fail case / test case    1. Open apidoc/Titanium/Titanium.yml in a text editor.  2. Put a space before """"""""name: Titanium"""""""" (i.e, put a space at the beginning of that line.)  Of course that space does not belong there, but we're breaking the file on purpose.  3. Save the file.  4. Run {{python apidoc/docgen.py -f jsca}}. You'll see a runtime error fly by.  This is just proof that there is something wrong with the JSCA file creation.  5. Run {{scons}}.  One of the last steps of the scons <USER>will be """"""""Generating JSCA..."""""""".  6. In the fail case, the build will still say it succeeded, and the zip file will be packaged as if nothing happened.  7. When testing the fix, the build *should fail* since an error is occurring (though not visible) during the JSCA file creation.  8. Check in dist/ folder when the build fails: the relevant zip(s) should not be there.""""""",Bug,"Title: Tooling: SDK build should fail outright if JSCA file creation has a runtime error
Description: """"A runtime error can occur during JSCA file creation (a late step in the SDK build <USER> and yet not stop the build. This means that the SDK can then be packaged (zipped up) with a faulty .jsca file, which of course should not be allowed. Instead, if the JSCA file creation <USER>contains an error, the build should fail hard. h3. Fail case / test case 1. Open apidoc/Titanium/Titanium.yml in a text editor. 2. Put a space before """"""""name: Titanium"""""""" (i.e, put a space at the beginning of that line.) Of course that space does not belong there, but we're breaking the file on purpose. 3. Save the file. 4. Run {{python apidoc/docgen.py -f jsca}}. You'll see a runtime error fly by. This is just proof that there is something wrong with the JSCA file creation. 5. Run {{scons}}. One of the last steps of the scons <USER>will be """"""""Generating JSCA..."""""""". 6. In the fail case, the build will still say it succeeded, and the zip file will be packaged as if nothing happened. 7. When testing the fix, the build *should fail* since an error is occurring (though not visible) during the JSCA file creation. 8. Check in dist/ folder when the build fails: the relevant zip(s) should not be there.""""",title+description
TIMOB-10214,12,"""TiAPI: Expose broadcast receiver API""","""""""In order to complete this project for the customer we need the receiver API exposed in Titanium - http://developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/receiver-element.html    Use case:  We need to use the broadcast / receiver API to launch a transparent activity that sits on top of the phone app when a phone call is received and during the call.  <USER>has some more details and a sample native app.""""""",New Feature,"Title: TiAPI: Expose broadcast receiver API
Description: """"In order to complete this project for the customer we need the receiver API exposed in Titanium - http://developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/receiver-element.html Use case: We need to use the broadcast / receiver API to launch a transparent activity that sits on top of the phone app when a phone call is received and during the call. <USER>has some more details and a sample native app.""""",title+description
TIMOB-10213,12,"""TiAPI: Ti.Locale.getString() and L() accept arbitrary functions""","""""""h2. Expected result    Ti.Locale.getString() and it's alias L() shouldn't accept a function as an argument. Both should throw an error is one is supplied.    h2. Actual result    You can pass arbitrary functions, for example:    Ti.Locale.getString(alert('foo'))  <- shows the alert box  Ti.Locale.getString(console.log('foo'))  <- logs to the console    """"""",Bug,"Title: TiAPI: Ti.Locale.getString() and L() accept arbitrary functions
Description: """"h2. Expected result Ti.Locale.getString() and it's alias L() shouldn't accept a function as an argument. Both should throw an error is one is supplied. h2. Actual result You can pass arbitrary functions, for example: Ti.Locale.getString(alert('foo')) <- shows the alert box Ti.Locale.getString(console.log('foo')) <- logs to the console """"",title+description
