TIMOB-10022,12,"""Android: Unwanted transparent background on lightweight window in some cases only under Honeycomb (not ICS or greater, not Gingerbread or lower)""","""""""If a lightweight window has a view on it which has a backgroundColor specified, sometimes that whole window's background will become transparent and show the window below it.  Sample app.js:    {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({   exitOnClose: true,   backgroundColor: """"""""blue""""""""  });    win.addEventListener(""""""""click"""""""", function() {   var w = Ti.UI.createWindow({    backgroundColor: """"""""white""""""""   });   w.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({    bottom: """"""""5dp"""""""", backgroundColor: """"""""red"""""""",    color: """"""""yellow"""""""", left: """"""""0dp"""""""", right: """"""""0dp"""""""",    title: """"""""I am a label on the second window""""""""   }));     w.addEventListener(""""""""click"""""""", function() {w.close();});   w.open();  });    win.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({   text: """"""""I am a label on the first window""""""""  }));    win.open();  {code}    * Run the app on 3.1 or 3.2 Honeycomb.    * Tap the window to open the second window.    * In the failcase, the first window will still show through (in addition to the label on the second window.)    As of this time, 2.4% of Android users are using Honeycomb, and this failcase is fairly specific and easily worked around (use a heavy window).  I think it's low priority.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Unwanted transparent background on lightweight window in some cases only under Honeycomb (not ICS or greater, not Gingerbread or lower)
Description: """"If a lightweight window has a view on it which has a backgroundColor specified, sometimes that whole window's background will become transparent and show the window below it. Sample app.js: {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ exitOnClose: true, backgroundColor: """"""""blue"""""""" }); win.addEventListener(""""""""click"""""""", function() { var w = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: """"""""white"""""""" }); w.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({ bottom: """"""""5dp"""""""", backgroundColor: """"""""red"""""""", color: """"""""yellow"""""""", left: """"""""0dp"""""""", right: """"""""0dp"""""""", title: """"""""I am a label on the second window"""""""" })); w.addEventListener(""""""""click"""""""", function() {w.close();}); w.open(); }); win.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({ text: """"""""I am a label on the first window"""""""" })); win.open(); {code} * Run the app on 3.1 or 3.2 Honeycomb. * Tap the window to open the second window. * In the failcase, the first window will still show through (in addition to the label on the second window.) As of this time, 2.4% of Android users are using Honeycomb, and this failcase is fairly specific and easily worked around (use a heavy window). I think it's low priority.""""",title+description
TIMOB-10021,12,"""iOS: Cannot save rotated camera images after converting to Blob""","""""""h3. Issue    Customer needs to to take a picture in landscape orientation and convert it to a Blob so it can be uploaded to their server. Since, we can't force the orientation of camera, the workaround is to take the image in any orientation and then rotate it to landscape. However, after calling toBlob() on the rotated image inside the returned TiBlob is not rotated. It is still the original image.    Here is the sample code:        h3. Steps to Reproduce    1. Start the app and take a picture and save it.  2. It is saved to an imageview. Click on the imageview to rotate the image.  3. View the saved photo. The image is saved as the originally taken and not the rotated.    There is also a related JIRA for Android: https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-7848  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Cannot save rotated camera images after converting to Blob
Description: """"h3. Issue Customer needs to to take a picture in landscape orientation and convert it to a Blob so it can be uploaded to their server. Since, we can't force the orientation of camera, the workaround is to take the image in any orientation and then rotate it to landscape. However, after calling toBlob() on the rotated image inside the returned TiBlob is not rotated. It is still the original image. Here is the sample code: h3. Steps to Reproduce 1. Start the app and take a picture and save it. 2. It is saved to an imageview. Click on the imageview to rotate the image. 3. View the saved photo. The image is saved as the originally taken and not the rotated. There is also a related JIRA for Android: https://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-7848 """"",title+description
