TIMOB-9381,12,"""Blackberry: Validate the UI optimizations""","""""""Ti.UI docs mentions the following:    """"""""Optimizations  UI objects are optimized by Titanium to not be realized into the drawing context and placed into the device UI surface until needed. That means that you can create UI objects, set their properties and add them to their hierarchy without much worry about memory or performance.  When the native drawing surface needs to render a specific view or control, Titanium will automatically create the view as needed. Additionally, Titanium is optimized to also release memory once the view is no longer needed, on screen or in low memory situations. However, it's a good idea to help Titanium along in certain cases where you are no longer using objects. For example, you should call close on a Window instance when you are no longer using it. You can safely call open on the window again to re-open it.""""""""      Profiling should be realized to verify if any modifications are necessary to align with this documented behavior.""""""",Story,"Title: Blackberry: Validate the UI optimizations
Description: """"Ti.UI docs mentions the following: """"""""Optimizations UI objects are optimized by Titanium to not be realized into the drawing context and placed into the device UI surface until needed. That means that you can create UI objects, set their properties and add them to their hierarchy without much worry about memory or performance. When the native drawing surface needs to render a specific view or control, Titanium will automatically create the view as needed. Additionally, Titanium is optimized to also release memory once the view is no longer needed, on screen or in low memory situations. However, it's a good idea to help Titanium along in certain cases where you are no longer using objects. For example, you should call close on a Window instance when you are no longer using it. You can safely call open on the window again to re-open it."""""""" Profiling should be realized to verify if any modifications are necessary to align with this documented behavior.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9379,12,"""Anvil:  implement Driver and harness for Android, iOS and Mobile Web""","""""""Implement new Anvil Driver and harness for Android, iOS and Mobile Web.  Test creation and porting will be a different ticket.  This task revolves around getting a working Driver for all existing platforms.""""""",Sub-task,"Title: Anvil: implement Driver and harness for Android, iOS and Mobile Web
Description: """"Implement new Anvil Driver and harness for Android, iOS and Mobile Web. Test creation and porting will be a different ticket. This task revolves around getting a working Driver for all existing platforms.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9377,12,"""ANDROID: startActivityForResult callback is not executed""","""""""h2. Problem    When you invoke external apps using startActivityForResult, callback function that must be executed to extract return info. It never happens until you close main Window.    I have found that the problem comes in the way of how to create the main window. Specifically the problem is the parameter """"""""navBarHidden"""""""". If you use it in Titanium.UI.createWindow, startActivityForResult goes wrong.    h2. Test case    {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  var win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({      title: 'SelectFile',      backgroundColor: '#fff',      navBarHidden: true  });  var selectFile = Titanium.UI.createButton({      top: 10, left: 10, right: 10, height: 35, title: 'Select File'  });  win.add(selectFile);    win.addEventListener('android:back', function(e) {   Ti.API.debug(""""""""You press back Button"""""""");          win.close();  });    win.addEventListener('close', function(e) {   Ti.API.debug(""""""""main Window closed"""""""");  });    win.open();    Ti.API.debug(""""""""mainWindow open"""""""");    selectFile.addEventListener('click', function() {  Ti.API.debug(""""""""selectFile clicked"""""""");  var intent = Ti.Android.createIntent({     action: Ti.Android.ACTION_GET_CONTENT,     type: """"""""*/*"""""""",      });    intent.addCategory(Ti.Android.CATEGORY_OPENABLE);   var chooser = Ti.Android.createIntentChooser(intent, """"""""Select File"""""""");    try {    Ti.API.debug(""""""""Lets go with intent"""""""");    var activity = Ti.Android.currentActivity.startActivityForResult(chooser, function(e) {     Ti.API.debug(""""""""e:""""""""+JSON.stringify(e));     if (e.resultCode === Ti.Android.RESULT_OK){      Ti.API.debug(""""""""e.resultCode:""""""""+e.resultCode);      Ti.UI.createNotification({                      duration: Ti.UI.NOTIFICATION_DURATION_LONG,                      message: 'File selected'                  }).show();     }       })    }    catch(e) {     Ti.API.debug(e); alert(""""""""No app installed!"""""""");    }   });        h2. Discussions    http://developer.<USER>com/question/137709/startactivityforresult-and-callback-function-problem""""""",Bug,"Title: ANDROID: startActivityForResult callback is not executed
Description: """"h2. Problem When you invoke external apps using startActivityForResult, callback function that must be executed to extract return info. It never happens until you close main Window. I have found that the problem comes in the way of how to create the main window. Specifically the problem is the parameter """"""""navBarHidden"""""""". If you use it in Titanium.UI.createWindow, startActivityForResult goes wrong. h2. Test case {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} var win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ title: 'SelectFile', backgroundColor: '#fff', navBarHidden: true }); var selectFile = Titanium.UI.createButton({ top: 10, left: 10, right: 10, height: 35, title: 'Select File' }); win.add(selectFile); win.addEventListener('android:back', function(e) { Ti.API.debug(""""""""You press back Button""""""""); win.close(); }); win.addEventListener('close', function(e) { Ti.API.debug(""""""""main Window closed""""""""); }); win.open(); Ti.API.debug(""""""""mainWindow open""""""""); selectFile.addEventListener('click', function() { Ti.API.debug(""""""""selectFile clicked""""""""); var intent = Ti.Android.createIntent({ action: Ti.Android.ACTION_GET_CONTENT, type: """"""""*/*"""""""", }); intent.addCategory(Ti.Android.CATEGORY_OPENABLE); var chooser = Ti.Android.createIntentChooser(intent, """"""""Select File""""""""); try { Ti.API.debug(""""""""Lets go with intent""""""""); var activity = Ti.Android.currentActivity.startActivityForResult(chooser, function(e) { Ti.API.debug(""""""""e:""""""""+JSON.stringify(e)); if (e.resultCode === Ti.Android.RESULT_OK){ Ti.API.debug(""""""""e.resultCode:""""""""+e.resultCode); Ti.UI.createNotification({ duration: Ti.UI.NOTIFICATION_DURATION_LONG, message: 'File selected' }).show(); } }) } catch(e) { Ti.API.debug(e); alert(""""""""No app installed!""""""""); } }); h2. Discussions http://developer.<USER>com/question/137709/startactivityforresult-and-callback-function-problem""""",title+description
TIMOB-9376,12,"""iOS: 'click' event fires on a view upon finger-down instead of finger-up""","""""""h4.Problem  The 'click' event of a view fires when the view is touched, even before the finger (or mouse) is released.    A true 'click' does not (should not) exist until the component receives a mouse-down followed by a mouse-up without the mouse moving in between.  The iOS implementation is not adhering to this definition.    h4.Expected behavior  When you touch and hold your finger (or mouse) down on a view, only the touchstart, longpress, and longclick (Android only) events should fire.  The click event should not fire until the finger (or mouse) is lifted, and then only if the finger (or mouse) has not moved since the touch started.    h4.Actual behavior  When you touch and hold your finger (or mouse) down on a view on the iPhone simulator, the click event fires.    Works on Android correctly.  Broken on iOS.    Therefore, this is also a parity issue.    h4.Testcase  app.js:  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: 'click' event fires on a view upon finger-down instead of finger-up
Description: """"h4.Problem The 'click' event of a view fires when the view is touched, even before the finger (or mouse) is released. A true 'click' does not (should not) exist until the component receives a mouse-down followed by a mouse-up without the mouse moving in between. The iOS implementation is not adhering to this definition. h4.Expected behavior When you touch and hold your finger (or mouse) down on a view, only the touchstart, longpress, and longclick (Android only) events should fire. The click event should not fire until the finger (or mouse) is lifted, and then only if the finger (or mouse) has not moved since the touch started. h4.Actual behavior When you touch and hold your finger (or mouse) down on a view on the iPhone simulator, the click event fires. Works on Android correctly. Broken on iOS. Therefore, this is also a parity issue. h4.Testcase app.js: """"",title+description
TIMOB-9375,12,"""Android: Command-Line: Titanium project can be created through command line interface with java keyword in App id""","""""""Using the Titanium Create command through the command line, a titanium mobile project can be created that has a java keyword in the App Id. The same cannot be created using the Titanium Studio interface.  This is not a regression and occurs as far back as 1.8.2    For example:  titanium create --platform=android --android=<path to android sdk> --id=COM.<USER>case --name=case --dir=/<USER>Testing    Actual: The project gets created successfully. However while trying to run the created project an error is shown as [ERROR] Do not use java keywords for project app id, such as case    Expected: The creation of project having java keyword in App Id should be restricted through command line as well""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Command-Line: Titanium project can be created through command line interface with java keyword in App id
Description: """"Using the Titanium Create command through the command line, a titanium mobile project can be created that has a java keyword in the App Id. The same cannot be created using the Titanium Studio interface. This is not a regression and occurs as far back as 1.8.2 For example: titanium create --platform=android --android=<path to android sdk> --id=COM.<USER>case --name=case --dir=/<USER>Testing Actual: The project gets created successfully. However while trying to run the created project an error is shown as [ERROR] Do not use java keywords for project app id, such as case Expected: The creation of project having java keyword in App Id should be restricted through command line as well""""",title+description
TIMOB-9374,12,"""iOS: Titanium.Map.Annotation: When clicked anywhere on the map(outside the annotation) while annotation is open, alert displays with clicksource as null and annotation closes.""","""""""When clicked anywhere on the map(outside the annotation) while annotation is open, alert displays with clicksource as null.(Refer test case TIMOB-7272)    Steps to Reproduce:  1. Launch application.  2. Click on the pin. Annotation is displayed.  3. Click outside the annotation.    Actual: The alert is displayed with clicksource as null and annotation closes.   Expected: Annotation should not close.    Note: Also occured on 1.8.2              """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Titanium.Map.Annotation: When clicked anywhere on the map(outside the annotation) while annotation is open, alert displays with clicksource as null and annotation closes.
Description: """"When clicked anywhere on the map(outside the annotation) while annotation is open, alert displays with clicksource as null.(Refer test case TIMOB-7272) Steps to Reproduce: 1. Launch application. 2. Click on the pin. Annotation is displayed. 3. Click outside the annotation. Actual: The alert is displayed with clicksource as null and annotation closes. Expected: Annotation should not close. Note: Also occured on 1.8.2 """"",title+description
TIMOB-9373,12,"""iOS: TableView Events: After long press on two rows simultaneously,one row remains highllighted even after Touch release""","""""""After long press on two rows simultaneously,one row remains selected even after touch release    This is not a regression.It even occurs as far as 1.8.2    Steps to reproduce:  1. Install and launch KS   2. Go to Base UI > Views > Table Views > Touch Events  3. Press and hold on a row with """"""""Row Label"""""""" under your finger  4. without releasing the first row,Press and hold another row with """"""""Row Label"""""""" under your finger.  5. Slightly swipe and release the finger of the row that you pressed first,without releasing the second.  6. Now remove the finger from the second row as well.    Actual: One row( the second selected row) remains highlighted even after the touch release and continues to remain highlighted even on Clicking the other rows of the table view. The selection of the row is removed only after clicking on the same row again.   Expected: Row should not remain highlighted after the touch release.    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: TableView Events: After long press on two rows simultaneously,one row remains highllighted even after Touch release
Description: """"After long press on two rows simultaneously,one row remains selected even after touch release This is not a regression.It even occurs as far as 1.8.2 Steps to reproduce: 1. Install and launch KS 2. Go to Base UI > Views > Table Views > Touch Events 3. Press and hold on a row with """"""""Row Label"""""""" under your finger 4. without releasing the first row,Press and hold another row with """"""""Row Label"""""""" under your finger. 5. Slightly swipe and release the finger of the row that you pressed first,without releasing the second. 6. Now remove the finger from the second row as well. Actual: One row( the second selected row) remains highlighted even after the touch release and continues to remain highlighted even on Clicking the other rows of the table view. The selection of the row is removed only after clicking on the same row again. Expected: Row should not remain highlighted after the touch release. """"",title+description
TIMOB-9372,12,"""TiAPI: Define vertical swipe event behavior for Ti.UI.TableView""","""""""Should TableView support vertical swiping? iOS does not. Mobile Web it does. TableViews scroll, so the swipe and scrolling conflict. Need to settle on a convention and create parity tickets for each platforms.""""""",Bug,"Title: TiAPI: Define vertical swipe event behavior for Ti.UI.TableView
Description: """"Should TableView support vertical swiping? iOS does not. Mobile Web it does. TableViews scroll, so the swipe and scrolling conflict. Need to settle on a convention and create parity tickets for each platforms.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9371,12,"""Android: Ti.Platform.displayCaps.platformHeight and platformWidth do not work properly on different Android screen resolutions""","""""""h3. Problem    Please run the sample code below:        In iOS, you see the blue draggable view alright but in Android, depending on the screen size the view is not visible. I tested with QVGA (where it works) and HVGA(where it does not work) avd screens. Please see the attached screenshot too.    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Ti.Platform.displayCaps.platformHeight and platformWidth do not work properly on different Android screen resolutions
Description: """"h3. Problem Please run the sample code below: In iOS, you see the blue draggable view alright but in Android, depending on the screen size the view is not visible. I tested with QVGA (where it works) and HVGA(where it does not work) avd screens. Please see the attached screenshot too. """"",title+description
TIMOB-9370,12,"""MobileWeb: Improve performance of scrollableview""","""""""ScrollableView is very slow on device. Too many layouts are generated, too many containers are created, and the type of animation used is inefficient on mobile devices. This part of the code needs to be rearchitected to be more efficient.""""""",Sub-task,"Title: MobileWeb: Improve performance of scrollableview
Description: """"ScrollableView is very slow on device. Too many layouts are generated, too many containers are created, and the type of animation used is inefficient on mobile devices. This part of the code needs to be rearchitected to be more efficient.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9368,12,"""Code Processor: Implement Accidental Global Variable Declaration Code Processor plugin. ""","""""""It is easy to accidentally create global variables that can have difficult to diagnose consequences. For example:    var x,        y;        z;    creates a global variable 'z'. This plugin should throw a warning whenever a global variable is created without referencing the global object. In other words, this is a best practice enforcer plugin.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Code Processor: Implement Accidental Global Variable Declaration Code Processor plugin.
Description: """"It is easy to accidentally create global variables that can have difficult to diagnose consequences. For example: var x, y; z; creates a global variable 'z'. This plugin should throw a warning whenever a global variable is created without referencing the global object. In other words, this is a best practice enforcer plugin.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9367,12,"""CodeProcessor: Implement Reseved Keyword Warning Code Processor plugin""","""""""There are many variable names that cannot be used in Mobile Web projects that can be used in other platforms, such as """"""""window"""""""", """"""""status"""""""", """"""""document"""""""", etc. This plugin should detect these instances and throw an error/warning.""""""",New Feature,"Title: CodeProcessor: Implement Reseved Keyword Warning Code Processor plugin
Description: """"There are many variable names that cannot be used in Mobile Web projects that can be used in other platforms, such as """"""""window"""""""", """"""""status"""""""", """"""""document"""""""", etc. This plugin should detect these instances and throw an error/warning.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9363,12,"""Android: Application forced close after taking picture from camera or selecting picture from gallery on first load.""","""""""In android application when I am taking picture from camera and hitting """"""""OK"""""""" or selecting image from gallery,application will forced close.This is happen when I install application on android device and using first time. after forced close when I again restart application and use this functionality,its working fine. When I tried with android 2.2 and Titanium SDK 1.8.0.1 it's working proper even on first load also and without any error and forced close.      """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Application forced close after taking picture from camera or selecting picture from gallery on first load.
Description: """"In android application when I am taking picture from camera and hitting """"""""OK"""""""" or selecting image from gallery,application will forced close.This is happen when I install application on android device and using first time. after forced close when I again restart application and use this functionality,its working fine. When I tried with android 2.2 and Titanium SDK 1.8.0.1 it's working proper even on first load also and without any error and forced close. """"",title+description
TIMOB-9362,12,"""Android: first use of camera crashes on SDK 2.0.1+ (works on 1.8.2-)""","""""""There is a consistent crash when using the camera on Android:    - Install your application (eg. kitchensink) by downloading the .apk (NOT by deploying to the Android device via Eclipse).     - The first time you use the camera, after you've taken the picture, as soon as you hit """"""""Save"""""""", the app will crash before your camera success callback is called    Once you restart the app the camera save function works fine - ie. the crash only occurs on first use of camera.    To isolate this I created the simplest possible app that only fires up the camera and takes a picture. The camera code is taken directly from KitchenSink. You can also replicate the bug using KitchenSink.    The app works fine when built using the 1.7.2 and 1.8.2 SDK, and crashes on first use when built with the 2.0.1 or 2.0.2 SDK.    So it seems somewhere between 1.8.2 and 2.0.1 the camera code for Android broke.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: first use of camera crashes on SDK 2.0.1+ (works on 1.8.2-)
Description: """"There is a consistent crash when using the camera on Android: - Install your application (eg. kitchensink) by downloading the .apk (NOT by deploying to the Android device via Eclipse). - The first time you use the camera, after you've taken the picture, as soon as you hit """"""""Save"""""""", the app will crash before your camera success callback is called Once you restart the app the camera save function works fine - ie. the crash only occurs on first use of camera. To isolate this I created the simplest possible app that only fires up the camera and takes a picture. The camera code is taken directly from KitchenSink. You can also replicate the bug using KitchenSink. The app works fine when built using the 1.7.2 and 1.8.2 SDK, and crashes on first use when built with the 2.0.1 or 2.0.2 SDK. So it seems somewhere between 1.8.2 and 2.0.1 the camera code for Android broke.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9361,12,"""Android: WebView has no error event""","""""""WRT Github pull request : https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/910    When an Android webView attempts to load a non-existent url (for example), no error is fired (as documented!)     {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid}  // example: app.js    var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({    backgroundColor:'#fff'    });    var web = Ti.UI.createWebView({    // a clearly incorrect url    url:'http://google.cooom'    });    web.addEventListener('error',function(e){     Ti.API.info('ERROR: '+JSON.stringify(e));   alert('ERROR: type:'+e.type+'\nurl:'+e.url);    });    win.add(web);  win.open();    {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: WebView has no error event
Description: """"WRT Github pull request : https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/910 When an Android webView attempts to load a non-existent url (for example), no error is fired (as documented!) {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid} // example: app.js var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor:'#fff' }); var web = Ti.UI.createWebView({ // a clearly incorrect url url:'http://google.cooom' }); web.addEventListener('error',function(e){ Ti.API.info('ERROR: '+JSON.stringify(e)); alert('ERROR: type:'+e.type+'\nurl:'+e.url); }); win.add(web); win.open(); {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-9359,12,"""iOS: Window content disappears when open, close and re-open again""","""""""See the sample code below, WebView completely disappears from Window when the Window re-opened. It does NOT happen if bugged window source code loaded from external JS file (i.e., loaded with """"""""url"""""""" property).    Code has been tested with 2.0.1GA2, 2.0.2GA and latest Git master (2.1.0 2012-06-03)    Issue may be related with the ticket TIMOB-2912    FILE: app.js          FILE: local.html    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Window content disappears when open, close and re-open again
Description: """"See the sample code below, WebView completely disappears from Window when the Window re-opened. It does NOT happen if bugged window source code loaded from external JS file (i.e., loaded with """"""""url"""""""" property). Code has been tested with 2.0.1GA2, 2.0.2GA and latest Git master (2.1.0 2012-06-03) Issue may be related with the ticket TIMOB-2912 FILE: app.js FILE: local.html """"",title+description
TIMOB-9358,12,"""Android: KS XML RSS test does not load""","""""""Reproduction steps:    1. Launch KS  2. Go to platform > XML RSS    Expected results:  The xml test loads with a list of articles    Actual results:  the RSS XML test does not load, and is a black screen.  In the logs, you will see """"""""(TiHttpClient-2) [9,122279] HTTP Error (java.lang.ClassCastException): org.apache.http.message.BasicHttpRequest""""""""  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: KS XML RSS test does not load
Description: """"Reproduction steps: 1. Launch KS 2. Go to platform > XML RSS Expected results: The xml test loads with a list of articles Actual results: the RSS XML test does not load, and is a black screen. In the logs, you will see """"""""(TiHttpClient-2) [9,122279] HTTP Error (java.lang.ClassCastException): org.apache.http.message.BasicHttpRequest"""""""" """"",title+description
TIMOB-9357,12,"""MobileWeb: View Touch Event Coordinates Parity, Global and Local""","""""""h1. Problem  The coordinates provided in touch events are inconsistent with our other platforms.    In particular:  - The local point (evt.x) is the absolute position. It should be relative to the containing view.  - I cannot tell what the global point is in reference to. It seems miscalculated, but that is only an observation.    h2. Reproduction  The following creates a vertical scroll view with a series of boxes in it. Drag one of the boxes to the right, and it will move faster than your finger as the global point is miscalculated. There are several console logs to illuminate the disparity in the platforms.  {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({      backgroundColor: '#fff'  });    var scroll = Ti.UI.createScrollView({      scrollType: 'vertical',      contentHeight: 3010,      canCancelEvents: false,      bottom: 60  });  var width = 150, height = 90;  for (var i = 0; i < 30; i++) {      scroll.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({          draggable: true,          text: 'Drag Me Horizontally ' + (i + 1), textAlign: 'center',          color: '#000',          top: i * 100 + 10,          width: width, height: height,          left: 0,          backgroundColor: '#eee'      }));  }  scroll.addEventListener('touchstart', curry('Touch Start'));  scroll.addEventListener('touchmove', curry('Touch Move'));  scroll.addEventListener('touchcancel', curry('Touch Cancel'));  scroll.addEventListener('touchend', curry('Touch End'));  win.add(scroll);  win.open();    function curry(eventName) {      return function (evt) {          if (evt.source && evt.source.draggable) {              var global = evt.source.convertPointToView({ x: evt.x, y: evt.y }, scroll);              console.log(eventName + ' evt.x: ' + evt.x);              console.log(eventName + ' global.x: ' + global.x);              evt.source.left = global.x - width / 2;          }      }  }  {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: View Touch Event Coordinates Parity, Global and Local
Description: """"h1. Problem The coordinates provided in touch events are inconsistent with our other platforms. In particular: - The local point (evt.x) is the absolute position. It should be relative to the containing view. - I cannot tell what the global point is in reference to. It seems miscalculated, but that is only an observation. h2. Reproduction The following creates a vertical scroll view with a series of boxes in it. Drag one of the boxes to the right, and it will move faster than your finger as the global point is miscalculated. There are several console logs to illuminate the disparity in the platforms. {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: '#fff' }); var scroll = Ti.UI.createScrollView({ scrollType: 'vertical', contentHeight: 3010, canCancelEvents: false, bottom: 60 }); var width = 150, height = 90; for (var i = 0; i < 30; i++) { scroll.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({ draggable: true, text: 'Drag Me Horizontally ' + (i + 1), textAlign: 'center', color: '#000', top: i * 100 + 10, width: width, height: height, left: 0, backgroundColor: '#eee' })); } scroll.addEventListener('touchstart', curry('Touch Start')); scroll.addEventListener('touchmove', curry('Touch Move')); scroll.addEventListener('touchcancel', curry('Touch Cancel')); scroll.addEventListener('touchend', curry('Touch End')); win.add(scroll); win.open(); function curry(eventName) { return function (evt) { if (evt.source && evt.source.draggable) { var global = evt.source.convertPointToView({ x: evt.x, y: evt.y }, scroll); console.log(eventName + ' evt.x: ' + evt.x); console.log(eventName + ' global.x: ' + global.x); evt.source.left = global.x - width / 2; } } } {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-9356,12,"""MobileWeb: Storing a label to a variable named """"status"""" causes the label to be caste to a string.""","""""""h1. Problem  Storing a label in a variable and then calling window.add(with that variable) causes an app exception. Directly adding the label to the window does not, nor does cascading an assignment with the window.add call.    h2. Error  {quote}  Uncaught TypeError: Object [object Object] has no method '_setParent' :8083/:4421  declare._add :8083/:4421  declare.add :8083/:13596  (anonymous function)  {quote}  Line 8083 is:  {code:title=app.js}  _add: function(view) {      view._setParent(this);      this.children.push(view);      this.containerNode.appendChild(view.domNode);      view._hasBeenLaidOut = false;      this._triggerLayout(this._isAttachedToActiveWin());  },      h2. Workaround  See the reproduction's """"""""else"""""""" case.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Storing a label to a variable named """"status"""" causes the label to be caste to a string.
Description: """"h1. Problem Storing a label in a variable and then calling window.add(with that variable) causes an app exception. Directly adding the label to the window does not, nor does cascading an assignment with the window.add call. h2. Error {quote} Uncaught TypeError: Object [object Object] has no method '_setParent' :8083/:4421 declare._add :8083/:4421 declare.add :8083/:13596 (anonymous function) {quote} Line 8083 is: {code:title=app.js} _add: function(view) { view._setParent(this); this.children.push(view); this.containerNode.appendChild(view.domNode); view._hasBeenLaidOut = false; this._triggerLayout(this._isAttachedToActiveWin()); }, h2. Workaround See the reproduction's """"""""else"""""""" case.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9355,12,"""iOS: Splash Screen Orientation - Status Bar not shown in initial portrait mode on launching application with device's top pointing right""","""""""Status Bar not showing up in initial portrait mode on launching application with device's top pointing right    This is not a Regression.It occurs as far back as 1.8.2.    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Install the app on to the device.  2. Hold device so that top is pointing right and launch app.    Tiapp.xml        Expected Result:  Splash screen and status bar launches in portrait mode and then the status bar is in landscape left mode     Actual Result:  Splash screen launches in portrait mode  but status bar launches in landscape left mode   """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Splash Screen Orientation - Status Bar not shown in initial portrait mode on launching application with device's top pointing right
Description: """"Status Bar not showing up in initial portrait mode on launching application with device's top pointing right This is not a Regression.It occurs as far back as 1.8.2. Steps to Reproduce: 1. Install the app on to the device. 2. Hold device so that top is pointing right and launch app. Tiapp.xml Expected Result: Splash screen and status bar launches in portrait mode and then the status bar is in landscape left mode Actual Result: Splash screen launches in portrait mode but status bar launches in landscape left mode """"",title+description
TIMOB-9354,12,"""MobileWeb: Too many click and singletap events fired for Tab and TabGroup""","""""""h2. Expected behavior    Click events should be fired on the tab regardless of whether or not the tab is currently selected.    h2. Actual behavior    The click event in a tab element of a tabGroup is not fired in either Titanium versions listed above    Note that this also did not work on Android 1.7.5, but the issue has been fixed in Android 1.8.0.1. Thus, this is a parity issue to match a recent Android feature request (see linked ticket).    h2. Test case    With the following code, no click event is fired on iOS when tab1 is clicked:    {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  Ti.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000');    var tabGroup = Ti.UI.createTabGroup();    var win1 = Ti.UI.createWindow({    title:'Tab 1',    backgroundColor:'#fff'  });  var tab1 = Ti.UI.createTab({    title:'Tab 1',    window:win1  });    var label1 = Ti.UI.createLabel({    color:'#999',    text:'I am Window 1',    textAlign:'center',    width:'auto'  });    win1.add(label1);    var win2 = Ti.UI.createWindow({    title:'Tab 2',    backgroundColor:'#fff'  });  var tab2 = Ti.UI.createTab({    title:'Tab 2',    window:win2  });    var label2 = Ti.UI.createLabel({    color:'#999',    text:'I am Window 2',    textAlign:'center',    width:'auto'  });    win2.add(label2);    tabGroup.addTab(tab1);  tabGroup.addTab(tab2);    tabGroup.open();    tab1.addEventListener('click', function(e) {    Ti.API.info(""""""""tab1 clicked."""""""");  });  {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Too many click and singletap events fired for Tab and TabGroup
Description: """"h2. Expected behavior Click events should be fired on the tab regardless of whether or not the tab is currently selected. h2. Actual behavior The click event in a tab element of a tabGroup is not fired in either Titanium versions listed above Note that this also did not work on Android 1.7.5, but the issue has been fixed in Android 1.8.0.1. Thus, this is a parity issue to match a recent Android feature request (see linked ticket). h2. Test case With the following code, no click event is fired on iOS when tab1 is clicked: {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} Ti.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var tabGroup = Ti.UI.createTabGroup(); var win1 = Ti.UI.createWindow({ title:'Tab 1', backgroundColor:'#fff' }); var tab1 = Ti.UI.createTab({ title:'Tab 1', window:win1 }); var label1 = Ti.UI.createLabel({ color:'#999', text:'I am Window 1', textAlign:'center', width:'auto' }); win1.add(label1); var win2 = Ti.UI.createWindow({ title:'Tab 2', backgroundColor:'#fff' }); var tab2 = Ti.UI.createTab({ title:'Tab 2', window:win2 }); var label2 = Ti.UI.createLabel({ color:'#999', text:'I am Window 2', textAlign:'center', width:'auto' }); win2.add(label2); tabGroup.addTab(tab1); tabGroup.addTab(tab2); tabGroup.open(); tab1.addEventListener('click', function(e) { Ti.API.info(""""""""tab1 clicked.""""""""); }); {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-9352,12,"""MobileWeb: Add selectAll() function for TextField and Textarea""","""""""Ticket TIMOB-8299 introduced a new selectAll() function for TextField and Textarea. This should be super simple to support using the built-in select() function.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Add selectAll() function for TextField and Textarea
Description: """"Ticket TIMOB-8299 introduced a new selectAll() function for TextField and Textarea. This should be super simple to support using the built-in select() function.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9350,12,"""Android: Exception when Titanium.App.Properties.getDouble is called for a value stored as an Int""","""""""h3. Problem    When calling Titanium.App.Properties.getDouble() on iOS for a value stored via Ti.App.Properties.setInt(), the exception (as shown in the screenshot) is thrown.    h3. Steps to Reproduce    Run the app.js file below:        The above code works fine on iOS.    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Exception when Titanium.App.Properties.getDouble is called for a value stored as an Int
Description: """"h3. Problem When calling Titanium.App.Properties.getDouble() on iOS for a value stored via Ti.App.Properties.setInt(), the exception (as shown in the screenshot) is thrown. h3. Steps to Reproduce Run the app.js file below: The above code works fine on iOS. """"",title+description
TIMOB-9349,12,"""TiAPI: Ti.UI.WebView event handling for nested Titanium Mobile Web apps""","""""""WebViews insert a little bridge that allows the HTML page inside the WebView to fire events to the WebView. If the WebView contains a Mobile Web app, then there's a collision of Ti.App.fireEvent(), Ti.App.addEventListener(), and Ti.App.removeEventListener().    We need to standardize on an API for specifying a target for fireEvent().    Should fireEvent() of the inner Mobile Web app fire the event both for everything in the WebView AND the WebView? Should we have an additional argument to fireEvent that identifies a target? Should we allow a prefix such as """"""""parent://"""""""" to the event name?""""""",Bug,"Title: TiAPI: Ti.UI.WebView event handling for nested Titanium Mobile Web apps
Description: """"WebViews insert a little bridge that allows the HTML page inside the WebView to fire events to the WebView. If the WebView contains a Mobile Web app, then there's a collision of Ti.App.fireEvent(), Ti.App.addEventListener(), and Ti.App.removeEventListener(). We need to standardize on an API for specifying a target for fireEvent(). Should fireEvent() of the inner Mobile Web app fire the event both for everything in the WebView AND the WebView? Should we have an additional argument to fireEvent that identifies a target? Should we allow a prefix such as """"""""parent://"""""""" to the event name?""""",title+description
TIMOB-9348,12,"""MobileWeb: Rearchitect how annotations are stored internally""","""""""Annotations are currently stored in an array. If an annotation is removed, the array element is nulled, but not removed. If you add and remove annotations a lot, the array becomes too big. Plus the annotations array is gettable with all those nulls. Adding a hash to lookup annotations by title and splicing annotations out of the array should solve the problem""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Rearchitect how annotations are stored internally
Description: """"Annotations are currently stored in an array. If an annotation is removed, the array element is nulled, but not removed. If you add and remove annotations a lot, the array becomes too big. Plus the annotations array is gettable with all those nulls. Adding a hash to lookup annotations by title and splicing annotations out of the array should solve the problem""""",title+description
TIMOB-9347,12,"""MobileWeb: Remove inactive windows/tabs/etc from the DOM and add them back when reactivated.""","""""""When a window is inactivated because a new window is opened, a new tab is selected, etc, we should remove the inactivated window from the DOM and remove it from layout calculations to improve performance. Slow reflow on mobile devices is killing performance.""""""",Sub-task,"Title: MobileWeb: Remove inactive windows/tabs/etc from the DOM and add them back when reactivated.
Description: """"When a window is inactivated because a new window is opened, a new tab is selected, etc, we should remove the inactivated window from the DOM and remove it from layout calculations to improve performance. Slow reflow on mobile devices is killing performance.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9346,12,"""MobileWeb: ScrollableView with no views causes error on swipe""","""""""There's a bug in ScrollableView where if it doesn't have any views, the swipe event causes _add() to get fired a ton and error when trying to set view._hidden = hidden.    """"""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: ScrollableView with no views causes error on swipe
Description: """"There's a bug in ScrollableView where if it doesn't have any views, the swipe event causes _add() to get fired a ton and error when trying to set view._hidden = hidden. """"",title+description
TIMOB-9345,12,"""MobileWeb: WebView bridge overrides Ti namespace of page inside the WebView""","""""""When loading a mobile web app in a webview, it squashes the titanium object from the inner mobile web app. Need to only define the bridge if it's not already a titanium page.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: WebView bridge overrides Ti namespace of page inside the WebView
Description: """"When loading a mobile web app in a webview, it squashes the titanium object from the inner mobile web app. Need to only define the bridge if it's not already a titanium page.""""",title+description
