TIMOB-9342,12,"""MobileWeb: Ensure all UI elements properly disconnect events and nodes on destroy()""","""""""There are a couple of places where not all events, gestures, domnodes are not detached/destroyed when the UI element is explicitly destroyed.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Ensure all UI elements properly disconnect events and nodes on destroy()
Description: """"There are a couple of places where not all events, gestures, domnodes are not detached/destroyed when the UI element is explicitly destroyed.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9341,12,"""Android: Support Scroll View canCancelEvents""","""""""h1. Feature  iOS supports a """"""""canCancelEvents"""""""" property on scroll views to let you control if events are cancelled when a scroll view scrolls.    h2. Expected Behavior  It should behave as iOS does. When """"""""canCancelEvents"""""""" is false, touchCancel shouldn't fire when scrolling the scroll view. But if """"""""canCancelEvents"""""""" is true, then use the existing behavior of canceling a touch after moving enough in a scrollable direction.    ... Note that canCancelEvents is presently broken on iOS: [TIMOB-7893].    h2. Reproduction  Drop the following in an app.js. It adds a vertically scrollable list of draggable rectangles. On iOS, once [TIMOB-7893] gets fixed, you can drag them horizontally. You can also scroll vertically without interrupting the drag. On Android, any vertical movement cancels the scroll.  {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({      backgroundColor: '#fff'  });    var status = Ti.UI.createLabel({      text: ' ', textAlign: 'center',      zIndex: 1,      right: 0, bottom: 0, left: 0,      height: 60,      backgroundColor: 'black',      color: 'white'  });  win.add(status);    var scroll = Ti.UI.createScrollView({      scrollType: 'vertical',      contentHeight: 3010,      canCancelEvents: false,      bottom: 60  });  var width = 150, height = 90;  for (var i = 0; i < 30; i++) {      scroll.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({          draggable: true,          text: 'Drag Me Horizontally ' + (i + 1), textAlign: 'center',          color: '#000',          top: i * 100 + 10,          width: width, height: height,          backgroundColor: '#eee'      }));  }  scroll.addEventListener('touchstart', curry('Touch Start'));  scroll.addEventListener('touchmove', curry('Touch Move'));  scroll.addEventListener('touchcancel', curry('Touch Cancel'));  scroll.addEventListener('touchend', curry('Touch End'));  win.add(scroll);  win.open();    function curry(eventName) {      return function (evt) {          if (evt.source && evt.source.draggable) {              status.text = eventName + ' at { x: ' + (evt.x | 0) + ', y: ' + (evt.y | 0) + ' }';              var global = evt.source.convertPointToView({ x: evt.x, y: evt.y }, scroll);              evt.source.left = global.x - width / 2;          }      }  }  {code}""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Support Scroll View canCancelEvents
Description: """"h1. Feature iOS supports a """"""""canCancelEvents"""""""" property on scroll views to let you control if events are cancelled when a scroll view scrolls. h2. Expected Behavior It should behave as iOS does. When """"""""canCancelEvents"""""""" is false, touchCancel shouldn't fire when scrolling the scroll view. But if """"""""canCancelEvents"""""""" is true, then use the existing behavior of canceling a touch after moving enough in a scrollable direction. ... Note that canCancelEvents is presently broken on iOS: [TIMOB-7893]. h2. Reproduction Drop the following in an app.js. It adds a vertically scrollable list of draggable rectangles. On iOS, once [TIMOB-7893] gets fixed, you can drag them horizontally. You can also scroll vertically without interrupting the drag. On Android, any vertical movement cancels the scroll. {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: '#fff' }); var status = Ti.UI.createLabel({ text: ' ', textAlign: 'center', zIndex: 1, right: 0, bottom: 0, left: 0, height: 60, backgroundColor: 'black', color: 'white' }); win.add(status); var scroll = Ti.UI.createScrollView({ scrollType: 'vertical', contentHeight: 3010, canCancelEvents: false, bottom: 60 }); var width = 150, height = 90; for (var i = 0; i < 30; i++) { scroll.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({ draggable: true, text: 'Drag Me Horizontally ' + (i + 1), textAlign: 'center', color: '#000', top: i * 100 + 10, width: width, height: height, backgroundColor: '#eee' })); } scroll.addEventListener('touchstart', curry('Touch Start')); scroll.addEventListener('touchmove', curry('Touch Move')); scroll.addEventListener('touchcancel', curry('Touch Cancel')); scroll.addEventListener('touchend', curry('Touch End')); win.add(scroll); win.open(); function curry(eventName) { return function (evt) { if (evt.source && evt.source.draggable) { status.text = eventName + ' at { x: ' + (evt.x | 0) + ', y: ' + (evt.y | 0) + ' }'; var global = evt.source.convertPointToView({ x: evt.x, y: evt.y }, scroll); evt.source.left = global.x - width / 2; } } } {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-9340,12,"""iOS: Titanium classes not found when compiling for device""","""""""h3. Problem  A Titnaium class, like Ti.UI.createWindow(), will not be included in the app if deployed to device and file in which it is declared is in the iphone folder.    h3. To reporduce:  1. Create a new app  2. Place this is app.js    3. Create a new file and call it mainwindow.js  4. Place this file in the iphone folder (inside of the Resources)  5. Place this code in that file:    6. Run the app in the simulator  7. In the sim everything runs fine.  8. Run the app in the real device  9. red screen of death will pop up!    h3. My guess of the source of the problem  - compiler.py - ignoreDirs = ['.git','.svn','_svn','CVS','android','iphone','mobileweb'];    The code gets copied to the final built, but apparently the add_symbol() is never called in those files in the """"""""iphone"""""""" dir inside the Resources dir.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Titanium classes not found when compiling for device
Description: """"h3. Problem A Titnaium class, like Ti.UI.createWindow(), will not be included in the app if deployed to device and file in which it is declared is in the iphone folder. h3. To reporduce: 1. Create a new app 2. Place this is app.js 3. Create a new file and call it mainwindow.js 4. Place this file in the iphone folder (inside of the Resources) 5. Place this code in that file: 6. Run the app in the simulator 7. In the sim everything runs fine. 8. Run the app in the real device 9. red screen of death will pop up! h3. My guess of the source of the problem - compiler.py - ignoreDirs = ['.git','.svn','_svn','CVS','android','iphone','mobileweb']; The code gets copied to the final built, but apparently the add_symbol() is never called in those files in the """"""""iphone"""""""" dir inside the Resources dir.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9338,12,"""iOS: Picker inside ScrollView is not working in iOS 4.3""","""""""h2. Problem description  When creating a Picker inside a ScrollView in iOS 4.3, the picker does not scroll, but the ScrollView does instead. The same code works with iOS 5+    h2. Steps to reproduce    1. create an app with the code below  2. run the app with iOS 4.3  2. scroll the picker  3. the picker does not scroll, but the scrollview does, instead        h2. Expected result  Picker should work and the view should not scroll    h2. Additional notes  Customer ticket: http://support-admin.<USER>com/display/17325""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Picker inside ScrollView is not working in iOS 4.3
Description: """"h2. Problem description When creating a Picker inside a ScrollView in iOS 4.3, the picker does not scroll, but the ScrollView does instead. The same code works with iOS 5+ h2. Steps to reproduce 1. create an app with the code below 2. run the app with iOS 4.3 2. scroll the picker 3. the picker does not scroll, but the scrollview does, instead h2. Expected result Picker should work and the view should not scroll h2. Additional notes Customer ticket: http://support-admin.<USER>com/display/17325""""",title+description
TIMOB-9337,12,"""iOS: Clicking on table view search result row closes the search.""","""""""*Description*    In a table view search bar, after searching for a table row, clicking on the result will immediately close the search.    *Sample Code*        *Steps to Reproduce*    1. Search """"""""apples"""""""".  2. Click on search result row.  3. Search will close.    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Clicking on table view search result row closes the search.
