TIMOB-9186,12,"""iOS: Implement backgroundImage for toolbars in iOS using setBackgroundImage:forToolbarPosition:barMetrics:""","""""""This is a suggested improvement for toolbars. iOS 5 now has ability to apply a proper BG image to toolbars. This means we can now make nice toolbars without the Glassy effect. Applied fix both to Ti.UI.iOS.Toolbar as well as to Window.setToolbar.     Why no pull request?    Because I'm not 100% confident in what I've done. It works for me, and is a good starting point - but someone with a more obj-c/titanium experience should probably review it. For example, you'll see some duplication. Feedback welcome!    I've attached a diff of the changes I made (against the 2.0.X branch) as well as example app.js showing usage.""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Implement backgroundImage for toolbars in iOS using setBackgroundImage:forToolbarPosition:barMetrics:
Description: """"This is a suggested improvement for toolbars. iOS 5 now has ability to apply a proper BG image to toolbars. This means we can now make nice toolbars without the Glassy effect. Applied fix both to Ti.UI.iOS.Toolbar as well as to Window.setToolbar. Why no pull request? Because I'm not 100% confident in what I've done. It works for me, and is a good starting point - but someone with a more obj-c/titanium experience should probably review it. For example, you'll see some duplication. Feedback welcome! I've attached a diff of the changes I made (against the 2.0.X branch) as well as example app.js showing usage.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9185,12,"""Tooling: support void return types""","""""""Currently, if we explicitly specify a return type of void, validate.py throws warnings:        This works in docgen because it just formats the unrecognized return type, but it does throw a warning in validate.    For some methods, we need to specify void as one of several return types, like:        So we should really support it as a type value.  """"""",New Feature,"Title: Tooling: support void return types
Description: """"Currently, if we explicitly specify a return type of void, validate.py throws warnings: This works in docgen because it just formats the unrecognized return type, but it does throw a warning in validate. For some methods, we need to specify void as one of several return types, like: So we should really support it as a type value. """"",title+description
TIMOB-9184,12,"""Module : Barcode module crashes when an i18n/en/strings.xml file is pressent in root of an application. ""","""""""The barcode module 2.3.0 in Titanium crashes when an i18n/en/strings.xml file is pressent in root of an application.  h4. Repo Steps  1. Create a new Mobile App select the Single Window Template (it will create the i18n folder). Copy the content of the Example app.js from the module folder in to your project's app.js  2. Run the app in Android device. Click the Scan Code button and the app will crash.""""""",Bug,"Title: Module : Barcode module crashes when an i18n/en/strings.xml file is pressent in root of an application.
Description: """"The barcode module 2.3.0 in Titanium crashes when an i18n/en/strings.xml file is pressent in root of an application. h4. Repo Steps 1. Create a new Mobile App select the Single Window Template (it will create the i18n folder). Copy the content of the Example app.js from the module folder in to your project's app.js 2. Run the app in Android device. Click the Scan Code button and the app will crash.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9183,12,"""iOS: Swipe event does not work for up and down""","""""""h3. The """"""""swipe"""""""" event on the Ti.UI.View only works for left and right, up and down are missing.        h3. As we can see from running this code, the event only fires when swiped left and right. Swiping up and down does nothing.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Swipe event does not work for up and down
Description: """"h3. The """"""""swipe"""""""" event on the Ti.UI.View only works for left and right, up and down are missing. h3. As we can see from running this code, the event only fires when swiped left and right. Swiping up and down does nothing.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9182,12,"""MobileWeb: Blob's nativePath default value needs to be null""","""""""Turns out the default value of the nativePath needs to be null and not an empty string.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Blob's nativePath default value needs to be null
Description: """"Turns out the default value of the nativePath needs to be null and not an empty string.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9181,12,"""Ti API: UI composite specification update""","""""""Related to TIMOB-8121, when parent view has dimension value set to SIZE and child view has FILL, the way that views will size themselves is undefined/unspecified. It seems that all platforms have some behavior defined. Because of that, this case should be specified so that all platform implement same behavior.      Currently, Mobile web in that situation looks for parent view that has dimension defined and size itself according to the size of that parent (it FILLs parent). IMHO, more intuitive behavior would be if parent would SIZE itself according to child size (if there is a child with fixed dimension value).       For example:    Window      ParentView (SIZE)          ChildView (FILL)             View (50px)    My suggestion is that ParentView should have 50px (and ChildView also), while Mobile web solution would size ChildView to size of Window (and ParentView also).    It seems to me that parent elements should have priority in cases like this.    """"""",New Feature,"Title: Ti API: UI composite specification update
Description: """"Related to TIMOB-8121, when parent view has dimension value set to SIZE and child view has FILL, the way that views will size themselves is undefined/unspecified. It seems that all platforms have some behavior defined. Because of that, this case should be specified so that all platform implement same behavior. Currently, Mobile web in that situation looks for parent view that has dimension defined and size itself according to the size of that parent (it FILLs parent). IMHO, more intuitive behavior would be if parent would SIZE itself according to child size (if there is a child with fixed dimension value). For example: Window ParentView (SIZE) ChildView (FILL) View (50px) My suggestion is that ParentView should have 50px (and ChildView also), while Mobile web solution would size ChildView to size of Window (and ParentView also). It seems to me that parent elements should have priority in cases like this. """"",title+description
TIMOB-9180,12,"""CLI: 'create' builtin for commonJS""","""""""Need to add options for creating commonJS javascript modules. This should include the necessary components for packaging the module:    * build.py script  * documentation folder  * example folder  * LICENSE file  * manifest file  * src folder  * timodule.xml    It may be possible to use the mobileweb option from the current titanium.py with some modifications.""""""",Sub-task,"Title: CLI: 'create' builtin for commonJS
Description: """"Need to add options for creating commonJS javascript modules. This should include the necessary components for packaging the module: * build.py script * documentation folder * example folder * LICENSE file * manifest file * src folder * timodule.xml It may be possible to use the mobileweb option from the current titanium.py with some modifications.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9179,12,"""CLI: 'build', 'install', 'run' for CommonJS modules""","""""""Need the ability to package javascript modules (commonJS) to include:    * Code security (obfuscation or encryption)  * Documentation  * Example  * Manifest  * License    Can perhaps use the build.py script used for mobileweb modules as a model    May be a duplicate of mobileweb module support.""""""",Sub-task,"Title: CLI: 'build', 'install', 'run' for CommonJS modules
Description: """"Need the ability to package javascript modules (commonJS) to include: * Code security (obfuscation or encryption) * Documentation * Example * Manifest * License Can perhaps use the build.py script used for mobileweb modules as a model May be a duplicate of mobileweb module support.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9178,12,"""Tooling: Enforce ti.verify for javascript modules""","""""""We need to be able to support ti.verify on javascript (commonJS and MobileWeb) modules. This is a requirement for some of the enterprise-related modules that we are developing. In addition, module developers would like to put their javascript modules on the MarketPlace with ti.verify being used to confirm authorization to use the module.""""""",Story,"Title: Tooling: Enforce ti.verify for javascript modules
Description: """"We need to be able to support ti.verify on javascript (commonJS and MobileWeb) modules. This is a requirement for some of the enterprise-related modules that we are developing. In addition, module developers would like to put their javascript modules on the MarketPlace with ti.verify being used to confirm authorization to use the module.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9177,12,"""MobileWeb: Update animate() to cancel animations""","""""""Apparently, the only way to stop an animation is to call animate() without any arguments.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Update animate() to cancel animations
Description: """"Apparently, the only way to stop an animation is to call animate() without any arguments.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9176,12,"""MobileWeb: Implement Windows and TabGroups focus/blur events""","""""""The base class of Window and TabGroup, SuperView, needs to add back the logic that tracks all SuperViews on a stack so that focus/blur events are fired correctly. This was there, but was ripped out by accident when we ripped out the back button support.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Implement Windows and TabGroups focus/blur events
Description: """"The base class of Window and TabGroup, SuperView, needs to add back the logic that tracks all SuperViews on a stack so that focus/blur events are fired correctly. This was there, but was ripped out by accident when we ripped out the back button support.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9175,12,"""iOS: Blob.nativePath throws exception for non-File Blobs""","""""""The following code throws an exception:        I would expect this to return some kind of value to indicate that nativePath is not set. Instead, it throws an exception:    [ERROR] Script Error = *** -[NSURL initFileURLWithPath:]: nil string parameter at app.js (line 28).    In this circumstance, Android returns {{null}}, and MobileWeb returns the empty string. I believe that returning {{null}} is the intended behavior on iOS.  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Blob.nativePath throws exception for non-File Blobs
Description: """"The following code throws an exception: I would expect this to return some kind of value to indicate that nativePath is not set. Instead, it throws an exception: [ERROR] Script Error = *** -[NSURL initFileURLWithPath:]: nil string parameter at app.js (line 28). In this circumstance, Android returns {{null}}, and MobileWeb returns the empty string. I believe that returning {{null}} is the intended behavior on iOS. """"",title+description
