TIMOB-9171,12,"""iOS: Window: Cannot compile an application to device when Window is not created""","""""""Regression: This does not occur in 2.0.1GA2     Step to Reproduce:  1. Create a new application  2. Add in the code below:    3. Compile to iOS device    Actual:  [INFO] Performing clean build  [ERROR] /Users/<USER>Documents/Titanium Studio Workspace/Failure/build/iphone/Classes/TiUIWindow.m:37:7: error: use of undeclared identifier 'TiUIWindowProxy'; did you mean 'TiUIWindow'? [3]  [ERROR] /Users/<USER>Documents/Titanium Studio Workspace/Failure/build/iphone/Classes/TiUIWindow.m:37:23: error: expected expression [1]  [ERROR] /Users/<USER>Documents/Titanium Studio Workspace/Failure/build/iphone/Classes/TiUIWindow.m:37:30: error: expected ']' [1]  [ERROR] /Users/<USER>Documents/Titanium Studio Workspace/Failure/build/iphone/Classes/TiUIWindow.m:37:53: error: missing '[' at start of message send expression [3]  [ERROR]   [ERROR] Error: Traceback (most recent call last):    File """"""""/Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/2.0.2.v20120517153200/iphone/builder.py"""""""", line 1465, in main      execute_xcode(""""""""iphoneos%s"""""""" % iphone_version,args,False)    File """"""""/Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/2.0.2.v20120517153200/iphone/builder.py"""""""", line 1224, in execute_xcode      output = run.run(args,False,False,o)    File """"""""/Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/2.0.2.v20120517153200/iphone/run.py"""""""", line 41, in run      sys.exit(rc)  SystemExit: 65    Expected:  Should compile with no error. Worked in 2.0.1GA2    Workaround:  Add this code to it: win = Ti.UI.createWindow();""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Window: Cannot compile an application to device when Window is not created
Description: """"Regression: This does not occur in 2.0.1GA2 Step to Reproduce: 1. Create a new application 2. Add in the code below: 3. Compile to iOS device Actual: [INFO] Performing clean build [ERROR] /Users/<USER>Documents/Titanium Studio Workspace/Failure/build/iphone/Classes/TiUIWindow.m:37:7: error: use of undeclared identifier 'TiUIWindowProxy'; did you mean 'TiUIWindow'? [3] [ERROR] /Users/<USER>Documents/Titanium Studio Workspace/Failure/build/iphone/Classes/TiUIWindow.m:37:23: error: expected expression [1] [ERROR] /Users/<USER>Documents/Titanium Studio Workspace/Failure/build/iphone/Classes/TiUIWindow.m:37:30: error: expected ']' [1] [ERROR] /Users/<USER>Documents/Titanium Studio Workspace/Failure/build/iphone/Classes/TiUIWindow.m:37:53: error: missing '[' at start of message send expression [3] [ERROR] [ERROR] Error: Traceback (most recent call last): File """"""""/Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/2.0.2.v20120517153200/iphone/builder.py"""""""", line 1465, in main execute_xcode(""""""""iphoneos%s"""""""" % iphone_version,args,False) File """"""""/Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/2.0.2.v20120517153200/iphone/builder.py"""""""", line 1224, in execute_xcode output = run.run(args,False,False,o) File """"""""/Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/2.0.2.v20120517153200/iphone/run.py"""""""", line 41, in run sys.exit(rc) SystemExit: 65 Expected: Should compile with no error. Worked in 2.0.1GA2 Workaround: Add this code to it: win = Ti.UI.createWindow();""""",title+description
TIMOB-9170,12,"""Android: run V8 against x86 emulator""","""""""h2. Problem    Currently developers familiar with how to using Android's command line tooling can run Titanium-built APKs against Android new x86 emulator, so long as those APKs were built to run against the Rhino runtime. If the apps are built to run against the V8 runtime, they fail with the following errors from logcat indicating issues with missing stlport libraries. If necessary I can recreate these logs, but they should occur anytime you run a Titanium-built android app using V8 on the Android x86 emulator.    h2. Proposed Solution    The way I understand it is that V8 is compiled with the Android NDK. All code compiled with the Android NDK is architecture-specific, and to this point it has only been built for ARM architectures. I'm sure I'm oversimplifying, but a build of V8 against the x86 architecture would likely make it possible for V8-driven Titanium Android apps to be run on the Android x86 emulator.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: run V8 against x86 emulator
Description: """"h2. Problem Currently developers familiar with how to using Android's command line tooling can run Titanium-built APKs against Android new x86 emulator, so long as those APKs were built to run against the Rhino runtime. If the apps are built to run against the V8 runtime, they fail with the following errors from logcat indicating issues with missing stlport libraries. If necessary I can recreate these logs, but they should occur anytime you run a Titanium-built android app using V8 on the Android x86 emulator. h2. Proposed Solution The way I understand it is that V8 is compiled with the Android NDK. All code compiled with the Android NDK is architecture-specific, and to this point it has only been built for ARM architectures. I'm sure I'm oversimplifying, but a build of V8 against the x86 architecture would likely make it possible for V8-driven Titanium Android apps to be run on the Android x86 emulator.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9169,12,"""iOS: Text Area: The focus on text area does not happen through focus method call after toggling the enabled state of text area""","""""""The focus on text area does not happen and the keyboard is not shown when focus for the text area is called after disabling and then enabling (i.e. toggling the editable state) the text area.    Steps:  1. Create and open the app  2. click on text area or Focus button  3. Click on Toggle """"""""editable"""""""" button  4. Click on Focus  5. Again Click on Toggle """"""""editable"""""""" button  6. Now click on Focus    Expected Result:  After Step2: Text area is enabled and keyboard shows  After Step4: Focus on text area does not happen and keyboard does not show up  After Step6: Focus on text area happen and keyboard show up    Actual Result:  After Step6: The focus on the text area does not happen on click of Focus button. However, if the user taps on text area and gains focus on it, the focus button starts behaving correctly.    App.js  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Text Area: The focus on text area does not happen through focus method call after toggling the enabled state of text area
Description: """"The focus on text area does not happen and the keyboard is not shown when focus for the text area is called after disabling and then enabling (i.e. toggling the editable state) the text area. Steps: 1. Create and open the app 2. click on text area or Focus button 3. Click on Toggle """"""""editable"""""""" button 4. Click on Focus 5. Again Click on Toggle """"""""editable"""""""" button 6. Now click on Focus Expected Result: After Step2: Text area is enabled and keyboard shows After Step4: Focus on text area does not happen and keyboard does not show up After Step6: Focus on text area happen and keyboard show up Actual Result: After Step6: The focus on the text area does not happen on click of Focus button. However, if the user taps on text area and gains focus on it, the focus button starts behaving correctly. App.js """"",title+description
TIMOB-9168,12,"""Drillbit: ti.cloud - If you try to run the ti.cloud tests for Android (v8), drillbit will hang at build""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  0. Pull latest changes from 2_0_X branch  1. Create a default TiMobile project  2. Go to my.<USER>com and find app  3. Click on Manage button  4. Click on the Users link and create the following users with password:  drillbituser, password  chatuser, password    5. Next, unzip the attachment and place it in <your_git_workspace>/titanium_mobile/drillbit/tests  6. From the newly created TiMobile project, copy the tiapp.xml and paste it into <your_git_workspace>/titanium_mobile/drillbit/tests/cloud  7. Launch 2.2 (google api 8) Android emulator  8. Run drillbit for Android    Actual: Drillbit will hang at build (see screen shot)  Expected: Drillbit should not hang on build when testing ti.cloud    Note:  * The above steps works for iOS Simulator 5.1  * Using 1.2.0.RC4""""""",Bug,"Title: Drillbit: ti.cloud - If you try to run the ti.cloud tests for Android (v8), drillbit will hang at build
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 0. Pull latest changes from 2_0_X branch 1. Create a default TiMobile project 2. Go to my.<USER>com and find app 3. Click on Manage button 4. Click on the Users link and create the following users with password: drillbituser, password chatuser, password 5. Next, unzip the attachment and place it in <your_git_workspace>/titanium_mobile/drillbit/tests 6. From the newly created TiMobile project, copy the tiapp.xml and paste it into <your_git_workspace>/titanium_mobile/drillbit/tests/cloud 7. Launch 2.2 (google api 8) Android emulator 8. Run drillbit for Android Actual: Drillbit will hang at build (see screen shot) Expected: Drillbit should not hang on build when testing ti.cloud Note: * The above steps works for iOS Simulator 5.1 * Using 1.2.0.RC4""""",title+description
TIMOB-9167,12,"""iOS: Range and sanity check memory allocations via malloc that would reasonably be over 2KB.""","""""""An analysis was performed, and checking for the possibility malloc producing a NULL value is not being done. In the majority of cases, these allocations are small constant sizes (such as the size of an internet address structure) and do not need checks (The iOS watchdog would be shutting things down long beforehand). However, I did note a few places where such checks may be of use:    * AsyncUdpSocket.m uses a DEFAULT_MAX_RECEIVE_BUFFER_SIZE of 9KB.  * AudioStreamer relies on getting a value from iOS for the size of the buffer to allocate.  * NSData * decode64 (NSData * thedata) may be dealing with base64 encodings of binary blobs in the multi-kilobytes.  * -\[TiDataStream writeToStream:chunkSize:callback:\] and -\[TiDataStream pumpToCallback:chunkSize:synch:\] does not check the sign or value of size before using it in a malloc, nor does its subclass TiFilesystemStreamProxy.  * TiNetworkTCPSocketProxy uses a bufferSize of 4KB.  * TiUIWebView and UtilsModule, when preparing to Base64Encode, do not check the malloc caused by an estimated encoded data size.""""""",Story,"Title: iOS: Range and sanity check memory allocations via malloc that would reasonably be over 2KB.
Description: """"An analysis was performed, and checking for the possibility malloc producing a NULL value is not being done. In the majority of cases, these allocations are small constant sizes (such as the size of an internet address structure) and do not need checks (The iOS watchdog would be shutting things down long beforehand). However, I did note a few places where such checks may be of use: * AsyncUdpSocket.m uses a DEFAULT_MAX_RECEIVE_BUFFER_SIZE of 9KB. * AudioStreamer relies on getting a value from iOS for the size of the buffer to allocate. * NSData * decode64 (NSData * thedata) may be dealing with base64 encodings of binary blobs in the multi-kilobytes. * -\[TiDataStream writeToStream:chunkSize:callback:\] and -\[TiDataStream pumpToCallback:chunkSize:synch:\] does not check the sign or value of size before using it in a malloc, nor does its subclass TiFilesystemStreamProxy. * TiNetworkTCPSocketProxy uses a bufferSize of 4KB. * TiUIWebView and UtilsModule, when preparing to Base64Encode, do not check the malloc caused by an estimated encoded data size.""""",title+description
TIMOB-9166,12,"""iOS: Button background image scaling issues when button height is smaller than its background image""","""""""h3. Problem    There is a scaling issue when a button's height is set to be smaller than its background image. It appears that though the background top cap and left cap are set they are not respected when the background image is scaled. Setting the button height to be the height of the background image fixes this problem--as shown in the screenshot.    This did not happen Release 1.8.2 but occurs from the head of 1.8.x branch(1.8.3) to Release 2.0.1GA2    h3. Sample Code        The background image is also attached.    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Button background image scaling issues when button height is smaller than its background image
Description: """"h3. Problem There is a scaling issue when a button's height is set to be smaller than its background image. It appears that though the background top cap and left cap are set they are not respected when the background image is scaled. Setting the button height to be the height of the background image fixes this problem--as shown in the screenshot. This did not happen Release 1.8.2 but occurs from the head of 1.8.x branch(1.8.3) to Release 2.0.1GA2 h3. Sample Code The background image is also attached. """"",title+description
