TIMOB-8982,12,"""iOS: Ti.UI.SIZE on the view, does not work on its child labels, that need to wrap text""","""""""In the example,     we have two adjacent labels and a view in window and we want the view that contains the two labels to size its height to the largest label. The first label contains text that should wrap onto multiple lines. Instead of doing that it ellipsizes the text.    This behavior was possible in 1.8 versions of the SDK.    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Ti.UI.SIZE on the view, does not work on its child labels, that need to wrap text
Description: """"In the example, we have two adjacent labels and a view in window and we want the view that contains the two labels to size its height to the largest label. The first label contains text that should wrap onto multiple lines. Instead of doing that it ellipsizes the text. This behavior was possible in 1.8 versions of the SDK. """"",title+description
TIMOB-8981,12,"""iOS: Prevent analytics.db from being backed up to iCloud""","""""""Analytics.db, which is used by Ti Analytics, is being backed up to the iCloud by default, when the storage on device is backed up to iCloud.    The default analytics.db created has a size of 16.0 KB.    This could be found under Documents -> Application Support -> analytics -> analytics.db of the application sandbox.    *Steps to Reproduce*  1. Create a Titanium Mobile project with default template.  2. Load the app on to an iOS device and start the app.  3. Go to Settings app -> iCloud -> Storage & Backup and click """"""""Back Up Now"""""""".  4. After it completes, click Manage Storage and select the backup for that particular device and see the list of apps.  5. It could be seen that the size of backup for this particular app would be 16.0 KB or more.    *Workaround*  1. Turn the analytics off before deploying the app to device through tiapp.xml by setting <analytics> to false.""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Prevent analytics.db from being backed up to iCloud
Description: """"Analytics.db, which is used by Ti Analytics, is being backed up to the iCloud by default, when the storage on device is backed up to iCloud. The default analytics.db created has a size of 16.0 KB. This could be found under Documents -> Application Support -> analytics -> analytics.db of the application sandbox. *Steps to Reproduce* 1. Create a Titanium Mobile project with default template. 2. Load the app on to an iOS device and start the app. 3. Go to Settings app -> iCloud -> Storage & Backup and click """"""""Back Up Now"""""""". 4. After it completes, click Manage Storage and select the backup for that particular device and see the list of apps. 5. It could be seen that the size of backup for this particular app would be 16.0 KB or more. *Workaround* 1. Turn the analytics off before deploying the app to device through tiapp.xml by setting <analytics> to false.""""",title+description
TIMOB-8980,12,"""Android: Horizontal layout should provide consistent behavior when wrapping""","""""""On iOS, Android, and Mobile Web, horizontal layout wraps its children into rows if it has more children than it can fit in the allotted width. There ends the similarity.    On iOS (and soon Mobile Web), the rows are positioned against the top of the view, and the 'top', 'bottom' values are interpreted _relative to the row's top and bottom_.    On Android, the top and bottom values are interpreted relative to the _view's top and bottom_, meaning that you can get children out of order, on top of each other, etc. Higgeldy-piggeldy.     I contend that this serves no rational purpose and should be changed.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Horizontal layout should provide consistent behavior when wrapping
Description: """"On iOS, Android, and Mobile Web, horizontal layout wraps its children into rows if it has more children than it can fit in the allotted width. There ends the similarity. On iOS (and soon Mobile Web), the rows are positioned against the top of the view, and the 'top', 'bottom' values are interpreted _relative to the row's top and bottom_. On Android, the top and bottom values are interpreted relative to the _view's top and bottom_, meaning that you can get children out of order, on top of each other, etc. Higgeldy-piggeldy. I contend that this serves no rational purpose and should be changed.""""",title+description
TIMOB-8979,12,"""iOS: Ti.Media.openPhotoGallery's cancel event doesn't trigger on iPad""","""""""h3. Code        h3. Expected Behavior    The alert in cancel event should get fired.     h3. Actual Behavior    This does not work on the iPad simulator but works fine on iPhone simulator      """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Ti.Media.openPhotoGallery's cancel event doesn't trigger on iPad
Description: """"h3. Code h3. Expected Behavior The alert in cancel event should get fired. h3. Actual Behavior This does not work on the iPad simulator but works fine on iPhone simulator """"",title+description
TIMOB-8978,12,"""Ti API: View.size, View.rect should report values in defaultunit, not system unit""","""""""Currently, it appears that Ti.View.size and Ti.View.rect are reported in system default units, regardless of the setting of ti.ui.defaultunit. On Android, this means that these values are reported in pixels, even if you're specifying your entire UI in DIPs.    (On iOS, they're reported in DIPs, which is usually what you want anyway; and in mobile web, px and DIP are the same thing.)    Is there any reason that we couldn't report these values in defaultunit so that users can do simple math with them without having to convert them back into the unit of their choice?      """"""",Bug,"Title: Ti API: View.size, View.rect should report values in defaultunit, not system unit
Description: """"Currently, it appears that Ti.View.size and Ti.View.rect are reported in system default units, regardless of the setting of ti.ui.defaultunit. On Android, this means that these values are reported in pixels, even if you're specifying your entire UI in DIPs. (On iOS, they're reported in DIPs, which is usually what you want anyway; and in mobile web, px and DIP are the same thing.) Is there any reason that we couldn't report these values in defaultunit so that users can do simple math with them without having to convert them back into the unit of their choice? """"",title+description
TIMOB-8977,12,"""CLI: Research automatically resize images as part of build""","""""""As a developer, I want to create one application icon, one loading screen and have Titanium automatically generate all of the screens and icons necessary for different form factors. See example and code at http://crushmedia.net/2012/04/23/automated-build-of-titanium-iconloading-files/. Based on ImageMagick which is Apache 2.0 and cross-platform.    Might be able to use 9-patch for when aspect ratios are not the same? http://developer.android.com/guide/developing/tools/draw9patch.html""""""",New Feature,"Title: CLI: Research automatically resize images as part of build
Description: """"As a developer, I want to create one application icon, one loading screen and have Titanium automatically generate all of the screens and icons necessary for different form factors. See example and code at http://crushmedia.net/2012/04/23/automated-build-of-titanium-iconloading-files/. Based on ImageMagick which is Apache 2.0 and cross-platform. Might be able to use 9-patch for when aspect ratios are not the same? http://developer.android.com/guide/developing/tools/draw9patch.html""""",title+description
TIMOB-8976,12,"""Android: window focus event it's not triggered when going back to the parent window""","""""""h2. Problem description  Focus eventListener is set in the parent window and it's correctly triggered when opening the window. Moving back from a child window to the parent window does not trigger the Focus event.  This happens both for heavyweight and lightweight children window from 1.8.2 SDK. On 1.7.6 it is correctly triggered only if the child window is a heavyweight.    h2. Customer engagement notes  With the same code on iOS, Focus event it's always triggered.  ING will be using Lightweight windows inside a tabGroup and need to have the same behavior, using same code, between iOS and Android.  This is blocking issue for the customer.    h2. Code to reproduce  Using the sample code below, button in win1 opens a Lightweight child window (win3). When win3 it's closed, focus event it's not triggered using all SDK versions.  Uncomment line 36 (fullscreen:false) in order to make 'win3' heavyweight. When win3 it's closed, focus event it's triggered only using SDK 1.7.6. With any other higher version it's not.    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: window focus event it's not triggered when going back to the parent window
Description: """"h2. Problem description Focus eventListener is set in the parent window and it's correctly triggered when opening the window. Moving back from a child window to the parent window does not trigger the Focus event. This happens both for heavyweight and lightweight children window from 1.8.2 SDK. On 1.7.6 it is correctly triggered only if the child window is a heavyweight. h2. Customer engagement notes With the same code on iOS, Focus event it's always triggered. ING will be using Lightweight windows inside a tabGroup and need to have the same behavior, using same code, between iOS and Android. This is blocking issue for the customer. h2. Code to reproduce Using the sample code below, button in win1 opens a Lightweight child window (win3). When win3 it's closed, focus event it's not triggered using all SDK versions. Uncomment line 36 (fullscreen:false) in order to make 'win3' heavyweight. When win3 it's closed, focus event it's triggered only using SDK 1.7.6. With any other higher version it's not. """"",title+description
TIMOB-8975,12,"""iOS: Enable XSLT transformation, on XML pages, so that formatted XML is rendered in webview""","""""""I have attached the XSL project.     The webview in app.js loads a XML page, which in turns refers to XSL page.     Ideally, the result should be a xml page rendered with styles defined in XSL.    """"""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Enable XSLT transformation, on XML pages, so that formatted XML is rendered in webview
Description: """"I have attached the XSL project. The webview in app.js loads a XML page, which in turns refers to XSL page. Ideally, the result should be a xml page rendered with styles defined in XSL. """"",title+description
TIMOB-8974,12,"""iOS: May be using executionContext for proxy creation when pageContext should be used""","""""""Currently, iOS has many situations in which `-[TiProxy _initWithPageContext:]` does not pass a page context, but rather an execution context. In many situations this is acceptable and they may be identical, but it could be leading to subtle memory-related bugs and cross-context issues. We need to investigate the problem and make sure that each proxy is initialized with an appropriate JS context for its scope and lifespan.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: May be using executionContext for proxy creation when pageContext should be used
Description: """"Currently, iOS has many situations in which `-[TiProxy _initWithPageContext:]` does not pass a page context, but rather an execution context. In many situations this is acceptable and they may be identical, but it could be leading to subtle memory-related bugs and cross-context issues. We need to investigate the problem and make sure that each proxy is initialized with an appropriate JS context for its scope and lifespan.""""",title+description
TIMOB-8973,12,"""iOS: Locking/lock management evaluation""","""""""Currently for iOS, we may have some serious lock management problems. It has been mentioned, in particular, that the `childLock` used for view child add/remove/management could lead to deadlocks from multiple read locks across threads in some situations. Locking is also drastically impacting our speed and ability to perform certain types of operations in iOS (in particular, add/remove).    This task is related to, but not dependent upon, TIMOB-6990 (partially complete; [sptramer/timob-6990|https://github.com/sptramer/titanium_mobile/tree/timob-6990]). That task only takes care of locking situations involving contexts.""""""",Story,"Title: iOS: Locking/lock management evaluation
Description: """"Currently for iOS, we may have some serious lock management problems. It has been mentioned, in particular, that the `childLock` used for view child add/remove/management could lead to deadlocks from multiple read locks across threads in some situations. Locking is also drastically impacting our speed and ability to perform certain types of operations in iOS (in particular, add/remove). This task is related to, but not dependent upon, TIMOB-6990 (partially complete; [sptramer/timob-6990|https://github.com/sptramer/titanium_mobile/tree/timob-6990]). That task only takes care of locking situations involving contexts.""""",title+description
TIMOB-8972,12,"""BlackBerry: Fix problem with passing complex Titanium objects to Cascades""",,Bug,Title: BlackBerry: Fix problem with passing complex Titanium objects to Cascades,title-only
TIMOB-8971,12,"""Android: Kitchen Sink: The basic image does not load, and when you click the cloud image also do not loads""","""""""Steps to Reproduce:  1. Launch Kitchen Sink  2. Run Base UI > Views > Image Views > Basic  3. Tap on where the titanium logo usually loads    Actual Result:  The titanium logo is not loaded, and when you click the area it do not shows the cloud image as well.    Expected Result:  To load the titanium logo and when you click the area, the cloud image loads    On android 2.2.6 and 4.0.2, its showing behavior as per TIMOB-3679.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Kitchen Sink: The basic image does not load, and when you click the cloud image also do not loads
Description: """"Steps to Reproduce: 1. Launch Kitchen Sink 2. Run Base UI > Views > Image Views > Basic 3. Tap on where the titanium logo usually loads Actual Result: The titanium logo is not loaded, and when you click the area it do not shows the cloud image as well. Expected Result: To load the titanium logo and when you click the area, the cloud image loads On android 2.2.6 and 4.0.2, its showing behavior as per TIMOB-3679.""""",title+description
TIMOB-8970,12,"""MobileWeb: Rearchitect animation system""","""""""The current animation system relies on CSS3 transitions. This is great for all devices except IE9 and Windows Phone 7.5.    To support those platforms, we need to add a code path for old school setTimeout() animation.    One possible solution is to scrap CSS3 transitions completely and use requestAnimationFrame with a setInterval() fallback. This would offer greater compatibility and flexibility over transitions.    Here's some articles that might be of interest:    * http://paulirish.com/2011/requestanimationframe-for-smart-animating/  * http://creativejs.com/resources/requestanimationframe  * https://gist.github.com/1002116""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Rearchitect animation system
Description: """"The current animation system relies on CSS3 transitions. This is great for all devices except IE9 and Windows Phone 7.5. To support those platforms, we need to add a code path for old school setTimeout() animation. One possible solution is to scrap CSS3 transitions completely and use requestAnimationFrame with a setInterval() fallback. This would offer greater compatibility and flexibility over transitions. Here's some articles that might be of interest: * http://paulirish.com/2011/requestanimationframe-for-smart-animating/ * http://creativejs.com/resources/requestanimationframe * https://gist.github.com/1002116""""",title+description
