Description: """"In KitchenSink: Base UI > Views > Image Views > Animated Tap on Resume button does not resume the paused animation. When 'resume animation' is tapped """""""" W/TextView(18469): TextView does not support text selection. Action mode cancelled."""""""" is displayed in the log file for some of the devices(LG Slate 3.1,Samsung Galaxy Tab-3.2,Xoom 4.0.2) Steps to reproduce: 1: tap on Image Views 2: tap on Animated 3: tap on Pause Animation 4: tap on Resume Animation Actual Result: Animation does not Resume Expected Result: Animation should Resume """"",title+description
TIMOB-8654,12,"""ACS : Creating a custom failed and produced the error """"Exception in event callback. Argument data was an unexpected type! Expected: object, Received: string""""""","""""""Steps to reproduce:    1. Create a default mobile app with cloud services enabled  2. Copy the attached resources folder  3. Run the app on iOS device or Android Device  4. On iOS launch the console in Xcode, on Android launch ddms or logcat  5. Navigate to the """"""""Custom objects"""""""" row  6. Press the """"""""Create Object"""""""" row  7. Fill out the fields with the required information  8. Press the """"""""Add New Property"""""""" button and fill in the field  9. Press the """"""""Create button""""""""    Actual result:  The following message is logged and the object isn't created: """"""""Exception in event callback. Argument data was an unexpected type! Expected: object, Received: string""""""""    Expected result:  The custom object is created without error""""""",Bug,"Title: ACS : Creating a custom failed and produced the error """"Exception in event callback. Argument data was an unexpected type! Expected: object, Received: string""""
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Create a default mobile app with cloud services enabled 2. Copy the attached resources folder 3. Run the app on iOS device or Android Device 4. On iOS launch the console in Xcode, on Android launch ddms or logcat 5. Navigate to the """"""""Custom objects"""""""" row 6. Press the """"""""Create Object"""""""" row 7. Fill out the fields with the required information 8. Press the """"""""Add New Property"""""""" button and fill in the field 9. Press the """"""""Create button"""""""" Actual result: The following message is logged and the object isn't created: """"""""Exception in event callback. Argument data was an unexpected type! Expected: object, Received: string"""""""" Expected result: The custom object is created without error""""",title+description
TIMOB-8652,12,"""Core: Create a new Titanium Command Line Interface""","""""""This is the top level issue for tracking the new CLI rewrite.     This is a list of high level CLI components and functional components:  h5. App Projects  * Support for existing project structure  ** Javascript and free-form images (""""""""Resources"""""""")  ** Density / configuration specific images  ** Localization  ** JSS  ** Platform specific folder structures  ** Installed modules  ** Build and staging area  * App Project creation  ** Initial configuration  ** Templates  * Build phases based on deploy type (development, production)  ** Staging  ** Code generation  *** Module use detection / optimization  *** Code obfuscation / protection  ** Building  ** Packaging  *** Signing / Provisioning    h5. Module Projects  * Module types: Android, iOS, CommonJS (others?)  * Support for existing project structure  ** Native code location  ** Module YML documentation  ** Example code / project  ** Build and staging area  ** 3rd party dependencies  * Module project creation  ** Templates  * Build phases based on deploy type (development, production)  ** Staging  ** Code generation  *** Code obfuscation / protection for JS  ** Building  *** YML -> HTML generation  ** Packaging    h5. Metadata and Project configuration  * Application ID, author, etc  * Module dependencies (for module projects + app projects)  * Parsing (tiapp.xml + timodule.xml, manifest)    h5. Emulator/Simulator/Device management  * Create/Update/Delete virtual devices (Android, MobileWeb only)  * Emulator/Simulator Launch (Based on virtual device / OS configs)  * Deploy packaged app to emulator / simulator / device  * Detect / list attached device(s)  * Attach Logging to existing device    h5. YML processing and code generation  * YML validator  * """"""""docgen"""""""" Documentation generator  ** HTML generation  ** JSCA generation  ** JSON generation  ** VSDOC generation (?)    h5. CLI unit tests    h5. CLI modules     h5. Odds and Ends  * Common Obfuscation / minification engine  * Fastdev""""""",Epic,"Title: Core: Create a new Titanium Command Line Interface
Description: """"This is the top level issue for tracking the new CLI rewrite. This is a list of high level CLI components and functional components: h5. App Projects * Support for existing project structure ** Javascript and free-form images (""""""""Resources"""""""") ** Density / configuration specific images ** Localization ** JSS ** Platform specific folder structures ** Installed modules ** Build and staging area * App Project creation ** Initial configuration ** Templates * Build phases based on deploy type (development, production) ** Staging ** Code generation *** Module use detection / optimization *** Code obfuscation / protection ** Building ** Packaging *** Signing / Provisioning h5. Module Projects * Module types: Android, iOS, CommonJS (others?) * Support for existing project structure ** Native code location ** Module YML documentation ** Example code / project ** Build and staging area ** 3rd party dependencies * Module project creation ** Templates * Build phases based on deploy type (development, production) ** Staging ** Code generation *** Code obfuscation / protection for JS ** Building *** YML -> HTML generation ** Packaging h5. Metadata and Project configuration * Application ID, author, etc * Module dependencies (for module projects + app projects) * Parsing (tiapp.xml + timodule.xml, manifest) h5. Emulator/Simulator/Device management * Create/Update/Delete virtual devices (Android, MobileWeb only) * Emulator/Simulator Launch (Based on virtual device / OS configs) * Deploy packaged app to emulator / simulator / device * Detect / list attached device(s) * Attach Logging to existing device h5. YML processing and code generation * YML validator * """"""""docgen"""""""" Documentation generator ** HTML generation ** JSCA generation ** JSON generation ** VSDOC generation (?) h5. CLI unit tests h5. CLI modules h5. Odds and Ends * Common Obfuscation / minification engine * Fastdev""""",title+description
TIMOB-8651,12,"""iOS: Ability to disable scrolling in a ScrollView""","""""""Expose the UIScrollView setScrollEnabled property to the JavaScript.    Code in TiUIScrollView.m    {code:title=TiUIScrollView.m}  -(void)setScrollEnabled_:(id)args  {   [[self scrollView] setScrollEnabled:[TiUtils boolValue:args def:YES]];  }    var scroll = Ti.UI.createScrollView({      scrollEnabled: false  });  {code}""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Ability to disable scrolling in a ScrollView
Description: """"Expose the UIScrollView setScrollEnabled property to the JavaScript. Code in TiUIScrollView.m {code:title=TiUIScrollView.m} -(void)setScrollEnabled_:(id)args { [[self scrollView] setScrollEnabled:[TiUtils boolValue:args def:YES]]; } var scroll = Ti.UI.createScrollView({ scrollEnabled: false }); {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-8649,12,"""Android: TextField: Soft keyboard disappears when device is rotated to landscape mode on Droid 2.2.3""","""""""Description:  Soft keyboard disappears when device is rotated to landscape mode.   The same issue also exists on:   - Hide Soft Keyboard (Android) (KitchenSink-Base UI-Hide Soft Keyboard (Android));  - Keyboard (KitchenSink-Controls-Textfield-Keyboard).  This bug occurs only on Droid 2.2.3 and exists also on 1.8.2.    Steps:  1. Run KitchenSink  2. Go to Controls-TextField-Events  3. Tap to text field to get soft keyboard up  4. Rotate device while keyboard is up    Expected result:  Soft keyboard should not disappear    Actual result:  Soft keyboard disappears""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: TextField: Soft keyboard disappears when device is rotated to landscape mode on Droid 2.2.3
Description: """"Description: Soft keyboard disappears when device is rotated to landscape mode. The same issue also exists on: - Hide Soft Keyboard (Android) (KitchenSink-Base UI-Hide Soft Keyboard (Android)); - Keyboard (KitchenSink-Controls-Textfield-Keyboard). This bug occurs only on Droid 2.2.3 and exists also on 1.8.2. Steps: 1. Run KitchenSink 2. Go to Controls-TextField-Events 3. Tap to text field to get soft keyboard up 4. Rotate device while keyboard is up Expected result: Soft keyboard should not disappear Actual result: Soft keyboard disappears""""",title+description
TIMOB-8648,12,"""Android: android:back listener reacts when using """"back"""" to dismiss keyboard""","""""""h4.Problem  On a window with a TextField, tapping the TextField to bring up the keyboard, then tapping the android:back button closes the window.  Doing the same thing on the emulator simply dismisses the keyboard.    h4.Actual Behavior  Clicking the android:back button when the keyboard is displayed closes the window (because the 'android:back' event is fired).    h4.Expected Behavior  Clicking the android:back button when the keyboard is displayed should just dismiss the keyboard, and not fire the 'android:back' event.    h4. Original Testcase  app.js  {code}  var win1=Ti.UI.createWindow({                    layout:'vertical',                    url:'win1.js',                    backgroundColor:'gray',                    exitOnClose:true,                    navBarHidden:true                  });  win1.open();  {code}    *NOTE*: The """"""""android:back"""""""" event is buggy on some devices. It may never fire at all.            You may need to try various devices to reproduce the failure case.            To verify the expected behavior change the event to """"""""androidback"""""""" (no colon).    win1.js  {code}  var win1=Ti.UI.currentWindow;    var TheLabel=Ti.UI.createLabel({          color:'black',          text:'Click the edit, then hit the back button.  Keyboard should be dismissed, but the window should not close.',          width:'auto'       });  win1.add(TheLabel);    var TheTextField=Ti.UI.createTextField(                     {color:'black',                      width:150                     });  win1.add(TheTextField);    function AndroidBackClicked()  {    win1.close();  }  win1.addEventListener('android:back',AndroidBackClicked);  {code}    h4. Test case to verify 'androidback' does not fire when dismissing keyboard.  {code:javascript}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow();  win.add(Ti.UI.createTextField({width: '75%'}));    win.addEventListener('androidback', function() {    alert('back event fired!');  });    win.open();    {code}    1. Run application on a device that uses a soft keyboard.  2. Focus the textfield to bring up the soft keyboard.  3. Hit the """"""""back"""""""" button on the device. You should see NO alert.  4. Hit the """"""""back"""""""" button again. You SHOULD see an alert.    Fail: You see two alerts (one for each """"""""back"""""""" press).  Pass: You only get an alert in step 4.    h4. Test: Closing a series of LWs using the """"""""androidback"""""""" event.  {code:javascript}  function openWindows() {    ['blue', 'red', 'white'].forEach(function(color) {      var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor: color});      win.addEventListener('androidback', function() {        alert('closing window');        win.close();      });      win.open();    });  }    var mainWindow = Ti.UI.createWindow();    var button = Ti.UI.createButton({title: 'Open windows'});  button.addEventListener('click', openWindows);  mainWindow.add(button);    mainWindow.open();  {code}    1. Run application and click """"""""Open windows"""""""".  2. Hit the back button. You should see an alert and the window should close.  3. Repeat step 2 for each window until all of them are closed and you are back to the main window.  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: android:back listener reacts when using """"back"""" to dismiss keyboard
Description: """"h4.Problem On a window with a TextField, tapping the TextField to bring up the keyboard, then tapping the android:back button closes the window. Doing the same thing on the emulator simply dismisses the keyboard. h4.Actual Behavior Clicking the android:back button when the keyboard is displayed closes the window (because the 'android:back' event is fired). h4.Expected Behavior Clicking the android:back button when the keyboard is displayed should just dismiss the keyboard, and not fire the 'android:back' event. h4. Original Testcase app.js {code} var win1=Ti.UI.createWindow({ layout:'vertical', url:'win1.js', backgroundColor:'gray', exitOnClose:true, navBarHidden:true }); win1.open(); {code} *NOTE*: The """"""""android:back"""""""" event is buggy on some devices. It may never fire at all. You may need to try various devices to reproduce the failure case. To verify the expected behavior change the event to """"""""androidback"""""""" (no colon). win1.js {code} var win1=Ti.UI.currentWindow; var TheLabel=Ti.UI.createLabel({ color:'black', text:'Click the edit, then hit the back button. Keyboard should be dismissed, but the window should not close.', width:'auto' }); win1.add(TheLabel); var TheTextField=Ti.UI.createTextField( {color:'black', width:150 }); win1.add(TheTextField); function AndroidBackClicked() { win1.close(); } win1.addEventListener('android:back',AndroidBackClicked); {code} h4. Test case to verify 'androidback' does not fire when dismissing keyboard. {code:javascript} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow(); win.add(Ti.UI.createTextField({width: '75%'})); win.addEventListener('androidback', function() { alert('back event fired!'); }); win.open(); {code} 1. Run application on a device that uses a soft keyboard. 2. Focus the textfield to bring up the soft keyboard. 3. Hit the """"""""back"""""""" button on the device. You should see NO alert. 4. Hit the """"""""back"""""""" button again. You SHOULD see an alert. Fail: You see two alerts (one for each """"""""back"""""""" press). Pass: You only get an alert in step 4. h4. Test: Closing a series of LWs using the """"""""androidback"""""""" event. {code:javascript} function openWindows() { ['blue', 'red', 'white'].forEach(function(color) { var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor: color}); win.addEventListener('androidback', function() { alert('closing window'); win.close(); }); win.open(); }); } var mainWindow = Ti.UI.createWindow(); var button = Ti.UI.createButton({title: 'Open windows'}); button.addEventListener('click', openWindows); mainWindow.add(button); mainWindow.open(); {code} 1. Run application and click """"""""Open windows"""""""". 2. Hit the back button. You should see an alert and the window should close. 3. Repeat step 2 for each window until all of them are closed and you are back to the main window. """"",title+description
TIMOB-8647,12,"""Android: Picker: Name of months is cut off and comes with dots on Droid 2.2.3""","""""""Description:  While running Android """"""""useSpinner""""""""-date on Droid 2.2.3, I noticed that after some steps name of months is cut off and comes with dots.  This bug occurs only on Droid 2.2.3 and also exists on 1.8.2.    Steps:  1. Run KitchenSink  2. Go to Controls-Picker-Android """"""""useSpinner""""""""-date  3. Click """"""""Change locate"""""""" button  4. Rotate device on landscape mode  5. Click """"""""Change locate"""""""" button one more time    Expected result:  Name of months should be fully visible    Actual result:  Name of months is cut off and comes with dots""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Picker: Name of months is cut off and comes with dots on Droid 2.2.3
Description: """"Description: While running Android """"""""useSpinner""""""""-date on Droid 2.2.3, I noticed that after some steps name of months is cut off and comes with dots. This bug occurs only on Droid 2.2.3 and also exists on 1.8.2. Steps: 1. Run KitchenSink 2. Go to Controls-Picker-Android """"""""useSpinner""""""""-date 3. Click """"""""Change locate"""""""" button 4. Rotate device on landscape mode 5. Click """"""""Change locate"""""""" button one more time Expected result: Name of months should be fully visible Actual result: Name of months is cut off and comes with dots""""",title+description
TIMOB-8646,12,"""Android: Database: V8: Runtime error is occuring while launching the app.""","""""""On launching the app, a runtime error is displayed on android 2.2 . Snapshot of the same is attached.    Steps to reproduce:  1) Create an app with code below and add speed.db file in the project(attached in the bug).  2) Install and launch the app.        Expected result : A window should be displayed : """"""""I am window 1""""""""   Actual result : A runtime error is displayed.  Note: It is showing expected behavior on android 3.2 and 4.0.2  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Database: V8: Runtime error is occuring while launching the app.
Description: """"On launching the app, a runtime error is displayed on android 2.2 . Snapshot of the same is attached. Steps to reproduce: 1) Create an app with code below and add speed.db file in the project(attached in the bug). 2) Install and launch the app. Expected result : A window should be displayed : """"""""I am window 1"""""""" Actual result : A runtime error is displayed. Note: It is showing expected behavior on android 3.2 and 4.0.2 """"",title+description
TIMOB-8645,12,"""Android: V8 Debugger displays incorrect Thread state.""","""""""Android V8 Debugger displays incorrect state for """"""""Stepping"""""""". In the cases below, the """"""""Stepping"""""""" state flashes just long enough to read and then it changes to """"""""Suspended"""""""" as the step reaches a break.      Steps to reproduce:  1. Create a new Titanium mobile project  2. Add/overwrite the newly created files in the Resources folder, with attached: app.js, import.js, nextwin.js  3. Copy attached *.bkpt files to the new project's Resources folder  4. Reference the Studio Debugger Test Plan (wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan), and observe the following:    Test Case: 1013 - BASIC BREAKPOINT TEST  Step: 21  Expected: Confirm thread is described as (stepping)  Actual: thread state displayed """"""""Suspended""""""""       Test Case: 1023 - SUSPENDED TEST  Step: 6  Expected: In Debug view, confirm Thread [kroll$1](Stepping)  Actual: thread state displayed """"""""Suspended""""""""   """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: V8 Debugger displays incorrect Thread state.
Description: """"Android V8 Debugger displays incorrect state for """"""""Stepping"""""""". In the cases below, the """"""""Stepping"""""""" state flashes just long enough to read and then it changes to """"""""Suspended"""""""" as the step reaches a break. Steps to reproduce: 1. Create a new Titanium mobile project 2. Add/overwrite the newly created files in the Resources folder, with attached: app.js, import.js, nextwin.js 3. Copy attached *.bkpt files to the new project's Resources folder 4. Reference the Studio Debugger Test Plan (wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan), and observe the following: Test Case: 1013 - BASIC BREAKPOINT TEST Step: 21 Expected: Confirm thread is described as (stepping) Actual: thread state displayed """"""""Suspended"""""""" Test Case: 1023 - SUSPENDED TEST Step: 6 Expected: In Debug view, confirm Thread [kroll$1](Stepping) Actual: thread state displayed """"""""Suspended"""""""" """"",title+description
TIMOB-8644,12,"""Android: V8 Debugger's naming conventions for displayed Frame and Thread titles are not consistent.  ""","""""""Based on results recorded in Debugger Test Plan and results logged from iOS tests, the Thread and Frame title naming conventions used for V8 debugging is not consistent.    Steps to reproduce:  1. Create a new Titanium mobile project  2. Add/overwrite the newly created files in the Resources folder, with attached: app.js, import.js, nextwin.js (Zippped)  3. Copy attached *.bkpt files to the new project's Resources folder  4. Reference the Studio Debugger Test Plan (wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan), and observe the following:      Test Case: 1013 - SUSPEND TEST  Step: 9  Expected:  ---------  In Debug view, confirm 2 call frames:   [Eval script] [app.js:9]   [Top-level script] [app.js:40]     Actual:     -------  f() [app.js:9]   Ti.UI.createButton.title() [app.js:40]         Test Case: 1013 - SUSPEND TEST  Step: 23  Expected:  ---------  In Debug view, confirm 3 call frames:    cfb() [app.js:74]    cfa() [app.js:79]   [Top-level script] [app.js:91]    Actual:     -------  cfb() [app.js:74]   cfa() [app.js:79]   Ti.UI.createButton.title() [app.js:91]       * Also note Attached log and capture files      """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: V8 Debugger's naming conventions for displayed Frame and Thread titles are not consistent.
Description: """"Based on results recorded in Debugger Test Plan and results logged from iOS tests, the Thread and Frame title naming conventions used for V8 debugging is not consistent. Steps to reproduce: 1. Create a new Titanium mobile project 2. Add/overwrite the newly created files in the Resources folder, with attached: app.js, import.js, nextwin.js (Zippped) 3. Copy attached *.bkpt files to the new project's Resources folder 4. Reference the Studio Debugger Test Plan (wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan), and observe the following: Test Case: 1013 - SUSPEND TEST Step: 9 Expected: --------- In Debug view, confirm 2 call frames: [Eval script] [app.js:9] [Top-level script] [app.js:40] Actual: ------- f() [app.js:9] Ti.UI.createButton.title() [app.js:40] Test Case: 1013 - SUSPEND TEST Step: 23 Expected: --------- In Debug view, confirm 3 call frames: cfb() [app.js:74] cfa() [app.js:79] [Top-level script] [app.js:91] Actual: ------- cfb() [app.js:74] cfa() [app.js:79] Ti.UI.createButton.title() [app.js:91] * Also note Attached log and capture files """"",title+description
TIMOB-8643,12,"""Android: V8 Debugger's indicator of current line of execution is not always accurate. ""","""""""Based on results recorded in Debugger Test Plan and results logged from Android Rhino and iOS tests, the indicated """"""""current line of execution"""""""" during V8 debugging is not consistent.    Steps to reproduce:  1. Create a new Titanium mobile project  2. Add/overwrite the newly created files in the Resources folder, with attached: app.js, import.js, nextwin.js  3. Copy attached *.bkpt files to the new project's Resources folder  4. Reference the Studio Debugger Test Plan (wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan), and observe the following:    Test Case: 1013 - BASIC BREAKPOINT TEST  Step: 5  Expected: line 2 of app.js  - is highlighted (as current line of execution)  Actual:   line 6 of import.js - is highlighted     Test Case: 1013  Step: 7  Expected: line 84 of app.js  - is highlighted (as current line of execution)  Actual:   line 80 of app.js  - is highlighted     Test Case: 1023 - SUSPEND TEST  Step: 5  Expected: line 91 of app.js  - is highlighted (as current line of execution)  Actual:   line 77 of app.js  - is highlighted       * Also note Attached log and capture files  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: V8 Debugger's indicator of current line of execution is not always accurate.
