TIMOB-8487,12,"""iOS: Pressing iPhone/iPod Touch volume up button while camera is open for recording starts recording, volume down does nothing""","""""""When the camera is opened for recording (video in this case), with the """"""""Cancel"""""""" and red """"""""Record"""""""" buttons displayed, the iPod and iPhone volume controls (on the side of the device) either do:  - Volume up: start recording  - Volume down: does nothing    This may be documented somewhere, but I cannot find anything about this. If it's by design, is there a way to work around this? I understand the up button is convenient to provide a """"""""camera button"""""""" feel.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Pressing iPhone/iPod Touch volume up button while camera is open for recording starts recording, volume down does nothing
Description: """"When the camera is opened for recording (video in this case), with the """"""""Cancel"""""""" and red """"""""Record"""""""" buttons displayed, the iPod and iPhone volume controls (on the side of the device) either do: - Volume up: start recording - Volume down: does nothing This may be documented somewhere, but I cannot find anything about this. If it's by design, is there a way to work around this? I understand the up button is convenient to provide a """"""""camera button"""""""" feel.""""",title+description
TIMOB-8485,12,"""Android: user wants to get device Manufacturer""","""""""h2. Request description  Customer needs to know the manufacturer of the device.     The reason is for there are different behaviors depending on different manufacturers models.    In particular, there is a different behavior on Samsung devices while handling separators for phone numbers (', ' for Samsung, '; ' for all the others).  Model manufacturer is not always included in device Ti.Platform.Model    h2. Additional notes  Ticket link: http://support-admin.<USER>com/display/APP-978898""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: user wants to get device Manufacturer
Description: """"h2. Request description Customer needs to know the manufacturer of the device. The reason is for there are different behaviors depending on different manufacturers models. In particular, there is a different behavior on Samsung devices while handling separators for phone numbers (', ' for Samsung, '; ' for all the others). Model manufacturer is not always included in device Ti.Platform.Model h2. Additional notes Ticket link: http://support-admin.<USER>com/display/APP-978898""""",title+description
TIMOB-8483,12,"""MobileWeb: Create fallback for transition end events""","""""""IE9 doesn't support transitions, and thus animations. To help apps that use animations break less, we should provide a fallback for the transitionend event so that the callback is at least called.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Create fallback for transition end events
Description: """"IE9 doesn't support transitions, and thus animations. To help apps that use animations break less, we should provide a fallback for the transitionend event so that the callback is at least called.""""",title+description
TIMOB-8482,12,"""Android: View doesn't show added components""","""""""h3. The problem    Components (Label and TextField in this case) added to the View are not showed on the screen in some cases.    h3. Test case      To reproduce this issue, run the following example:          h3. The result    As you should see, two Views in ScrollView have 50% height and components added to them are not visible on the screen.      h3. Expected result      All components should be visible on the screen. If component dimension defined as percentage is commented out, all components are visible on the screen.      In my opinion, components should be able to use percentages for dimension definition. In this particular case, component dimension should be looked up - because View has height defined as """"""""auto"""""""" (and ScrollView's contentHeight is set to 'auto'), component size should be determined by the size of the Window (first component higher in the hierarchy that have defined dimension value - that could also be ScrollView because it has height defined as 100%). After that, View should calculate height according to the size of their child components (View have Labels and TextFields).""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: View doesn't show added components
Description: """"h3. The problem Components (Label and TextField in this case) added to the View are not showed on the screen in some cases. h3. Test case To reproduce this issue, run the following example: h3. The result As you should see, two Views in ScrollView have 50% height and components added to them are not visible on the screen. h3. Expected result All components should be visible on the screen. If component dimension defined as percentage is commented out, all components are visible on the screen. In my opinion, components should be able to use percentages for dimension definition. In this particular case, component dimension should be looked up - because View has height defined as """"""""auto"""""""" (and ScrollView's contentHeight is set to 'auto'), component size should be determined by the size of the Window (first component higher in the hierarchy that have defined dimension value - that could also be ScrollView because it has height defined as 100%). After that, View should calculate height according to the size of their child components (View have Labels and TextFields).""""",title+description
TIMOB-8481,12,"""Android: blob representing an image has height and width properties always 0""","""""""h2. Problem  Returned values relative to Width or Height of a blob representing an image, are 0.    h2.Test Case  {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({   backgroundColor:'gray'  });      Ti.Media.openPhotoGallery({      success: function(e) {          alert('e.media.width:' + e.media.width +           '\n e.media.height:' + e.media.height +           '\n e.media.mimeType:' + e.media.mimeType);      }  });      win.open();    {code}    Values displayed:   W/TiAPI   ( 5241): e.media.height: 0  W/TiAPI   ( 5241): e.media.width: 0  W/TiAPI   ( 5241): e.media.mimeType: image/jpeg    Customer have opened a question on the developer q&a:  http://developer.<USER>com/question/132278/timediaopenphotogallery-image-properties-on-android""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: blob representing an image has height and width properties always 0
Description: """"h2. Problem Returned values relative to Width or Height of a blob representing an image, are 0. h2.Test Case {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor:'gray' }); Ti.Media.openPhotoGallery({ success: function(e) { alert('e.media.width:' + e.media.width + '\n e.media.height:' + e.media.height + '\n e.media.mimeType:' + e.media.mimeType); } }); win.open(); {code} Values displayed: W/TiAPI ( 5241): e.media.height: 0 W/TiAPI ( 5241): e.media.width: 0 W/TiAPI ( 5241): e.media.mimeType: image/jpeg Customer have opened a question on the developer q&a: http://developer.<USER>com/question/132278/timediaopenphotogallery-image-properties-on-android""""",title+description
TIMOB-8480,12,"""iOS: Ti.UI.TextField with attribute """"paddingLeft"""" or """"paddingRight"""" is partially unclickable""","""""""h2. Problem description  Text fields where the attribute """"""""paddingLeft"""""""" or """"""""paddingRight"""""""" is present, are unclickable on the left-hand or right-hand side, respectively.      Expected behavior: Text fields where the attribute """"""""paddingLeft"""""""" or """"""""paddingRight"""""""" is present, are completely clickable (as they are when those two attributes are not present).      h2. Reproducible use case    The following code adds a text field to a window. The text field has both paddingLeft and paddingRight set, making both the left and right part of the text field unclickable.        h2. Additional notes  http://developer.<USER>com/question/119753/textfield-paddingleft-problem""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Ti.UI.TextField with attribute """"paddingLeft"""" or """"paddingRight"""" is partially unclickable
Description: """"h2. Problem description Text fields where the attribute """"""""paddingLeft"""""""" or """"""""paddingRight"""""""" is present, are unclickable on the left-hand or right-hand side, respectively. Expected behavior: Text fields where the attribute """"""""paddingLeft"""""""" or """"""""paddingRight"""""""" is present, are completely clickable (as they are when those two attributes are not present). h2. Reproducible use case The following code adds a text field to a window. The text field has both paddingLeft and paddingRight set, making both the left and right part of the text field unclickable. h2. Additional notes http://developer.<USER>com/question/119753/textfield-paddingleft-problem""""",title+description
TIMOB-8479,12,"""iOS: Event touchStart is not triggered when beginning a scroll in a scrollableView containing a scrollView""","""""""1) create a scrollableView      2) create a scrollView containning 2 imageViews and add the scrollView in the scrollableView      3) add an touchStart Event Listener on scrollableView or on the scrollableView  """"""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Event touchStart is not triggered when beginning a scroll in a scrollableView containing a scrollView
