TIMOB-8225,12,"""Android:KitchenSink-Facebook: Publish Status and Wall post with GRAPH API fails""","""""""Publish Status with GRAPH API gives an Alert with Error message(#341)  and does not post anything on the Facebook wall.  Publish wall Post with GRAPH API gives an Alert with Error message (#341)  and does not post anything on the Facebook wall.    This occurs in 1.8.2 also.  screen shot of the error message is attached.    Steps to Reproduce    1: Tap on KitchenSink>MashUps>Facebook>Publish Stream       Actual Result: Alert with Error message (#341) """"""""Feed action request limit reached""""""""     Expected Result: publish a status with <USER>logo for Publish Status with Graph API     and publish a wall post with a link to Mozilla MDN Javascript page.                """"""",Bug,"Title: Android:KitchenSink-Facebook: Publish Status and Wall post with GRAPH API fails
Description: """"Publish Status with GRAPH API gives an Alert with Error message(#341) and does not post anything on the Facebook wall. Publish wall Post with GRAPH API gives an Alert with Error message (#341) and does not post anything on the Facebook wall. This occurs in 1.8.2 also. screen shot of the error message is attached. Steps to Reproduce 1: Tap on KitchenSink>MashUps>Facebook>Publish Stream Actual Result: Alert with Error message (#341) """"""""Feed action request limit reached"""""""" Expected Result: publish a status with <USER>logo for Publish Status with Graph API and publish a wall post with a link to Mozilla MDN Javascript page. """"",title+description
TIMOB-8224,12,"""Mobileweb: Ti.UI.Window.open event does not mean its views are ready""","""""""h2. Problem    Executing code against views in mobileweb from the view's containing window's {{open}} event does not ensure that the view has completely loaded. I would expect for mobileweb that the {{Window.open}} event would be much like jQuery's {{ready}}, meaning that you are free to start manipulating the elements contained within it. This is not the case.    h2. Test Case    All of the properties of {{view}} in this test case will animate from zero. I assume this is because the view's properties are not fully loaded when the {{window.open}} event fires. The comments portion with the {{setTimeout}} call will execute as expected, albeit with a 2 second delay.         h2. Proposed Solution    Have {{Window.open}} fire only when it and all of its child components have also loaded. This way developers can be assured that all necessary manipulations are safe in the {{open}} event.""""""",Bug,"Title: Mobileweb: Ti.UI.Window.open event does not mean its views are ready
Description: """"h2. Problem Executing code against views in mobileweb from the view's containing window's {{open}} event does not ensure that the view has completely loaded. I would expect for mobileweb that the {{Window.open}} event would be much like jQuery's {{ready}}, meaning that you are free to start manipulating the elements contained within it. This is not the case. h2. Test Case All of the properties of {{view}} in this test case will animate from zero. I assume this is because the view's properties are not fully loaded when the {{window.open}} event fires. The comments portion with the {{setTimeout}} call will execute as expected, albeit with a 2 second delay. h2. Proposed Solution Have {{Window.open}} fire only when it and all of its child components have also loaded. This way developers can be assured that all necessary manipulations are safe in the {{open}} event.""""",title+description
