TIMOB-8018,12,"""Android: WebView: beforeload event fires twice if url is invalid""","""""""When an invalid url is loaded from a webview, two beforeload events are fired (when one is expected).    A valid url results in one beforeload event, as expected.    iOS has the correct behavior (beforeload is loaded once if an invalid url is loaded).    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Run code, click 'Open Invalid URL'.    {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({   backgroundColor:'blue'  });    var buttonGoogle = Ti.UI.createButton({   title:'Open Google',   top:'20%',   height:'30%',   left:'10%',   right:'10%'  });    buttonGoogle.addEventListener('click', function(){   var webView = Ti.UI.createWebView({     url:'http://www.google.com'    });      webView.addEventListener('load', function(){    alert('load: ' + webView.url);    });      webView.addEventListener('beforeload', function(){    alert('beforeload: ' + webView.url);    });       webView.addEventListener('error', function(){    alert('error: ' + webView.url);    });       win.add(webView);      var button = Ti.UI.createButton({    title:'Close',    right:0,    bottom:0,    height:'10%',    width:'20%'   });      button.addEventListener('click', function(){    win.remove(webView);   });      webView.add(button);  });    win.add(buttonGoogle);    var buttonInvalid = Ti.UI.createButton({   title:'Open Invalid URL',   bottom:'20%',   height:'30%',   left:'10%',   right:'10%'  });    buttonInvalid.addEventListener('click', function(){   var webView = Ti.UI.createWebView({     url:'http://xxx'    });      webView.addEventListener('load', function(){    alert('load: ' + webView.url);    });      webView.addEventListener('beforeload', function(){    alert('beforeload: ' + webView.url);    });       webView.addEventListener('error', function(){    alert('error: ' + webView.url);    });       win.add(webView);      var button = Ti.UI.createButton({    title:'Close',    right:0,    bottom:0,    height:'10%',    width:'20%'   });      button.addEventListener('click', function(){    win.remove(webView);   });      webView.add(button);  });    win.add(buttonInvalid);    win.open();  {code}    Expected Result:    One beforeload event should fire, with alerts.    Actual Result:    Two beforeload events fire.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: WebView: beforeload event fires twice if url is invalid
Description: """"When an invalid url is loaded from a webview, two beforeload events are fired (when one is expected). A valid url results in one beforeload event, as expected. iOS has the correct behavior (beforeload is loaded once if an invalid url is loaded). Steps to Reproduce: 1. Run code, click 'Open Invalid URL'. {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor:'blue' }); var buttonGoogle = Ti.UI.createButton({ title:'Open Google', top:'20%', height:'30%', left:'10%', right:'10%' }); buttonGoogle.addEventListener('click', function(){ var webView = Ti.UI.createWebView({ url:'http://www.google.com' }); webView.addEventListener('load', function(){ alert('load: ' + webView.url); }); webView.addEventListener('beforeload', function(){ alert('beforeload: ' + webView.url); }); webView.addEventListener('error', function(){ alert('error: ' + webView.url); }); win.add(webView); var button = Ti.UI.createButton({ title:'Close', right:0, bottom:0, height:'10%', width:'20%' }); button.addEventListener('click', function(){ win.remove(webView); }); webView.add(button); }); win.add(buttonGoogle); var buttonInvalid = Ti.UI.createButton({ title:'Open Invalid URL', bottom:'20%', height:'30%', left:'10%', right:'10%' }); buttonInvalid.addEventListener('click', function(){ var webView = Ti.UI.createWebView({ url:'http://xxx' }); webView.addEventListener('load', function(){ alert('load: ' + webView.url); }); webView.addEventListener('beforeload', function(){ alert('beforeload: ' + webView.url); }); webView.addEventListener('error', function(){ alert('error: ' + webView.url); }); win.add(webView); var button = Ti.UI.createButton({ title:'Close', right:0, bottom:0, height:'10%', width:'20%' }); button.addEventListener('click', function(){ win.remove(webView); }); webView.add(button); }); win.add(buttonInvalid); win.open(); {code} Expected Result: One beforeload event should fire, with alerts. Actual Result: Two beforeload events fire.""""",title+description
TIMOB-8017,12,"""Orientation issue - LovedMugs""","""""""Hi Neeraj,    Can you please provide guideance on what may be happening here. <USER>B. seems to think that here is a similar platform issue that exists already. Please see the steps to re-produce issue and it's picture.    Orientation Issue    See the steps to re-produce below:  1. Go to Gallery  2. Turn to Landscape orientation  3. Select Choose Pic button on bottom left  4. Turn to Portrait mode (should be on camera roll)  5. Select a picture from the camera roll  6. See that screen is dis-oriented (see attached picture)    Sincerely,  Britt  """"""",Bug,"Title: Orientation issue - LovedMugs
Description: """"Hi Neeraj, Can you please provide guideance on what may be happening here. <USER>B. seems to think that here is a similar platform issue that exists already. Please see the steps to re-produce issue and it's picture. Orientation Issue See the steps to re-produce below: 1. Go to Gallery 2. Turn to Landscape orientation 3. Select Choose Pic button on bottom left 4. Turn to Portrait mode (should be on camera roll) 5. Select a picture from the camera roll 6. See that screen is dis-oriented (see attached picture) Sincerely, Britt """"",title+description
TIMOB-8016,12,"""iOS: 4.3: WebView: frequent SIGSEGV crash""","""""""Observed frequent SIGSEGV crash on 4.3 devices only when opening a WebView.    Does not occur in 5.0, does not occur in Simulator, does not occur in 1.8.2. Regression.    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Run code.    {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({   backgroundColor:'blue'  });    var buttonGoogle = Ti.UI.createButton({   title:'Open Google',   top:'20%',   height:'30%',   left:'10%',   right:'10%'  });    buttonGoogle.addEventListener('click', function(){   var webView = Ti.UI.createWebView({     url:'http://www.google.com'    });       win.add(webView);      var button = Ti.UI.createButton({    title:'Close',    right:0,    bottom:0,    height:'10%',    width:'20%'   });      button.addEventListener('click', function(){    win.remove(webView);   });      webView.add(button);  });    win.add(buttonGoogle);    var buttonInvalid = Ti.UI.createButton({   title:'Open Invalid URL',   bottom:'20%',   height:'30%',   left:'10%',   right:'10%'  });    buttonInvalid.addEventListener('click', function(){   var webView = Ti.UI.createWebView({     url:'http://xxx'    });       win.add(webView);      var button = Ti.UI.createButton({    title:'Close',    right:0,    bottom:0,    height:'10%',    width:'20%'   });      button.addEventListener('click', function(){    win.remove(webView);   });      webView.add(button);  });    win.add(buttonInvalid);    win.open();  {code}    2. Press 'Open Google', close. Press 'Open Invalid', close. Repeat.    Expected Result:    No crash.    Actual Result:    Crash occurs within 1 to 10 seconds.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: 4.3: WebView: frequent SIGSEGV crash
Description: """"Observed frequent SIGSEGV crash on 4.3 devices only when opening a WebView. Does not occur in 5.0, does not occur in Simulator, does not occur in 1.8.2. Regression. Steps to Reproduce: 1. Run code. {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor:'blue' }); var buttonGoogle = Ti.UI.createButton({ title:'Open Google', top:'20%', height:'30%', left:'10%', right:'10%' }); buttonGoogle.addEventListener('click', function(){ var webView = Ti.UI.createWebView({ url:'http://www.google.com' }); win.add(webView); var button = Ti.UI.createButton({ title:'Close', right:0, bottom:0, height:'10%', width:'20%' }); button.addEventListener('click', function(){ win.remove(webView); }); webView.add(button); }); win.add(buttonGoogle); var buttonInvalid = Ti.UI.createButton({ title:'Open Invalid URL', bottom:'20%', height:'30%', left:'10%', right:'10%' }); buttonInvalid.addEventListener('click', function(){ var webView = Ti.UI.createWebView({ url:'http://xxx' }); win.add(webView); var button = Ti.UI.createButton({ title:'Close', right:0, bottom:0, height:'10%', width:'20%' }); button.addEventListener('click', function(){ win.remove(webView); }); webView.add(button); }); win.add(buttonInvalid); win.open(); {code} 2. Press 'Open Google', close. Press 'Open Invalid', close. Repeat. Expected Result: No crash. Actual Result: Crash occurs within 1 to 10 seconds.""""",title+description
TIMOB-8015,12,"""Android: TextArea - Need to drag from TextArea in order to make soft keyboard appear""","""""""Steps to reproduce:    1. Run the following app.js:  {code:title=app.js}  var l1 = Titanium.UI.createLabel({      height:Ti.UI.SIZE,   width:182,      font:{fontSize:12,fontFamily:'ARIAL', fontWeight:'solid'},   color:'#000',   textAlign:'left',   borderWidth:0,   top:20,   borderWidth:2,   borderColor:'#bbb',   borderRadius:10   });     var tf = Titanium.UI.createTextArea({      height:55,   width:200,      font:{fontSize:12,fontFamily:'ARIAL', fontWeight:'solid'},      color:'#000',      textAlign:'left',   color:'#777',   paddingLeft:0,   borderWidth:0,   borderStyle:Titanium.UI.INPUT_BORDERSTYLE_NONE,   top: 100,   borderWidth:2,   borderRadius:10  });    tf.scrollable = false;    tf.addEventListener('change',function(e)  {   l1.text = tf.value;     });      var win1 = Titanium.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'white'});  win1.add(l1);  win1.add(tf);  win1.addEventListener('click', function(e){   tf.blur();  });  win1.open();  {code}    2. Press on textArea    *Actual:* Soft keyboard does not come up    3. Next, press on textArea, drag down, and release    *Actual:* Soft keyboard appears    *Expected:* Soft keyboard should appear after pressing on textArea    *Note:*  * If tf.blur() is comment out, soft keyboard comes up fine  * KS > Controls > Text Area > Basic works as expected  * Code works fine in iOS""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: TextArea - Need to drag from TextArea in order to make soft keyboard appear
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Run the following app.js: {code:title=app.js} var l1 = Titanium.UI.createLabel({ height:Ti.UI.SIZE, width:182, font:{fontSize:12,fontFamily:'ARIAL', fontWeight:'solid'}, color:'#000', textAlign:'left', borderWidth:0, top:20, borderWidth:2, borderColor:'#bbb', borderRadius:10 }); var tf = Titanium.UI.createTextArea({ height:55, width:200, font:{fontSize:12,fontFamily:'ARIAL', fontWeight:'solid'}, color:'#000', textAlign:'left', color:'#777', paddingLeft:0, borderWidth:0, borderStyle:Titanium.UI.INPUT_BORDERSTYLE_NONE, top: 100, borderWidth:2, borderRadius:10 }); tf.scrollable = false; tf.addEventListener('change',function(e) { l1.text = tf.value; }); var win1 = Titanium.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'white'}); win1.add(l1); win1.add(tf); win1.addEventListener('click', function(e){ tf.blur(); }); win1.open(); {code} 2. Press on textArea *Actual:* Soft keyboard does not come up 3. Next, press on textArea, drag down, and release *Actual:* Soft keyboard appears *Expected:* Soft keyboard should appear after pressing on textArea *Note:* * If tf.blur() is comment out, soft keyboard comes up fine * KS > Controls > Text Area > Basic works as expected * Code works fine in iOS""""",title+description
TIMOB-8014,12,"""iOS: Ti.Network.Socket.TCP """"timeout"""" property's unit is seconds and not milli-second""","""""""When the following app run on Android, it outputs """""""">>Socket error:Unable to connect, IO error"""""""" in TextArea after the 10 seconds since the button pressed.  On the other hand, on iPhone, it output nothing after 10 seconds.  And it output """""""">>Socket error:Attempt to connect to host timed out"""""""" after 10 seconds, by replacing """"""""timeout: 10000"""""""" to """"""""timeout: 10"""""""".  It seems the unit of timeout property in iOS is second, besides the API Doc explicitly describe """"""""in milliseconds"""""""".    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Ti.Network.Socket.TCP """"timeout"""" property's unit is seconds and not milli-second
Description: """"When the following app run on Android, it outputs """""""">>Socket error:Unable to connect, IO error"""""""" in TextArea after the 10 seconds since the button pressed. On the other hand, on iPhone, it output nothing after 10 seconds. And it output """""""">>Socket error:Attempt to connect to host timed out"""""""" after 10 seconds, by replacing """"""""timeout: 10000"""""""" to """"""""timeout: 10"""""""". It seems the unit of timeout property in iOS is second, besides the API Doc explicitly describe """"""""in milliseconds"""""""". """"",title+description
TIMOB-8013,12,"""iOS: WebView: beforeload event fires twice if URL is valid""","""""""WebView beforeload event is fired twice if a valid URL is loaded. An invalid url will only fire the beforeload event once (as expected).    Works correctly on Android (one beforeload event is fired when a valid URL is loaded).    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Run code, press 'Open Google'.        Expected Result:    Only one beforeload event should fire.    Actual Result:    Two beforeload events fire.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: WebView: beforeload event fires twice if URL is valid
