TIMOB-7651,12,"""Ti API: Ti.UI.Tab.close arguments differ by platform""","""""""On iOS, the Tab.close() method takes two arguments:     - a required Window argument (the window to close)  - an optional options dict (close options)    On Android and Mobile Web, close() only takes the second argument. So:    myTab.close(window)     Fails on Android, and:    myTab.close()    Fails on iOS.    As an additional complication, however, I believe tab.close() is mostly used on iOS, so the tab group can track which window is open. On Android and MobileWeb, applications can achieve the same ends by calling window.close() directly.    """"""",Bug,"Title: Ti API: Ti.UI.Tab.close arguments differ by platform
Description: """"On iOS, the Tab.close() method takes two arguments: - a required Window argument (the window to close) - an optional options dict (close options) On Android and Mobile Web, close() only takes the second argument. So: myTab.close(window) Fails on Android, and: myTab.close() Fails on iOS. As an additional complication, however, I believe tab.close() is mostly used on iOS, so the tab group can track which window is open. On Android and MobileWeb, applications can achieve the same ends by calling window.close() directly. """"",title+description
TIMOB-7650,12,"""iOS: Label parity on \t and \n""","""""""iOS has some inconsistencies with the other two platforms on how it handles tabs and newlines.    Tabstop in iOS is only 1, whereas Android and Mobileweb have a tabstop of 2. A tabstop of 1 isn't very useful because it acts exactly like a space.    If the text ends in a new-line, then the extra line is printed in Android and Mobile Web, but not in iOS.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Label parity on \t and \n
Description: """"iOS has some inconsistencies with the other two platforms on how it handles tabs and newlines. Tabstop in iOS is only 1, whereas Android and Mobileweb have a tabstop of 2. A tabstop of 1 isn't very useful because it acts exactly like a space. If the text ends in a new-line, then the extra line is printed in Android and Mobile Web, but not in iOS.""""",title+description
TIMOB-7648,12,"""Tooling: titanium.py create with ipad as an additional platform argument does not create a manifest""","""""""When a new project is created using the command line titanium.py create script and ipad is used as an additional platform argument (i.e., android & ipad, iphone & ipad), no manifest file is created. As a consequence, the project cannot be imported into studio.    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Run similar code in command line, taking into account OS and user directories.    {code:title=Command Line titanium.py create (OS X Lion)}  //  dhydeMBPLion:~ <USER> /Users/<USER>Library/Application\ Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/1.8.1/titanium.py create --platform=ipad,android --type=project --dir=/Users/<USER>Downloads --name=NewProject --id=com.<USER>project  //  {code}    Expected Result:    New project should be created with a manifest.    Actual Result:    No manifest file is created. Project cannot be imported into studio.""""""",Bug,"Title: Tooling: titanium.py create with ipad as an additional platform argument does not create a manifest
Description: """"When a new project is created using the command line titanium.py create script and ipad is used as an additional platform argument (i.e., android & ipad, iphone & ipad), no manifest file is created. As a consequence, the project cannot be imported into studio. Steps to Reproduce: 1. Run similar code in command line, taking into account OS and user directories. {code:title=Command Line titanium.py create (OS X Lion)} // dhydeMBPLion:~ <USER> /Users/<USER>Library/Application\ Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/1.8.1/titanium.py create --platform=ipad,android --type=project --dir=/Users/<USER>Downloads --name=NewProject --id=com.<USER>project // {code} Expected Result: New project should be created with a manifest. Actual Result: No manifest file is created. Project cannot be imported into studio.""""",title+description
TIMOB-7647,12,"""Tooling: titanium.py create with platform set to ipad only does not create an iphone folder in the new project""","""""""When a new project is created using the command line titanium.py create script and the platform is set to ipad only, no iphone folder is created. As a consequence, when the project is imported into studio, no targets are selected.    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Run similar code in command line, taking into account OS and user directories.    {code:title=Command Line titanium.py create (OS X Lion)}  //  dhydeMBPLion:~ <USER> /Users/<USER>Library/Application\ Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/1.8.1/titanium.py create --platform=ipad --type=project --dir=/Users/<USER>Downloads --name=NewProject --id=com.<USER>project  //  {code}    Expected Result:    NewProject/iphone should be created.    Actual Result:    No iphone folder is created. When project imported into studio, no targets are selected.""""""",Bug,"Title: Tooling: titanium.py create with platform set to ipad only does not create an iphone folder in the new project
Description: """"When a new project is created using the command line titanium.py create script and the platform is set to ipad only, no iphone folder is created. As a consequence, when the project is imported into studio, no targets are selected. Steps to Reproduce: 1. Run similar code in command line, taking into account OS and user directories. {code:title=Command Line titanium.py create (OS X Lion)} // dhydeMBPLion:~ <USER> /Users/<USER>Library/Application\ Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/1.8.1/titanium.py create --platform=ipad --type=project --dir=/Users/<USER>Downloads --name=NewProject --id=com.<USER>project // {code} Expected Result: NewProject/iphone should be created. Actual Result: No iphone folder is created. When project imported into studio, no targets are selected.""""",title+description
TIMOB-7641,12,"""Rotated web view does not return to original text size after unrotating""","""""""1. Compile the attached project BugTest.zip  2. Run in the simulator  3. Note the height of the characters in the web view. (WebViewBug.01.Start.png)  4. Rotate the device, notice that the character sizes increase. (WebViewBug.02.Rotated.png)  5. Unrotate the device.  EXPECTED RESULT: character heights should revert back to #3.  ACTUAL RESULT: character heights are the same as the rotated view #4. (WebViewBug.03.Unrotated.png)    Please see the differences highlighted in WebViewBug.04.Difference.png.""""""",Bug,"Title: Rotated web view does not return to original text size after unrotating
Description: """"1. Compile the attached project BugTest.zip 2. Run in the simulator 3. Note the height of the characters in the web view. (WebViewBug.01.Start.png) 4. Rotate the device, notice that the character sizes increase. (WebViewBug.02.Rotated.png) 5. Unrotate the device. EXPECTED RESULT: character heights should revert back to #3. ACTUAL RESULT: character heights are the same as the rotated view #4. (WebViewBug.03.Unrotated.png) Please see the differences highlighted in WebViewBug.04.Difference.png.""""",title+description
TIMOB-7640,12,"""Add list of allowable property values to Titanium Mobile API documentation""","""""""As prompted by this (http://developer.<USER>com/question/132035/titanium-studio-strange-behaviour#answer-230216) developer Q & A, users are confused as to why they don't get content assist for the allowable values for properties in Titanium Mobile.    Titanium Studio could show these values, if they were encoded in the JSCA file.    Suggestion is to amend the TiDoc format to add in a list of allowable property values, and then at least augment the docs with values for the most commonly used properties.    It might be that many properties share a common list of values, so perhaps references could be used to avoid duplication.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Add list of allowable property values to Titanium Mobile API documentation
Description: """"As prompted by this (http://developer.<USER>com/question/132035/titanium-studio-strange-behaviour#answer-230216) developer Q & A, users are confused as to why they don't get content assist for the allowable values for properties in Titanium Mobile. Titanium Studio could show these values, if they were encoded in the JSCA file. Suggestion is to amend the TiDoc format to add in a list of allowable property values, and then at least augment the docs with values for the most commonly used properties. It might be that many properties share a common list of values, so perhaps references could be used to avoid duplication.""""",title+description
