TIMOB-7356,12,"""iOS: build.py - Console error and no build on first attempt when importing clean iOS project with no iphone folder in project/build/""","""""""While using Studio 1.0.7 we started noticing the occasional first attempt to build iOS projects failing and the following text in console:  [INFO] One moment, building ...  [INFO] Detected missing project but that's OK. re-creating it...    This condition is caused by a lack of a build/iphone folder in the project which prompts the message from builder.py. The missing folder is created, and the second attempt should rebuild. We have not seen this error prior to 1.0.7. The only way I have been able to reproduce this situation so far is by importing a project and trying to build. This project presumably had no build/iphone directory    builder.py snippet starting at line 655:      Steps to reproduce:  1) Import a project into Studio with no build/iPhone folder  2) Attempt to run in simulator    Result:  Build fails with error in the console    Expected:  If missing a folder, make it and build""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: build.py - Console error and no build on first attempt when importing clean iOS project with no iphone folder in project/build/
Description: """"While using Studio 1.0.7 we started noticing the occasional first attempt to build iOS projects failing and the following text in console: [INFO] One moment, building ... [INFO] Detected missing project but that's OK. re-creating it... This condition is caused by a lack of a build/iphone folder in the project which prompts the message from builder.py. The missing folder is created, and the second attempt should rebuild. We have not seen this error prior to 1.0.7. The only way I have been able to reproduce this situation so far is by importing a project and trying to build. This project presumably had no build/iphone directory builder.py snippet starting at line 655: Steps to reproduce: 1) Import a project into Studio with no build/iPhone folder 2) Attempt to run in simulator Result: Build fails with error in the console Expected: If missing a folder, make it and build""""",title+description
TIMOB-7355,12,"""Android: children[] 'undefined' for table rows within event handler""","""""""h2. Expected Results    I should be able to read from e.row.children[] within a table's event listener. This works fine on iOS (simulator and my iPad running iOS5) and works with TiAPI 1.7.5/1.7.6 on Android.     h2. Actual results    Code below throws {{Uncaught TypeError: Cannot set property 'text' of undefined}}    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: children[] 'undefined' for table rows within event handler
Description: """"h2. Expected Results I should be able to read from e.row.children[] within a table's event listener. This works fine on iOS (simulator and my iPad running iOS5) and works with TiAPI 1.7.5/1.7.6 on Android. h2. Actual results Code below throws {{Uncaught TypeError: Cannot set property 'text' of undefined}} """"",title+description
TIMOB-7354,12,"""iOS: Table Search - In KS, cannot hide search bar in Table Search test""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  1. Launch KS from 1_8_X branch  2. Run Base UI > Views > Table Views > Table Search  3. Click """"""""Show"""""""" then """"""""Hide""""""""    Actual: Cannot hide the search bar.  Expected: Should be able to hide the table search bar after pressing """"""""Hide"""""""".    Note:   * Was able to hide table's search bar in 1.8.0.1  * No unusual messages or errors in the console. Only message being printed out when pressing the hide button is:    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Table Search - In KS, cannot hide search bar in Table Search test
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Launch KS from 1_8_X branch 2. Run Base UI > Views > Table Views > Table Search 3. Click """"""""Show"""""""" then """"""""Hide"""""""" Actual: Cannot hide the search bar. Expected: Should be able to hide the table search bar after pressing """"""""Hide"""""""". Note: * Was able to hide table's search bar in 1.8.0.1 * No unusual messages or errors in the console. Only message being printed out when pressing the hide button is: """"",title+description
TIMOB-7353,12,"""iOS: PDF Links are not clickable when loaded in WebView: iOS""","""""""When loading a PDF into a WebView, you cannot click links that are present in the PDF. Whether they be text links or links created in acrobat with the link tool. The WebView is just unresponsive. If you open that same PDF in mobile safari, the links work so it is definitely possible in a WebView. The steps to reproduce:    1) Create a new Ti mobile project and choose for iPhone/iPad.  2) Open app.js and delete all the contents.  3) Paste the following  {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid}  Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000');    var win1 = Titanium.UI.createWindow({        backgroundColor:'#fff'  });  var wv = Ti.UI.createWebView({   width:320,   height:240,   scalesPageToFit:true  });  win1.add(wv);  wv.url = """"""""http://mv.dop.com/example_slide.pdf"""""""";  win1.open();  {code}    4) Save and build the project.    The WebView will show up in the middle of the device and you are not able to click any of the links. Looks like a bug to me, right? I've made numerous threads on this in the Ti Community and have not been able to find a solution.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: PDF Links are not clickable when loaded in WebView: iOS
Description: """"When loading a PDF into a WebView, you cannot click links that are present in the PDF. Whether they be text links or links created in acrobat with the link tool. The WebView is just unresponsive. If you open that same PDF in mobile safari, the links work so it is definitely possible in a WebView. The steps to reproduce: 1) Create a new Ti mobile project and choose for iPhone/iPad. 2) Open app.js and delete all the contents. 3) Paste the following {code:title=app.js|borderStyle=solid} Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var win1 = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor:'#fff' }); var wv = Ti.UI.createWebView({ width:320, height:240, scalesPageToFit:true }); win1.add(wv); wv.url = """"""""http://mv.dop.com/example_slide.pdf""""""""; win1.open(); {code} 4) Save and build the project. The WebView will show up in the middle of the device and you are not able to click any of the links. Looks like a bug to me, right? I've made numerous threads on this in the Ti Community and have not been able to find a solution.""""",title+description
TIMOB-7351,12,"""Android: ImageView.images - animated images do not start on load and animating property value incorrect""","""""""h2. Problem    The {{animating}} property defaults to {{false}} on creation, but is then set to {{true}} on load, even though the animation does not run.    The {{start()}} method needs to be invoked to start the animation, and the {{animating}} property remains set at the same value ({{true}}).    Hence, to resolve this, ensure that the {{start()}} method works correctly, and the {{animating}} property reflects the actual animation state. Hence, surely {{animating}} should be {{false}} on load until the {{start}} event is fired?    h2. Test case    # create a new project  # replace the {{app.js}} code with the code below  # extract the attached zip of images to the {{/images}} folder. Note that the campfire images included in the archive are taken from the KS  # launch project  # on second launch, enable the {{start()}} method in the load eventListener    {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  Ti.UI.backgroundColor = 'white';  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({    exitOnClose: true,    layout: 'vertical'  });    var switchAnimation = Ti.UI.createSwitch({    title: 'Start',    value:false,    top: 20  });  win.add(switchAnimation);    var imageArray = [];    var imgName = 'campFire';  var imgExt = 'gif';  var imgNum = 17;    for (var i=1;i<=imgNum;i++){    imageArray.push('/images/' + imgName + ((i<10) ? '0'+i : i) + '.' + imgExt);  }    var imageSeries = Ti.UI.createImageView({    defaultImage: '/images/campFire01.gif',    images: imageArray,    duration:100,    repeatCount:0,    top: 20,    height:200  });  win.add(imageSeries);    Ti.API.info('Before load 1): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating); // returns false    imageSeries.addEventListener('load', function(e){    Ti.API.info('After load 1): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating); // returns true    // e.source.start();  // uncomment this to allow the animation to run properly    Ti.API.info('After load 2): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating); // returns true  });    switchAnimation.addEventListener('click', function(e){    if(imageSeries.animating === false){      e.value = true;      imageSeries.start();      Ti.API.info('After switch 3): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating);    } else {      e.value = false;      imageSeries.stop();      Ti.API.info('After switch 4): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating);    }  });    win.open();      With start() invoked in the load event listener, animation DOES start, and gives the same results.    {code:lang=none|title=console}  887                   TiAPI  I  (kroll$1: app://app.js) [247,923] Before load 1): imageSeries.animating = false  887                   TiAPI  I  (kroll$1: app://app.js) [622,1384] After load 1): imageSeries.animating = true  887                   TiAPI  I  (kroll$1: app://app.js) [1,1385] After load 2): imageSeries.animating = true  {code}    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: ImageView.images - animated images do not start on load and animating property value incorrect
Description: """"h2. Problem The {{animating}} property defaults to {{false}} on creation, but is then set to {{true}} on load, even though the animation does not run. The {{start()}} method needs to be invoked to start the animation, and the {{animating}} property remains set at the same value ({{true}}). Hence, to resolve this, ensure that the {{start()}} method works correctly, and the {{animating}} property reflects the actual animation state. Hence, surely {{animating}} should be {{false}} on load until the {{start}} event is fired? h2. Test case # create a new project # replace the {{app.js}} code with the code below # extract the attached zip of images to the {{/images}} folder. Note that the campfire images included in the archive are taken from the KS # launch project # on second launch, enable the {{start()}} method in the load eventListener {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} Ti.UI.backgroundColor = 'white'; var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ exitOnClose: true, layout: 'vertical' }); var switchAnimation = Ti.UI.createSwitch({ title: 'Start', value:false, top: 20 }); win.add(switchAnimation); var imageArray = []; var imgName = 'campFire'; var imgExt = 'gif'; var imgNum = 17; for (var i=1;i<=imgNum;i++){ imageArray.push('/images/' + imgName + ((i<10) ? '0'+i : i) + '.' + imgExt); } var imageSeries = Ti.UI.createImageView({ defaultImage: '/images/campFire01.gif', images: imageArray, duration:100, repeatCount:0, top: 20, height:200 }); win.add(imageSeries); Ti.API.info('Before load 1): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating); // returns false imageSeries.addEventListener('load', function(e){ Ti.API.info('After load 1): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating); // returns true // e.source.start(); // uncomment this to allow the animation to run properly Ti.API.info('After load 2): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating); // returns true }); switchAnimation.addEventListener('click', function(e){ if(imageSeries.animating === false){ e.value = true; imageSeries.start(); Ti.API.info('After switch 3): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating); } else { e.value = false; imageSeries.stop(); Ti.API.info('After switch 4): imageSeries.animating = ' + imageSeries.animating); } }); win.open(); With start() invoked in the load event listener, animation DOES start, and gives the same results. {code:lang=none|title=console} 887 TiAPI I (kroll$1: app://app.js) [247,923] Before load 1): imageSeries.animating = false 887 TiAPI I (kroll$1: app://app.js) [622,1384] After load 1): imageSeries.animating = true 887 TiAPI I (kroll$1: app://app.js) [1,1385] After load 2): imageSeries.animating = true {code} """"",title+description
TIMOB-7347,12,"""iOS: TextArea - In KS, Scroll Views Text Area test is printing out error messages to the console""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  1. Launch KS from 1_8_X branch  2. Go to Base UI > Views > Scroll Views > Scroll Views TextArea  3. Type at least two lines of text in all the TextAreas    Actual: Event listener error is printed out to the console. See attachment.  Expected: Errors should not appear in the console.    Note: Error messages do not appear in KS 1.8.0.1""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: TextArea - In KS, Scroll Views Text Area test is printing out error messages to the console
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Launch KS from 1_8_X branch 2. Go to Base UI > Views > Scroll Views > Scroll Views TextArea 3. Type at least two lines of text in all the TextAreas Actual: Event listener error is printed out to the console. See attachment. Expected: Errors should not appear in the console. Note: Error messages do not appear in KS 1.8.0.1""""",title+description
