TIMOB-7185,12,"""TI API: Add Ti.Filesystem.privateDocuments""","""""""For iOS (at least) we need to add a property to access {{Library/Private Documents}} which is the new location of database files and is generally useful for material which should not necessarily be backed up to iCloud (Apple has extensive docs about what which directories should be used for and the backup schemes for each).    Unsure if this makes sense for Android.    Any fix should include a new drillbit check for the API point and a sensible per-platform return value.""""""",New Feature,"Title: TI API: Add Ti.Filesystem.privateDocuments
Description: """"For iOS (at least) we need to add a property to access {{Library/Private Documents}} which is the new location of database files and is generally useful for material which should not necessarily be backed up to iCloud (Apple has extensive docs about what which directories should be used for and the backup schemes for each). Unsure if this makes sense for Android. Any fix should include a new drillbit check for the API point and a sensible per-platform return value.""""",title+description
TIMOB-7184,12,"""iOS: Drillbit failure on JSS test""","""""""Currently there is a failure in the drillbit JSS test when run in conjunction with any other tests, even those which do not touch filesystem. The test still passes when the drillbit dir is cleaned and the test is run individually.    This could be any number of things involving JSS file detection or certain types of includes, or simply drillbit screwing with the build system and causing more softlink problems. In any event, the file is *definitely* present in the {{test_harness}} build so it's not like it doesn't exist - it's just not being loaded for some reason.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Drillbit failure on JSS test
Description: """"Currently there is a failure in the drillbit JSS test when run in conjunction with any other tests, even those which do not touch filesystem. The test still passes when the drillbit dir is cleaned and the test is run individually. This could be any number of things involving JSS file detection or certain types of includes, or simply drillbit screwing with the build system and causing more softlink problems. In any event, the file is *definitely* present in the {{test_harness}} build so it's not like it doesn't exist - it's just not being loaded for some reason.""""",title+description
TIMOB-7181,12,"""MobileWeb: HTTPClient post data must be serialized to a string""","""""""The HTTPClient send() method needs to serialize the post data into a string in the format param1=value1&param2=value2.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: HTTPClient post data must be serialized to a string
Description: """"The HTTPClient send() method needs to serialize the post data into a string in the format param1=value1&param2=value2.""""",title+description
TIMOB-7180,12,"""MobileWeb: Minify, strip comments, and include only necessary CSS in production builds""","""""""During a production build, need to strip comments, minify, and only include the CSS that's needed based on the dependencies.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Minify, strip comments, and include only necessary CSS in production builds
Description: """"During a production build, need to strip comments, minify, and only include the CSS that's needed based on the dependencies.""""",title+description
TIMOB-7179,12,"""Android: Orientation: orientation change has slow performance on Android 4.0 device""","""""""Orientation change has slow performance. It takes almost two seconds for the app to change orientations. Video attached (4.0 device - Galaxy Nexus).    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Create and run a default app.  2. Rotate the device.  3. Observe orientation change.    Expected Result:    App should re-orient almost instantly.    Actual Result:    App takes 2 seconds to re-orient.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Orientation: orientation change has slow performance on Android 4.0 device
Description: """"Orientation change has slow performance. It takes almost two seconds for the app to change orientations. Video attached (4.0 device - Galaxy Nexus). Steps to Reproduce: 1. Create and run a default app. 2. Rotate the device. 3. Observe orientation change. Expected Result: App should re-orient almost instantly. Actual Result: App takes 2 seconds to re-orient.""""",title+description
