TIMOB-7015,12,"""API: Media - change audio playback rate""","""""""h2. Problem    I need an option to change the default playback rate. [Titanium.Media.AudioPlayer|http://developer.<USER>com/apidoc/mobile/latest/Titanium.Media.AudioPlayer-object] does not have a {{setPlaybackRate}} property.    This request is for all platforms - mobile and mobile web      h2. Use case    {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  var oAudio = Ti.Media.createAudioPlayer();  oAudio.src = """"""""URL_OF_AUDIO.mp3"""""""";  oAudio.playbackRate = 2.0;  oAudio.play();  {code}    h2. Logs    n/a. The audio stil plays at original speed instead of double speed.    h2. Discussions    http://developer.<USER>com/question/128001/speedup-and-slowdown-audio-playback    """"""",New Feature,"Title: API: Media - change audio playback rate
Description: """"h2. Problem I need an option to change the default playback rate. [Titanium.Media.AudioPlayer|http://developer.<USER>com/apidoc/mobile/latest/Titanium.Media.AudioPlayer-object] does not have a {{setPlaybackRate}} property. This request is for all platforms - mobile and mobile web h2. Use case {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} var oAudio = Ti.Media.createAudioPlayer(); oAudio.src = """"""""URL_OF_AUDIO.mp3""""""""; oAudio.playbackRate = 2.0; oAudio.play(); {code} h2. Logs n/a. The audio stil plays at original speed instead of double speed. h2. Discussions http://developer.<USER>com/question/128001/speedup-and-slowdown-audio-playback """"",title+description
TIMOB-7012,12,"""Android: Modal WIndow: Kindle Fire: v8/rhino: performance on text field entry is slow and unusable when a window is opened as a modal""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  1. Copy the code below to app.js and compile    2. Click on open  3. Click the text field and enter data    Actual Result:  The entry is slow and unusable, sometimes enter characters as if it is a longpress    Expected Result:  Should be usable""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Modal WIndow: Kindle Fire: v8/rhino: performance on text field entry is slow and unusable when a window is opened as a modal
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Copy the code below to app.js and compile 2. Click on open 3. Click the text field and enter data Actual Result: The entry is slow and unusable, sometimes enter characters as if it is a longpress Expected Result: Should be usable""""",title+description
TIMOB-7011,12,"""iOS: Changes to JS files after running on device are not seen on simulator""","""""""h3. Fail case / test case    1. Create a new project in Titanium Studio.  Make sure it targets iPhone.  2. Just put {{alert('hello');}} in app.js.  3. Run on Simulator.  4. Run on Device.  5. Change the alert statement to {{alert('hello and goodbye');}}.  6. Run on Simulator.    *Result*: In fail case, you'll still see just """"""""hello"""""""" as the text of the alert.  When testing the fix, you should see """"""""hello and goodbye"""""""".""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Changes to JS files after running on device are not seen on simulator
Description: """"h3. Fail case / test case 1. Create a new project in Titanium Studio. Make sure it targets iPhone. 2. Just put {{alert('hello');}} in app.js. 3. Run on Simulator. 4. Run on Device. 5. Change the alert statement to {{alert('hello and goodbye');}}. 6. Run on Simulator. *Result*: In fail case, you'll still see just """"""""hello"""""""" as the text of the alert. When testing the fix, you should see """"""""hello and goodbye"""""""".""""",title+description
TIMOB-7008,12,"""Android: tableViewRow - longpress event does not fire on TableViewRow""","""""""h2.Problem  Adding the eventListener of longpress to a TableViewRow does not fire the event.    h2.Test Case  Make a project with the below app.js.  Long press on the row and notice that the event never fires.  {code:title=app.js}  var win1 = Titanium.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'#000000'});    var row = Titanium.UI.createTableViewRow({height:'auto'});  var view = Titanium.UI.createView({backgroundColor:'red',width:'90%',height:'100%'});  row.add(view);  row.addEventListener('longpress', TableViewRowLongPress);    var table = Titanium.UI.createTableView();  table.appendRow(row);  win1.add(table);    function TableViewRowLongPress(evt)  {    Ti.API.info('****TableViewRow longpress');  }    win1.open();  {code}     h2.More Information  The above Test Case is not normally how we add data to a TableView.   When assigning the data property of the TableView to an array you do not have access to the TableViewRow to attach a longpress listener.  What would really be nice is to have the longpress event handled by the TableView itself and have the same information passed in the evt parameter as the click event (ie. row, rowData).     """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: tableViewRow - longpress event does not fire on TableViewRow
Description: """"h2.Problem Adding the eventListener of longpress to a TableViewRow does not fire the event. h2.Test Case Make a project with the below app.js. Long press on the row and notice that the event never fires. {code:title=app.js} var win1 = Titanium.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'#000000'}); var row = Titanium.UI.createTableViewRow({height:'auto'}); var view = Titanium.UI.createView({backgroundColor:'red',width:'90%',height:'100%'}); row.add(view); row.addEventListener('longpress', TableViewRowLongPress); var table = Titanium.UI.createTableView(); table.appendRow(row); win1.add(table); function TableViewRowLongPress(evt) { Ti.API.info('****TableViewRow longpress'); } win1.open(); {code} h2.More Information The above Test Case is not normally how we add data to a TableView. When assigning the data property of the TableView to an array you do not have access to the TableViewRow to attach a longpress listener. What would really be nice is to have the longpress event handled by the TableView itself and have the same information passed in the evt parameter as the click event (ie. row, rowData). """"",title+description
TIMOB-7006,12,"""Android: App.Properties - storing integer larger than 2147483647 causes exception""","""""""h2. Problem    The largest integer that can be saved without causing an exception is 2147483647, which is (2^31)-1.    Note that large numbers can be successfully saved on:  * iOS (according to reporter)  * Android + Titanium 1.8.0.1 + v8  * Android + Titanium 1.8.0.1 + rhino    h2. Test case    {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  Ti.App.Properties.setInt(""""""""lowInt"""""""", 2147483647);  Ti.API.info(""""""""****** lowInt Saved ******"""""""");  Ti.App.Properties.setInt(""""""""highInt"""""""", 2147483648);  Ti.API.info(""""""""****** highInt Saved ******"""""""");  Ti.App.Properties.setInt(""""""""veryHighInt"""""""", 1325269931753);  Ti.API.info(""""""""****** veryHighInt Saved ******"""""""");      h2. Workaround    Use {{Ti.App.Properties.\[g|s\]etDouble()}}:    {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  Ti.App.Properties.setDouble(""""""""veryHighInt"""""""", 1325269931753);  Ti.API.info(""""""""****** veryHighInt Saved ******"""""""");  Ti.App.Properties.setDouble(""""""""veryHighIntPlusOneThousand"""""""", Ti.App.Properties.getDouble(""""""""veryHighInt"""""""") + 1000);  Ti.API.info(""""""""****** veryHighIntPlusOneThousand Saved ******"""""""");  Ti.API.info(""""""""----> veryHighIntPlusOneThousand = """""""" + Ti.App.Properties.getDouble(""""""""veryHighIntPlusOneThousand""""""""));    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: App.Properties - storing integer larger than 2147483647 causes exception
Description: """"h2. Problem The largest integer that can be saved without causing an exception is 2147483647, which is (2^31)-1. Note that large numbers can be successfully saved on: * iOS (according to reporter) * Android + Titanium 1.8.0.1 + v8 * Android + Titanium 1.8.0.1 + rhino h2. Test case {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} Ti.App.Properties.setInt(""""""""lowInt"""""""", 2147483647); Ti.API.info(""""""""****** lowInt Saved ******""""""""); Ti.App.Properties.setInt(""""""""highInt"""""""", 2147483648); Ti.API.info(""""""""****** highInt Saved ******""""""""); Ti.App.Properties.setInt(""""""""veryHighInt"""""""", 1325269931753); Ti.API.info(""""""""****** veryHighInt Saved ******""""""""); h2. Workaround Use {{Ti.App.Properties.\[g|s\]etDouble()}}: {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} Ti.App.Properties.setDouble(""""""""veryHighInt"""""""", 1325269931753); Ti.API.info(""""""""****** veryHighInt Saved ******""""""""); Ti.App.Properties.setDouble(""""""""veryHighIntPlusOneThousand"""""""", Ti.App.Properties.getDouble(""""""""veryHighInt"""""""") + 1000); Ti.API.info(""""""""****** veryHighIntPlusOneThousand Saved ******""""""""); Ti.API.info(""""""""----> veryHighIntPlusOneThousand = """""""" + Ti.App.Properties.getDouble(""""""""veryHighIntPlusOneThousand"""""""")); """"",title+description
TIMOB-7005,12,"""API: UI - provide ability to apply padding to views""","""""""Since you're trying to mimic the web environment, mimicking the CSS box model would be a good idea. One of the properties that's currently missing is padding.     Yes, a workaround is putting views inside views. But it's ridiculously clunky, and doesn't always do what you want anyways. It's one of the things that should just work. """"""",New Feature,"Title: API: UI - provide ability to apply padding to views
Description: """"Since you're trying to mimic the web environment, mimicking the CSS box model would be a good idea. One of the properties that's currently missing is padding. Yes, a workaround is putting views inside views. But it's ridiculously clunky, and doesn't always do what you want anyways. It's one of the things that should just work. """"",title+description
TIMOB-7004,12,""" Android: The image path lost his reference after doing a require and export.""","""""""h3. Problem    The image path lost his reference after doing a require and export.    h3. Reproducible steps:     1. Run the attached code with 1.8.0.1 Titanium SDK.  Please see the 1.8.0.1 attached image.    2. Run the attached code with 1.7.5 Titanium SDK.  Please see the 1.7.5 attached image.    Notice that in 1.7.5 Titanium SDK the all images are displayed well on the first window.    h3. Expected behavior:  Customer wants to have the same behavior in 1.8.0.1 Titanium SDK like the 1.7.5 attached image.    h3. More info:    The images are displayed well in 1.7.5 Titanium SDK.      h3. Tested with the next specs:    Simulator  Android version : 2.3.3    iPhone 4  Version of the device: 5.0.1    Titanium Studio, build: 1.0.8.201112291658  Titanium SDK: 1.8.0.1 & 1.7.5    Samsung Fit  Android version of the device: 2.2.1    {code: title=1.8.0.1 Titanium SDK  & Android 2.3.3-Bad Behavior}  1.8.0.1 Titanium SDK    01-05 12:56:10.154: I/DEBUG(31): debuggerd: Feb  3 2011 14:45:34  01-05 12:56:10.194: D/qemud(38): entering main loop  01-05 12:56:10.234: I/Vold(29): Vold 2.1 (the revenge) firing up  01-05 12:56:10.234: D/Vold(29): USB mass storage support is not enabled in the kernel  01-05 12:56:10.234: D/Vold(29): usb_configuration switch is not enabled in the kernel  01-05 12:56:10.234: D/Vold(29): Volume sdcard state changing -1 (Initializing) -> 0 (No-Media)  01-05 12:56:10.264: I/Netd(30): Netd 1.0 starting  01-05 12:56:10.583: D/Vold(29): Volume sdcard state changing 0 (No-Media) -> 1 (Idle-Unmounted)  01-05 12:56:10.734: D/qemud(38): fdhandler_accept_event: accepting on fd 9  01-05 12:56:10.734: D/qemud(38): created client 0xe078 listening on fd 10  01-05 12:56:10.754: D/qemud(38): client_fd_receive: attempting registration for service 'boot-properties'  01-05 12:56:10.754: D/qemud(38): client_fd_receive:    -> received channel id 1  01-05 12:56:10.764: D/qemud