TIMOB-6793,12,"""iOS: Animation with autoreverse sets color incorrectly on completion""","""""""When I create an animation that specifies both {{backgroundColor}} and {{autoreverse}}, it animates as expected, but when the animation completes the view's background color reverts to the background color assigned to the animation, not the view's original color. Consider the following code:    {{app.js}}    When I click the green view, it animates to red, then back to green. But as soon as  the animation completes, the view switches back to red.    Expected behavior:    View stays green after animation completes.  """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Animation with autoreverse sets color incorrectly on completion
Description: """"When I create an animation that specifies both {{backgroundColor}} and {{autoreverse}}, it animates as expected, but when the animation completes the view's background color reverts to the background color assigned to the animation, not the view's original color. Consider the following code: {{app.js}} When I click the green view, it animates to red, then back to green. But as soon as the animation completes, the view switches back to red. Expected behavior: View stays green after animation completes. """"",title+description
TIMOB-6791,12,"""iOS: textArea should accept borderStyles being set just as textField does""","""""""TextArea does not accept borderStyle as TextField does.  Both TextArea and TextField should react the same way to borderStyle being set.    h4. Steps  Step 1: run the code below  Step 2: notice that there is no border on the textArea  Step 3: change createTextArea to createTextField  Step 4: run the code again  Step 5: notice that the textField does have a border    They should both have the same border.    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: textArea should accept borderStyles being set just as textField does
Description: """"TextArea does not accept borderStyle as TextField does. Both TextArea and TextField should react the same way to borderStyle being set. h4. Steps Step 1: run the code below Step 2: notice that there is no border on the textArea Step 3: change createTextArea to createTextField Step 4: run the code again Step 5: notice that the textField does have a border They should both have the same border. """"",title+description
TIMOB-6789,12,"""Android: KrollFunction Doesn't Allow for Return Values""","""""""h1. Problem  With the 1.8.0.1 changes and KrollFunction being introduced, return values were overlooked.    h2. Why it was Overlooked  Nothing in core or our private modules utilizes this portion of the API. But our demo module in the public repository makes use of it, and there may be third party developers that are using it.    h2. How to Test  Create a new module:  {quote}  titanium create --type=module --name=testcb --id=ti.testcb --platform=android  {quote}    Update the TestcbModule.java:  {code:title=TestcbModule.java}  package ti.testcb;    import java.util.HashMap;    import org.<USER>kroll.KrollModule;  import org.<USER>kroll.KrollFunction;  import org.<USER>kroll.annotations.Kroll;    @Kroll.module(name=""""""""Testcb"""""""", id=""""""""ti.testcb"""""""")  public class TestcbModule extends KrollModule  {          public TestcbModule()          {                  super();          }            @Kroll.method               public String initiateCrank(KrollFunction crank) {                  return crank.call(getKrollObject(), new HashMap()).toString();          }  }      h2. Pull Request  Master: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/1072  1_8_X: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/1073""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: KrollFunction Doesn't Allow for Return Values
Description: """"h1. Problem With the 1.8.0.1 changes and KrollFunction being introduced, return values were overlooked. h2. Why it was Overlooked Nothing in core or our private modules utilizes this portion of the API. But our demo module in the public repository makes use of it, and there may be third party developers that are using it. h2. How to Test Create a new module: {quote} titanium create --type=module --name=testcb --id=ti.testcb --platform=android {quote} Update the TestcbModule.java: {code:title=TestcbModule.java} package ti.testcb; import java.util.HashMap; import org.<USER>kroll.KrollModule; import org.<USER>kroll.KrollFunction; import org.<USER>kroll.annotations.Kroll; @Kroll.module(name=""""""""Testcb"""""""", id=""""""""ti.testcb"""""""") public class TestcbModule extends KrollModule { public TestcbModule() { super(); } @Kroll.method public String initiateCrank(KrollFunction crank) { return crank.call(getKrollObject(), new HashMap()).toString(); } } h2. Pull Request Master: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/1072 1_8_X: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/1073""""",title+description
TIMOB-6788,12,"""iOS: Update bad ARCHS_STANDARD_32_BIT settings in jscore project""","""""""This doesn't affect us because we haven't had to compile a new JSCore against iOS 5 (yet) but it's coming. This suffers from the same problem as the debugger, where the ARCHS_STANDARD_32_BIT redefinition excludes armv6 symbols from the library.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Update bad ARCHS_STANDARD_32_BIT settings in jscore project
Description: """"This doesn't affect us because we haven't had to compile a new JSCore against iOS 5 (yet) but it's coming. This suffers from the same problem as the debugger, where the ARCHS_STANDARD_32_BIT redefinition excludes armv6 symbols from the library.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6784,12,"""iOS: Database - data not transferred when populated database migrated from Titanium's old database folder to Private Documents""","""""""As per TIMOB-6273 the decision was made to store all databases in """"""""Private Documents"""""""" instead of """"""""Application Support/Database"""""""". This change requires any existing database on devices to be moved from the """"""""old"""""""" folder to the new location. The code and comments in this change indicate that it was intended to migrate the database, but multiple tests on both simulator and device show that the """"""""old"""""""" database is not being copied, but the database is being installed in the new location    Steps to reproduce:  {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  //Resources/data.mydb.sqlite is a """"""""model"""""""" database.  var db = Ti.Database.install('/data/mydb.sqlite','mydb');  {code}    # Run once using an """"""""old"""""""" (pre December 6th) and verify that """"""""Application Support/database/mydb.sql"""""""" has been created.  # Run once more using a new SDK and verify that the previously created database still exists, but a new database has been created in """"""""Private Documents"""""""".  # The Ti.Database.DB now points to the new database in Private Documents and the user can't access his/her data.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Database - data not transferred when populated database migrated from Titanium's old database folder to Private Documents
Description: """"As per TIMOB-6273 the decision was made to store all databases in """"""""Private Documents"""""""" instead of """"""""Application Support/Database"""""""". This change requires any existing database on devices to be moved from the """"""""old"""""""" folder to the new location. The code and comments in this change indicate that it was intended to migrate the database, but multiple tests on both simulator and device show that the """"""""old"""""""" database is not being copied, but the database is being installed in the new location Steps to reproduce: {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} //Resources/data.mydb.sqlite is a """"""""model"""""""" database. var db = Ti.Database.install('/data/mydb.sqlite','mydb'); {code} # Run once using an """"""""old"""""""" (pre December 6th) and verify that """"""""Application Support/database/mydb.sql"""""""" has been created. # Run once more using a new SDK and verify that the previously created database still exists, but a new database has been created in """"""""Private Documents"""""""". # The Ti.Database.DB now points to the new database in Private Documents and the user can't access his/her data.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6783,12,"""Android: AndroidManifest - V8 and Rhino, modifying custom AndroidManifest does not copy to real AndroidManifest during build time""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  1. Create a TiMob project and launch to Android emulator  2. Copy AndroidManifest.xml from *build* folder and add it to *platform > android* (platform folder is the same level as resources)  3. In the AndroidManifest.xml, modify the TiActivity tag to the following:         4. Clean and run    Results: The AndroidManifest.xml in the build folder still contains:       5. You get the same results if you changed only the TiTabActivity to:        6. But, if both tags from step 3 and 5 are changed accordingly, then the tags get copied to the AndroidManifest in the build folder    Expected: Changes to the custom AndroidManifest should be reflected in the AndroidManifest in the build folder """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: AndroidManifest - V8 and Rhino, modifying custom AndroidManifest does not copy to real AndroidManifest during build time
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Create a TiMob project and launch to Android emulator 2. Copy AndroidManifest.xml from *build* folder and add it to *platform > android* (platform folder is the same level as resources) 3. In the AndroidManifest.xml, modify the TiActivity tag to the following: 4. Clean and run Results: The AndroidManifest.xml in the build folder still contains: 5. You get the same results if you changed only the TiTabActivity to: 6. But, if both tags from step 3 and 5 are changed accordingly, then the tags get copied to the AndroidManifest in the build folder Expected: Changes to the custom AndroidManifest should be reflected in the AndroidManifest in the build folder """"",title+description
