TIMOB-6699,12,"""Android: ImageView added to window with no height/width specified does not scale to window (parity bug)""","""""""h3. Fail Case / Test Case    1. Create a new app.    2. Replace the new app's app.js with the one here below.    3. Run the app.    h3. Symptom    When the app window opens, it'll just get the small KS_nav_ui.png image on it (in the fail case -- if you're testing the fix, you should see the image expanded and scaled to the window.)    In iOS, if you run this app, it will expand/scale to the window.    {code:title=app.js}  Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000');  var win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({        title:'Test',      backgroundColor:'#000',   exitOnClose: true  });    win.add(Ti.UI.createImageView({   image: """"""""KS_nav_ui.png""""""""  }));      win.open();  {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: ImageView added to window with no height/width specified does not scale to window (parity bug)
Description: """"h3. Fail Case / Test Case 1. Create a new app. 2. Replace the new app's app.js with the one here below. 3. Run the app. h3. Symptom When the app window opens, it'll just get the small KS_nav_ui.png image on it (in the fail case -- if you're testing the fix, you should see the image expanded and scaled to the window.) In iOS, if you run this app, it will expand/scale to the window. {code:title=app.js} Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ title:'Test', backgroundColor:'#000', exitOnClose: true }); win.add(Ti.UI.createImageView({ image: """"""""KS_nav_ui.png"""""""" })); win.open(); {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-6698,12,"""iOS: Debugger - debugger variables are not alphabetically sorted""","""""""In the Android debugger, debugger variables are alphabetically sorted. Screenshot attached.  Should be sorted by local and global like v8 and iOS    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Create a new project in studio called debug_test .  2. Move the .js files from the debug test project in titanium_mobile_tests_internal.   3. Refer to the test plan at http://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan .  4. Execute test case 1014 up to Step 5 (in iOS).    Expected Result:    Variables should be in global and local order.    Actual Result:    Variables are in alphabetical order.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Debugger - debugger variables are not alphabetically sorted
Description: """"In the Android debugger, debugger variables are alphabetically sorted. Screenshot attached. Should be sorted by local and global like v8 and iOS Steps to Reproduce: 1. Create a new project in studio called debug_test . 2. Move the .js files from the debug test project in titanium_mobile_tests_internal. 3. Refer to the test plan at http://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan . 4. Execute test case 1014 up to Step 5 (in iOS). Expected Result: Variables should be in global and local order. Actual Result: Variables are in alphabetical order.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6697,12,"""Android: Debugger - V8 - debugger variables not alphabetically sorted""","""""""In the V8 debugger, debugger variables are not alphabetically sorted. Screenshot attached.  They are sorted in Rhino.    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Create a new project in studio called debug_test .  2. Move the .js files from the debug test project in titanium_mobile_tests_internal.   3. Refer to the test plan at http://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan .  4. Execute test case 1014 up to Step 5 (in V8).    Expected Result:    Variables should be in alphabetical order.    Actual Result:    Variables are not in alphabetical order.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Debugger - V8 - debugger variables not alphabetically sorted
Description: """"In the V8 debugger, debugger variables are not alphabetically sorted. Screenshot attached. They are sorted in Rhino. Steps to Reproduce: 1. Create a new project in studio called debug_test . 2. Move the .js files from the debug test project in titanium_mobile_tests_internal. 3. Refer to the test plan at http://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan . 4. Execute test case 1014 up to Step 5 (in V8). Expected Result: Variables should be in alphabetical order. Actual Result: Variables are not in alphabetical order.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6696,12,"""Android: When exiting the emulator on windows there isn't the """"Android Emulator has exited"""" console output in studio like on OSX and Linux""","""""""Steps to reproduce    1. Create a new ti mobile project  2. Launch an emulator  3. After the emulator has fully launched the app, exit the emulator    Expected Result:  Console output in ti studio saying """"""""Android Emulator has exited""""""""    Actual Result:  There isn't any console output once the emulator is exited    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: When exiting the emulator on windows there isn't the """"Android Emulator has exited"""" console output in studio like on OSX and Linux
Description: """"Steps to reproduce 1. Create a new ti mobile project 2. Launch an emulator 3. After the emulator has fully launched the app, exit the emulator Expected Result: Console output in ti studio saying """"""""Android Emulator has exited"""""""" Actual Result: There isn't any console output once the emulator is exited """"",title+description
TIMOB-6695,12,"""Android: Debugger - Rhino - debugger integer variables display as floats""","""""""In Rhino, debugger integer variables display as floats. Integers display correctly in V8 and iOS.    This is a general issue that could be tested in any Titanium Mobile project (containing integers and breakpoints) running Rhino in debug mode.    Tested in: <GitHub>/titanium_mobile_tests_internal/FeatureTest/Debugger    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Create a new project in studio called debug_test .  2. Move the .js files from the debug test project in titanium_mobile_tests_internal.   3. Refer to the test plan at http://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan .  4. Execute test case 1014 up to Step 5 (in Rhino).    Expected Result:    Variables - Name: y, Value: -1    Actual Result:    Variables - Name: y, Value: -1.0""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Debugger - Rhino - debugger integer variables display as floats
Description: """"In Rhino, debugger integer variables display as floats. Integers display correctly in V8 and iOS. This is a general issue that could be tested in any Titanium Mobile project (containing integers and breakpoints) running Rhino in debug mode. Tested in: <GitHub>/titanium_mobile_tests_internal/FeatureTest/Debugger Steps to Reproduce: 1. Create a new project in studio called debug_test . 2. Move the .js files from the debug test project in titanium_mobile_tests_internal. 3. Refer to the test plan at http://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan . 4. Execute test case 1014 up to Step 5 (in Rhino). Expected Result: Variables - Name: y, Value: -1 Actual Result: Variables - Name: y, Value: -1.0""""",title+description
TIMOB-6694,12,"""Android: Fastdev: v8/rhino: Adding an external js file and restarting with fastdev running does not pick up the new file""","""""""Steps To Reproduce:  1. Run TC1011 from https://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Fastdev+Test+Case    Actual Result:  After clicking on the button you get a runtime error: 'Uncaught Error: Requested module not found: win'    Expected Result:  To load the content of win.js """"""""Got a new window""""""""    Workaround:  Need to recompile the entire project to see the new window    Note:  Last working with 1.7.5""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Fastdev: v8/rhino: Adding an external js file and restarting with fastdev running does not pick up the new file
Description: """"Steps To Reproduce: 1. Run TC1011 from https://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Fastdev+Test+Case Actual Result: After clicking on the button you get a runtime error: 'Uncaught Error: Requested module not found: win' Expected Result: To load the content of win.js """"""""Got a new window"""""""" Workaround: Need to recompile the entire project to see the new window Note: Last working with 1.7.5""""",title+description
TIMOB-6693,12,"""Android: Rhino Debugger - Extra data is being printed out to the console""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  1. Create a new project in TiStudio called debug_test  2. Move the js files form the debug test project in titanium_mobile_tests_internal  3. Follow steps in test case 1013 from http://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan    Result: After step 10, extra data is being printed out to the console. See attachment.  Expected: The extra data should not be printed out to the console.    Note: The extra data does not appear in V8 or in 1.7.5 """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Rhino Debugger - Extra data is being printed out to the console
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Create a new project in TiStudio called debug_test 2. Move the js files form the debug test project in titanium_mobile_tests_internal 3. Follow steps in test case 1013 from http://wiki.<USER>org/display/tp/Debugger+Test+Plan Result: After step 10, extra data is being printed out to the console. See attachment. Expected: The extra data should not be printed out to the console. Note: The extra data does not appear in V8 or in 1.7.5 """"",title+description
TIMOB-6692,12,"""Android: ImageView with just one dimension size specified and placed on a TableViewRow can easily lead to Out Of Memory exceptions""","""""""h3. Fail Case / Test Case    1. Create a new app.    2. Replace the contents of the app's app.js with the code below.    3. Run the app.    h4. Symptom    Even though the app only contains a 30-row table view with a small (46x43) image on each row, it can easily run out of memory.  You might even see (depending on memory on your device) that not every row gets the image to begin with, because memory already ran out while rendering the table.  Otherwise, scroll up and down in the table view, and check logcat -- eventually you'll start seeing long exception traces for an out of memory condition.    {code:title=app.js}  Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000');  var win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({        title:'Test',      backgroundColor:'#000',   exitOnClose: true  });    var rows = [];  for (var i = 0; i < 30; i++) {   var row = Ti.UI.createTableViewRow({height: """"""""40dp""""""""});   var iv = Ti.UI.createImageView({image: """"""""KS_nav_ui.png"""""""", height: """"""""43""""""""});   row.add(iv);   rows.push(row);  }    var tv = Ti.UI.createTableView({data: rows});  win.add(tv);  win.open();  {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: ImageView with just one dimension size specified and placed on a TableViewRow can easily lead to Out Of Memory exceptions
Description: """"h3. Fail Case / Test Case 1. Create a new app. 2. Replace the contents of the app's app.js with the code below. 3. Run the app. h4. Symptom Even though the app only contains a 30-row table view with a small (46x43) image on each row, it can easily run out of memory. You might even see (depending on memory on your device) that not every row gets the image to begin with, because memory already ran out while rendering the table. Otherwise, scroll up and down in the table view, and check logcat -- eventually you'll start seeing long exception traces for an out of memory condition. {code:title=app.js} Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ title:'Test', backgroundColor:'#000', exitOnClose: true }); var rows = []; for (var i = 0; i < 30; i++) { var row = Ti.UI.createTableViewRow({height: """"""""40dp""""""""}); var iv = Ti.UI.createImageView({image: """"""""KS_nav_ui.png"""""""", height: """"""""43""""""""}); row.add(iv); rows.push(row); } var tv = Ti.UI.createTableView({data: rows}); win.add(tv); win.open(); {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-6691,12,"""Android: 2DMatrix multiply method ignores passed in argument""","""""""The multiply method in 2DMatrix on Android does not do anything with the argument given to it.  The operation should occur on the argument rather than the """"""""this"""""""" object.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: 2DMatrix multiply method ignores passed in argument
Description: """"The multiply method in 2DMatrix on Android does not do anything with the argument given to it. The operation should occur on the argument rather than the """"""""this"""""""" object.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6689,12,"""iOS: CommonJS - relative path in require() causes nested include() statements to fail""","""""""Expected: require('ui').createMainWindow().open() should load module and any include() statements within ui.js should work    Actual: throws file not found error on the include() statement; need to use require('/ui').createMainWindow().open()    Attached file demonstrates problem.    The relative path worked in 1.7.6 and the CI build of 1.8 noted in the code comments    Possibly related to TIMOB-6602""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: CommonJS - relative path in require() causes nested include() statements to fail
Description: """"Expected: require('ui').createMainWindow().open() should load module and any include() statements within ui.js should work Actual: throws file not found error on the include() statement; need to use require('/ui').createMainWindow().open() Attached file demonstrates problem. The relative path worked in 1.7.6 and the CI build of 1.8 noted in the code comments Possibly related to TIMOB-6602""""",title+description
TIMOB-6688,12,"""iOS: CommonJS - Module variables not updated""","""""""h2. Expected Results    A variable is set in a module, this module is then included in other modules. It is updated by one module for use in the other.    The values are updated.    Note that Android V8 and Rhino works as expected    h2. Actual Results    The variable is not updated, and thus remains null on iOS    h2. Code  {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  var FSUT = {};    FSUT.GV  = require('FSUT-GV');  FSUT.CO  = require('FSUT-CO');    FSUT.CO.showValues();    FSUT.CO.updateValues();    FSUT.CO.showValues();          {code:lang=javascript|title=FSUT-CO.js}    var FSUT = {};      FSUT.GV = require('FSUT-GV');    exports.showValues = function()  {   alert(FSUT.GV.VARS.fieldA + """""""" .... """""""" + FSUT.GV.VARS.fieldB);  };  exports.updateValues = function()  {   FSUT.GV.updateFields('NEW VALUE', 'fieldA');  };  {code}    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: CommonJS - Module variables not updated
Description: """"h2. Expected Results A variable is set in a module, this module is then included in other modules. It is updated by one module for use in the other. The values are updated. Note that Android V8 and Rhino works as expected h2. Actual Results The variable is not updated, and thus remains null on iOS h2. Code {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} var FSUT = {}; FSUT.GV = require('FSUT-GV'); FSUT.CO = require('FSUT-CO'); FSUT.CO.showValues(); FSUT.CO.updateValues(); FSUT.CO.showValues(); {code:lang=javascript|title=FSUT-CO.js} var FSUT = {}; FSUT.GV = require('FSUT-GV'); exports.showValues = function() { alert(FSUT.GV.VARS.fieldA + """""""" .... """""""" + FSUT.GV.VARS.fieldB); }; exports.updateValues = function() { FSUT.GV.updateFields('NEW VALUE', 'fieldA'); }; {code} """"",title+description
TIMOB-6687,12,"""Android: FTS support in SQLite""","""""""h3.Feature Request  Feature for supporting the FTS inside the SQLite.    FTS gives the ability to do the full-text search quickly. Some more information from the SQLite page: http://www.sqlite.org/fts3.html .   """"""",New Feature,"Title: Android: FTS support in SQLite
Description: """"h3.Feature Request Feature for supporting the FTS inside the SQLite. FTS gives the ability to do the full-text search quickly. Some more information from the SQLite page: http://www.sqlite.org/fts3.html . """"",title+description
TIMOB-6686,12,"""Android: V8: Constants on Proxy instances aren't working""","""""""When using the V8 runtime, Constants that are defined on KrollProxy subclasses don't correctly show up on instances of that proxy. This pattern is currently used in the XML module for DOM constants.    There are two test cases for this:    * In the Drillbit {{xml}} test, the test {{apiXmlNodeProperties}} should pass  * This test app should also print {{ELEMENT_NODE=1}}:  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: V8: Constants on Proxy instances aren't working
Description: """"When using the V8 runtime, Constants that are defined on KrollProxy subclasses don't correctly show up on instances of that proxy. This pattern is currently used in the XML module for DOM constants. There are two test cases for this: * In the Drillbit {{xml}} test, the test {{apiXmlNodeProperties}} should pass * This test app should also print {{ELEMENT_NODE=1}}: """"",title+description
TIMOB-6685,12,"""Android: V8: Enable 3rd party module build for Windows""","""""""Our NDK Makefiles / build scripts don't currently work in Windows due to use of unix utilities like gperf. This may just require updated documentation for cygwin setup, but we may also require further changes.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: V8: Enable 3rd party module build for Windows
