TIMOB-6651,12,"""Android: Modules: V8 First call into 3rd party jar file causes crash""","""""""Using the barcode module (for example) and building the app for V8, the application's main window will display correctly. Press the """"""""Scan"""""""" button and the application will crash. This is the first call into the module which results in a call to the 3rd party jar file. This also occurs on other modules that use 3rd party jar files.    Crash log is as follows:  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Modules: V8 First call into 3rd party jar file causes crash
Description: """"Using the barcode module (for example) and building the app for V8, the application's main window will display correctly. Press the """"""""Scan"""""""" button and the application will crash. This is the first call into the module which results in a call to the 3rd party jar file. This also occurs on other modules that use 3rd party jar files. Crash log is as follows: """"",title+description
TIMOB-6650,12,"""iOS: ScrollView - [ERROR] Adding an event listener to a proxy that isn't already in the context""","""""""This error shows in the console log when setting the contentOffset of the scrollView in this example  [ERROR] Adding an event listener to a proxy that isn't already in the context.    If you comment out the contentOffset in the scroll eventListener, there is no error.    Step 1: run the code below  Step 2: scroll the view  Step 3: notice the error in the console    {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({   backgroundColor : '#fff'  });    var layer1 = Titanium.UI.createScrollView({   zIndex : 1,   contentWidth : 'auto',   contentHeight : 'auto',   top : 0,   layout : 'horizontal',   contentOffset : {    x : 0,    y : 0   }  });    var controlScroll = Titanium.UI.createScrollView({   zIndex : 10,   contentWidth : 1000,   contentHeight : 480,   showHorizontalScrollIndicator : true,   top : 0,   layout : 'horizontal'  });  controlScroll.addEventListener('scroll', function() {   var x = controlScroll.contentOffset.x;   layer1.contentOffset = {    x : (x * 1.3)   };  })    for(var i = 0; i < 10; i++) {;   layer1.add(makeView('blue'));  }    function makeView(color) {   var view = Ti.UI.createView({    left : 100,    width : 20,    height : 480,    backgroundColor : color   });     return view;  }    win.add(layer1);  win.add(controlScroll);    win.open();  {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: ScrollView - [ERROR] Adding an event listener to a proxy that isn't already in the context
Description: """"This error shows in the console log when setting the contentOffset of the scrollView in this example [ERROR] Adding an event listener to a proxy that isn't already in the context. If you comment out the contentOffset in the scroll eventListener, there is no error. Step 1: run the code below Step 2: scroll the view Step 3: notice the error in the console {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor : '#fff' }); var layer1 = Titanium.UI.createScrollView({ zIndex : 1, contentWidth : 'auto', contentHeight : 'auto', top : 0, layout : 'horizontal', contentOffset : { x : 0, y : 0 } }); var controlScroll = Titanium.UI.createScrollView({ zIndex : 10, contentWidth : 1000, contentHeight : 480, showHorizontalScrollIndicator : true, top : 0, layout : 'horizontal' }); controlScroll.addEventListener('scroll', function() { var x = controlScroll.contentOffset.x; layer1.contentOffset = { x : (x * 1.3) }; }) for(var i = 0; i < 10; i++) {; layer1.add(makeView('blue')); } function makeView(color) { var view = Ti.UI.createView({ left : 100, width : 20, height : 480, backgroundColor : color }); return view; } win.add(layer1); win.add(controlScroll); win.open(); {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-6649,12,"""Android: Rhino: Breakpoints in require()'ed modules are not getting hit.""","""""""1. Create an application w/ the app.js and mymodule.js below.  2. Set a breakpoint on one of the lines in app.js and the line in mymodule.js.  3. Debug the application from Studio. First breakpoint in app.js should fire.  4. Resume application. Second breakpoint should now fire.    Expected:    Both breakpoints created in #2 should break after the application launches.    Actual:    None of the breakpoints fire.      {code:title=app.js}  var x = 1;  var y = 2;  var z = require('mymodule').z;    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Rhino: Breakpoints in require()'ed modules are not getting hit.
Description: """"1. Create an application w/ the app.js and mymodule.js below. 2. Set a breakpoint on one of the lines in app.js and the line in mymodule.js. 3. Debug the application from Studio. First breakpoint in app.js should fire. 4. Resume application. Second breakpoint should now fire. Expected: Both breakpoints created in #2 should break after the application launches. Actual: None of the breakpoints fire. {code:title=app.js} var x = 1; var y = 2; var z = require('mymodule').z; """"",title+description
TIMOB-6648,12,"""Android: onCreateOptionsMenu Regressions in 1.9.0 from 1.7.5""","""""""h1. Problem  The onCreateOptionsMenu is very brittle with heavyweight windows in master / 1.9.0 compared to 1.7.5.    These two use cases no longer work:  * Ti.Android.currentActivity.onCreateOptionsMenu does not work.  * win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu after """"""""open"""""""" is called but before the open event fires does not work.    These two use cases do work:  * win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu after the open event fires works.  * win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu before """"""""open"""""""" is called.    h2. Expected Behavior  Use cases that worked in 1.7.5 should work here as well.    h2. Example Code  Drop the following in an app.js, and change the """"""""useApproach"""""""" constant to the approach you want to see in action.  {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({      backgroundColor: 'white',      exitOnClose: true,      navBarHidden: true  });    win.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({      text: 'Press your Android device\'s menu button!',      color: '#000'  }));    var useApproach = 1;  switch (useApproach) {      case 1:          /*           WORKS in 1.7.5. BROKEN in 1.8.0.1.           Does not work. Ti.Android.currentActivity won't be accurate because we created a heavyweight window above.           */          win.open();          Ti.Android.currentActivity.onCreateOptionsMenu = function (e) {              e.menu.add({ title: 'Approach 1', itemId: 1 });          };          break;      case 2:          /*           WORKS in 1.7.5. BROKEN in 1.8.0.1.           Does not work. It looks like """"""""activity"""""""" is not accurate yet.           */          win.open();          win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu = function (e) {              e.menu.add({ title: 'Approach 2', itemId: 2 });          };          break;      case 3:          /*           WORKS in both 1.7.5 and 1.8.0.1.           It looks like """"""""activity"""""""" is accurate before we call """"""""open"""""""".           */          win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu = function (e) {              e.menu.add({ title: 'Approach 3', itemId: 2 });          };          win.open();          break;      case 4:          /*           WORKS in both 1.7.5 and 1.8.0.1.           If we wait until the open event fires, we can then define the onCreateOptionsMenu properly.           */          win.addEventListener('open', function (evt) {              win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu = function (e) {                  e.menu.add({ title: 'Approach 4', itemId: 3 });              };          });          win.open();          break;  }  {code}    h2. Console  The following shows up in the console regardless of if the menu shows up or not:  {quote}  12-13 15:09:27.082: E/imdg81(2450): IsShutDownStarted()  12-13 15:09:27.086: I/KeyInputQueue(2450): Input event  12-13 15:09:27.207: E/imdg81(2450): IsShutDownStarted()  12-13 15:09:27.207: I/KeyInputQueue(2450): Input event  {quote}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: onCreateOptionsMenu Regressions in 1.9.0 from 1.7.5
Description: """"h1. Problem The onCreateOptionsMenu is very brittle with heavyweight windows in master / 1.9.0 compared to 1.7.5. These two use cases no longer work: * Ti.Android.currentActivity.onCreateOptionsMenu does not work. * win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu after """"""""open"""""""" is called but before the open event fires does not work. These two use cases do work: * win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu after the open event fires works. * win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu before """"""""open"""""""" is called. h2. Expected Behavior Use cases that worked in 1.7.5 should work here as well. h2. Example Code Drop the following in an app.js, and change the """"""""useApproach"""""""" constant to the approach you want to see in action. {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: 'white', exitOnClose: true, navBarHidden: true }); win.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({ text: 'Press your Android device\'s menu button!', color: '#000' })); var useApproach = 1; switch (useApproach) { case 1: /* WORKS in 1.7.5. BROKEN in 1.8.0.1. Does not work. Ti.Android.currentActivity won't be accurate because we created a heavyweight window above. */ win.open(); Ti.Android.currentActivity.onCreateOptionsMenu = function (e) { e.menu.add({ title: 'Approach 1', itemId: 1 }); }; break; case 2: /* WORKS in 1.7.5. BROKEN in 1.8.0.1. Does not work. It looks like """"""""activity"""""""" is not accurate yet. */ win.open(); win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu = function (e) { e.menu.add({ title: 'Approach 2', itemId: 2 }); }; break; case 3: /* WORKS in both 1.7.5 and 1.8.0.1. It looks like """"""""activity"""""""" is accurate before we call """"""""open"""""""". */ win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu = function (e) { e.menu.add({ title: 'Approach 3', itemId: 2 }); }; win.open(); break; case 4: /* WORKS in both 1.7.5 and 1.8.0.1. If we wait until the open event fires, we can then define the onCreateOptionsMenu properly. */ win.addEventListener('open', function (evt) { win.activity.onCreateOptionsMenu = function (e) { e.menu.add({ title: 'Approach 4', itemId: 3 }); }; }); win.open(); break; } {code} h2. Console The following shows up in the console regardless of if the menu shows up or not: {quote} 12-13 15:09:27.082: E/imdg81(2450): IsShutDownStarted() 12-13 15:09:27.086: I/KeyInputQueue(2450): Input event 12-13 15:09:27.207: E/imdg81(2450): IsShutDownStarted() 12-13 15:09:27.207: I/KeyInputQueue(2450): Input event {quote}""""",title+description
TIMOB-6645,12,"""Tableview Scrollable:false breaks with textField in row""","""""""h4. Issue  When placing a tableview with two textfields and scrollable:false inside a scrollview to allow movement for the keyboard.  The tableview moves and cuts of the top of the table, then does not always return to the right position on Blur    h4. Code      h4.Repro Steps  1. Drop code into app.js and run app  2. Click in the password field  3. See Username field get cutoff  4. Blur password, table slides down, but still has username field cutoff""""""",Bug,"Title: Tableview Scrollable:false breaks with textField in row
Description: """"h4. Issue When placing a tableview with two textfields and scrollable:false inside a scrollview to allow movement for the keyboard. The tableview moves and cuts of the top of the table, then does not always return to the right position on Blur h4. Code h4.Repro Steps 1. Drop code into app.js and run app 2. Click in the password field 3. See Username field get cutoff 4. Blur password, table slides down, but still has username field cutoff""""",title+description
TIMOB-6644,12,"""iOS: Button - setting backgroundImage causes the button not provide visual feedback when it is clicked""","""""""Ti.UI.Button does not give feedback that it is being clicked if it has backgroundImage set.  It was working in 1.7.5 but is not working in 1.8.0.1 RC1    Step 1: add the attached image to your Resources folder  Step 2: run the code below  Step 3: click the button  Step 4: notice that that button does not give any feedback that it is being clicked    {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'#fff'});    var share = Ti.UI.createButton({   // title: 'Share',   top: 50,   left: 50,   width: 130,   height: 35,   backgroundImage:'share.png'  });  win.add(share);    win.open();  {code}    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Button - setting backgroundImage causes the button not provide visual feedback when it is clicked
Description: """"Ti.UI.Button does not give feedback that it is being clicked if it has backgroundImage set. It was working in 1.7.5 but is not working in 1.8.0.1 RC1 Step 1: add the attached image to your Resources folder Step 2: run the code below Step 3: click the button Step 4: notice that that button does not give any feedback that it is being clicked {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'#fff'}); var share = Ti.UI.createButton({ // title: 'Share', top: 50, left: 50, width: 130, height: 35, backgroundImage:'share.png' }); win.add(share); win.open(); {code} """"",title+description
TIMOB-6629,12,"""Android: (V8) Error require()'ing native modules since commit 0c23329""","""""""cf.the breaking change:    https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/0c23329    Fail case:    1. build any module (bump, etc.) to create its zip.  2. Create an app and copy the module's zip to the app's root folder.  3. Modify tiapp.xml in the app to include the module.  4. Modify app.js in the app to require() the module.  5. Run the app.    You'll get the error """"""""Requested module not found: bootstrap""""""""  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: (V8) Error require()'ing native modules since commit 0c23329
Description: """"cf.the breaking change: https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/0c23329 Fail case: 1. build any module (bump, etc.) to create its zip. 2. Create an app and copy the module's zip to the app's root folder. 3. Modify tiapp.xml in the app to include the module. 4. Modify app.js in the app to require() the module. 5. Run the app. You'll get the error """"""""Requested module not found: bootstrap"""""""" """"",title+description
TIMOB-6628,12,"""Android: Modules:  add meta data for the module manifest for the module dependencies""","""""""Update the build scripts for modules to insert meta data for dependencies into the module manifest.  IE:  required ndk version, sdk version, google apis, etc.    Requires further discussion to nail down requirements""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Modules: add meta data for the module manifest for the module dependencies
Description: """"Update the build scripts for modules to insert meta data for dependencies into the module manifest. IE: required ndk version, sdk version, google apis, etc. Requires further discussion to nail down requirements""""",title+description
