TIMOB-6576,12,"""iOS: Scroll Views - Scrolling becomes choppy when adding more Scroll View in KS's Scroll Views Basic test ""","""""""Steps to reproduce:  1. Install and launch KitchenSink  2. Navigate to: Base UI>Views>Scroll Views>Basic  3. Press """"""""Add to Scroll View"""""""" several times  4. Scroll to the bottom    Actual: Scrolling becomes choppy.  Expected: Scrolling should not become choppy when adding scroll views.    Note: Poor scrolling performance occurs in 1.7.5 as well.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Scroll Views - Scrolling becomes choppy when adding more Scroll View in KS's Scroll Views Basic test
Description: """"Steps to reproduce: 1. Install and launch KitchenSink 2. Navigate to: Base UI>Views>Scroll Views>Basic 3. Press """"""""Add to Scroll View"""""""" several times 4. Scroll to the bottom Actual: Scrolling becomes choppy. Expected: Scrolling should not become choppy when adding scroll views. Note: Poor scrolling performance occurs in 1.7.5 as well.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6575,12,"""iOS: packaging - Failing to include all optional icons during packaging causes errors in Xcode pre-submission verification""","""""""During packaging testing I created a universal app but included only the Apple """"""""required"""""""" icons as opposed to all 7 icons including the optional icons. After packaging I attempted to """"""""verify"""""""" from the Xcode organizer. The verification failed citing the missing optional icons.    Please see attached image, but disregard the bundle ID mismatch. I was expecting that.    Steps to reproduce:  1) Create a new titanium mobile application  2) Include on the 57x57 and 72x72 icons required for a universal application  3) Package for distribution  4) In the organizer, click """"""""verify""""""""    Result:  Optional and not included icons are reported as errors    Expected:  Verification completes with no errors    """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: packaging - Failing to include all optional icons during packaging causes errors in Xcode pre-submission verification
Description: """"During packaging testing I created a universal app but included only the Apple """"""""required"""""""" icons as opposed to all 7 icons including the optional icons. After packaging I attempted to """"""""verify"""""""" from the Xcode organizer. The verification failed citing the missing optional icons. Please see attached image, but disregard the bundle ID mismatch. I was expecting that. Steps to reproduce: 1) Create a new titanium mobile application 2) Include on the 57x57 and 72x72 icons required for a universal application 3) Package for distribution 4) In the organizer, click """"""""verify"""""""" Result: Optional and not included icons are reported as errors Expected: Verification completes with no errors """"",title+description
TIMOB-6574,12,"""iOS: Kitchensink - Base UI>Views>Coverflow views on iOS5 scroll differently than with iOS 4.3.x""","""""""In iOS 4.3.x devices, Coverflow images don't scroll (only transition) when the user finger-scrolls left and right.  In iOS 5.x devices, Coverflow images scroll continuously along with the user's finger until the image transitions.    Steps to Reproduce:    1. Run KitchenSink.  2. Go to: Base UI > Views > Coverflow View > Coverflow Local Files.  3. Touch and hold the main image. Slowly scroll left and right to change images.    Expected Result:    Images should continuously scroll until they transition.    Actual Result:    Images don't scroll, only transition (iOS 4.3.x).  Images do scroll in iOS 5.x devices.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Kitchensink - Base UI>Views>Coverflow views on iOS5 scroll differently than with iOS 4.3.x
Description: """"In iOS 4.3.x devices, Coverflow images don't scroll (only transition) when the user finger-scrolls left and right. In iOS 5.x devices, Coverflow images scroll continuously along with the user's finger until the image transitions. Steps to Reproduce: 1. Run KitchenSink. 2. Go to: Base UI > Views > Coverflow View > Coverflow Local Files. 3. Touch and hold the main image. Slowly scroll left and right to change images. Expected Result: Images should continuously scroll until they transition. Actual Result: Images don't scroll, only transition (iOS 4.3.x). Images do scroll in iOS 5.x devices.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6573,12,"""iOS: Some modules designed for CommonJS not exporting, even with the """"export"""" variable set""","""""""From the Titans mailing list:    Using the CommonJS module """"""""underscore"""""""" (available at http://documentcloud.github.com/underscore/) does not properly export when using {{require()}}. The relevant snippet from the code seems to be:    {code:title=underscore.js}    if (typeof exports !== 'undefined') {      if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) {        exports = module.exports = _;      }      exports._ = _;    } else if (typeof define === 'function' && define.amd) {      // Register as a named module with AMD.      define('underscore', function() {        return _;      });    } else {      // Exported as a string, for Closure Compiler """"""""advanced"""""""" mode.      root['_'] = _;    }    var _ = require('underscore')._;    [ERROR] Script Error = -[KrollCallback countByEnumeratingWithState:objects:count:]: unrecognized selector sent to instance 0x8c27fb0.  {code}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Some modules designed for CommonJS not exporting, even with the """"export"""" variable set
Description: """"From the Titans mailing list: Using the CommonJS module """"""""underscore"""""""" (available at http://documentcloud.github.com/underscore/) does not properly export when using {{require()}}. The relevant snippet from the code seems to be: {code:title=underscore.js} if (typeof exports !== 'undefined') { if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) { exports = module.exports = _; } exports._ = _; } else if (typeof define === 'function' && define.amd) { // Register as a named module with AMD. define('underscore', function() { return _; }); } else { // Exported as a string, for Closure Compiler """"""""advanced"""""""" mode. root['_'] = _; } var _ = require('underscore')._; [ERROR] Script Error = -[KrollCallback countByEnumeratingWithState:objects:count:]: unrecognized selector sent to instance 0x8c27fb0. {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-6572,12,"""iOS: Debugger crashing during module debug""","""""""It appears that certain modules are causing crashes with the debugger at this time. In particular, the relevant crash log excerpt is:        So there could be problems with certain types of source checking when we crash.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Debugger crashing during module debug
Description: """"It appears that certain modules are causing crashes with the debugger at this time. In particular, the relevant crash log excerpt is: So there could be problems with certain types of source checking when we crash.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6568,12,"""Android: Modules: Rhino: (1_8_X) Creating 2nd instance of view proxy causes app to crash""","""""""Millennial Media module demonstrates the problem, but a consolidated version of the app.js here shows it quickly. Creating a single instance of the view that is exposed from the module is successful -- the view is created and the ad is displayed. When you try to create a 2nd instance of the view the app crashes.    To reproduce,  1. Build the MillennialMedia module from my fork of the Titanium modules repository, branch """"""""apilevel2""""""""  2. Install the module  3. Create a new mobile application in TiStudio  4. Add the module reference to the tiapp.xml file, using version 2.0  5. Add the ti.android.runtime property to the tiapp.xml file and set the value to """"""""rhino""""""""  6. Use the following app.js  7. Build and run the application    [Modified app.js from the example folder of the module]      The stack trace from the crash is as follows:    """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Modules: Rhino: (1_8_X) Creating 2nd instance of view proxy causes app to crash
Description: """"Millennial Media module demonstrates the problem, but a consolidated version of the app.js here shows it quickly. Creating a single instance of the view that is exposed from the module is successful -- the view is created and the ad is displayed. When you try to create a 2nd instance of the view the app crashes. To reproduce, 1. Build the MillennialMedia module from my fork of the Titanium modules repository, branch """"""""apilevel2"""""""" 2. Install the module 3. Create a new mobile application in TiStudio 4. Add the module reference to the tiapp.xml file, using version 2.0 5. Add the ti.android.runtime property to the tiapp.xml file and set the value to """"""""rhino"""""""" 6. Use the following app.js 7. Build and run the application [Modified app.js from the example folder of the module] The stack trace from the crash is as follows: """"",title+description
TIMOB-6567,12,"""Android: V8: Terminating the application from debugger does not work and produces error.""","""""""Actual:  If you try terminating the application from Studio debugger, V8 will print an error to the log and the application will lock up.    Expected:  Application should terminate.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: V8: Terminating the application from debugger does not work and produces error.
Description: """"Actual: If you try terminating the application from Studio debugger, V8 will print an error to the log and the application will lock up. Expected: Application should terminate.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6566,12,"""Android: V8: Use dynamic linking / loading of stlport_shared to avoid symbol conflicts in modules""","""""""The NDK docs specify that apps should link against stlport_shared if they intent to load multiple shared libraries as part of the application. Since our 3rd party modules are loaded the same way our kroll-v8 shared lib is, we should change to compiling against / loading stlport_shared in our runtime.""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: V8: Use dynamic linking / loading of stlport_shared to avoid symbol conflicts in modules
Description: """"The NDK docs specify that apps should link against stlport_shared if they intent to load multiple shared libraries as part of the application. Since our 3rd party modules are loaded the same way our kroll-v8 shared lib is, we should change to compiling against / loading stlport_shared in our runtime.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6565,12,"""iOS: Drillbit 'filesystem' failure on build 1.8.0.1.2977025""","""""""There is a new drillbit failure in the latest build for iOS:    {code:title=filesystem.js}  fileMove false 371 should be true, was: false  {code}    The full drillbit report is available internally.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Drillbit 'filesystem' failure on build 1.8.0.1.2977025
Description: """"There is a new drillbit failure in the latest build for iOS: {code:title=filesystem.js} fileMove false 371 should be true, was: false {code} The full drillbit report is available internally.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6560,12,"""Android: tableView - separators alternate different sizes when anyDensity false""","""""""When anyDensity is false, the separators between the tableViewRows alternate between think and thin. This is not the case when anyDensity is true.    See attached screenshot.    h4. Steps to reproduce  Step 1: add the anyDensity: false property to your tiapp.xml  Step 2: run the code below  Step 3: notice the size of the separators between the rows    {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'#fff'});    var data = [   {title:'Row'},   {title:'Row'},   {title:'Row'},   {title:'Row'},   {title:'Row'},   {title:'Row'},   {title:'Row'},   {title:'Row'},   {title:'Row'},   {title:'Row'},  ]    var tv = Ti.UI.createTableView({   data: data  });  win.add(tv);    win.open();      h4. Workaround  Add your own separator to the row  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: tableView - separators alternate different sizes when anyDensity false
Description: """"When anyDensity is false, the separators between the tableViewRows alternate between think and thin. This is not the case when anyDensity is true. See attached screenshot. h4. Steps to reproduce Step 1: add the anyDensity: false property to your tiapp.xml Step 2: run the code below Step 3: notice the size of the separators between the rows {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'#fff'}); var data = [ {title:'Row'}, {title:'Row'}, {title:'Row'}, {title:'Row'}, {title:'Row'}, {title:'Row'}, {title:'Row'}, {title:'Row'}, {title:'Row'}, {title:'Row'}, ] var tv = Ti.UI.createTableView({ data: data }); win.add(tv); win.open(); h4. Workaround Add your own separator to the row """"",title+description
TIMOB-6559,12,"""iOS: Improve TableView rendering by better handling of opaque views""","""""""Because of the way we handle non-opaque views (like UILabels) on a table view cell, we don't get maximum performance of table views and thus the OS has to do compositing and it's slower.  This can easily be seen on device when running instruments.""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Improve TableView rendering by better handling of opaque views
Description: """"Because of the way we handle non-opaque views (like UILabels) on a table view cell, we don't get maximum performance of table views and thus the OS has to do compositing and it's slower. This can easily be seen on device when running instruments.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6558,12,"""iOS: CFBundleShortVersionString is inaccurate for packaged apps""","""""""During distribution, it looks like the CFBundleShortVersionString is always getting mangled to be """"""""1.0"""""""" regardless of the version of the app. This could be an Apple bug, part of the distribution we don't control, or another issue.    We should also be evaluating distribution as a whole.    Testing requires only packaging for distribution (not actual submission).""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: CFBundleShortVersionString is inaccurate for packaged apps
Description: """"During distribution, it looks like the CFBundleShortVersionString is always getting mangled to be """"""""1.0"""""""" regardless of the version of the app. This could be an Apple bug, part of the distribution we don't control, or another issue. We should also be evaluating distribution as a whole. Testing requires only packaging for distribution (not actual submission).""""",title+description
TIMOB-6557,12,"""iOS: Improve view rendering speeds on add""","""""""According to <USER> some developers are experiencing some lag in view rendering performance (~50ms) that is affecting specific types of application development. This ticket is for specific re-evaluation of the view add/rendering code, which should be considered part of the layout/composite spec eval and implementation pass for 1.9.    This could be as simple as batching operations together. The layout specification would need to be updated to provide support for batching all UI operations, not just layouts on specific views (although we might want that, too).    This may also be able to be solved by any work on concurrency that allows specific operations on the main thread (i.e. Ti.mainThread(function f() { /* view stuff */ });).""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Improve view rendering speeds on add
Description: """"According to <USER> some developers are experiencing some lag in view rendering performance (~50ms) that is affecting specific types of application development. This ticket is for specific re-evaluation of the view add/rendering code, which should be considered part of the layout/composite spec eval and implementation pass for 1.9. This could be as simple as batching operations together. The layout specification would need to be updated to provide support for batching all UI operations, not just layouts on specific views (although we might want that, too). This may also be able to be solved by any work on concurrency that allows specific operations on the main thread (i.e. Ti.mainThread(function f() { /* view stuff */ });).""""",title+description
