TIMOB-6403,12,"""Add lineHeight property for labels""","""""""This is a very common CSS property that should be available in Titanium. Lack of this hinders development very much. Setting multiple labels is not really an option, cause that reqires custom wrapping, etc etc and generally is _very_ messy.""""""",New Feature,"Title: Add lineHeight property for labels
Description: """"This is a very common CSS property that should be available in Titanium. Lack of this hinders development very much. Setting multiple labels is not really an option, cause that reqires custom wrapping, etc etc and generally is _very_ messy.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6399,12,"""Android: orientation - v8/rhino - Some orientation modes are not functioning correctly""","""""""Description:  While testing using our orientation test plan, I noted that two tests were failing to limit the available orientations. These were specifying LANDSCAPE_RIGHT and UPSIDE_PORTRAIT. Landscape left and portrait worked as expected, limiting the UI orientation to those specified.    Try the following code with with either of the listed orientations:        Steps to reproduce:  1) Use the sample code above  2) Install on android device  3) Rotate the device to unsupported orientations    Result:  UI rotates to all orientations    Expected:  UI remains in specified orientation    Note:  Seems to also occur with 1.7.5 GA""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: orientation - v8/rhino - Some orientation modes are not functioning correctly
Description: """"Description: While testing using our orientation test plan, I noted that two tests were failing to limit the available orientations. These were specifying LANDSCAPE_RIGHT and UPSIDE_PORTRAIT. Landscape left and portrait worked as expected, limiting the UI orientation to those specified. Try the following code with with either of the listed orientations: Steps to reproduce: 1) Use the sample code above 2) Install on android device 3) Rotate the device to unsupported orientations Result: UI rotates to all orientations Expected: UI remains in specified orientation Note: Seems to also occur with 1.7.5 GA""""",title+description
TIMOB-6398,12,"""MobileWeb: Some properties passed in to a UI element constructor are ignored.""","""""""The Ti._5.preset() and Ti._5.presetUserDefinedElements() are used to initialize properties based on the args passed in. They perform a check on the property and only set the value if the property was previously undefined. Many (most?) of the properties are set to null (which is not undefined) causing the arguments to be ignored.    These methods needs to be replaced with better versions, and UI properties need to be initialized to proper default values.""""""",Bug,"Title: MobileWeb: Some properties passed in to a UI element constructor are ignored.
Description: """"The Ti._5.preset() and Ti._5.presetUserDefinedElements() are used to initialize properties based on the args passed in. They perform a check on the property and only set the value if the property was previously undefined. Many (most?) of the properties are set to null (which is not undefined) causing the arguments to be ignored. These methods needs to be replaced with better versions, and UI properties need to be initialized to proper default values.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6396,12,"""iOS: XML drillbit test fails with malloc() double-free""","""""""Currently when running drillbit, on some systems/architectures, the XML test will crash due to the following error:        Analysis shows that this is happening in Node dealloc.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: XML drillbit test fails with malloc() double-free
Description: """"Currently when running drillbit, on some systems/architectures, the XML test will crash due to the following error: Analysis shows that this is happening in Node dealloc.""""",title+description
TIMOB-6394,12,"""iOS: Ti.Network.HTTPClient.send() always converts to POST if arguments are supplied""","""""""Discovered from a message on the Titans mailing list, and confirmed by a brief source analysis:    If we pass any arguments to {{Ti.Network.HTTPClient.send()}}, then the message is always converted to a POST, because the postData/postBody are set in the logic handling. We should be respecting the user's GET/POST protocol designation.    Note that Android has the correct behavior (it always respects GET/POST).""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Ti.Network.HTTPClient.send() always converts to POST if arguments are supplied
Description: """"Discovered from a message on the Titans mailing list, and confirmed by a brief source analysis: If we pass any arguments to {{Ti.Network.HTTPClient.send()}}, then the message is always converted to a POST, because the postData/postBody are set in the logic handling. We should be respecting the user's GET/POST protocol designation. Note that Android has the correct behavior (it always respects GET/POST).""""",title+description
