TIMOB-5228,12,"""Show left/ right arrows permanently in a Scrollable View with multiple images""","""""""On Scrollable views with multiple images, the left and right arrows show up to indicate the user that they can scroll through. However, they show up only for a few seconds and they disappear later upon not touching. When the image is swiped, they appear again.      Is there any way to show these arrows all the time so as to visually let the user know that he/ she can swipe the images.    *Sample Code*      The above code presents the normal expected behavior, but can the arrows be shown permanently?""""""",New Feature,"Title: Show left/ right arrows permanently in a Scrollable View with multiple images
Description: """"On Scrollable views with multiple images, the left and right arrows show up to indicate the user that they can scroll through. However, they show up only for a few seconds and they disappear later upon not touching. When the image is swiped, they appear again. Is there any way to show these arrows all the time so as to visually let the user know that he/ she can swipe the images. *Sample Code* The above code presents the normal expected behavior, but can the arrows be shown permanently?""""",title+description
TIMOB-5227,12,"""Android: navBarHidden blocks exitOnClose property""","""""""h3.Problem  While opening second window and putting the exitOnClose: true property together with navBarHidden, make the application not getting closed while pressing the Back Hardware button.    h3.Reproducible Steps  1. Execute the sample code  2. Press the """"""""Press Me"""""""" button  3. Press the Back button on your Android device (should exit the application, but it doesn't)    h3.Workaround   Remove navBarHidden properties from all the windows or close all the previous opened windows.    h3.Sample Code      h3.Associated HelpDesk Ticket  http://appc.me/a/APP-548793""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: navBarHidden blocks exitOnClose property
Description: """"h3.Problem While opening second window and putting the exitOnClose: true property together with navBarHidden, make the application not getting closed while pressing the Back Hardware button. h3.Reproducible Steps 1. Execute the sample code 2. Press the """"""""Press Me"""""""" button 3. Press the Back button on your Android device (should exit the application, but it doesn't) h3.Workaround Remove navBarHidden properties from all the windows or close all the previous opened windows. h3.Sample Code h3.Associated HelpDesk Ticket http://appc.me/a/APP-548793""""",title+description
TIMOB-5225,12,"""Slider change event object properties {x,y,globalPoint} are undefined""","""""""When the *change* event is fired on a Slider, the *x*, *y*, and *globalPoint* properties are being returned as undefined.  Here is a simple example showing the bug, as well as a naive workaround that will allow you to ascertain the relative *x* position of the Slider using its width and current value. Obviously, this workaround only works with a slider whose range is from 0 to 1, but a more robust workaround could use the min and max ranges to create a 0 to 1 multiplier.     """"""",Bug,"Title: Slider change event object properties {x,y,globalPoint} are undefined
Description: """"When the *change* event is fired on a Slider, the *x*, *y*, and *globalPoint* properties are being returned as undefined. Here is a simple example showing the bug, as well as a naive workaround that will allow you to ascertain the relative *x* position of the Slider using its width and current value. Obviously, this workaround only works with a slider whose range is from 0 to 1, but a more robust workaround could use the min and max ranges to create a 0 to 1 multiplier. """"",title+description
TIMOB-5224,12,"""Android: Expose """"pinch"""" events in Ti.UI.View ""","""""""We need to have the event handler for pinch gestures available for TIUIView objects and their derivatives    This is needed for many projects we've been a apart of.    We recently implemented this in the soon to be available openGL module development project (ios only so far) and it seems to work for our needs there.    Having this available for all views and objects that are based on view objects would be very useful.    Here are the code snippets from the IOS side:    Added this to the view initialization:           UIPinchGestureRecognizer *pinchRecognizer;         pinchRecognizer=[[UIPinchGestureRecognizer alloc]                           initWithTarget:self                          action:@selector(foundPinch:)];         [controller.view addGestureRecognizer:pinchRecognizer];         [pinchRecognizer release];    here's the callback:    - (void)foundPinch:(UIPinchGestureRecognizer *)recognizer {     NSDictionary *event = [NSDictionary dictionaryWithObjectsAndKeys:NUMDOUBLE(recognizer.scale), @""""""""scale"""""""", NUMDOUBLE(recognizer.velocity), @""""""""velocity"""""""", nil];     [self.proxy fireEvent:@""""""""pinch"""""""" withObject:event];  }      here's how we use it now on the opengl view     opengl.addEventListener('pinch', function {     scale = e.scale;  });    h2. Test Case       """"""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Expose """"pinch"""" events in Ti.UI.View
Description: """"We need to have the event handler for pinch gestures available for TIUIView objects and their derivatives This is needed for many projects we've been a apart of. We recently implemented this in the soon to be available openGL module development project (ios only so far) and it seems to work for our needs there. Having this available for all views and objects that are based on view objects would be very useful. Here are the code snippets from the IOS side: Added this to the view initialization: UIPinchGestureRecognizer *pinchRecognizer; pinchRecognizer=[[UIPinchGestureRecognizer alloc] initWithTarget:self action:@selector(foundPinch:)]; [controller.view addGestureRecognizer:pinchRecognizer]; [pinchRecognizer release]; here's the callback: - (void)foundPinch:(UIPinchGestureRecognizer *)recognizer { NSDictionary *event = [NSDictionary dictionaryWithObjectsAndKeys:NUMDOUBLE(recognizer.scale), @""""""""scale"""""""", NUMDOUBLE(recognizer.velocity), @""""""""velocity"""""""", nil]; [self.proxy fireEvent:@""""""""pinch"""""""" withObject:event]; } here's how we use it now on the opengl view opengl.addEventListener('pinch', function { scale = e.scale; }); h2. Test Case """"",title+description
TIMOB-5223,12,"""iOS: When device has passcode getting the application data from xcode throws """"The operation header was invalid""""""","""""""Steps To Reproduce:  1. Compile an application in TiStudio  2. Sync application to device  3. Launch xCode and open window -> organizer  4. Click on applications under device and click download  5. Set passcode on device and click download again    Actual:  The operation header was invalid alert is displayed    Expected:  No alert to be displayed    Note:  First time downloading you get the library folder when viewing the package content  Second time downloading you don't get any folder""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: When device has passcode getting the application data from xcode throws """"The operation header was invalid""""
Description: """"Steps To Reproduce: 1. Compile an application in TiStudio 2. Sync application to device 3. Launch xCode and open window -> organizer 4. Click on applications under device and click download 5. Set passcode on device and click download again Actual: The operation header was invalid alert is displayed Expected: No alert to be displayed Note: First time downloading you get the library folder when viewing the package content Second time downloading you don't get any folder""""",title+description
