TIMOB-4801,12,"""Android: Enable flashplayer settings(plugins) for webview in Android""","""""""Run the code below in app.js. The youtube video cannot be played inside the webview. Instead Ti.Platform.openURL has to be used.  """"""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Enable flashplayer settings(plugins) for webview in Android
Description: """"Run the code below in app.js. The youtube video cannot be played inside the webview. Instead Ti.Platform.openURL has to be used. """"",title+description
TIMOB-4799,12,"""Ti.UI.backgroundColor/Ti.UI.backgroundImage disappear when orientation is changed""","""""""1) Create project in TiStudio and replace app.js with     Ti.UI.backgroundColor = '#f00';    var win = Ti.UI.createWindow();  win.open();    or    Ti.UI.backgroundImage = 'KS_nav_ui.png';    var win = Ti.UI.createWindow();  win.open();    (Or use attached Resources folders)    2) Open app in portrait orientation.      Results:  Appropriate background appears.    3) Switch to landscape orientation.    Expected: Background remains.  Results: Background disappears.  <USER>splash screen appears.  Splash screen stays regardless of orientation changes.     Note:    Talked to <USER>about this.  He says it isn't a major issue because he doesn't see a circumstance where someone would set the background this way.     It is a parity issue.  Setting the background this way works as expected on iOS devices.""""""",Bug,"Title: Ti.UI.backgroundColor/Ti.UI.backgroundImage disappear when orientation is changed
Description: """"1) Create project in TiStudio and replace app.js with Ti.UI.backgroundColor = '#f00'; var win = Ti.UI.createWindow(); win.open(); or Ti.UI.backgroundImage = 'KS_nav_ui.png'; var win = Ti.UI.createWindow(); win.open(); (Or use attached Resources folders) 2) Open app in portrait orientation. Results: Appropriate background appears. 3) Switch to landscape orientation. Expected: Background remains. Results: Background disappears. <USER>splash screen appears. Splash screen stays regardless of orientation changes. Note: Talked to <USER>about this. He says it isn't a major issue because he doesn't see a circumstance where someone would set the background this way. It is a parity issue. Setting the background this way works as expected on iOS devices.""""",title+description
