TIMOB-4782,12,"""iPad Orientation Regression""","""""""If you set landscape only on an iPad app, 1.7.2 and up do not recognize it, and it launches in Portrait.    Test Code:  {code:title=app.js}  Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000');    var win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({      title:'Tab 1',      backgroundColor:'#fff'  });    var label1 = Titanium.UI.createLabel({   color:'#999',   text:'I am Window 1',   font:{fontSize:20,fontFamily:'Helvetica Neue'},   textAlign:'center',   width:'auto'  });  win.add(label1);  win.open()      """"""",Bug,"Title: iPad Orientation Regression
Description: """"If you set landscape only on an iPad app, 1.7.2 and up do not recognize it, and it launches in Portrait. Test Code: {code:title=app.js} Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var win = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ title:'Tab 1', backgroundColor:'#fff' }); var label1 = Titanium.UI.createLabel({ color:'#999', text:'I am Window 1', font:{fontSize:20,fontFamily:'Helvetica Neue'}, textAlign:'center', width:'auto' }); win.add(label1); win.open() """"",title+description
TIMOB-4781,12,"""Drillbit: Add iPhone Simulator drop-down selector versions to Drillbit UI""","""""""Since we're doing this already for Android, it would be nice if we could add the various versions of the iPhone Simulator to a new drop down list that Drillbit would use to launch the selected version.    Sabil, feel free to sync up with me or Chase who is working on the mirror of this issue for Android  """"""",New Feature,"Title: Drillbit: Add iPhone Simulator drop-down selector versions to Drillbit UI
Description: """"Since we're doing this already for Android, it would be nice if we could add the various versions of the iPhone Simulator to a new drop down list that Drillbit would use to launch the selected version. Sabil, feel free to sync up with me or Chase who is working on the mirror of this issue for Android """"",title+description
TIMOB-4780,12,"""Tools: Add a command that installs platform-specific resources and XML to an existing Titanium project""","""""""Initially when a new Titanium Mobile project is created, we create it with some default placeholder images, configuration in tiapp.xml, and other things. If a project is created with only one platform target, then only the resources for that platform are copied into the project.    It would be nice if Titanium developers could issue an SDK command to copy the default project resources for a platform into their existing project if it wasn't initially targeted to that platform.    Currently we have a number of commands on our """"""""titanium.py"""""""" script, so this would be a new command along the lines of:    titanium.py copy_resources --platform=<platform> <project dir>""""""",New Feature,"Title: Tools: Add a command that installs platform-specific resources and XML to an existing Titanium project
Description: """"Initially when a new Titanium Mobile project is created, we create it with some default placeholder images, configuration in tiapp.xml, and other things. If a project is created with only one platform target, then only the resources for that platform are copied into the project. It would be nice if Titanium developers could issue an SDK command to copy the default project resources for a platform into their existing project if it wasn't initially targeted to that platform. Currently we have a number of commands on our """"""""titanium.py"""""""" script, so this would be a new command along the lines of: titanium.py copy_resources --platform=<platform> <project dir>""""",title+description
TIMOB-4779,12,"""'location' event listener on Ti.Geolocation on Android is unreliable""","""""""Behaviour reproducible in the KitchenSink [geolocation.js|https://github.com/<USER>KitchenSink/blob/master/Resources/examples/geolocation.js] example on Titanium 1.5 to 1.6+.        {quote}  Sometimes the listener fails to return any location even after a few minutes. However, if I open a Ti.Map.MapView with the userLocation attribute set to true, my current location is pointed out on the map within 20 seconds. Other location-based apps installed on the Android phone also manage to successfully geolocate me.  {quote}    (From Q&A post at http://developer.<USER>com/question/118712/tigeolocation-on-android-seems-flaky)    Key points from our conversation with <USER>about this issue:    * Accuracy level has been set to *Ti.Geolocation.ACCURACY_BEST*. Our code is more or less the same as the KS example code.  * Reports of geolocation failure from a *wide range of devices*. We have seen nearly 60% of users dropping out because the app didn't geolocate them. Even our existing users keep asking us to improve geolocation - esp. because they see that the map within our app shows the current location, but our geolocation code fails to work.  * The important thing is that this *doesn't happen all the time*. Single-shot GPS readings do come back at a reasonable rate when the geolocation is working. But when it isn't working, we have to wait for minutes together with no guarantee of success.  * Opening & locating ourselves in Gowalla or *other location-based apps* ensures that the Kitchen Sink demo always works the next time we open it.    We *don't* run the geolocation call within the app.js context. We open a heavyweight window using a URL from app.js. From this heavyweight window, we open another window using a URL. It is in this window's context that the geolocation call occurs.    """"""",Bug,"Title: 'location' event listener on Ti.Geolocation on Android is unreliable
Description: """"Behaviour reproducible in the KitchenSink [geolocation.js|https://github.com/<USER>KitchenSink/blob/master/Resources/examples/geolocation.js] example on Titanium 1.5 to 1.6+. {quote} Sometimes the listener fails to return any location even after a few minutes. However, if I open a Ti.Map.MapView with the userLocation attribute set to true, my current location is pointed out on the map within 20 seconds. Other location-based apps installed on the Android phone also manage to successfully geolocate me. {quote} (From Q&A post at http://developer.<USER>com/question/118712/tigeolocation-on-android-seems-flaky) Key points from our conversation with <USER>about this issue: * Accuracy level has been set to *Ti.Geolocation.ACCURACY_BEST*. Our code is more or less the same as the KS example code. * Reports of geolocation failure from a *wide range of devices*. We have seen nearly 60% of users dropping out because the app didn't geolocate them. Even our existing users keep asking us to improve geolocation - esp. because they see that the map within our app shows the current location, but our geolocation code fails to work. * The important thing is that this *doesn't happen all the time*. Single-shot GPS readings do come back at a reasonable rate when the geolocation is working. But when it isn't working, we have to wait for minutes together with no guarantee of success. * Opening & locating ourselves in Gowalla or *other location-based apps* ensures that the Kitchen Sink demo always works the next time we open it. We *don't* run the geolocation call within the app.js context. We open a heavyweight window using a URL from app.js. From this heavyweight window, we open another window using a URL. It is in this window's context that the geolocation call occurs. """"",title+description
TIMOB-4778,12,"""Using Point-Notation in I18N Keys""","""""""At the moment, its not possible to use the """"""""."""""""" point in the I18N-Key.        I think, for readability this point notation is really good!    The strings.xml looks like this:        """"""",Bug,"Title: Using Point-Notation in I18N Keys
Description: """"At the moment, its not possible to use the """"""""."""""""" point in the I18N-Key. I think, for readability this point notation is really good! The strings.xml looks like this: """"",title+description
