TIMOB-4489,12,"""iOS: Including 'scheduleLocalnotification' inside a function results in application crash""","""""""This is a testcase to run a background service and creating a local notification. The background service and local notification in the kitchensink example runs fine. But if we include the 'scheduleLocalnotification'(in bg.js) inside a function, the application crashes.      Step 1: Copy the following code to app.js    Step 2: Copy the following code in bg.js ( bg.js and app.js under resources folder)      Step3: Run the app.  Step4: Pause the app using the menu button.  Step5: A notification will be displayed.  Step6: Resume the app by clicking 'Resume' on notification alert.  Step7: Application crashes.    h2. Tested On  iOS 4.3, TiSDK 1.7.1    Works fine on 1.6.2      """"""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Including 'scheduleLocalnotification' inside a function results in application crash
Description: """"This is a testcase to run a background service and creating a local notification. The background service and local notification in the kitchensink example runs fine. But if we include the 'scheduleLocalnotification'(in bg.js) inside a function, the application crashes. Step 1: Copy the following code to app.js Step 2: Copy the following code in bg.js ( bg.js and app.js under resources folder) Step3: Run the app. Step4: Pause the app using the menu button. Step5: A notification will be displayed. Step6: Resume the app by clicking 'Resume' on notification alert. Step7: Application crashes. h2. Tested On iOS 4.3, TiSDK 1.7.1 Works fine on 1.6.2 """"",title+description
TIMOB-4488,12,"""Android: Expose WebView onReceivedSslError""","""""""h1. Feature  Expose the onReceivedSslError method of the web view on Android so that developers can handle what happens when an SSL certificate is rejected.    h2. Why  The default behavior is to reject any SSL certificate that doesn't validate. By exposing this, the developer could ask the user if they want to proceed.    h2. Sample Code  The following could utilize this feature. I bet there's a better way to expose the SSLError constants.  {code:title=app.js}  var window = Ti.UI.createWindow({      backgroundColor:'#fff'  });    var webView = Ti.UI.createScrollView({      width: 320, height: 480,      contentWidth: 2000  });  webView.addEventListener('onReceivedSslError', function(evt) {      // What error happened? Check the constants. These are taken from the following URL:      // http://developer.android.com/reference/android/net/http/SslError.html      switch (evt.error) {          case evt.SSL_EXPIRED:              break;          case evt.SSL_IDMISMATCH:              break;          case evt.SSL_MAX_ERROR:              break;          case evt.SSL_NOTYETVALID:              break;          case evt.SSL_UNTRUSTED:              break;      }      // Should we proceed?      evt.handler.proceed();  });  window.add(webView);    window.open();  {code}    h2. Relevant Documentation  http://developer.android.com/reference/android/webkit/WebViewClient.html#onReceivedSslError(android.webkit.WebView, android.webkit.SslErrorHandler, android.net.http.SslError)    h3. Associated Helpdesk Ticket  http://appc.me/c/APP-235542""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Expose WebView onReceivedSslError
Description: """"h1. Feature Expose the onReceivedSslError method of the web view on Android so that developers can handle what happens when an SSL certificate is rejected. h2. Why The default behavior is to reject any SSL certificate that doesn't validate. By exposing this, the developer could ask the user if they want to proceed. h2. Sample Code The following could utilize this feature. I bet there's a better way to expose the SSLError constants. {code:title=app.js} var window = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor:'#fff' }); var webView = Ti.UI.createScrollView({ width: 320, height: 480, contentWidth: 2000 }); webView.addEventListener('onReceivedSslError', function(evt) { // What error happened? Check the constants. These are taken from the following URL: // http://developer.android.com/reference/android/net/http/SslError.html switch (evt.error) { case evt.SSL_EXPIRED: break; case evt.SSL_IDMISMATCH: break; case evt.SSL_MAX_ERROR: break; case evt.SSL_NOTYETVALID: break; case evt.SSL_UNTRUSTED: break; } // Should we proceed? evt.handler.proceed(); }); window.add(webView); window.open(); {code} h2. Relevant Documentation http://developer.android.com/reference/android/webkit/WebViewClient.html#onReceivedSslError(android.webkit.WebView, android.webkit.SslErrorHandler, android.net.http.SslError) h3. Associated Helpdesk Ticket http://appc.me/c/APP-235542""""",title+description
TIMOB-4487,12,"""iOS: Expose dragStart and dragEnd on Scroll View""","""""""h1. Feature  Expose two new events on a scroll view: """"""""dragStart"""""""" and """"""""dragEnd"""""""". These would occur with scrollViewWillBeginDragging and scrollViewDidEndDragging.    h2. Sample Code  The following code would use these events.  {code:title=app.js}  var window = Ti.UI.createWindow({      backgroundColor:'#fff'  });    var scrollView = Ti.UI.createScrollView({      width: 320, height: 480,      contentWidth: 2000  });  scrollView.addEventListener('dragStart', function(e) {      Ti.API.info('drag start!');      window.backgroundColor = '#aaa';  });  scrollView.addEventListener('dragEnd', function(e) {      Ti.API.info('drag end! Will decelerate? ' + (e.decelerate ? 'Yes' : 'No'));      window.backgroundColor = '#fff';  });  scrollView.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({      text: 'Swipe Me',      width: 2000, height: 50,      textAlign: 'left'  }));  scrollView.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({      text: '...and dragStart and dragEnd should fire!',      width: 2000, height: 50,      textAlign: 'right'  }));  window.add(scrollView);    window.open();  {code}    h2. Associated Helpdesk Ticket  http://appc.me/c/APP-413136""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Expose dragStart and dragEnd on Scroll View
Description: """"h1. Feature Expose two new events on a scroll view: """"""""dragStart"""""""" and """"""""dragEnd"""""""". These would occur with scrollViewWillBeginDragging and scrollViewDidEndDragging. h2. Sample Code The following code would use these events. {code:title=app.js} var window = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor:'#fff' }); var scrollView = Ti.UI.createScrollView({ width: 320, height: 480, contentWidth: 2000 }); scrollView.addEventListener('dragStart', function(e) { Ti.API.info('drag start!'); window.backgroundColor = '#aaa'; }); scrollView.addEventListener('dragEnd', function(e) { Ti.API.info('drag end! Will decelerate? ' + (e.decelerate ? 'Yes' : 'No')); window.backgroundColor = '#fff'; }); scrollView.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({ text: 'Swipe Me', width: 2000, height: 50, textAlign: 'left' })); scrollView.add(Ti.UI.createLabel({ text: '...and dragStart and dragEnd should fire!', width: 2000, height: 50, textAlign: 'right' })); window.add(scrollView); window.open(); {code} h2. Associated Helpdesk Ticket http://appc.me/c/APP-413136""""",title+description
