TIMOB-4469,12,"""Android:  Filesystem exists() method used on file in resourcesDirectory always returns true even if the file is not present""","""""""Note: please see [this comment|#comment-164088] below for clearer testcase and accurate explanation of the bug.    1. create an empty mobile project.  2. Copy the following sample code. http://pastie.org/2102937  3. The image is not loaded even if it is present in the filesystem.    Works fine with Ti SDK 1.6.2""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Filesystem exists() method used on file in resourcesDirectory always returns true even if the file is not present
Description: """"Note: please see [this comment|#comment-164088] below for clearer testcase and accurate explanation of the bug. 1. create an empty mobile project. 2. Copy the following sample code. http://pastie.org/2102937 3. The image is not loaded even if it is present in the filesystem. Works fine with Ti SDK 1.6.2""""",title+description
TIMOB-4468,12,"""Android: Created module check out fails due to .svn directories""","""""""There's a HD ticket having this issue: http://support.<USER>com/display/APP-478438    Rationale:   Whenever a newly created iphone module is imported into subversion, check out fails due to bug in the build.py script in the presence of .svn directories.    Steps to replicate:    1) create new module:  """"""""/Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/1.7.0/titanium.py"""""""" create --platform=iphone --type=module --dir=. --name=testmodule1 --id=com.test.testmodule1 --version=0.1    2) import into subversion    3) in a fresh directory checkout from subversion    4) run ./build.py  fails with the following message:  ---  Traceback (most recent call last):    File """"""""./build.py"""""""", line 193, in <module>      package_module(manifest,mf,config)    File """"""""./build.py"""""""", line 173, in package_module      docs = generate_doc(config)    File """"""""./build.py"""""""", line 57, in generate_doc      md = open(os.path.join(docdir,file)).read()  IOError: [Errno 21] Is a directory  Build step 'Execute shell' marked build as failure  ---  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android: Created module check out fails due to .svn directories
Description: """"There's a HD ticket having this issue: http://support.<USER>com/display/APP-478438 Rationale: Whenever a newly created iphone module is imported into subversion, check out fails due to bug in the build.py script in the presence of .svn directories. Steps to replicate: 1) create new module: """"""""/Library/Application Support/Titanium/mobilesdk/osx/1.7.0/titanium.py"""""""" create --platform=iphone --type=module --dir=. --name=testmodule1 --id=com.test.testmodule1 --version=0.1 2) import into subversion 3) in a fresh directory checkout from subversion 4) run ./build.py fails with the following message: --- Traceback (most recent call last): File """"""""./build.py"""""""", line 193, in <module> package_module(manifest,mf,config) File """"""""./build.py"""""""", line 173, in package_module docs = generate_doc(config) File """"""""./build.py"""""""", line 57, in generate_doc md = open(os.path.join(docdir,file)).read() IOError: [Errno 21] Is a directory Build step 'Execute shell' marked build as failure --- """"",title+description
TIMOB-4466,12,"""iOS: High Density Screenshots""","""""""h1. Feature  Support high density screenshots for iOS.    h2. Sample Code  Drop the following in an app.js, and fire up the iPhone 4 simulator. It will take a screenshot of the blue window, then log and alert the path to the image. Go to it in Finder and see what size it is. The goal of this ticket is to make it the full resolution (640x960, instead of 320x480).  {code:title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: 'blue' });  win.open();  Ti.Media.takeScreenshot(function(event) {      var shotFile = Ti.Filesystem.getFile(Ti.Filesystem.applicationDataDirectory, 'screenshot.png');      if (shotFile.exists()) {          shotFile.deleteFile();      }      shotFile.write(event.media);      Ti.API.info(shotFile.nativePath);      alert(shotFile.nativePath);  });  {code}    h2. Pull Request  https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/149""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: High Density Screenshots
Description: """"h1. Feature Support high density screenshots for iOS. h2. Sample Code Drop the following in an app.js, and fire up the iPhone 4 simulator. It will take a screenshot of the blue window, then log and alert the path to the image. Go to it in Finder and see what size it is. The goal of this ticket is to make it the full resolution (640x960, instead of 320x480). {code:title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: 'blue' }); win.open(); Ti.Media.takeScreenshot(function(event) { var shotFile = Ti.Filesystem.getFile(Ti.Filesystem.applicationDataDirectory, 'screenshot.png'); if (shotFile.exists()) { shotFile.deleteFile(); } shotFile.write(event.media); Ti.API.info(shotFile.nativePath); alert(shotFile.nativePath); }); {code} h2. Pull Request https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/149""""",title+description
TIMOB-4465,12,"""Sony X10: Ti.Platform.displayCaps does not return correct values, causing UI objects to be shrunk in size""","""""""When the following code is run on a Sony X10 (Android device), the Ti.Platform.displayCaps are not correct, causing UI objects to be shrunk to an extent where they are unreadable. The following code demonstrates the issue:    {code:lang=javascript}  Ti.API.info(""""""""Display dimensions: """""""" + Ti.Platform.displayCaps.platformWidth + """""""" x """""""" + Ti.Platform.displayCaps.platformHeight);  {code}    When running 1.6.2 it returns """"""""Display dimensions: 320 x 569"""""""" (correct)  When running 1.7.0 it returns """"""""Display dimensions: 480 X 854"""""""" (too large)      See original QA  [http://developer.<USER>com/question/120119/weird-ui-issues]    """"""",Bug,"Title: Sony X10: Ti.Platform.displayCaps does not return correct values, causing UI objects to be shrunk in size
Description: """"When the following code is run on a Sony X10 (Android device), the Ti.Platform.displayCaps are not correct, causing UI objects to be shrunk to an extent where they are unreadable. The following code demonstrates the issue: {code:lang=javascript} Ti.API.info(""""""""Display dimensions: """""""" + Ti.Platform.displayCaps.platformWidth + """""""" x """""""" + Ti.Platform.displayCaps.platformHeight); {code} When running 1.6.2 it returns """"""""Display dimensions: 320 x 569"""""""" (correct) When running 1.7.0 it returns """"""""Display dimensions: 480 X 854"""""""" (too large) See original QA [http://developer.<USER>com/question/120119/weird-ui-issues] """"",title+description
TIMOB-4464,12,"""Explose listeners object via proxy property, to maintain consistency with iOS""","""""""The primary reason for this ticket is to maintain parity with iOS functionality.    Ticket [TIMOB-4463|http://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-4463] explains that iOS _used to_ (in 1.6) expose a listeners objects that held a reference to all the event listeners, although the same is not available in 1.7 (due to an oversight/bug)    Note that Android has never provided this facility, hence this ticket.    The user feels this is important for the following reasons:    {quote}  The main use case, is the allow a developer to remove an eventListener or set of event listeners in the case that :   * The event handler is an anonymous function  * You wish to remove all event listeners of a specific type - 'click' in the example above.  (see Ex2's removeAllListeners function in the bug description for an example)  * The named function used as the eventHandler is not known in the current scope (adding, or removing an event listener in a utility function)    Use of anonymous functions, that depend on closures to keep locally scoped variables in scope, are a big part of javascript development.  The role functions as first-class objects in javascript is one if it's greatest strengths.      Since removeEventListener requires that you pass a function as the second parameter, there is no way (other than accessing the .listeners property) to remove a bound anonymous handler.  {quote}    The following demonstrates the functionality on iOS:    {code:lang=javascript}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({   backgroundColor : """"""""blue"""""""",   exitOnClose:true,   layout:'vertical'  });    var myView=Ti.UI.createView({   backgroundColor:'green',   height:200,   width:200  });    win.add(myView);  win.open();    myView.addEventListener('click', function(){   Ti.API.info('Click event fired');  });    Ti.API.info(myView.listeners.click);  Ti.API.info(""""""""myView.listeners.click: """""""" + myView.listeners.click); // comment this for android to avoid exception    [INFO] myView.listeners: [object Object]    [INFO] myView.listeners.click: 1    [INFO] myView.listeners: [object Object]    [INFO] myView.listeners.click: function () {     Ti.API.info('Click event fired');    }    (kroll$1) [1054,1170] myView.listeners: undefined  {code}""""""",New Feature,"Title: Explose listeners object via proxy property, to maintain consistency with iOS
Description: """"The primary reason for this ticket is to maintain parity with iOS functionality. Ticket [TIMOB-4463|http://jira.<USER>org/browse/TIMOB-4463] explains that iOS _used to_ (in 1.6) expose a listeners objects that held a reference to all the event listeners, although the same is not available in 1.7 (due to an oversight/bug) Note that Android has never provided this facility, hence this ticket. The user feels this is important for the following reasons: {quote} The main use case, is the allow a developer to remove an eventListener or set of event listeners in the case that : * The event handler is an anonymous function * You wish to remove all event listeners of a specific type - 'click' in the example above. (see Ex2's removeAllListeners function in the bug description for an example) * The named function used as the eventHandler is not known in the current scope (adding, or removing an event listener in a utility function) Use of anonymous functions, that depend on closures to keep locally scoped variables in scope, are a big part of javascript development. The role functions as first-class objects in javascript is one if it's greatest strengths. Since removeEventListener requires that you pass a function as the second parameter, there is no way (other than accessing the .listeners property) to remove a bound anonymous handler. {quote} The following demonstrates the functionality on iOS: {code:lang=javascript} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor : """"""""blue"""""""", exitOnClose:true, layout:'vertical' }); var myView=Ti.UI.createView({ backgroundColor:'green', height:200, width:200 }); win.add(myView); win.open(); myView.addEventListener('click', function(){ Ti.API.info('Click event fired'); }); Ti.API.info(myView.listeners.click); Ti.API.info(""""""""myView.listeners.click: """""""" + myView.listeners.click); // comment this for android to avoid exception [INFO] myView.listeners: [object Object] [INFO] myView.listeners.click: 1 [INFO] myView.listeners: [object Object] [INFO] myView.listeners.click: function () { Ti.API.info('Click event fired'); } (kroll$1) [1054,1170] myView.listeners: undefined {code}""""",title+description
TIMOB-4463,12,"""listeners property of proxy objects no longer available in 1.7.X (inconsistant compared with 1.6.2)""","""""""Accessing a Proxy's listeners property doesn't work as expected in mobilesdk 1.7.0 (as it did in 1.6.2).    Given the proxy """"""""myView"""""""" (any view type):    * In 1.6.2 - myView.listeners would give you an object with key/value pairs equating to the listener type/array of callback functions bound as Listeners to the view.  * In 1.7.0 - myView.listeners gives you an object, however the value is now an integer, which seems to be equal to the length of what the callback array would be.    This code demonstrates the behavior:    {code:lang=javascript}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({   backgroundColor : """"""""blue"""""""",   exitOnClose:true,   layout:'vertical'  });    var myView=Ti.UI.createView({   backgroundColor:'green',   height:200,   width:200  });    win.add(myView);  win.open();    myView.addEventListener('click', function(){   Ti.API.info('Click event fired');  });    Ti.API.info(myView.listeners.click);  Ti.API.info(""""""""myView.listeners.click: """""""" + myView.listeners.click); // comment this for android to avoid exception    [INFO] myView.listeners: [object Object]    [INFO] myView.listeners.click: function () {     Ti.API.info('Click event fired');    }    [INFO] myView.listeners: [object Object]    [INFO] myView.listeners.click: 1  {code}    See a full justification for this functionality [in this comment|#comment-156716].    Note that this functionality has never been available for Android, as [this comment|#comment-157207] demonstrates.""""""",Bug,"Title: listeners property of proxy objects no longer available in 1.7.X (inconsistant compared with 1.6.2)
Description: """"Accessing a Proxy's listeners property doesn't work as expected in mobilesdk 1.7.0 (as it did in 1.6.2). Given the proxy """"""""myView"""""""" (any view type): * In 1.6.2 - myView.listeners would give you an object with key/value pairs equating to the listener type/array of callback functions bound as Listeners to the view. * In 1.7.0 - myView.listeners gives you an object, however the value is now an integer, which seems to be equal to the length of what the callback array would be. This code demonstrates the behavior: {code:lang=javascript} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor : """"""""blue"""""""", exitOnClose:true, layout:'vertical' }); var myView=Ti.UI.createView({ backgroundColor:'green', height:200, width:200 }); win.add(myView); win.open(); myView.addEventListener('click', function(){ Ti.API.info('Click event fired'); }); Ti.API.info(myView.listeners.click); Ti.API.info(""""""""myView.listeners.click: """""""" + myView.listeners.click); // comment this for android to avoid exception [INFO] myView.listeners: [object Object] [INFO] myView.listeners.click: function () { Ti.API.info('Click event fired'); } [INFO] myView.listeners: [object Object] [INFO] myView.listeners.click: 1 {code} See a full justification for this functionality [in this comment|#comment-156716]. Note that this functionality has never been available for Android, as [this comment|#comment-157207] demonstrates.""""",title+description
TIMOB-4462,12,"""Android: Detect SIM Card""","""""""Customer would like the ability to detect the SIM card and detect a SIM card change.    Apps like WHATSAPP will immediately recognize that the SIM card has changed, and force the user to walk through the SMS-Verification-<USER>it does when installing the app at first.    The ability to identify a SIM Card change, allows apps to act on that action which can be a very important feature for user authentication.    Ticket Reference: http://appc.me/c/APP-852289    """"""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Detect SIM Card
Description: """"Customer would like the ability to detect the SIM card and detect a SIM card change. Apps like WHATSAPP will immediately recognize that the SIM card has changed, and force the user to walk through the SMS-Verification-<USER>it does when installing the app at first. The ability to identify a SIM Card change, allows apps to act on that action which can be a very important feature for user authentication. Ticket Reference: http://appc.me/c/APP-852289 """"",title+description
TIMOB-4458,12,"""Android Boosting the performance of adding annotations""","""""""There is a HD Support ticket having this issue: http://support.<USER>com/display/APP-578943    It looks like we update the overlay every time an annotation is added, and that is burdening the app.    Affecting therefore the performance of adding multiple annotations.  """"""",Bug,"Title: Android Boosting the performance of adding annotations
Description: """"There is a HD Support ticket having this issue: http://support.<USER>com/display/APP-578943 It looks like we update the overlay every time an annotation is added, and that is burdening the app. Affecting therefore the performance of adding multiple annotations. """"",title+description
