TIMOB-3257,12,"""iOS: Inconsistent httpClient behavior between Ti SDK 1.5 and 1.6""","""""""{html}<div><p>Issue Description: My code accesses the Google Docs List API to export online Google Document contents to a webview in the app. It works perfectly in TiDev 1.5 but does not work the same with 1.6 even with the same code base.</p> <p>Steps to reproduce:</p> <ol> <li> <p>Copy and paste the code from <a href= """"""""http://pastie.org/1626702"""""""">http://pastie.org/1626702</a> to your app.js. You can start with a new project in TiDev and just overwrite the existing app.js content with this.</p> </li> <li> <p>Create a new file called """"""""parse.js"""""""" and paste the code from here <a href= """"""""http://pastie.org/1626711"""""""">http://pastie.org/1626711</a>.</p> </li> <li> <p>If you have a Google account, login using your desktop and create a few dummy documents (these must be of 'Document' type).</p> </li> <li> <p>The code once copied and pasted is ready to go, so build the code in Titanium Developer using SDK v1.5 and login to your Google account through the app. Once logged in, click on a document in the list to see the document display in the webview.</p> </li> <li> <p>Repeat step 4 but this time use SDK v1.6. You should see that the Google page that comes up now is not the right one.</p> </li> </ol> <p>Expected behavior: The selected document's contents should appear in the webview (see attached screen-shot expected.png)</p> <p>Actual behavior: The Google sign up page is displayed instead in the webview, which usually indicates that an auth token was not received (see attached screen-shot actual.png)</p> <p>Target and Development platform (Titanium Mobile 1.6 on Mac OSX Snow Leopard 64bit for iOS)</p> <p>Logs and errors: The app does not generate any errors. Code wise, it seems to be fine.</p> <p>Initial analysis &amp; suspicions: I have a suspicion that httpClient in 1.6 seems to operate rather differently than in 1.5 perhaps when it comes to the sequence of asynchronous events.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Inconsistent httpClient behavior between Ti SDK 1.5 and 1.6
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Issue Description: My code accesses the Google Docs List API to export online Google Document contents to a webview in the app. It works perfectly in TiDev 1.5 but does not work the same with 1.6 even with the same code base.</p> <p>Steps to reproduce:</p> <ol> <li> <p>Copy and paste the code from <a href= """"""""http://pastie.org/1626702"""""""">http://pastie.org/1626702</a> to your app.js. You can start with a new project in TiDev and just overwrite the existing app.js content with this.</p> </li> <li> <p>Create a new file called """"""""parse.js"""""""" and paste the code from here <a href= """"""""http://pastie.org/1626711"""""""">http://pastie.org/1626711</a>.</p> </li> <li> <p>If you have a Google account, login using your desktop and create a few dummy documents (these must be of 'Document' type).</p> </li> <li> <p>The code once copied and pasted is ready to go, so build the code in Titanium Developer using SDK v1.5 and login to your Google account through the app. Once logged in, click on a document in the list to see the document display in the webview.</p> </li> <li> <p>Repeat step 4 but this time use SDK v1.6. You should see that the Google page that comes up now is not the right one.</p> </li> </ol> <p>Expected behavior: The selected document's contents should appear in the webview (see attached screen-shot expected.png)</p> <p>Actual behavior: The Google sign up page is displayed instead in the webview, which usually indicates that an auth token was not received (see attached screen-shot actual.png)</p> <p>Target and Development platform (Titanium Mobile 1.6 on Mac OSX Snow Leopard 64bit for iOS)</p> <p>Logs and errors: The app does not generate any errors. Code wise, it seems to be fine.</p> <p>Initial analysis &amp; suspicions: I have a suspicion that httpClient in 1.6 seems to operate rather differently than in 1.5 perhaps when it comes to the sequence of asynchronous events.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-3256,12,"""iOS: """"Horizontal"""" layout incorrectly wraps""","""""""h2. Problem  On iOS, when you have a view with a layout: 'horizontal' and the content exceeds the width of the view, new content is wrapped onto the next line (attached image demonstrates). This should be the behavior for a layout: 'flow', not 'horizontal'.    h2. Sample Code      {code:lang=javascript}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: '#fff' });    var scroll = Ti.UI.createScrollView({      contentHeight: 'auto',      layout: 'horizontal',      left: 20, right: 20  });  for (var i = 0; i &lt; 81; i++) {      scroll.add(Ti.UI.createImageView({          image: 'http://www.<USER>com/wp-content/themes/<USER>img/ti-logo.png',          height: 30, width: 72,          top: 20, left: 20      }));  }  win.add(scroll);    win.open();  {code}    h2. Workaround  Don't use layout at all. Instead, calculate the left of each item as it is added to the view.    h2. Tested On  Titanium SDK version: 1.6.0 (02/23/11 12:34 9882e81)    BROKEN on iOS Simulator 4.2  BROKEN on iPod Touch 4.2  WORKS on Android Simulator 2.2 APIs  WORKS on Android Device EPIC 4G 2.2  h2. Associated Helpdesk Ticket  There is no associated helpdesk ticket for this, but I did uncover this in a company sanctioned sample app.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: """"Horizontal"""" layout incorrectly wraps
Description: """"h2. Problem On iOS, when you have a view with a layout: 'horizontal' and the content exceeds the width of the view, new content is wrapped onto the next line (attached image demonstrates). This should be the behavior for a layout: 'flow', not 'horizontal'. h2. Sample Code {code:lang=javascript} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: '#fff' }); var scroll = Ti.UI.createScrollView({ contentHeight: 'auto', layout: 'horizontal', left: 20, right: 20 }); for (var i = 0; i &lt; 81; i++) { scroll.add(Ti.UI.createImageView({ image: 'http://www.<USER>com/wp-content/themes/<USER>img/ti-logo.png', height: 30, width: 72, top: 20, left: 20 })); } win.add(scroll); win.open(); {code} h2. Workaround Don't use layout at all. Instead, calculate the left of each item as it is added to the view. h2. Tested On Titanium SDK version: 1.6.0 (02/23/11 12:34 9882e81) BROKEN on iOS Simulator 4.2 BROKEN on iPod Touch 4.2 WORKS on Android Simulator 2.2 APIs WORKS on Android Device EPIC 4G 2.2 h2. Associated Helpdesk Ticket There is no associated helpdesk ticket for this, but I did uncover this in a company sanctioned sample app.""""",title+description
TIMOB-3255,12,"""encrypted database feature""","""""""{html}<div><p>Hi,</p>  <p>We just created a new app and are planning to create some more  with your platform. Works perfectly execpt for one major point. The  fact that all the scripting is publicly available. We made some  private services with api's for our apps, but as all the scripting  can be found in the apk file, the private services all of a sudden  can become public as everyone can use them. As you can imagine this  is a major porblem.<br>  What we did at the moment is obfuscate some parts, but not only  that is not really secure, it slows down the app a lot as well.</p>  <p>Compiling the javascript nativly would be the best of course,  but I don't see that happen soon. Therefor I was looking at the  encrypted database option and noticed that it was on the roadmap.  Could you please give us an estimated time of arrival of this  feature?</p>  <p>thanks for the project so far and thanks for you time.</p>  <p>Best regards,</p>  <p><USER>/p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: encrypted database feature
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Hi,</p> <p>We just created a new app and are planning to create some more with your platform. Works perfectly execpt for one major point. The fact that all the scripting is publicly available. We made some private services with api's for our apps, but as all the scripting can be found in the apk file, the private services all of a sudden can become public as everyone can use them. As you can imagine this is a major porblem.<br> What we did at the moment is obfuscate some parts, but not only that is not really secure, it slows down the app a lot as well.</p> <p>Compiling the javascript nativly would be the best of course, but I don't see that happen soon. Therefor I was looking at the encrypted database option and noticed that it was on the roadmap. Could you please give us an estimated time of arrival of this feature?</p> <p>thanks for the project so far and thanks for you time.</p> <p>Best regards,</p> <p><USER>/p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-3254,12,"""Setting data in HTTPClient.send will force a POST connectionType""","""""""{html}<div><p>Whenever I try to send data with an <strong>HTTPClient</strong> it forces the connectionType on POST, even if I set it on GET with HTTPClient.open(""""""""GET"""""""", url)</p> <p>I figured out a temporary workaround for this thing, but it would be great if it could be fixed.</p> <pre> <code class= """"""""javascript"""""""">if (method == """"""""GET"""""""" &amp;&amp; data != null ) {     if ( typeof(data) == 'object' ) {         var pairs = [];         for ( var key in data ) {             if (data.hasOwnProperty(key) ) { pairs.push(key + """"""""="""""""" + data[key]); }         }         url += """"""""?"""""""" + pairs.join('&amp;');     } else if ( typeof(data) == 'string' ) {         url += """"""""?"""""""" + data;     }     data = null; } var loader = Titanium.Network.createHTTPClient(); loader.open(method, url); /* loader.onload ... */ loader.send(data);</code> </pre></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Setting data in HTTPClient.send will force a POST connectionType
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Whenever I try to send data with an <strong>HTTPClient</strong> it forces the connectionType on POST, even if I set it on GET with HTTPClient.open(""""""""GET"""""""", url)</p> <p>I figured out a temporary workaround for this thing, but it would be great if it could be fixed.</p> <pre> <code class= """"""""javascript"""""""">if (method == """"""""GET"""""""" &amp;&amp; data != null ) { if ( typeof(data) == 'object' ) { var pairs = []; for ( var key in data ) { if (data.hasOwnProperty(key) ) { pairs.push(key + """"""""="""""""" + data[key]); } } url += """"""""?"""""""" + pairs.join('&amp;'); } else if ( typeof(data) == 'string' ) { url += """"""""?"""""""" + data; } data = null; } var loader = Titanium.Network.createHTTPClient(); loader.open(method, url); /* loader.onload ... */ loader.send(data);</code> </pre></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-3253,12,"""Android: Distorted Buttons on QVGA Display""","""""""{html}<div><p>Ticket Reference: <a href=  """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/76183"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/76183</a></p>  <p>Buttons border is offset by a pixel on QVGA displays. Visible on  emulator using Kitchen Sink&gt;Base UI&gt;Window Properties.</p>  <p>Does not affect button images, just generated buttons.</p>  <p>See screen shot: <a href=  """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/s3/retrieve/attachments.helpdesk.<USER>com/76183/ticket/distorted_buttons_htc_wildfire.png"""""""">  http://developer.<USER>com/s3/retrieve/attachments.helpdesk....</a></p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Distorted Buttons on QVGA Display
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Ticket Reference: <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/76183"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/76183</a></p> <p>Buttons border is offset by a pixel on QVGA displays. Visible on emulator using Kitchen Sink&gt;Base UI&gt;Window Properties.</p> <p>Does not affect button images, just generated buttons.</p> <p>See screen shot: <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/s3/retrieve/attachments.helpdesk.<USER>com/76183/ticket/distorted_buttons_htc_wildfire.png""""""""> http://developer.<USER>com/s3/retrieve/attachments.helpdesk....</a></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-3251,12,"""Android: Support Gingerbread ActionBar Widget""","""""""{html}<div><p><a href= """"""""http://developer.android.com/guide/topics/ui/actionbar.html"""""""">http://developer.android.com/guide/topics/ui/actionbar.html</a></p> <p>Pretty please?</p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Support Gingerbread ActionBar Widget
