TIMOB-3137,12,"""Android: Expose Window Title Bars and Their Background Colors to Titanium""","""""""{html}<div><h1>Feature</h1>  <p>Expose windows' title bars and background colors; this could  utilize the existing properties and functions that iOS offers to  control the nav bar.</p>  <h2>Associated Helpdesk Ticket</h2>  <p><a href=  """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72591#c346471"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72591#c346471</a></p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Expose Window Title Bars and Their Background Colors to Titanium
Description: """"{html}<div><h1>Feature</h1> <p>Expose windows' title bars and background colors; this could utilize the existing properties and functions that iOS offers to control the nav bar.</p> <h2>Associated Helpdesk Ticket</h2> <p><a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72591#c346471"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72591#c346471</a></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-3136,12,"""iOS: Bug MediaModule.m Front/Rear Camera""","""""""h1. Problem  Two problems in MediaModule.m:    - camera_front and camera_rear are reversed. The front camera is the back, and the back camera is the front  - vibrate's call to ensure_ui_thread specifies the callback method as the beep method; it happens that these two methods are identical, so it doesn't really matter... but...    h2. Solution  Fix the code.    h2. How To Test  Drop the following in an app.js and run it on a device with a front camera (like an iPad 2). It should use the front camera.  {code:title=app.js}  Ti.Media.showCamera({      mediaTypes: Ti.Media.MEDIA_TYPE_PHOTO  });  Ti.Media.switchCamera(Ti.Media.CAMERA_FRONT);  Ti.Media.vibrate();  {code}    h2. Reference Ticket  http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72631    h2. Pull Request  https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/627""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Bug MediaModule.m Front/Rear Camera
Description: """"h1. Problem Two problems in MediaModule.m: - camera_front and camera_rear are reversed. The front camera is the back, and the back camera is the front - vibrate's call to ensure_ui_thread specifies the callback method as the beep method; it happens that these two methods are identical, so it doesn't really matter... but... h2. Solution Fix the code. h2. How To Test Drop the following in an app.js and run it on a device with a front camera (like an iPad 2). It should use the front camera. {code:title=app.js} Ti.Media.showCamera({ mediaTypes: Ti.Media.MEDIA_TYPE_PHOTO }); Ti.Media.switchCamera(Ti.Media.CAMERA_FRONT); Ti.Media.vibrate(); {code} h2. Reference Ticket http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72631 h2. Pull Request https://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/pull/627""""",title+description
TIMOB-3135,12,"""iOS: Facebook doesn't check individual app support for BG modes""","""""""{html}<div><p>From some customers in HD:</p>  <p>""""""""Ok so I have a pretty serious concern with this new login  model. For most of my apps (clients request due to data sync) don't  have full background support, meaning they start from the beginning  when the app returns from background. Now this login model goes in  direct conflict with my apps because when the app closes and safari  opens and then goes back to the app, the app loads as if its  starting the first time. I'm sure i'm not the only one with this  issue.""""""""</p>  <p>What happens when facebook doesn't check individual app support  for BG modes?</p>  <p>HD ticket to follow: <a href=  """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/71691#c343111"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/71691#c343111</a></p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS: Facebook doesn't check individual app support for BG modes
Description: """"{html}<div><p>From some customers in HD:</p> <p>""""""""Ok so I have a pretty serious concern with this new login model. For most of my apps (clients request due to data sync) don't have full background support, meaning they start from the beginning when the app returns from background. Now this login model goes in direct conflict with my apps because when the app closes and safari opens and then goes back to the app, the app loads as if its starting the first time. I'm sure i'm not the only one with this issue.""""""""</p> <p>What happens when facebook doesn't check individual app support for BG modes?</p> <p>HD ticket to follow: <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/71691#c343111"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/71691#c343111</a></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-3134,12,"""Android: Alert Dialog appears behind activities and is invisible to user""","""""""{html}<div><h1>Problem</h1> <p>When an alert dialog and a modal window are launched at the same time, they end up racing each other to see who displays first. This causes an exception to be thrown.</p> <h1>Regression</h1> <p>In 1.5.0, the alert dialog would show over the modal window, and no exception would be thrown. This has been introduced since then.</p> <h1>Sample Code</h1> <pre> <code class= """"""""javascript"""""""">var tabGroup = Titanium.UI.createTabGroup();   // main window var tmpWindow = Titanium.UI.createWindow({       backgroundColor: 'green', }); var tmpTab = Titanium.UI.createTab({       window:tmpWindow,     title:'first' });   tabGroup.addTab(tmpTab); tabGroup.open();   Login = {};   Login.loginDialog = Ti.UI.createWindow({    backgroundColor: 'red', });   Login.loginDialog.open({modal:true});   var alertDialog = Titanium.UI.createAlertDialog({     title: 'Welcome',     message: 'Stuff in here',     buttonNames: ['red pill', 'blue pill'] }); alertDialog.show();</code> </pre> <h1>Trace Log</h1> <pre> <code>[TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): Activity org.<USER>jss.JssActivity has leaked window com.android.internal.policy.impl.PhoneWindow$DecorView@43ec9740 that was originally added here [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): android.view.WindowLeaked: Activity org.<USER>jss.JssActivity has leaked window com.android.internal.policy.impl.PhoneWindow$DecorView@43ec9740 that was originally added here [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.view.ViewRoot.&lt;init&gt;(ViewRoot.java:247) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.view.WindowManagerImpl.addView(WindowManagerImpl.java:148) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.view.WindowManagerImpl.addView(WindowManagerImpl.java:91) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.view.Window$LocalWindowManager.addView(Window.java:424) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.app.Dialog.show(Dialog.java:241) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at ti.modules.titanium.ui.widget.TiUIActivityIndicator.handleShow(TiUIActivityIndicator.java:200) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at ti.modules.titanium.ui.widget.TiUIActivityIndicator.show(TiUIActivityIndicator.java:124) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at ti.modules.titanium.ui.ActivityIndicatorProxy.handleShow(ActivityIndicatorProxy.java:45) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at org.<USER>titanium.proxy.TiViewProxy.handleMessage(TiViewProxy.java:212) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.os.Handler.dispatchMessage(Handler.java:95) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.os.Looper.loop(Looper.java:123) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.app.ActivityThread.main(ActivityThread.java:4627) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at java.lang.reflect.Method.invokeNative(Native Method) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at java.lang.reflect.Method.invoke(Method.java:521) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at com.android.internal.os.ZygoteInit$MethodAndArgsCaller.run(ZygoteInit.java:868) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at com.android.internal.os.ZygoteInit.main(ZygoteInit.java:626) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at dalvik.system.NativeStart.main(Native Method)</code> </pre> <h1>Workaround</h1> <p>The workaround is to remove the race -- only open one, and when it is finished, launch the second. Therefore, the workaround for the sample code would be the following (thanks belong to <USER>for this workaround):</p> <pre> <code class= """"""""javascript"""""""">Login.loginDialog.addEventListener(""""""""open"""""""", function(e) {     var alertDialog = Titanium.UI.createAlertDialog({         title: 'Welcome',         message: 'Stuff in here',         buttonNames: ['red pill', 'blue pill']     });     alertDialog.show(); }); Login.loginDialog.open({ modal: true });</code> </pre> <h1>Tested On</h1> <p>Titanium SDK version: 1.6.0 (02/10/11 14:34 9db0685...)<br> BROKEN on Android Simulator 2.2</p> <h2>Associated Helpdesk Ticket</h2> <p><a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72761"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72761</a></p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Alert Dialog appears behind activities and is invisible to user
Description: """"{html}<div><h1>Problem</h1> <p>When an alert dialog and a modal window are launched at the same time, they end up racing each other to see who displays first. This causes an exception to be thrown.</p> <h1>Regression</h1> <p>In 1.5.0, the alert dialog would show over the modal window, and no exception would be thrown. This has been introduced since then.</p> <h1>Sample Code</h1> <pre> <code class= """"""""javascript"""""""">var tabGroup = Titanium.UI.createTabGroup(); // main window var tmpWindow = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: 'green', }); var tmpTab = Titanium.UI.createTab({ window:tmpWindow, title:'first' }); tabGroup.addTab(tmpTab); tabGroup.open(); Login = {}; Login.loginDialog = Ti.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor: 'red', }); Login.loginDialog.open({modal:true}); var alertDialog = Titanium.UI.createAlertDialog({ title: 'Welcome', message: 'Stuff in here', buttonNames: ['red pill', 'blue pill'] }); alertDialog.show();</code> </pre> <h1>Trace Log</h1> <pre> <code>[TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): Activity org.<USER>jss.JssActivity has leaked window com.android.internal.policy.impl.PhoneWindow$DecorView@43ec9740 that was originally added here [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): android.view.WindowLeaked: Activity org.<USER>jss.JssActivity has leaked window com.android.internal.policy.impl.PhoneWindow$DecorView@43ec9740 that was originally added here [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.view.ViewRoot.&lt;init&gt;(ViewRoot.java:247) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.view.WindowManagerImpl.addView(WindowManagerImpl.java:148) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.view.WindowManagerImpl.addView(WindowManagerImpl.java:91) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.view.Window$LocalWindowManager.addView(Window.java:424) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.app.Dialog.show(Dialog.java:241) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at ti.modules.titanium.ui.widget.TiUIActivityIndicator.handleShow(TiUIActivityIndicator.java:200) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at ti.modules.titanium.ui.widget.TiUIActivityIndicator.show(TiUIActivityIndicator.java:124) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at ti.modules.titanium.ui.ActivityIndicatorProxy.handleShow(ActivityIndicatorProxy.java:45) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at org.<USER>titanium.proxy.TiViewProxy.handleMessage(TiViewProxy.java:212) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.os.Handler.dispatchMessage(Handler.java:95) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.os.Looper.loop(Looper.java:123) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at android.app.ActivityThread.main(ActivityThread.java:4627) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at java.lang.reflect.Method.invokeNative(Native Method) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at java.lang.reflect.Method.invoke(Method.java:521) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at com.android.internal.os.ZygoteInit$MethodAndArgsCaller.run(ZygoteInit.java:868) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at com.android.internal.os.ZygoteInit.main(ZygoteInit.java:626) [TRACE] E/WindowManager( 586): at dalvik.system.NativeStart.main(Native Method)</code> </pre> <h1>Workaround</h1> <p>The workaround is to remove the race -- only open one, and when it is finished, launch the second. Therefore, the workaround for the sample code would be the following (thanks belong to <USER>for this workaround):</p> <pre> <code class= """"""""javascript"""""""">Login.loginDialog.addEventListener(""""""""open"""""""", function(e) { var alertDialog = Titanium.UI.createAlertDialog({ title: 'Welcome', message: 'Stuff in here', buttonNames: ['red pill', 'blue pill'] }); alertDialog.show(); }); Login.loginDialog.open({ modal: true });</code> </pre> <h1>Tested On</h1> <p>Titanium SDK version: 1.6.0 (02/10/11 14:34 9db0685...)<br> BROKEN on Android Simulator 2.2</p> <h2>Associated Helpdesk Ticket</h2> <p><a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72761"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/72761</a></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-3133,12,"""iOS - iphone idiom on ipad - new splash screen had resizing issues""","""""""{html}<div><p>iphone idiom on ipad only.</p> <p>1.create a stub iphone project<br> 2.install it to ipad, launch and eyeball the screen</p> <p>results: the plash loads of center, looking a little low rez, then redraws/repositions it self ot its final location</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS - iphone idiom on ipad - new splash screen had resizing issues
Description: """"{html}<div><p>iphone idiom on ipad only.</p> <p>1.create a stub iphone project<br> 2.install it to ipad, launch and eyeball the screen</p> <p>results: the plash loads of center, looking a little low rez, then redraws/repositions it self ot its final location</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-3132,12,"""iOS: Platform Specific JS Not Considered in Build Scripts Exclusion Process""","""""""h4. Problem    Platform specific JavaScript files are not considered when the build scripts decide what modules to exclude. That means if you only use Ti.UI.createWindow in a platform specific JavaScript file, you will NOT be able to deploy to device.    NOTE: This will ONLY show up when you deploy to device! The simulator build scripts don't exclude modules, so this bug will not show up there.    h4. Error    When deploying to device, the following error will pop up:        h4. Sample Code    Place the following in your Resources/app.js. Note that you can uncomment the last line to force the build scripts to include Ti.UI.Window.        Now place the following in Resources/iphone/createWindow.js. Note that if you move it to Resources/createWindow.js (out of the iphone folder), everything will work.        If everything works (if the bug is NOT present), you will see a window with a green background.    h4. Tested On    Titanium SDK version: 1.6.0 (02/10/11 14:34 9db0685...)  WORKS on iPhone Simulator 4.2  BROKEN on iPod Touch 3rd Generation  WORKS on Android Device 2.1 (when placed in Resources/android folder)    h4. Workaround    Either move the code out of the iphone folder, or explicitly force Titanium to include the components that are being excluded by placing the following in your app.js:        h4. Associated Helpdesk Ticket    http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/73211""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Platform Specific JS Not Considered in Build Scripts Exclusion Process
Description: """"h4. Problem Platform specific JavaScript files are not considered when the build scripts decide what modules to exclude. That means if you only use Ti.UI.createWindow in a platform specific JavaScript file, you will NOT be able to deploy to device. NOTE: This will ONLY show up when you deploy to device! The simulator build scripts don't exclude modules, so this bug will not show up there. h4. Error When deploying to device, the following error will pop up: h4. Sample Code Place the following in your Resources/app.js. Note that you can uncomment the last line to force the build scripts to include Ti.UI.Window. Now place the following in Resources/iphone/createWindow.js. Note that if you move it to Resources/createWindow.js (out of the iphone folder), everything will work. If everything works (if the bug is NOT present), you will see a window with a green background. h4. Tested On Titanium SDK version: 1.6.0 (02/10/11 14:34 9db0685...) WORKS on iPhone Simulator 4.2 BROKEN on iPod Touch 3rd Generation WORKS on Android Device 2.1 (when placed in Resources/android folder) h4. Workaround Either move the code out of the iphone folder, or explicitly force Titanium to include the components that are being excluded by placing the following in your app.js: h4. Associated Helpdesk Ticket http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/73211""""",title+description
