TIMOB-1857,12,"""try/catch not working on database error""","""""""{html}<div><p>on the iphone running 4.x and sdk 1.4.1, using a try/catch does not capture the error and results in an app crash.</p> <p>See this help desk ticket for details and code:<br> <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/43371"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/43371</a></p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: try/catch not working on database error
Description: """"{html}<div><p>on the iphone running 4.x and sdk 1.4.1, using a try/catch does not capture the error and results in an app crash.</p> <p>See this help desk ticket for details and code:<br> <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/43371"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/helpdesk/view/43371</a></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1856,12,"""Android: Scrollview does not allow child view to use right:0 to specify full width""","""""""{html}<div><p>Sample app.js:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript"""""""">Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({     exitOnClose: true,     fullscreen: true,     backgroundColor: 'purple' }); var sv = Ti.UI.createScrollView({     left: 0, right: 0, top: 0, height: 100,     backgroundColor: 'green' }); var v = Ti.UI.createView({     left: 0, right: 0, top: 0, height: 50,     backgroundColor: 'red' }); sv.add(v); win.add(sv); win.open();</code> </pre> <p>You should see a smaller red view inside the green scrollview. But you don't. If you change <code>createView</code> argument <code>right:0</code> to <code>width:100</code> it works.</p> <p>Note that if the outer view is a standard <code>View</code> instead of a <code>ScrollView</code>, it works fine. So it seems to be only happening with <code>ScrollView</code>.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Scrollview does not allow child view to use right:0 to specify full width
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Sample app.js:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript"""""""">Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ exitOnClose: true, fullscreen: true, backgroundColor: 'purple' }); var sv = Ti.UI.createScrollView({ left: 0, right: 0, top: 0, height: 100, backgroundColor: 'green' }); var v = Ti.UI.createView({ left: 0, right: 0, top: 0, height: 50, backgroundColor: 'red' }); sv.add(v); win.add(sv); win.open();</code> </pre> <p>You should see a smaller red view inside the green scrollview. But you don't. If you change <code>createView</code> argument <code>right:0</code> to <code>width:100</code> it works.</p> <p>Note that if the outer view is a standard <code>View</code> instead of a <code>ScrollView</code>, it works fine. So it seems to be only happening with <code>ScrollView</code>.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1855,12,"""Performance Gains Using TabGroup instead of Window""","""""""{html}<div><p>Doing a normal window operation in an app like this yields a much slower app (slower as in UI rendering, modals opening and closing, views in a ScrollableView, etc.):</p> <pre> <code>var mainWindow = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ ... }); mainWindow.open();</code> </pre> <p>...than this, which makes the app much faster:</p> <pre> <code>var tabGroup = Titanium.UI.createTabGroup(); var mainWindow = Titanium.UI.createWindow({     navBarHidden:true,     tabBarHidden:true  }); var tab = Titanium.UI.createTab({     window:mainWindow  }); tabGroup.addTab(tab);  tabGroup.open();</code> </pre> <p>Both produce the same visual result, but I see significant performance gains using the invisible tabGroup option.</p> <p>I have tested this on multiple applications both in the simulator and on a device. This is for iPhone only.</p> <p>Hopefully this is helpful.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Performance Gains Using TabGroup instead of Window
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Doing a normal window operation in an app like this yields a much slower app (slower as in UI rendering, modals opening and closing, views in a ScrollableView, etc.):</p> <pre> <code>var mainWindow = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ ... }); mainWindow.open();</code> </pre> <p>...than this, which makes the app much faster:</p> <pre> <code>var tabGroup = Titanium.UI.createTabGroup(); var mainWindow = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ navBarHidden:true, tabBarHidden:true }); var tab = Titanium.UI.createTab({ window:mainWindow }); tabGroup.addTab(tab); tabGroup.open();</code> </pre> <p>Both produce the same visual result, but I see significant performance gains using the invisible tabGroup option.</p> <p>I have tested this on multiple applications both in the simulator and on a device. This is for iPhone only.</p> <p>Hopefully this is helpful.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1854,12,"""iOS: 1.4.2 - app on device crashes on launch""","""""""{html}<div><p>1.pull from head to get 1.4.2<br> 2.create a simple iphone or ipad project<br> 3.install and launch on device</p> <p>results: crash on launch no crash logs seem to be produced.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: 1.4.2 - app on device crashes on launch
Description: """"{html}<div><p>1.pull from head to get 1.4.2<br> 2.create a simple iphone or ipad project<br> 3.install and launch on device</p> <p>results: crash on launch no crash logs seem to be produced.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1853,12,"""Android: Support Loading Indicator for Video""","""""""{html}<div><p>See <a href=""""""""/projects/32238/tickets/1733"""""""" title= """"""""Ticket #1733"""""""">#1733</a> - Research has to be done to see if it's possible to determine when the video starts playing so we can fire and event and/or provide a Loading indicator.</p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Support Loading Indicator for Video
Description: """"{html}<div><p>See <a href=""""""""/projects/32238/tickets/1733"""""""" title= """"""""Ticket #1733"""""""">#1733</a> - Research has to be done to see if it's possible to determine when the video starts playing so we can fire and event and/or provide a Loading indicator.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1852,12,"""Android: Move AndroidManifest.custom.xml Out of build/android""","""""""{html}<div><p>We need to get the build folder to the point where it can be wiped out and recreated. I believe <code>android_native_refactor</code> has support for use the platform directory. Determine what's the best way to accomplish this so we require no files to be versioned in <code>build/android</code></p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Move AndroidManifest.custom.xml Out of build/android
Description: """"{html}<div><p>We need to get the build folder to the point where it can be wiped out and recreated. I believe <code>android_native_refactor</code> has support for use the platform directory. Determine what's the best way to accomplish this so we require no files to be versioned in <code>build/android</code></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
