TIMOB-1554,12,"""iOS  - Fix Math.random()""","""""""{html}<div><p>Fix Math.random() so that it actually generates random number.</p> <p>Yes, I know you can seed it seconds to make it random, but it would be better to fix it in the core.</p> <p>Here is the best way, from StackOverflow: <a href= """"""""http://stackoverflow.com/questions/160890/generating-random-numbers-in-objective-c""""""""> http://stackoverflow.com/questions/160890/generating-random-numbers...</a></p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS - Fix Math.random()
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Fix Math.random() so that it actually generates random number.</p> <p>Yes, I know you can seed it seconds to make it random, but it would be better to fix it in the core.</p> <p>Here is the best way, from StackOverflow: <a href= """"""""http://stackoverflow.com/questions/160890/generating-random-numbers-in-objective-c""""""""> http://stackoverflow.com/questions/160890/generating-random-numbers...</a></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1553,12,"""iOS: Navigating back in a navigationGroup makes the leftNavButton fade-in twice""","""""""Sample code from pastebin: http://pastebin.com/QeeGwMtF        Click the + button, then click Back, and see the leftNavButton fade-in twice.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Navigating back in a navigationGroup makes the leftNavButton fade-in twice
Description: """"Sample code from pastebin: http://pastebin.com/QeeGwMtF Click the + button, then click Back, and see the leftNavButton fade-in twice.""""",title+description
TIMOB-1551,12,"""iOS: Can't open modal window inside navigationGroup""","""""""{html}<div><p>If you build you're app using an navigationGroup it's not  possible to open a modal window.</p>  <p>If you try to open a modal window using the current window in  the navigationGroup nothing happens, since you're supposed to use  the navigationGroup to open the new window. And if you try to open  it using the navigationGroup the modal property is ignored, since  it's not supported for navigationGroup, and it just animates as  usual.</p>  <p>Please fix this.</p>  <p>Testcase: <a href=  """"""""http://pastebin.com/EV1gwQef"""""""">http://pastebin.com/EV1gwQef</a></p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Can't open modal window inside navigationGroup
Description: """"{html}<div><p>If you build you're app using an navigationGroup it's not possible to open a modal window.</p> <p>If you try to open a modal window using the current window in the navigationGroup nothing happens, since you're supposed to use the navigationGroup to open the new window. And if you try to open it using the navigationGroup the modal property is ignored, since it's not supported for navigationGroup, and it just animates as usual.</p> <p>Please fix this.</p> <p>Testcase: <a href= """"""""http://pastebin.com/EV1gwQef"""""""">http://pastebin.com/EV1gwQef</a></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1550,12,"""Kitchen Sink Facebook examples crash w/ Titanium Mobile 1.4.0 on Android""","""""""{html}<div><p>I was playing around with Titanium's Facebook API a bit today,  but wasn't very successful. The Kitchen Sink example (Login /  Logout) showed the login button, but after entering my credentials  the application crashed; trace output is at <a href=  """"""""http://gist.github.com/520810"""""""">http://gist.github.com/520810</a>.  I noted that the Kitchen Sink code uses the session proxy, so I  whipped up a quick sample of my own using apikey and secret. This  did not crash, however neither Titanium.Facebook nor the Facebook  login button ever receive the login or logout event. I tested on  Android (SDK 1.6) on Windows 7.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Kitchen Sink Facebook examples crash w/ Titanium Mobile 1.4.0 on Android
Description: """"{html}<div><p>I was playing around with Titanium's Facebook API a bit today, but wasn't very successful. The Kitchen Sink example (Login / Logout) showed the login button, but after entering my credentials the application crashed; trace output is at <a href= """"""""http://gist.github.com/520810"""""""">http://gist.github.com/520810</a>. I noted that the Kitchen Sink code uses the session proxy, so I whipped up a quick sample of my own using apikey and secret. This did not crash, however neither Titanium.Facebook nor the Facebook login button ever receive the login or logout event. I tested on Android (SDK 1.6) on Windows 7.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1549,12,"""Issues with modal windows and ipad 3.2.2 update""","""""""{html}<div><p>modal windows do not draw to the status bar (~5 pixels off)</p> <p>1.ks-ipad&gt;split view plain<br> 2.tap show modal and look where the modal and the status bar meet</p> <p>blue line appears (screen shot)</p> <hr> <p>split view + rotating with modal window = crash<br> 1.Ks-ipadsplitview plain in landscape<br> 2.tap split view - Plain<br> 3.tap show modal<br> 4.rotate portrait, rotate landscape</p> <hr> <p>split view with nav group - windows draw in the incorrect location after rotation OR app fails to respond to rotation after orientation change with modal window up.</p> <p>1.KS-iPad&gt;Splitview with nave group in landscape<br> 2.tap open window in detail window<br> 3.tap show modal in resulting window<br> 4.rotate to portrait, dismiss alert (alert may not change orientation at this point)<br> 5.rotate to landscape</p> <p>results: either the app no longer responds to orientation changes, or it is drawn slightly off screen.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Issues with modal windows and ipad 3.2.2 update
Description: """"{html}<div><p>modal windows do not draw to the status bar (~5 pixels off)</p> <p>1.ks-ipad&gt;split view plain<br> 2.tap show modal and look where the modal and the status bar meet</p> <p>blue line appears (screen shot)</p> <hr> <p>split view + rotating with modal window = crash<br> 1.Ks-ipadsplitview plain in landscape<br> 2.tap split view - Plain<br> 3.tap show modal<br> 4.rotate portrait, rotate landscape</p> <hr> <p>split view with nav group - windows draw in the incorrect location after rotation OR app fails to respond to rotation after orientation change with modal window up.</p> <p>1.KS-iPad&gt;Splitview with nave group in landscape<br> 2.tap open window in detail window<br> 3.tap show modal in resulting window<br> 4.rotate to portrait, dismiss alert (alert may not change orientation at this point)<br> 5.rotate to landscape</p> <p>results: either the app no longer responds to orientation changes, or it is drawn slightly off screen.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1548,12,"""iOS - In-App SMS""","""""""{html}<div><p>Add support for In-App SMS and a function to check if it's  available on the device.</p>  <p>More info:<br>  <a href=  """"""""http://blog.mugunthkumar.com/coding/iphone-tutorial-how-to-send-in-app-sms/"""""""">  http://blog.mugunthkumar.com/coding/iphone-tutorial-how-to-send-in-...</a></p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS - In-App SMS
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Add support for In-App SMS and a function to check if it's available on the device.</p> <p>More info:<br> <a href= """"""""http://blog.mugunthkumar.com/coding/iphone-tutorial-how-to-send-in-app-sms/""""""""> http://blog.mugunthkumar.com/coding/iphone-tutorial-how-to-send-in-...</a></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1547,12,"""iOS: Events not working during view animation""","""""""{html}<div><p>While a view is being animated, it does not respond to click  events (probably other touch events as well). Here's a sample  app.js to reproduce:</p>  <pre>  <code>  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'#fff'});    var view1 = Ti.UI.createView({ width: 60, height: 60, borderRadius: 30, top: -60, left: 0, backgroundColor: """"""""red"""""""" });    win.add(view1);    view1.animate({top: 300, duration: 5000});    view1.addEventListener(""""""""click"""""""",function() { alert(""""""""working"""""""") });    win.open();</code>  </pre></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Events not working during view animation
Description: """"{html}<div><p>While a view is being animated, it does not respond to click events (probably other touch events as well). Here's a sample app.js to reproduce:</p> <pre> <code> var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({backgroundColor:'#fff'}); var view1 = Ti.UI.createView({ width: 60, height: 60, borderRadius: 30, top: -60, left: 0, backgroundColor: """"""""red"""""""" }); win.add(view1); view1.animate({top: 300, duration: 5000}); view1.addEventListener(""""""""click"""""""",function() { alert(""""""""working"""""""") }); win.open();</code> </pre></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1546,12,"""iOS: scrollableView pagingControlHeight""","""""""{html}<div><p>The docs says the default pagingControlHeight is 20.</p>  <p>When I add some images to a scrollableView with  showPagingControl:true and pagingControlHeight:20, I get this:  <a href=""""""""http://snapplr.com/01n2"""""""">http://snapplr.com/01n2</a></p>  <p>If I comment out the pagingControlHeight:20 I get this: <a href=  """"""""http://snapplr.com/x59q"""""""">http://snapplr.com/x59q</a></p>  <p>It should be the same size.</p>  <p>Testcase: <a href=  """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/question/51641/scrollableview-pagingcontrolheight"""""""">  http://developer.<USER>com/question/51641/scrollableview-pag...</a></p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: scrollableView pagingControlHeight
Description: """"{html}<div><p>The docs says the default pagingControlHeight is 20.</p> <p>When I add some images to a scrollableView with showPagingControl:true and pagingControlHeight:20, I get this: <a href=""""""""http://snapplr.com/01n2"""""""">http://snapplr.com/01n2</a></p> <p>If I comment out the pagingControlHeight:20 I get this: <a href= """"""""http://snapplr.com/x59q"""""""">http://snapplr.com/x59q</a></p> <p>It should be the same size.</p> <p>Testcase: <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/question/51641/scrollableview-pagingcontrolheight""""""""> http://developer.<USER>com/question/51641/scrollableview-pag...</a></p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1545,12,"""Android: Unusual behavior when opening an app immediately after installing it""","""""""{html}<div><p>When you install an app, you get the option at the end of the installation to touch """"""""open"""""""" (to run the app) or just """"""""done"""""""". If you choose open and start the app, it behaves differently in terms of pause/resume than an app that you open from the normal applications list. (See also helpdesk ticket 35861.)</p> <ol> <li> <p>Use the native android browser go enter a URL to an APK on the internet.</p> </li> <li> <p>After it's downloaded, click on the apk to install</p> </li> <li> <p>On post install screen click 'open'</p> </li> <li> <p>After the app loads, click the home button</p> </li> <li> <p>Go to the list of applications, find the one you just installed and click it to open it again. You will see splash screen again instead of being brought back into app in previous state.</p> </li> <li> <p>repeat 4 and 5 a few times</p> </li> </ol> <p>After doing it a few times, start clicking the back button, and you'll end up backing through a bunch of instances your app's main screen.<br></p> <p>None of this weird stuff happens if you do <em>not</em> click 'open' upon completing the installation of the app but rather go start the app for the first time using the normal applications list.</p> <p>If you click back a bunch of times you will see multiple instances of the <USER>app and splash screen</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Unusual behavior when opening an app immediately after installing it
Description: """"{html}<div><p>When you install an app, you get the option at the end of the installation to touch """"""""open"""""""" (to run the app) or just """"""""done"""""""". If you choose open and start the app, it behaves differently in terms of pause/resume than an app that you open from the normal applications list. (See also helpdesk ticket 35861.)</p> <ol> <li> <p>Use the native android browser go enter a URL to an APK on the internet.</p> </li> <li> <p>After it's downloaded, click on the apk to install</p> </li> <li> <p>On post install screen click 'open'</p> </li> <li> <p>After the app loads, click the home button</p> </li> <li> <p>Go to the list of applications, find the one you just installed and click it to open it again. You will see splash screen again instead of being brought back into app in previous state.</p> </li> <li> <p>repeat 4 and 5 a few times</p> </li> </ol> <p>After doing it a few times, start clicking the back button, and you'll end up backing through a bunch of instances your app's main screen.<br></p> <p>None of this weird stuff happens if you do <em>not</em> click 'open' upon completing the installation of the app but rather go start the app for the first time using the normal applications list.</p> <p>If you click back a bunch of times you will see multiple instances of the <USER>app and splash screen</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1544,12,"""Android: Problems pausing/resuming app: ConcurrentModificationException""","""""""{html}<div><p>See also helpdesk ticket 35861. Appears to be concurrency problem in TiContext's <code>lifecycleListeners</code> list. Relevant portion of trace:</p> <pre> <code>E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): (main) [11,1437] Sending event: exception on thread: main msg:java.lang.RuntimeException: Unable to pause activity {com.xxxx.XxxxMobile/com.xxxx.XxxxMobile.GetxxxxActivity}: java.util.ConcurrentModificationException E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): java.lang.RuntimeException: Unable to pause activity {com.xxxx.XxxxMobile/com.xxxx.XxxxMobile.GetxxxxActivity}: java.util.ConcurrentModificationException E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at android.app.ActivityThread.performPauseActivity(ActivityThread.java:3567) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at android.app.ActivityThread.performPauseActivity(ActivityThread.java:3524) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at android.app.ActivityThread.handlePauseActivity(ActivityThread.java:3507) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at android.app.ActivityThread.access$2500(ActivityThread.java:135) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at android.app.ActivityThread$H.handleMessage(ActivityThread.java:2143) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at android.os.Handler.dispatchMessage(Handler.java:99) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at android.os.Looper.loop(Looper.java:144) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at android.app.ActivityThread.main(ActivityThread.java:4937) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at java.lang.reflect.Method.invokeNative(Native Method) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at java.lang.reflect.Method.invoke(Method.java:521) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at com.android.internal.os.ZygoteInit$MethodAndArgsCaller.run(ZygoteInit.java:868) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at com.android.internal.os.ZygoteInit.main(ZygoteInit.java:626) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at dalvik.system.NativeStart.main(Native Method) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): Caused by: java.util.ConcurrentModificationException E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291):     at java.util.ArrayList$ArrayListIterator.next(ArrayList.java:573)</code> </pre></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Problems pausing/resuming app: ConcurrentModificationException
Description: """"{html}<div><p>See also helpdesk ticket 35861. Appears to be concurrency problem in TiContext's <code>lifecycleListeners</code> list. Relevant portion of trace:</p> <pre> <code>E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): (main) [11,1437] Sending event: exception on thread: main msg:java.lang.RuntimeException: Unable to pause activity {com.xxxx.XxxxMobile/com.xxxx.XxxxMobile.GetxxxxActivity}: java.util.ConcurrentModificationException E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): java.lang.RuntimeException: Unable to pause activity {com.xxxx.XxxxMobile/com.xxxx.XxxxMobile.GetxxxxActivity}: java.util.ConcurrentModificationException E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at android.app.ActivityThread.performPauseActivity(ActivityThread.java:3567) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at android.app.ActivityThread.performPauseActivity(ActivityThread.java:3524) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at android.app.ActivityThread.handlePauseActivity(ActivityThread.java:3507) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at android.app.ActivityThread.access$2500(ActivityThread.java:135) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at android.app.ActivityThread$H.handleMessage(ActivityThread.java:2143) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at android.os.Handler.dispatchMessage(Handler.java:99) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at android.os.Looper.loop(Looper.java:144) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at android.app.ActivityThread.main(ActivityThread.java:4937) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at java.lang.reflect.Method.invokeNative(Native Method) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at java.lang.reflect.Method.invoke(Method.java:521) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at com.android.internal.os.ZygoteInit$MethodAndArgsCaller.run(ZygoteInit.java:868) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at com.android.internal.os.ZygoteInit.main(ZygoteInit.java:626) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at dalvik.system.NativeStart.main(Native Method) E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): Caused by: java.util.ConcurrentModificationException E/TiUncaughtHandler( 2291): at java.util.ArrayList$ArrayListIterator.next(ArrayList.java:573)</code> </pre></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1543,12,"""iOS - Support 2x remote images for iPhone 4""","""""""{html}<div><p>You currently support local highres images, but not remote.</p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: iOS - Support 2x remote images for iPhone 4
Description: """"{html}<div><p>You currently support local highres images, but not remote.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1541,12,"""Documentation: close, suspend and resume not documented""","""""""{html}<div><p>the 'close', 'pause' and 'resume' events are not documented in the 1.4 documentation.</p> <p>However, this may make sense since the 'pause' event listener is not run until the application resumes, and the resume event listener is called twice. Needs some fixing.</p> <p>I reproduced this on both simulator and iPhone 4.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Documentation: close, suspend and resume not documented
Description: """"{html}<div><p>the 'close', 'pause' and 'resume' events are not documented in the 1.4 documentation.</p> <p>However, this may make sense since the 'pause' event listener is not run until the application resumes, and the resume event listener is called twice. Needs some fixing.</p> <p>I reproduced this on both simulator and iPhone 4.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1540,12,"""Android: WebView scalesPageToFit, pinching & zooming not working""","""""""{html}<div><p>The <code>scalesPageToFit</code> property appears to be missing. Also, double-tapping for zoom (like in native Android browser) and pinching gestures don't work.</p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: WebView scalesPageToFit, pinching & zooming not working
Description: """"{html}<div><p>The <code>scalesPageToFit</code> property appears to be missing. Also, double-tapping for zoom (like in native Android browser) and pinching gestures don't work.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1539,12,"""Android: selectedBackgroundColor behavior incorrect (TableViewRow)""","""""""{html}<div><p>(Probably should be done in concert with <a href= """"""""/projects/32238/tickets/1509"""""""" title=""""""""Ticket #1509"""""""">#1509</a> re <code>selectedBackgroundImage</code>).</p> <p>Using this test app.js:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript"""""""">Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000');  var w = Ti.UI.createWindow({fullscreen: true}); var data = []; for (var i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {     var color = (i % 2) ? 'red' : 'blue';     data.push(Ti.UI.createTableViewRow({         selectedBackgroundColor: color,          title: 'Row ' + (i + 1)     })); }; var tv = Ti.UI.createTableView({     data: data }); w.add(tv); w.open();</code> </pre> <p>In iOS, if you touch one of the rows, it's <code>selectedBackgroundColor</code> will show until you finish the touch. In Android, if you touch one of the rows then the default orange-ish background appears. If you use the hardware scroll to scroll down the list (which selects each row one-at-a-time as you scroll), each row's <code>selectedBackgroundColor</code> will then appear, but then not disappear when you move to the next row. (So, using the example above, you end up with a red-blue-red-blue-red-blue etc. table by the time you get to the bottom row.)</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: selectedBackgroundColor behavior incorrect (TableViewRow)
Description: """"{html}<div><p>(Probably should be done in concert with <a href= """"""""/projects/32238/tickets/1509"""""""" title=""""""""Ticket #1509"""""""">#1509</a> re <code>selectedBackgroundImage</code>).</p> <p>Using this test app.js:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript"""""""">Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var w = Ti.UI.createWindow({fullscreen: true}); var data = []; for (var i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) { var color = (i % 2) ? 'red' : 'blue'; data.push(Ti.UI.createTableViewRow({ selectedBackgroundColor: color, title: 'Row ' + (i + 1) })); }; var tv = Ti.UI.createTableView({ data: data }); w.add(tv); w.open();</code> </pre> <p>In iOS, if you touch one of the rows, it's <code>selectedBackgroundColor</code> will show until you finish the touch. In Android, if you touch one of the rows then the default orange-ish background appears. If you use the hardware scroll to scroll down the list (which selects each row one-at-a-time as you scroll), each row's <code>selectedBackgroundColor</code> will then appear, but then not disappear when you move to the next row. (So, using the example above, you end up with a red-blue-red-blue-red-blue etc. table by the time you get to the bottom row.)</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1538,12,"""Android: Image File Detection From Assets is Slow""","""""""{html}<div><p>AssetManager doesn't provide a way to detect if a file exists. So the initial implementation dealt with it as an exception when checking for a valid file. Introduce a lookup cache to reduce the number of exceptions.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Image File Detection From Assets is Slow
Description: """"{html}<div><p>AssetManager doesn't provide a way to detect if a file exists. So the initial implementation dealt with it as an exception when checking for a valid file. Introduce a lookup cache to reduce the number of exceptions.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1537,12,"""Android: Setting keyboard on TextArea forces single-line""","""""""{html}<div><p>If you explicitly set the <code>keyboardType</code> when creating a TextArea, the control ends up being single-line with no word wrap. Simple test case app.js:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript""""""""> Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000');  var w = Ti.UI.createWindow({     fullscreen: true,     title: 'test' });  var ta = Titanium.UI.createTextArea({     value: """"""""Now is the time for all good men to come to the aid of their country."""""""",     height:100, width:300, top: 0, left: 0,     keyboardType:Titanium.UI.KEYBOARD_DEFAULT }); w.add(ta); w.open();</code> </pre> <p>Take out the <code>keyboardType</code> and you'll get the normal, desired multi-line word-wrapped control.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Setting keyboard on TextArea forces single-line
Description: """"{html}<div><p>If you explicitly set the <code>keyboardType</code> when creating a TextArea, the control ends up being single-line with no word wrap. Simple test case app.js:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript""""""""> Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var w = Ti.UI.createWindow({ fullscreen: true, title: 'test' }); var ta = Titanium.UI.createTextArea({ value: """"""""Now is the time for all good men to come to the aid of their country."""""""", height:100, width:300, top: 0, left: 0, keyboardType:Titanium.UI.KEYBOARD_DEFAULT }); w.add(ta); w.open();</code> </pre> <p>Take out the <code>keyboardType</code> and you'll get the normal, desired multi-line word-wrapped control.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1536,12,"""Android: TabGroup.activeTab returns index when should be tab object, AND index is wrong""","""""""{html}<div><p>Test case app.js:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript""""""""> Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var tabGroup = Titanium.UI.createTabGroup();  for (var i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {     var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({         title: 'Tab ' + i,         backgroundColor: '#fff'     });     var tab = Ti.UI.createTab({         title: 'Tab ' + i,         window: win     });     tabGroup.addTab(tab); }  tabGroup.addEventListener('focus', function(e) {         Ti.API.info('tabGroup.activeTab: ' + tabGroup.activeTab);     Ti.API.info('tabGroup.activeTab.title: ' + tabGroup.activeTab.title); });          tabGroup.open();</code> </pre> <p>tabGroup.activeTab will return index of the <em>previous</em> tab you were on, not the activeTab. In fact, according to our docs, it should return the tab object of the <em>current</em> tab.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: TabGroup.activeTab returns index when should be tab object, AND index is wrong
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Test case app.js:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript""""""""> Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var tabGroup = Titanium.UI.createTabGroup(); for (var i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) { var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ title: 'Tab ' + i, backgroundColor: '#fff' }); var tab = Ti.UI.createTab({ title: 'Tab ' + i, window: win }); tabGroup.addTab(tab); } tabGroup.addEventListener('focus', function(e) { Ti.API.info('tabGroup.activeTab: ' + tabGroup.activeTab); Ti.API.info('tabGroup.activeTab.title: ' + tabGroup.activeTab.title); }); tabGroup.open();</code> </pre> <p>tabGroup.activeTab will return index of the <em>previous</em> tab you were on, not the activeTab. In fact, according to our docs, it should return the tab object of the <em>current</em> tab.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1535,12,"""Android: TabGroup focus event args refer to previous tab, not current tab""","""""""{html}<div><p>Also, tabGroup.activeTab.title returns undefined.</p> <p>Test case:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript""""""""> Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var tabGroup = Titanium.UI.createTabGroup();  for (var i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {     var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({         title: 'Tab ' + i,         backgroundColor: '#fff'     });     var tab = Ti.UI.createTab({         title: 'Tab ' + i,         window: win     });     tabGroup.addTab(tab); }  tabGroup.addEventListener('focus', function(e) {     Ti.API.info('index: ' + e.index);     Ti.API.info('previousIndex: ' + e.previousIndex);     Ti.API.info('previousTab.title: ' + e.previousTab.title);     Ti.API.info('tab.title: ' + e.tab.title);     Ti.API.info('tabGroup.activeTab.title: ' + tabGroup.activeTab.title); });          tabGroup.open();</code> </pre> <p>After the app launches, click Tab 1 and you'll see the info output in the console refers to Tab 0.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: TabGroup focus event args refer to previous tab, not current tab
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Also, tabGroup.activeTab.title returns undefined.</p> <p>Test case:</p> <pre> <code class=""""""""javascript""""""""> Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var tabGroup = Titanium.UI.createTabGroup(); for (var i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) { var win = Ti.UI.createWindow({ title: 'Tab ' + i, backgroundColor: '#fff' }); var tab = Ti.UI.createTab({ title: 'Tab ' + i, window: win }); tabGroup.addTab(tab); } tabGroup.addEventListener('focus', function(e) { Ti.API.info('index: ' + e.index); Ti.API.info('previousIndex: ' + e.previousIndex); Ti.API.info('previousTab.title: ' + e.previousTab.title); Ti.API.info('tab.title: ' + e.tab.title); Ti.API.info('tabGroup.activeTab.title: ' + tabGroup.activeTab.title); }); tabGroup.open();</code> </pre> <p>After the app launches, click Tab 1 and you'll see the info output in the console refers to Tab 0.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1534,12,"""Android: Implement Tableview separatorColor, separatorStyle""","""""""{html}<div><p>separatorColor string the separator color color as a hex or named value<br> separatorStyle int the separator style constant. For iPhone, Titanium.UI.iPhone.TableViewSeparatorStyle</p> <p>See <a href= """"""""http://developer.android.com/reference/android/widget/ListView.html""""""""> ListView</a> We will need our own constants or if the style is the same in both then create common styles.</p> <p>void setDivider(Drawable divider)<br> Sets the drawable that will be drawn between each item in the list.</p> <p>void setDividerHeight(int height)<br> Sets the height of the divider that will be drawn between each item in the list.</p> <p>void setFooterDividersEnabled(boolean footerDividersEnabled)<br> Enables or disables the drawing of the divider for footer views.</p></div>{html}""""""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Implement Tableview separatorColor, separatorStyle
Description: """"{html}<div><p>separatorColor string the separator color color as a hex or named value<br> separatorStyle int the separator style constant. For iPhone, Titanium.UI.iPhone.TableViewSeparatorStyle</p> <p>See <a href= """"""""http://developer.android.com/reference/android/widget/ListView.html""""""""> ListView</a> We will need our own constants or if the style is the same in both then create common styles.</p> <p>void setDivider(Drawable divider)<br> Sets the drawable that will be drawn between each item in the list.</p> <p>void setDividerHeight(int height)<br> Sets the height of the divider that will be drawn between each item in the list.</p> <p>void setFooterDividersEnabled(boolean footerDividersEnabled)<br> Enables or disables the drawing of the divider for footer views.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
