TIMOB-1511,12,"""Kitchensink's Button bar sample bug in sdk 1.4.1""","""""""{html}<div><p>I try Kitchensink's Button bar sample in github master 1.4.1 aug 6 , the Buttonbar display in navBar and toolBar wrong, in 1.4.0 is ok.</p> <p>var bb3 = Titanium.UI.createButtonBar({<br></p> <pre> <code>labels:['One', 'Two'], backgroundColor:'#336699', //style:Titanium.UI.iPhone.SystemButtonStyle.BAR, //width:60</code> </pre> <p>});</p> <p>win.setRightNavButton(bb3);</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Kitchensink's Button bar sample bug in sdk 1.4.1
Description: """"{html}<div><p>I try Kitchensink's Button bar sample in github master 1.4.1 aug 6 , the Buttonbar display in navBar and toolBar wrong, in 1.4.0 is ok.</p> <p>var bb3 = Titanium.UI.createButtonBar({<br></p> <pre> <code>labels:['One', 'Two'], backgroundColor:'#336699', //style:Titanium.UI.iPhone.SystemButtonStyle.BAR, //width:60</code> </pre> <p>});</p> <p>win.setRightNavButton(bb3);</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1510,12,"""Android: Add support for saveToPhotoGallery (Titanium.Media.saveToPhotoGallery)""","""""""Currently only a log warning that it's not supported.</p></div>{html}    The code sample below demonstrates the feature required. Works fine on iphone.  {code:title=""""""""app.js""""""""}    // this sets the background color of the master UIView (when there are no windows/tab groups on it)  Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000');    var win1 = Titanium.UI.createWindow({          backgroundColor:'#fff'  });    var image_view = Ti.UI.createImageView({    image: '<USER>png',    height: 400,    width: 320   });     var button2 =  Ti.UI.createButton({    top:90,    title:'save to gallery',    height:40       });       win1.add(button2);   button2.addEventListener('click', function(e){     win1.add(image_view);    var img = image_view.toBlob();    Titanium.Media.saveToPhotoGallery(img,{     """"""""success"""""""": function(e){      alert('Saved to your camera roll.');      },     """"""""error"""""""": function(e){      alert(e.error);     }    });      });     win1.open();    {code}  """"""",New Feature,"Title: Android: Add support for saveToPhotoGallery (Titanium.Media.saveToPhotoGallery)
Description: """"Currently only a log warning that it's not supported.</p></div>{html} The code sample below demonstrates the feature required. Works fine on iphone. {code:title=""""""""app.js""""""""} // this sets the background color of the master UIView (when there are no windows/tab groups on it) Titanium.UI.setBackgroundColor('#000'); var win1 = Titanium.UI.createWindow({ backgroundColor:'#fff' }); var image_view = Ti.UI.createImageView({ image: '<USER>png', height: 400, width: 320 }); var button2 = Ti.UI.createButton({ top:90, title:'save to gallery', height:40 }); win1.add(button2); button2.addEventListener('click', function(e){ win1.add(image_view); var img = image_view.toBlob(); Titanium.Media.saveToPhotoGallery(img,{ """"""""success"""""""": function(e){ alert('Saved to your camera roll.'); }, """"""""error"""""""": function(e){ alert(e.error); } }); }); win1.open(); {code} """"",title+description
TIMOB-1509,12,"""selectedBackgroundImage property unsupported on Android""","""""""{html}<div><p>In the Kitchen Sink layout example (the first one, the Starbucks-ish one), selectedBackgroundImage is not used when a row is tapped - the default Android orange highlight still shows. Tested on a version of 1.4.1 pulled from master.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: selectedBackgroundImage property unsupported on Android
Description: """"{html}<div><p>In the Kitchen Sink layout example (the first one, the Starbucks-ish one), selectedBackgroundImage is not used when a row is tapped - the default Android orange highlight still shows. Tested on a version of 1.4.1 pulled from master.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1508,12,"""Android: Remote images in ImageView get reloaded inside a TableViewRow""","""""""{html}<div><p>We need to implement a remote Image cache so images aren't unnecessarily reloaded when scrolling a TableView up/down.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Remote images in ImageView get reloaded inside a TableViewRow
Description: """"{html}<div><p>We need to implement a remote Image cache so images aren't unnecessarily reloaded when scrolling a TableView up/down.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1507,12,"""Android: Remote images in ImageView/TableView sporadically causes NPE""","""""""{html}<div><p>It looks like loadDrawable() in TiBackgroundImageLoadTask (line 58) is returning a BitmapDrawable that has a null Bitmap in some instances, we need to more gracefully handle that situation. See helpdesk <a href=""""""""/projects/32238/tickets/35081"""""""" title= """"""""Ticket #35081"""""""">#35081</a>.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Remote images in ImageView/TableView sporadically causes NPE
Description: """"{html}<div><p>It looks like loadDrawable() in TiBackgroundImageLoadTask (line 58) is returning a BitmapDrawable that has a null Bitmap in some instances, we need to more gracefully handle that situation. See helpdesk <a href=""""""""/projects/32238/tickets/35081"""""""" title= """"""""Ticket #35081"""""""">#35081</a>.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1506,12,"""iOS: Dashboard view broken on ipad""","""""""{html}<div><p>Looking at TiUIDashboardView.m it seems like there might be some  iphone specific things baked in, like screen resolution, etc..  Setting height and width manually when creating the view seems to  break things more. When dashboard icons do display, the images are  distorted.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Dashboard view broken on ipad
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Looking at TiUIDashboardView.m it seems like there might be some iphone specific things baked in, like screen resolution, etc.. Setting height and width manually when creating the view seems to break things more. When dashboard icons do display, the images are distorted.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1505,12,"""Ti.JSON.stringify missing from WebView JS context""","""""""{html}<div><p>Ti.JSON.stringify used to be injected into all WebView Javascript contexts. See this commit from last year: <a href= """"""""http://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/9510dc89620677ba615a8a70904b51c4ab711959""""""""> http://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/9510dc89620677ba615a8a70904b51c4ab711959</a></p> <p>But now, it's missing. We were able to fix it by adding the following JS code to our web view:</p> <pre> <code class= """"""""javascript"""""""">Ti.JSON = { stringify: function(x) { return Ti.App._JSON(x, 0); }};</code> </pre> <p>It looks like this line should be added to the <code>kTitaniumJavascript</code> constant in iphone/Classes/TiUIWebView.m and probably to iphone/Classes/bridge.txt. It looks like <code>kTitaniumJavascript</code> is being generated from bridge.txt, but we couldn't find anything in the build <USER>that would do this.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Ti.JSON.stringify missing from WebView JS context
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Ti.JSON.stringify used to be injected into all WebView Javascript contexts. See this commit from last year: <a href= """"""""http://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/9510dc89620677ba615a8a70904b51c4ab711959""""""""> http://github.com/<USER>titanium_mobile/commit/9510dc89620677ba615a8a70904b51c4ab711959</a></p> <p>But now, it's missing. We were able to fix it by adding the following JS code to our web view:</p> <pre> <code class= """"""""javascript"""""""">Ti.JSON = { stringify: function(x) { return Ti.App._JSON(x, 0); }};</code> </pre> <p>It looks like this line should be added to the <code>kTitaniumJavascript</code> constant in iphone/Classes/TiUIWebView.m and probably to iphone/Classes/bridge.txt. It looks like <code>kTitaniumJavascript</code> is being generated from bridge.txt, but we couldn't find anything in the build <USER>that would do this.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1504,12,"""iOS: splitView popover barcolor""","""""""{html}<div><p>When the masterWindow in the splitview is a popover it should  ignore the property """"""""barColor"""""""", since the popover has it's own  style.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: splitView popover barcolor
Description: """"{html}<div><p>When the masterWindow in the splitview is a popover it should ignore the property """"""""barColor"""""""", since the popover has it's own style.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1503,12,"""KitchenSink Errors""","""""""{html}<div><p>When I open KitchenSink application in Eclipse (JavaScript edition) it shows 151 errors and 427 warnings. As the baseline sample app, this seems like it should be fairly clean as it could add subtle errors to users code as they use elements of this code as a starting point. Also, I have found a number of files in the kitchensink application which are no longer used.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: KitchenSink Errors
Description: """"{html}<div><p>When I open KitchenSink application in Eclipse (JavaScript edition) it shows 151 errors and 427 warnings. As the baseline sample app, this seems like it should be fairly clean as it could add subtle errors to users code as they use elements of this code as a starting point. Also, I have found a number of files in the kitchensink application which are no longer used.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1502,12,"""HTTPClient: problems with header parsing and responseData in onerror handler""","""""""{html}<div><p>Using SDK 1.4.0 release. I have a site that gives back a token in both the header (and as the body) of a HTTP 401 (auth required) response. For the life of me, I can't get HTTPClient to be able to get anything but NULL out of this.responseData/this.responseText. Also, this.getResponseHeader() for my custom header always shows up as NULL. If I change the returned code to 200 (OK), then I can get the responseText/responseData result no problem, but still can't parse my custom X-GUID response header.</p> <p>So what I'm trying to say is that I may be running into two separate problems here:</p> <ol> <li> <p>getResponseHeader doesn't appear to work properly on error (4xx/5xx) OR valid (2xx) responses: One user in the Q&amp;A section thinks it's because only the first header is being retrieved:<br> <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/question/49401/cookies-problem"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/question/49401/cookies-problem</a></p> </li> <li> <p>responseData/responseText is not populated on error responses (onerror callback handler), even if there is data in the response body. More info on this at: <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/question/50501/problem-getting-responsetextheaders-from-error-response""""""""> http://developer.<USER>com/question/50501/problem-getting-re...</a></p> </li> <li> <p>having HTTP auth problems with basic HTTP (but that may be already fixed going into 1.5.0, so I'll make another bug if that appears to be the case)</p> </li> </ol> <p>All in all, unless I'm being stupid here, it appears that HTTPClient is pretty broken for all but the most basic usecases in this 1.4.0 release. :/</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: HTTPClient: problems with header parsing and responseData in onerror handler
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Using SDK 1.4.0 release. I have a site that gives back a token in both the header (and as the body) of a HTTP 401 (auth required) response. For the life of me, I can't get HTTPClient to be able to get anything but NULL out of this.responseData/this.responseText. Also, this.getResponseHeader() for my custom header always shows up as NULL. If I change the returned code to 200 (OK), then I can get the responseText/responseData result no problem, but still can't parse my custom X-GUID response header.</p> <p>So what I'm trying to say is that I may be running into two separate problems here:</p> <ol> <li> <p>getResponseHeader doesn't appear to work properly on error (4xx/5xx) OR valid (2xx) responses: One user in the Q&amp;A section thinks it's because only the first header is being retrieved:<br> <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/question/49401/cookies-problem"""""""">http://developer.<USER>com/question/49401/cookies-problem</a></p> </li> <li> <p>responseData/responseText is not populated on error responses (onerror callback handler), even if there is data in the response body. More info on this at: <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/question/50501/problem-getting-responsetextheaders-from-error-response""""""""> http://developer.<USER>com/question/50501/problem-getting-re...</a></p> </li> <li> <p>having HTTP auth problems with basic HTTP (but that may be already fixed going into 1.5.0, so I'll make another bug if that appears to be the case)</p> </li> </ol> <p>All in all, unless I'm being stupid here, it appears that HTTPClient is pretty broken for all but the most basic usecases in this 1.4.0 release. :/</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1501,12,"""View.height and View.Width does not match View.Size""","""""""{html}<div><p>If a view is created using top, bottom, left and right without explicitely setting the height and width, then the values for height and width do not equal the values shown in the size property.</p> <p>see <a href= """"""""http://pastie.org/1081637"""""""">http://pastie.org/1081637</a> for an example</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: View.height and View.Width does not match View.Size
Description: """"{html}<div><p>If a view is created using top, bottom, left and right without explicitely setting the height and width, then the values for height and width do not equal the values shown in the size property.</p> <p>see <a href= """"""""http://pastie.org/1081637"""""""">http://pastie.org/1081637</a> for an example</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1500,12,"""iOS: UI.TableViewRow - opacity does not work""","""""""h2. Problem    Opacity has no effect on TableViewRow.    h2. Test case    {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js}  var win = Ti.UI.createWindow();  var table = Ti.UI.createTableView({    backgroundColor: 'red',    data: [      {        title: 'opacity 0.5',        opacity: 0.5,        backgroundColor: '#fff'      },      {        title: 'opacity 1.0',        backgroundColor: '#fff'      }    ]  });  win.add(table);  win.open();  {code}    h2. Discussions    Q&A: [tableviewrow opacity does not work|http://developer.<USER>com/question/27021/tableviewrow-opacity-does-not-work]    See duplicate TC-273 for a more recent ticket.""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: UI.TableViewRow - opacity does not work
Description: """"h2. Problem Opacity has no effect on TableViewRow. h2. Test case {code:lang=javascript|title=app.js} var win = Ti.UI.createWindow(); var table = Ti.UI.createTableView({ backgroundColor: 'red', data: [ { title: 'opacity 0.5', opacity: 0.5, backgroundColor: '#fff' }, { title: 'opacity 1.0', backgroundColor: '#fff' } ] }); win.add(table); win.open(); {code} h2. Discussions Q&A: [tableviewrow opacity does not work|http://developer.<USER>com/question/27021/tableviewrow-opacity-does-not-work] See duplicate TC-273 for a more recent ticket.""""",title+description
TIMOB-1499,12,"""WebView document ready event""","""""""{html}<div><p>WebView should have an event that fires when the DOM is loaded.</p> <p>Right now, we have 'load', but it only fires when everything has loaded, including images.</p> <p>My situation:<br> I'm loading a page I have no control over. I use some JS with evalJS to style the page. The problem is that the styling only appear after all the images has loaded.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: WebView document ready event
Description: """"{html}<div><p>WebView should have an event that fires when the DOM is loaded.</p> <p>Right now, we have 'load', but it only fires when everything has loaded, including images.</p> <p>My situation:<br> I'm loading a page I have no control over. I use some JS with evalJS to style the page. The problem is that the styling only appear after all the images has loaded.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1498,12,"""iOS: Tableview search visible while animating in""","""""""{html}<div><p>In KitchenSink 1.4, when I click the """"""""Table Search"""""""" row, while  animating in the new view, I can see the searchbar animating up.  This should not happen. The searchbar should not be visible until  the tableview is dragged down.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: iOS: Tableview search visible while animating in
Description: """"{html}<div><p>In KitchenSink 1.4, when I click the """"""""Table Search"""""""" row, while animating in the new view, I can see the searchbar animating up. This should not happen. The searchbar should not be visible until the tableview is dragged down.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
