TIMOB-1025,12,"""Self-signed SSL certificates cause issues for HTTP Client (Android)""","""""""{html}<div><p>We have a community member reporting issues using self-signed SSL certificates (in this case, on a local dev server where he would be using such a cert). A quick Google search reveals that special gymnastics might be necessary to get self-signed certs to work in Android:</p> <p><a href= """"""""http://www.google.com/search?q=android+self-signed+SSL+certificate""""""""> http://www.google.com/search?q=android+self-signed+SSL+certificate</a></p> <p>Will update this ticket with an example ASAP.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Self-signed SSL certificates cause issues for HTTP Client (Android)
Description: """"{html}<div><p>We have a community member reporting issues using self-signed SSL certificates (in this case, on a local dev server where he would be using such a cert). A quick Google search reveals that special gymnastics might be necessary to get self-signed certs to work in Android:</p> <p><a href= """"""""http://www.google.com/search?q=android+self-signed+SSL+certificate""""""""> http://www.google.com/search?q=android+self-signed+SSL+certificate</a></p> <p>Will update this ticket with an example ASAP.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-1024,12,"""Titanium 1.3.0 iPhone apps do not run on iPad in compatibility mode""","""""""{html}<div><p>A 1.3.0 iPhone application does not run on the iPad in compatibility mode - the compatibility mode frame shows up, but the app does not load inside it. This can be recreated by creating a new iPhone project and attempting to run the project using the 3.2 SDK. Confirmed by me and a number of other community members.</p> <p>Titanium Mobile 1.2.1 iPhone projects do not have this issue - they run in compatibility mode on the iPad as you would expect.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Titanium 1.3.0 iPhone apps do not run on iPad in compatibility mode
Description: """"{html}<div><p>A 1.3.0 iPhone application does not run on the iPad in compatibility mode - the compatibility mode frame shows up, but the app does not load inside it. This can be recreated by creating a new iPhone project and attempting to run the project using the 3.2 SDK. Confirmed by me and a number of other community members.</p> <p>Titanium Mobile 1.2.1 iPhone projects do not have this issue - they run in compatibility mode on the iPad as you would expect.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
