Description: """"{html}<div><p>if you change the images array it sort of works, but it has some visual problems</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-586,12,"""Titanium Mobile suggestion: map non-android compatible calls""","""""""{html}<div><p>Cross-platform development between iPhone, iPad, and Android would go a LOT smoother if you created stubs for methods that crash Android and either map them to an alternative interface, or at least just trap them and give the developer a message explaining that the call won't work on Android.</p> <p>For example, the following call works great on iPhone, but crashes on Android:</p> <p>win.setRightNavButton(btnTools);</p> <p>If you could trap that call (when in Android), and turn a call to setRightNavButton into a method that does a simple alert:</p> <p>alert('Warning: setRightNavButton is not implemented on the Android platform');</p> <p>In an ideal world, an alternative object would be created (a view, perhaps) that mimics the iPhone navbar, and setRightNavButton would create a button on that alternative object. This would give true cross-platform compatibility.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Titanium Mobile suggestion: map non-android compatible calls
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Cross-platform development between iPhone, iPad, and Android would go a LOT smoother if you created stubs for methods that crash Android and either map them to an alternative interface, or at least just trap them and give the developer a message explaining that the call won't work on Android.</p> <p>For example, the following call works great on iPhone, but crashes on Android:</p> <p>win.setRightNavButton(btnTools);</p> <p>If you could trap that call (when in Android), and turn a call to setRightNavButton into a method that does a simple alert:</p> <p>alert('Warning: setRightNavButton is not implemented on the Android platform');</p> <p>In an ideal world, an alternative object would be created (a view, perhaps) that mimics the iPhone navbar, and setRightNavButton would create a button on that alternative object. This would give true cross-platform compatibility.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-585,12,"""TI.UI.SearchBar autocapitalizationType Property to to disable auto caps""","""""""{html}<div><p>Have the TiUiSearchBar support no capitalization since this is quite hard to do without.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: TI.UI.SearchBar autocapitalizationType Property to to disable auto caps
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Have the TiUiSearchBar support no capitalization since this is quite hard to do without.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-584,12,"""Android: Large database crashes app""","""""""{html}<div><p>Android: Large database crashes app</p> <p>Installing a large database (6MB, in my test case) causes the application to crash. The same application works fine with the exact same database if it contains fewer rows (a 293KB database works fine.)</p> <p>Steps to reproduce:</p> <ol> <li>Copy large database to Resources folder<br></li> <li>In code, install the database<br></li> <li>Open a recordset (only need to select a few rows)<br></li> <li>Attempting to check rows.isValidRow() causes an immediate crash</li> </ol></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: Large database crashes app
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Android: Large database crashes app</p> <p>Installing a large database (6MB, in my test case) causes the application to crash. The same application works fine with the exact same database if it contains fewer rows (a 293KB database works fine.)</p> <p>Steps to reproduce:</p> <ol> <li>Copy large database to Resources folder<br></li> <li>In code, install the database<br></li> <li>Open a recordset (only need to select a few rows)<br></li> <li>Attempting to check rows.isValidRow() causes an immediate crash</li> </ol></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-583,12,"""Facebook Connect login status should work without button instantiated""","""""""{html}<div><p>Problem:<br> Ti.Facebook.loggedIn, and Ti.Facebook.isLoggedIn() return false before a Connect button is instantiated.</p> <p>Description:<br> It seems the Connect button must be instantiated to fire off the login event that populates those fields.</p> <p>This is not a major issue, but it does present a minor hiccup in the use case where a developer would like to determine if a user is logged into Connect to decide whether or not to show him the login button at the startup screen. In this use case, the Connect logout button would be placed on a separate tab.</p> <p>Workaround:<br> Display the login button, then add an event listener for 'login' to remove the button and continue with app startup.</p> <p>Severity: minor</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Facebook Connect login status should work without button instantiated
Description: """"{html}<div><p>Problem:<br> Ti.Facebook.loggedIn, and Ti.Facebook.isLoggedIn() return false before a Connect button is instantiated.</p> <p>Description:<br> It seems the Connect button must be instantiated to fire off the login event that populates those fields.</p> <p>This is not a major issue, but it does present a minor hiccup in the use case where a developer would like to determine if a user is logged into Connect to decide whether or not to show him the login button at the startup screen. In this use case, the Connect logout button would be placed on a separate tab.</p> <p>Workaround:<br> Display the login button, then add an event listener for 'login' to remove the button and continue with app startup.</p> <p>Severity: minor</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-582,12,"""Android: title in row needs to take account of left and right image""","""""""{html}<div><p>See <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/question/3791/tableviewrow-layout-problem""""""""> Q&amp;A tableViewRow layout problem</a></p> <p>The desired behavior seems to be for the title element to be contained between the leftImage and rightImage. Controls added to a row, do not respect the boundaries of the leftImage and rightImage since they can be directly positioned.</p> <p>I've attached a screenshot from iPhone.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: Android: title in row needs to take account of left and right image
Description: """"{html}<div><p>See <a href= """"""""http://developer.<USER>com/question/3791/tableviewrow-layout-problem""""""""> Q&amp;A tableViewRow layout problem</a></p> <p>The desired behavior seems to be for the title element to be contained between the leftImage and rightImage. Controls added to a row, do not respect the boundaries of the leftImage and rightImage since they can be directly positioned.</p> <p>I've attached a screenshot from iPhone.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-581,12,"""cannot add synthetic events to window""","""""""{html}<div><p>The documentation for window suggests you can add an fire a synthetic event for a window.</p> <p>See bottom of:<br> <a href= """"""""https://developer.<USER>com/apidoc/mobile/1.0/Titanium.UI.Window""""""""> https://developer.<USER>com/apidoc/mobile/1.0/Titanium.UI.Wi...</a></p> <p>However, the following example app.js does not work:</p> <p><a href= """"""""http://gist.github.com/335896"""""""">http://gist.github.com/335896</a></p> <p>The only synthetic events I've been able to successfully add/fire have been global application ones (Ti.App.addEventListener...). This however, has its own architectural issues namely, that you are now responsible for tearing it down yourself.</p> <p>Tested against iphone simulator.</p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: cannot add synthetic events to window
Description: """"{html}<div><p>The documentation for window suggests you can add an fire a synthetic event for a window.</p> <p>See bottom of:<br> <a href= """"""""https://developer.<USER>com/apidoc/mobile/1.0/Titanium.UI.Window""""""""> https://developer.<USER>com/apidoc/mobile/1.0/Titanium.UI.Wi...</a></p> <p>However, the following example app.js does not work:</p> <p><a href= """"""""http://gist.github.com/335896"""""""">http://gist.github.com/335896</a></p> <p>The only synthetic events I've been able to successfully add/fire have been global application ones (Ti.App.addEventListener...). This however, has its own architectural issues namely, that you are now responsible for tearing it down yourself.</p> <p>Tested against iphone simulator.</p></div>{html}""""",title+description
TIMOB-580,12,"""toolbar at bottom:0 moves up on orientation change""","""""""{html}<div><p>Controls =&gt; Toolbar</p> <p>rotate device, bottom toolbar moves up from bottom:0</p> <p><a href= """"""""http://helpdesk.<USER>net/tickets/1389"""""""">http://helpdesk.<USER>net/tickets/1389</a></p></div>{html}""""""",Bug,"Title: toolbar at bottom:0 moves up on orientation change
